fn documentChunk(j: usize) -> StringView => match (j) {
    0 =>
        "# Basics\n\nThis chapter covers what every program uses: values and types, variables, functions, operators, and control flow.\nEach example is a whole program you can run with `ceesafec run --interp file.cee`.\n\n## A program\n\nA program is one or more `.cee` files with a `main` function. `main` takes nothing and returns nothing or an `i32`\nexit status.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello, CeeSafe\");\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello, CeeSafe\n```\n\n`Console.write` and `Console.writeLine` print integers, booleans, floats and text.\n\n## Types\n\n| Type | Values |\n|---|---|\n| `i8` `i16` `i32` `i64` | signed integers, two's complement |\n| `u8` `u16` `u32` `u64` | unsigned integers |\n| `isize` `usize` | the target's pointer width; lengths and indexes are `usize` |\n| `f32` `f64` | IEEE 754 floating point |\n| `bool` | `true`, `false` |\n| `StringView` | a read-only view of UTF-8 text; string literals have this type |\n\nLater chapters add structs, enums, arrays, references, optionals (`T?`), results (`Result<T, E>`), boxes and\ncollections. There is no `char`: text is UTF-8 bytes, and a character literal such as `'k'` is an integer.\n\n## Variables\n\n`let` declares a variable that cannot be assigned again; `var` one that can. The type is inferred from the\ninitialiser, or written after a colon.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let name = \"CeeSafe\";              // StringView\n    let year: u16 = 2026;\n    var count = 0;                  // i32: an integer literal's default type\n    count += 1;\n    var total: u64;                 // declared now, assigned before it is read\n    total = 40 + 2;\n    Console.writeLine(name);\n    Console.writeLine(year);\n    Console.writeLine(count);\n    Console.writeLine(total);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nCeeSafe\n2026\n1\n42\n```\n\nReading a variable that might not be assigned yet is a compile error, and so is assigning to a `let`. Two locals in\nscope cannot have the same name: there is no shadowing.\n\n## Integer literals and the expected type\n\nAn integer literal takes its type from where it is used. In `let year: u16 = 2026;` it is a `u16`; in `x + 1` it is\n`x`'s type; with nothing to go by it is an `i32`. A suffix fixes it: `7u8`, `100_000u64`. Literals can be written in\nhex, binary and octal (`0xFF`, `0b1010`, `0o17`), with `_` between digits. A value that does not fit its type is a\ncompile error.\n\nA character literal is the character's Unicode value, typed like any other literal:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn isDigit(c: u8) -> bool => c >= '0' && c <= '9';\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(isDigit('7'));\n    Console.writeLine(isDigit('x'));\n    Console.writeLine('\\n' as u32);\n}\n```\n\n```text\ntrue\nfalse\n10\n```\n\n## Functions\n\n```ceesafe\nfn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nfn square(x: i64) -> i64 => x * x;        // an expression body\n\nfn greet(name: StringView) {              // no return type: returns nothing\n    Console.write(\"Hello, \");\n    Console.writeLine(name);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(add(2, square(3)));\n    greet(\"reader\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\n11\nHello, reader\n```\n\nParameters are immutable. Arguments are evaluated left to right. A function that returns a value must return on\nevery path, which the compiler checks.\n\n## Operators\n\nBoth operands of an operator must have the same type: there are **no implicit conversions**, not even between `i32`\nand `i64`.\n\n| Operators | On | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+ - * / %` | integers, floats | integer overflow stops the program; so does dividing by zero |\n| `& \\| ^ << >>` | unsigned integers | a shift by the width or more stops the program |\n| `== != < <= > >=` | integers, floats, `bool` (`==`, `!=`), text (`==`, `!=`) | |\n| `&& \\|\\| !` | `bool` | `&&` and `\\|\\|` short-circuit |\n\nWhen overflow is expected, say what should happen:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let big: u8 = 250;\n    Console.writeLine(big.wrappingAdd(10));       // 4: modulo 256\n    Console.writeLine(big.saturatingAdd(10));     // 255: clamped\n    match (big.checkedAdd(10)) {                  // u8?: nu",
    1 =>
        "ll on overflow\n        Some(sum) => { Console.writeLine(sum); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"overflow\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n255\noverflow\n```\n\nThe same three families exist for `Sub` and `Mul`, on every integer type.\n\n## Conversions\n\n`as` converts only when no value can be lost: `u8 as u32`, `i32 as i64`, `u32 as i64`, `bool as u8`, `i32 as f64`.\nAnything that could lose information is spelled out:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let big: u32 = 300;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<u8>(big));             // 44: the low 8 bits\n    let x: f64 = 2.75;\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<i32>(x));              // 2: toward zero; out of range stops the program\n    Console.writeLine(round<f64>(1234567890123u64));  // the nearest f64\n    let n: i32 = -1;\n    Console.writeLine(n as i64);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n44\n2\n1234567890123\n-1\n```\n\n## Control flow\n\n`if`, `while`, `for` and `match` put their condition in parentheses, and their bodies are blocks.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var n = 0;\n    while (n < 3) {\n        n += 1;\n    }\n    for (i in 0..3) {                    // 0, 1, 2\n        if (i == 1) {\n            continue;\n        }\n        Console.write(i);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine();\n    var tries = 0;\n    loop {                               // until a break\n        tries += 1;\n        if (tries == 5) {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(n + tries);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n02\n8\n```\n\n`for (x in values)` walks an array, a slice or a vector (see [Collections](collections.md)).\n\n### `if` and `match` are expressions too\n\n```ceesafe\nfn describe(n: i32) -> StringView => match (n) {\n    0 => \"zero\",\n    1 => \"one\",\n    _ => if (n < 0) \"negative\" else \"many\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(describe(0));\n    Console.writeLine(describe(7));\n    Console.writeLine(describe(-2));\n    let sign = if (-2 < 0) -1 else 1;\n    Console.writeLine(sign);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nzero\nmany\nnegative\n-1\n```\n\nAn `if` expression needs its `else`. A branch can also be a block that leaves, such as `{ return; }`, which is how\nan early exit fits inside an expression.\n\n## Constants\n\n```ceesafe\nconst SECONDS_PER_DAY: u32 = 24 * 60 * 60;\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(SECONDS_PER_DAY);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n86400\n```\n\nA constant is evaluated at compile time. An overflow there is a compile error rather than a run-time stop.\n\n## Comments\n\n`//` comments run to the end of the line, `/* … */` comments may span lines, and `///` before an item is its\ndocumentation, which [`ceesafec doc`](formatting-and-docs.md) collects.\n\n## Next\n\n[Structs, enums and patterns](structs-and-enums.md).\n",
    2 =>
        "# The ceesafec command\n\n`ceesafec` is the compiler and every tool around it. `ceesafec --help` prints the same summary as this page.\n\nInside a [package](packages.md), commands take no file names: they use the nearest `ceesafe.toml` in the current\ndirectory or above. Outside one, give the files.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `ceesafec new <name> [--lib] [--agent <names>] [--no-agent]` | make a package: `ceesafe.toml`, `src/main.cee` (or `src/lib.cee`), and `AGENTS.md` for [AI agents](for-agents.md) |\n| `ceesafec agents [--agent <names>]` | write or refresh the package's agent instructions |\n| `ceesafec fetch [--offline]` | resolve the package's dependencies and write `ceesafe.lock` |\n| `ceesafec check` | parse, type-check and borrow-check; the fastest way to find errors |\n| `ceesafec build` | compile to C, then with the C compiler to `out/<name>` (or `-o`) |\n| `ceesafec run --interp` | run the program in the reference interpreter |\n| `ceesafec parse` | parse only, reporting syntax errors |\n| `ceesafec fmt [--check \\| --stdout]` | format in the canonical style ([Formatting](formatting-and-docs.md)) |\n| `ceesafec doc [-o file] [--title t] [--missing]` | a Markdown page of the public items ([Documentation](formatting-and-docs.md#documentation)) |\n| `ceesafec lsp` | the language server, for [editors](editors.md) |\n| `ceesafec fix [--dry-run]` | apply the fixes the compiler is sure of, and check again until none applies |\n| `ceesafec explain <code>` | what a diagnostic code (`N0249`) means |\n| `ceesafec mcp` | the MCP server, for [AI agents](for-agents.md) |\n\n## Building\n\n| Option | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `-o <path>` | the output file (or directory, with `--emit c`) |\n| `--emit exe` | an executable (the default) |\n| `--emit c` | the generated C sources, to look at or to build yourself |\n| `--emit obj` | one relocatable object, for a kernel or another program's link (`--ld ld.lld` joins objects for ELF cross builds) |\n| `--cc <compiler>` | the C compiler; default `$CC`, else `cc`, or on Windows clang, gcc or Visual Studio's `cl`, found and set up ([tooling](../spec/tooling.md) §8) |\n| `--cflag <flag>` | an extra C compiler flag (repeatable); the default is `-O2` |\n| `-g` | a debug build: `#line` directives and `-g -O0` (see [Debugging](debugging.md)) |\n| `--line-directives` | `#line` directives without changing the optimisation level |\n| `--profile hosted \\| freestanding` | the [profile](kernel.md#the-freestanding-profile) |\n| `--no-float` | reject every float type and literal |\n| `--offline` | in a package: never run `git` |\n\nC files given with the CeeSafe files (`ceesafec build main.cee shim.c`) are compiled and linked with the program.\n\n## Seeing what the compiler decided\n\n| Option | Prints |\n|---|---|\n| `--explain-allocations` | each allocation site, and whether its box lives on the stack or the heap, and why |\n| `--explain-bounds-checks` | each index check, and whether it was removed |\n| `--explain-overflow-checks` | each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation, and whether its check was removed |\n| `--no-placement`, `--no-bce`, `--no-oce` | turn off box placement, bounds-check elimination, overflow-check elimination |\n| `--dump-ast`, `--dump-hir`, `--dump-nir` | the compiler's intermediate forms (`parse`, `check`, `build`) |\n\nThe optimisations never remove a check that could fail; their preconditions are in\n[Optimisations](../spec/optimisations.md).\n\n## Exit status and errors\n\n`ceesafec` exits with 0 on success, 1 when the program has errors, and 2 for a usage error. Diagnostics go to standard\nerror as `file:line:column: error[code]: message`, followed by the source line, notes, and a hint where there is one.\nThe codes are listed in [Diagnostics](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n\nWith `--diagnostics-format=json` (on `parse`, `check`, `build`, `run`, `fmt` and `fix`), they go to standard output\ninstead, as one JSON document with each diagnostic's position, message and fixes, for programs and\n[AI agents](for-agents.md) ([tooling](",
    3 =>
        "../spec/tooling.md) §5).\n\n## The interpreter\n\n`ceesafec run --interp` runs a program without a C compiler, in an interpreter that executes the compiler's own\nintermediate form. It stops with an exit status of 70 on any use of freed, out-of-bounds or uninitialised memory, or\na bad free, which the C build would not detect. The test suite runs every program both ways and compares the results.\n",
    4 =>
        "# Functions and closures\n\n## Functions are values\n\nA function can be stored in a variable and passed as an argument. Its type is written `fn(parameter types) -> result`:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn double(x: i32) -> i32 => x * 2;\nfn negate(x: i32) -> i32 => -x;\n\nfn applyTwice(f: fn(i32) -> i32, value: i32) -> i32 => f(f(value));\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(applyTwice(double, 3));\n    var op: fn(i32) -> i32 = double;\n    op = negate;\n    Console.writeLine(op(5));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n-5\n```\n\nA function value is one pointer, and calling it is an indirect call.\n\n## Closures\n\nA closure is a function written inline, `(parameters) => body`, that can use the variables around it. It borrows\nwhat it captures, so it is a kind of reference: its type is `borrow fn(…) -> R` if it only reads what it captures,\nand `borrow mut fn(…) -> R` if it changes something.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn countIf(values: Slice<i32>, keep: borrow fn(i32) -> bool) -> i32 {\n    var n = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        if (keep(v)) {\n            n += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return n;\n}\n\nfn forEach(values: Slice<i32>, visit: borrow mut fn(i32)) {\n    for (v in values) {\n        visit(v);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = [3, 8, 1, 9, 4];\n    let limit = 4;\n    Console.writeLine(countIf(values[..], (v) => v > limit));     // captures `limit`\n    var total = 0;\n    forEach(values[..], (v) => { total += v; });                   // changes `total`\n    Console.writeLine(total);\n    let twice = (x: i32) => x * 2;                                  // captures nothing: a plain fn\n    Console.writeLine(twice(21));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n25\n42\n```\n\n- Parameter types can be left out where the expected type gives them, as in the calls above.\n- The body is an expression, or a block: `(v) => { total += v; }`.\n- A closure that captures nothing is an ordinary function value, and can be stored anywhere a function can.\n\n### Closures do not outlive their block\n\nBecause a closure borrows its captures, the borrow checker treats it like any reference: it cannot be returned from\nthe function that made it, stored in a struct, or kept after a captured variable changes. That makes closures free\nof allocation and safe to use in kernels.\n\n### Owning closures\n\nA `move` literal moves what it captures into the closure, so the closure can outlive the function that made it. It\nis boxed, with an allocation you can see and handle, as a `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R>`. A closure that changes what it\ncaptured is a `Box<dyn mut fn(…) -> R>`, and calling it needs mutable access.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn adder(n: u64) -> Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64> {\n    return Box.create(move (x) => x + n)!;\n}\n\nfn counter() -> Box<dyn mut fn() -> u64> {\n    var count: u64 = 0;\n    return Box.create(move () => {\n        count += 1;\n        return count;\n    })!;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let add2 = adder(2);\n    Console.writeLine(add2(3));\n    var next = counter();\n    next();\n    Console.writeLine(next());\n    var steps = Vector<Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>>.create();\n    steps.push(adder(1))!;\n    steps.push(adder(10))!;\n    var x: u64 = 0;\n    for (step in steps) {\n        x = step(x);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(x);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n2\n11\n```\n\n- The literal goes straight into `Box.create(…)` where a `Box<dyn fn…>` is expected; its parameter types come from\n  there.\n- A captured variable is moved in, and cannot be used afterwards (a Copy one is copied). It cannot hold a reference.\n- The body changes the closure's own copy of what it captured, and cannot move it out.\n\n## Methods that take closures\n\nSlices, arrays (through `a[..]`) and vectors have `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, and vectors `retain`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Reading {\n    sensor: u32;\n    celsius: f64;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var readings = Vector<Reading>.create();\n    for (i in 0..8u32) {\n        readings.push(Reading { sensor: i % 3, celsius: 18.5 + (i as f64) * 0.75 })!;\n    }\n    let limit = 20.0;\n    readings.retain((r) => r.celsius > limit);\n    readings.sortBy((a, b) => if (a.celsius > b.celsius) ",
    5 =>
        "-1 else if (a.celsius < b.celsius) 1 else 0);\n    var sum = 0.0;\n    readings.forEach((r) => { sum += r.celsius; });\n    Console.writeLine(readings.length);\n    Console.writeLine(readings[0].celsius);\n    Console.writeLine(readings.any((r) => r.sensor == 0));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n23.75\ntrue\n```\n\n`sortBy` is an introsort: it allocates nothing and runs in O(n log n), but it is not stable. Its comparator returns a\nnegative number, zero or a positive number. These methods are written in CeeSafe, in `lib/core/slices.cee`.\n\n## Effect lists\n\nIn kernel code, a callback may run where sleeping or allocating is forbidden. A function type can say what its\nfunctions may do: `@effects() fn(i32)` may neither sleep nor allocate, and `@effects(alloc) fn(i32)` may allocate but\nnot sleep. The compiler checks every function and closure given for it. See [Kernel code](kernel.md).\n\n```ceesafe\nfn onEach(values: Slice<u32>, f: borrow mut @effects() fn(u32)) {\n    for (v in values) {\n        f(v);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var largest: u32 = 0;\n    let values = [4u32, 11, 7];\n    onEach(values[..], (v) => {\n        if (v > largest) {\n            largest = v;\n        }\n    });\n    Console.writeLine(largest);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n11\n```\n\n## Floats\n\n`f32` and `f64` follow IEEE 754: no operation on them panics, and NaN and the infinities behave as specified. They\nprint as the shortest text that reads back as the same value.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn mean(values: Slice<f64>) -> f64 {\n    var sum = 0.0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        sum += v;\n    }\n    return sum / round<f64>(values.length);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let samples = [1.5, 2.25, 4.0];\n    let m = mean(samples[..]);\n    Console.writeLine(m);\n    Console.writeLine(m.sqrt());\n    Console.writeLine(truncate<i64>(m * 1000.0));\n    Console.writeLine(0.1 + 0.2);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2.5833333333333335\n1.6072751268321592\n2583\n0.30000000000000004\n```\n\nIntegers become floats with `as` when exact (`i32 as f64`) or `round<f64>(n)`; floats become integers with\n`truncate<T>(x)` (toward zero; out of range stops the program), `checkedTruncate` or `saturatingTruncate`. The math\nmethods are `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor`, `ceil`, `min` and `max`. Kernel code compiles with `--no-float`, which rejects\nfloat types altogether.\n\n## Next\n\n[Threads](threads.md).\n",
    6 =>
        "# Arrays, slices and collections\n\n## Arrays\n\n`[T; N]` is `N` values of `T`, inline: on the stack, or inside the struct that holds it. Arrays are values (copied\nwhen assigned) and their element type must be Copy.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var squares = [0u32; 5];               // five zeros\n    for (i in 0..squares.length) {\n        squares[i] = truncate<u32>(i * i);    // usize to u32 could lose bits: say so\n    }\n    let primes = [2, 3, 5, 7];\n    var sum = 0;\n    for (p in primes) {\n        sum += p;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(squares[4]);\n    Console.writeLine(sum);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n16\n17\n```\n\nEvery index is checked: `a[i]` with `i >= a.length` stops the program. The compiler removes the check where it can\nprove the index is in range, as in both loops above.\n\n## Slices\n\n`Slice<T>` is a borrowed view of consecutive elements, and `SliceMut<T>` a borrowed view that can change them. They\nare a pointer and a length, they follow every rule for references, and they are how functions take \"some elements\":\n\n```ceesafe\nfn total(values: Slice<i64>) -> i64 {\n    var sum: i64 = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        sum += v;\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nfn double(values: SliceMut<i64>) {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        values[i] *= 2;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = [1i64, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    double(mut numbers[1..3]);              // elements 1 and 2\n    Console.writeLine(total(numbers[..]));\n    Console.writeLine(total(numbers[3..]));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n20\n9\n```\n\n`a[lo..hi]`, `a[lo..]`, `a[..hi]` and `a[..]` take elements `lo` up to `hi`; the range is checked. A slice borrows\nthe whole array it came from, so the array cannot change while the slice is used.\n\nSlices also copy and fill in one checked operation (`d.copyFrom(s)`, `d.fill(v)`), swap elements, and sort and\nsearch with closures (see [Functions and closures](closures.md)).\n\n## Vectors\n\n`Vector<T>` owns a growable list on the heap. Pushing can fail, so `push` returns a `Result`.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();          // allocates nothing yet\n    for (i in 0..5) {\n        v.push(i * 10)!;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(v.length);\n    Console.writeLine(v[2]);\n    match (v.pop()) {                      // i32?: null when empty\n        Some(last) => { Console.writeLine(last); }\n        null => {}\n    }\n    v[0] = 99;\n    var sum = 0;\n    for (x in v) {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(sum);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n20\n40\n159\n```\n\nA vector can be passed where a `Slice<T>` is expected (it lends its elements), and `mut v` where a `SliceMut<T>` is.\n`v.view()` and `v.viewMut()` give the slices explicitly. Other members: `capacity`, `clear()`, `truncate(n)`,\n`swap(i, j)`, and `Vector<T>.withCapacity(n)`.\n\n**Invalidation is a compile error.** Growing a vector may move its elements, so while any element or view of it is\nborrowed, `push`, `pop`, `clear` and writes are rejected. This is the most common thing the borrow checker catches\nin code that would crash in C++.\n\n## Text: `StringView` and `String`\n\n`StringView` is a read-only view of UTF-8 text: string literals are views, and so are slices of strings. `String`\nowns growable text.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn greet(name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n    var s = String.withCapacity(name.length + 7)?;\n    s.append(\"Hello, \")?;\n    s.append(name)?;\n    s.append(\"! You are visitor \")?;\n    s.appendNumber(42)?;\n    return Ok(s);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let message = greet(\"Ada\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(message);\n    let word: StringView = \"CeeSafe language\";\n    Console.writeLine(word[0..7]);\n    Console.writeLine(word.length);\n    Console.writeLine(word == \"CeeSafe language\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello, Ada! You are visitor 42\nCeeSafe\n16\ntrue\n```\n\n- `length` is in bytes; `s[i]` is a byte (`u8`); `s.bytes()` is a `Slice<u8>`.\n- Slicing (`s[lo..hi]`) checks that both ends fall on character boundaries.\n- A `String` can only receive valid UTF-8 (`append` takes a view, which always is), so its contents are always valid\n  ",
    7 =>
        "text.\n- `==` compares bytes.\n\n## Stable references: `SlotMap` and `StableVector`\n\nSometimes values must refer to each other, as in a graph. A reference stored in a value would borrow the collection\nfor as long as the value lives. A `SlotMap` gives out **handles** instead: plain values that name an element, can be\nstored anywhere, and are checked on every use, so a handle to a removed element finds nothing instead of something\nelse.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32;\n    next: Handle<Node>?;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var nodes = SlotMap<Node>.create();\n    let a = nodes.insert(Node { value: 1, next: null })!;\n    let b = nodes.insert(Node { value: 2, next: a })!;\n    match (nodes.get(b)) {\n        Some(node) => { Console.writeLine(node.value); }\n        null => {}\n    }\n    let removed = nodes.remove(a);\n    Console.writeLine(nodes.get(a) == null);             // the handle is stale now\n    Console.writeLine(nodes.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\ntrue\n1\n```\n\n`StableVector<T>` is a grow-only sequence whose elements never move, so an element can stay borrowed while more are\npushed.\n\n## Next\n\n[Generics and interfaces](generics.md).\n",
    8 =>
        "# CeeSafe for C and C++ programmers\n\nCeeSafe compiles to C, calls C directly, and is designed to live inside C codebases, kernels included. What it adds is a\ncompiler that tracks who owns each piece of memory and who is looking at it, and rejects the programs that would use\nit wrongly. What it removes is undefined behaviour.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| C / C++ | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `int32_t x = 1;` | `let x: i32 = 1;` (or `var` to change it) | |\n| `T *`, `const T *` | `borrow mut T`, `borrow T` in safe code; `*mut T`, `*T` in `unsafe` | references are never null and never dangle |\n| `malloc` / `free`, `new` / `delete` | `Box.create(v)`; freed by its owner | allocation failure is a `Result`, not `NULL` or an exception |\n| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | `Box<T>` | |\n| `std::vector<T>` | `Vector<T>` | growing it while an element is referenced is a compile error |\n| `std::string`, `std::string_view` | `String`, `StringView` | UTF-8 only |\n| `std::optional<T>` | `T?` | |\n| destructors, RAII | `drop { … }` blocks; destruction at scope end | moves leave nothing behind to destroy twice |\n| `std::move(x)` | assignment moves by default; `move x` to end a use early | using a moved value is a compile error |\n| exceptions, `errno`, return codes | `Result<T, E>` and `?` | |\n| `assert`, `abort` | panics: bounds, overflow, unwrap | always on |\n| templates | generics with interface bounds | checked once, before use; no SFINAE |\n| `#include`, the preprocessor | packages and `import` | no macros, no conditional compilation |\n| `struct` layout | the same as C's, in declaration order | `@repr(C)` to cross the boundary |\n| `volatile` | `Volatile<T>`, `p.readVolatile()` | |\n| `-fsanitize=address,undefined` | needed only for `unsafe` code; `ceesafec run --interp` checks it | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\n    int &first = v[0];\n    v.push_back(4);          // may reallocate: `first` now dangles\n    std::printf(\"%d\\n\", first);\n}\n```\n\nIn C++ this compiles and has undefined behaviour. In CeeSafe it does not compile:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();\n    v.push(1)!;\n    let first = borrow v[0];\n    v.push(4)!;\n    Console.writeLine(first);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:5: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'v' as mutable while 'v[0]' is borrowed\n  |\n5 |     v.push(4)!;\n  |     ^\n  = note: 'v[0]' is borrowed here (4:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (6:5)\n```\n\nThe fixes are the ones you would reach for anyway: copy the value (`let first = v[0];`), or take the reference after\nthe last push.\n\n## No undefined behaviour\n\nEvery operation that is undefined in C is defined in CeeSafe, by a check or by a rule:\n\n| Undefined in C | In CeeSafe |\n|---|---|\n| signed overflow | stops the program (`wrappingAdd` and friends where wrapping is meant) |\n| out-of-bounds access | stops the program |\n| use after free, double free | compile errors |\n| reading uninitialised memory | a compile error |\n| null dereference | there are no null references; optionals must be checked |\n| shift by the width or more | stops the program |\n| unsequenced modifications | evaluation order is left to right |\n| strict aliasing violations | the generated C never relies on them |\n\nThe generated C is written so that it has no undefined behaviour itself, and is warning-free under strict flags, so\nthe C compiler's optimiser has nothing to exploit.\n\n## Working with an existing C codebase\n\n- Call C functions from an `extern \"C\"` block, inside `unsafe`.\n- Export CeeSafe functions with `@export`; `ceesafec` writes `ceesafe_exports.h` for your C code to include.\n- Use your codebase's own structs from its headers (`@header`, `@ctype`) instead of re-declaring them: the C compiler\n  checks every field CeeSafe uses.\n- Build a freestanding object with `--profile freestanding --emit obj` and link it with your build.\n\nSee [Unsafe code and C](../unsafe-and-c.md) and [Kernel code]",
    9 =>
        "(../kernel.md).\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nThe preprocessor, inline assembly, unions (enums with payloads replace tagged unions), bit fields, pointer\narithmetic in safe code, varargs, goto, and in C++, classes with inheritance and virtual functions, operator\noverloading, overloads, exceptions, and `constexpr` evaluation.\n",
    10 =>
        "# CeeSafe for C#, Java and TypeScript programmers\n\nCeeSafe's syntax was chosen to look like C# and TypeScript: braces, `fn name(x: T) -> R`, `let` and `var`, generics in\nangle brackets, `public` and `internal`, `using`, interfaces. Underneath, it works very differently: there is no\ngarbage collector, no classes, no inheritance, no exceptions and no null references. Memory is managed at compile\ntime, by tracking ownership.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| C# / Java / TypeScript | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `var x = 1;` / `const x = 1;` | `var x = 1;` / `let x = 1;` | |\n| `class` | `struct` with methods | no inheritance; values, not references |\n| `interface`, `class C : I` | `interface`, `impl I for C` | interfaces are generic bounds, not types |\n| `new Point(1, 2)` | `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }` | on the stack, or `Box.create(…)` on the heap |\n| garbage collection | ownership: destroyed when the owner goes away | deterministic, at a point the language defines |\n| `IDisposable`, `using` | `drop { … }`, `using` | every owner runs `drop`, not only `using` |\n| `null` references | `T?`, which must be checked | a plain `T` is never null |\n| exceptions, `try`/`catch` | `Result<T, E>`, `?`, `match` | panics are bugs and cannot be caught |\n| `ref`, `out` | `borrow mut T` parameters, `mut x` at the call | |\n| `List<T>`, `string` | `Vector<T>`, `String` / `StringView` | |\n| LINQ, lambdas | `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain`; `(x) => x > 2` | lambdas borrow and cannot escape |\n| `async` / `await`, threads | not yet | |\n| NuGet, Maven, npm | `ceesafe.toml`, path and git dependencies | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```csharp\nclass Account {\n    public long Balance { get; private set; }\n    public void Deposit(long amount) => Balance += amount;\n}\n\nvar a = new Account();\na.Deposit(100);\nConsole.WriteLine(a.Balance);\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    private balance: i64;\n\n    public fn create() -> Account => Account { balance: 0 };\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n    public fn current(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;     // a method cannot share a field's name\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var a = Account.create();\n    a.deposit(100);\n    Console.writeLine(a.current());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n100\n```\n\nThe receiver says how a method uses its object: `borrow this` reads it, `borrow mut this` changes it, and `this`\ntakes it by value. Calling `deposit` needs `a` to be a `var`.\n\n## What changes\n\n**Values, not references.** Assigning a struct copies it (if every field is copyable) or moves it, never shares it.\nTwo variables never point to the same object unless one *borrows* the other, and a borrow is checked: while something\nis borrowed mutably, nothing else can use it.\n\n**Destruction is deterministic.** An object is destroyed when its owner goes out of scope, by assignment, or when the\nobject that owns it is destroyed. A `drop` block runs then, exactly once: closing a file or releasing a lock does not\ndepend on a collector or a `finally`.\n\n**Errors are return values.** A function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`; the caller matches it, or passes it on\nwith `?`. Out of memory is an error value too. Bugs (an index out of range, an overflow) stop the program instead of\nthrowing.\n\n**No null.** `StringView` can never be null; `StringView?` can, and the compiler requires a check before use.\n\n**Generics are compiled per type,** like C++ templates and unlike Java's erasure, and checked once, like C#'s.\nInterfaces constrain type parameters (`<T: Shape>`), but there is no `List<Shape>` holding different shapes: use an\nenum.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nClasses and inheritance, interfaces as types, reflection, attributes on everything, properties, events, LINQ's query\nsyntax, string interpolation, exceptions, `async`, threads, and a large standard library.\n",
    11 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Go programmers\n\nGo and CeeSafe share some taste: a small language, errors as values, `defer`, a canonical formatter, packages as\ndirectories, and fast feedback. They differ in how memory is managed. Go has a garbage collector and a runtime; CeeSafe\nhas neither, and tracks ownership at compile time instead, which is what lets it run in a kernel.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Go | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x := 1`, `var x int32` | `let x = 1;`, `var x: i32 = 0;` | `let` cannot be reassigned |\n| `func f(a int) (int, error)` | `fn f(a: i32) -> Result<i32, E>` | |\n| `if err != nil { return err }` | `?` | |\n| `panic`, `recover` | panics abort; no recovery | |\n| `defer` | `defer` | runs at the end of the block, not the function |\n| slices `[]T` (growable) | `Vector<T>` (owns, grows) and `Slice<T>` (borrows) | a slice cannot outlive what it views |\n| maps | not built in yet | `SlotMap` for handles |\n| `nil` | `null`, only for optionals (`T?`) | |\n| interfaces, satisfied implicitly | interfaces, implemented explicitly with `impl` | generic bounds, resolved at compile time |\n| generics `[T any]` | `<T>` | |\n| goroutines, channels | not yet | |\n| `gofmt` | `ceesafec fmt` | one canonical style, no options |\n| `go.mod`, `go.sum` | `ceesafe.toml`, `ceesafe.lock` | path and git dependencies, pinned by commit and hash |\n| `go vet`, the race detector | the compiler: ownership, borrows, context effects | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"errors\"\n    \"fmt\"\n)\n\nfunc divide(a, b int) (int, error) {\n    if b == 0 {\n        return 0, errors.New(\"division by zero\")\n    }\n    return a / b, nil\n}\n\nfunc average(xs []int) (int, error) {\n    sum := 0\n    for _, x := range xs {\n        sum += x\n    }\n    return divide(sum, len(xs))\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    avg, err := average([]int{3, 5, 10})\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Println(err)\n        return\n    }\n    fmt.Println(avg)\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nenum MathError {\n    DivisionByZero,\n}\n\nfn divide(a: i64, b: i64) -> Result<i64, MathError> {\n    if (b == 0) {\n        return Err(MathError.DivisionByZero);\n    }\n    return Ok(a / b);\n}\n\nfn average(xs: Slice<i64>) -> Result<i64, MathError> {\n    var sum: i64 = 0;\n    for (x in xs) {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    return divide(sum, truncate<i64>(xs.length));    // usize to i64 could lose bits: say so\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = [3i64, 5, 10];\n    match (average(values[..])) {\n        Ok(avg) => { Console.writeLine(avg); }\n        Err(MathError.DivisionByZero) => { Console.writeLine(\"division by zero\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n```\n\n`xs.length` is a `usize`, and CeeSafe has no implicit conversions: `as` converts only where nothing can be lost, so a\n`usize` becomes an `i64` through `truncate<i64>`.\n\n## What changes\n\n**No garbage collector.** Every value has one owner and is destroyed when the owner goes away. Where Go lets any\nnumber of pointers share an object and keeps it alive, CeeSafe lets one owner keep it and others *borrow* it for a while,\nand checks that no borrow outlives the object.\n\n**Allocation is visible and fallible.** Values live on the stack unless the code asks for a box, a vector or a string,\nand each of those returns a `Result`: out of memory is an error, not a crash.\n\n**Errors propagate with `?`** instead of `if err != nil`. The error type is part of the signature, usually an enum.\n\n**Interfaces are explicit and static.** A type implements an interface with an `impl` block, and interfaces are used\nas generic bounds, resolved at compile time. There is no interface value holding \"some type\", and no type switch.\n\n**Overflow is checked.** Integer arithmetic that overflows stops the program; Go wraps silently.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\nGoroutines, channels and `select`, maps, interface values and type switches, struct embedding, the standard library,\nand Go's fast tooling for large codebases.\n",
    12 =>
        "# Coming from another language\n\nCeeSafe borrows deliberately from several languages, and each will feel partly familiar:\n\n- its **syntax** from C# and TypeScript: braces, `fn name(x: T) -> R`, `let` and `var`, generics in `<…>`;\n- its **ownership and borrowing** from Rust, with less annotation and a simpler checker;\n- its **explicit allocators** from Zig, with use-after-free made a compile error;\n- its **regions** from Cyclone, and its **compile-through-C** design and compiler-managed memory from Goose;\n- its **deterministic destruction** from C++'s RAII, without the undefined behaviour.\n\nThese pages map what you know to CeeSafe, show the same program side by side, and point out the differences that will\ntrip you up.\n\n| If you know | Read | The biggest changes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust | [CeeSafe for Rust programmers](rust.md) | no lifetime syntax on plain borrows; allocators per allocation; checks in every build; no macros or traits-as-types |\n| Zig | [CeeSafe for Zig programmers](zig.md) | use-after-free and dangling references are compile errors; checks never turn off; generics instead of `comptime` |\n| C or C++ | [CeeSafe for C and C++ programmers](c-and-cpp.md) | the compiler tracks ownership and borrows; no undefined behaviour; no preprocessor |\n| C#, Java or TypeScript | [CeeSafe for C#, Java and TypeScript programmers](csharp-java-typescript.md) | no garbage collector, no classes or inheritance, no exceptions, no null references |\n| Go | [CeeSafe for Go programmers](go.md) | no garbage collector; errors propagate with `?`; interfaces are explicit and static; no goroutines yet |\n\nA comparison of the languages as a whole, in one table, is on the repository's\n[README](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/README.md#how-nova-compares) (Nova's, the same language at the fork).\n",
    13 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Rust programmers\n\nCeeSafe's safety model is Rust's: single ownership, moves, shared and exclusive borrows, deterministic destruction, no\ngarbage collector. What differs is how much you write down, where memory comes from, and how much the language does\nfor you. This page assumes you know Rust well.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Rust | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `let x = 1;` / `let mut x = 1;` | `let x = 1;` / `var x = 1;` | |\n| shadowing (`let x = x + 1;`) | not allowed | two locals in scope never share a name |\n| `&T`, `&mut T` | `borrow T`, `borrow mut T` | |\n| `f(&x)`, `f(&mut x)` | `f(x)`, `f(mut x)` | a shared borrow is implicit at the call; a mutable one is marked |\n| `fn f<'a>(a: &'a T, b: &T) -> &'a T` | `fn f(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a) T` | the result names its sources; no lifetime parameters |\n| `struct S<'a> { s: &'a str }` | `struct S<region r> { s: StringView<r>; }` | only types that store references declare regions |\n| `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>`, `String`, `&str` | `Box<T>`, `Vector<T>`, `String`, `StringView` | |\n| `Option<T>`: `Some(x)`, `None` | `T?`: `Some(x)`, `null` | a `T` converts to `T?` where one is expected |\n| `Result<T, E>`, `?` | `Result<T, E>`, `?` | the same |\n| `.unwrap()` | `!` (postfix) | `r!`, `o!` |\n| `panic!` (unwinds by default) | panics always abort | no unwinding, no `catch_unwind` |\n| `impl Drop for S` | `drop { … }` inside the struct | |\n| `trait`, `impl Trait for T`, `T: Trait` | `interface`, `impl Interface for T`, `T: Interface` | bounds only: no `dyn` |\n| `#[derive(Clone, Copy)]` | automatic | a struct is Copy when all its fields are and it has no `drop` |\n| `x as u8` (truncates) | `truncate<u8>(x)` | `as` only converts where nothing can be lost |\n| `wrapping_add`, `checked_add`, `saturating_add` | `wrappingAdd`, `checkedAdd`, `saturatingAdd` | |\n| `\\|x\\| x + 1` | `(x) => x + 1` | closures borrow their captures and cannot escape |\n| `match x { A => …, }` | `match (x) { E.A => { … } }` | variants are qualified; statement arms are blocks |\n| `println!(\"{}\", x)` | `Console.writeLine(x)` | no macros, no format strings yet |\n| `#![no_std]` | `--profile freestanding` | |\n| `unsafe { }` with a `// SAFETY:` convention | `unsafe { }` with a required `/// Safety:` doc line | the compiler enforces it |\n| Cargo, `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` | `ceesafec`, `ceesafe.toml`, `ceesafe.lock` | path and git dependencies only; no registry |\n| `rustfmt`, `rustdoc` | `ceesafec fmt`, `ceesafec doc` | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```rust\nfn longest_word(text: &str) -> Option<&str> {\n    let mut best: Option<&str> = None;\n    for word in text.split(' ') {\n        if best.map_or(true, |b| word.len() > b.len()) {\n            best = Some(word);\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match longest_word(\"the quick brownish fox\") {\n        Some(w) => println!(\"{}\", w),\n        None => println!(\"empty\"),\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nfn longestWord<region r>(text: StringView<r>) -> StringView<r>? {\n    var bestStart: usize = 0;\n    var bestLength: usize = 0;\n    var start: usize = 0;\n    for (i in 0..text.length + 1) {\n        if (i == text.length || text[i] == ' ') {\n            if (i - start > bestLength) {\n                bestStart = start;\n                bestLength = i - start;\n            }\n            start = i + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    if (bestLength == 0) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    return text[bestStart..bestStart + bestLength];\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (longestWord(\"the quick brownish fox\")) {\n        Some(w) => { Console.writeLine(w); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"empty\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nbrownish\n```\n\nThe CeeSafe version has no iterator adaptors (there is no `split` yet), returns explicitly, and its signature says the\nresult views the input through the region `r`. With a single reference parameter, as here, the region could also be\nleft out: `fn longestWord(text: StringView) -> StringView?` means the same.\n\n## Ownership and borrowing: what is the same\n\nMoves, Copy types,",
    14 =>
        " \"one writer or many readers\", borrows ending at their last use, two-phase borrows for\n`v.push(v.length)`, references that cannot outlive their referent, and destruction at scope end in reverse order: all\nas in Rust. Error messages name the borrow, where it was made and where it is used later.\n\n## What is different\n\n**Less annotation.** A function that returns a reference names the parameters it may borrow from,\n`-> borrow(a, b) T`, instead of declaring lifetime parameters. Types that store references declare `region`\nparameters, and so do functions that pass such types through; plain functions over references never do. Regions are\nerased: `Token<a>` and `Token<b>` are the same type.\n\n**A simpler checker.** CeeSafe's borrow checker is intraprocedural and keeps one set of loans per value, where Rust\nreasons about lifetimes. Borrows end at their last use, as with NLL, but some programs Rust accepts are rejected; the\nusual fixes are to end a borrow earlier, copy a value out, or use an index or a slot-map handle. Reference locals are\n`let`, never `var`: assigning to a reference writes through it.\n\n**Allocators per allocation.** Every allocating type takes its allocator as a type parameter (`Box<T, A>`,\n`Vector<T, A>`), and every allocation returns a `Result`: there is no infallible `push`. Arenas, pools, fixed buffers\nand scratch frames are built in, and allocations from a region borrow it, so they cannot outlive it.\n\n**Checks in every build.** Integer overflow panics in release builds too; there is no `--release` that turns it off.\nBounds and overflow checks the compiler proves unnecessary are removed, and `--explain-bounds-checks` shows which.\n\n**No traits as types.** Interfaces are bounds and nothing else: no `dyn`, no default methods, no associated types, no\nblanket `impl<T>`. Heterogeneous collections are enums.\n\n**Closures do not escape.** A closure borrows what it captures and has a reference type (`borrow fn(i32) -> bool`,\n`borrow mut fn(i32)`), so it cannot be returned or stored. There is no `move` closure, `Fn`/`FnMut`/`FnOnce` trait or\n`impl Fn`. Calls through a closure are indirect.\n\n**No macros, no iterator chains.** `for` walks ranges, arrays, slices and vectors; slices and vectors have `sortBy`,\n`forEach`, `any`, `all` and `retain`. There is no `Iterator` trait yet.\n\n**Through C.** CeeSafe compiles to C11, which any C toolchain or kernel build can take, including MSVC and compilers for\ntargets LLVM does not support. The generated C is readable and maps back to CeeSafe lines.\n\n**Kernel checking.** Context effects (`@context(atomic)`, `@effects(sleep)`) reject sleeping under a spin lock across\ncalls, at compile time. Rust has no equivalent in the language.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\n`Rc`/`Arc` and interior mutability (`Cell`, `RefCell`), threads and `Send`/`Sync` (there are no user threads yet),\n`dyn Trait`, iterators, pattern guards (`if` in a match arm), slice patterns, `impl` blocks on generic types, the\nstandard library's breadth, and crates.io. Several were on Nova's [roadmap](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/roadmap.md) or\n[open decisions](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/open-decisions.md) at the fork.\n",
    15 =>
        "# CeeSafe for Zig programmers\n\nZig and CeeSafe agree on a lot: no hidden allocation, allocators as explicit values, errors as values, `defer`, no\ngarbage collector, first-class C interop, and code that can run in a kernel. They differ on what guarantees safety.\nZig checks bounds, overflow and null at run time in its safe build modes, and leaves use-after-free and dangling\npointers to the programmer. CeeSafe makes those compile errors, through ownership and borrowing, and keeps its run-time\nchecks on in every build.\n\n## At a glance\n\n| Zig | CeeSafe | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `const x = 1;` / `var x: u32 = 1;` | `let x = 1;` / `var x: u32 = 1;` | |\n| `[4]u8`, `[]u8`, `[]const u8` | `[u8; 4]`, `SliceMut<u8>`, `Slice<u8>` | slices are borrows: they cannot outlive their array |\n| `?T`, `null`, `orelse`, `.?` | `T?`, `null`, `match`, `!` | |\n| `!T`, `error.X`, `try`, `catch` | `Result<T, E>`, `Err(E.X)`, `?`, `match` | errors are ordinary enums |\n| `catch unreachable` | `!` (postfix) | |\n| `defer`, `errdefer` | `defer` | locals are destroyed automatically: most `defer`s are unnecessary |\n| `allocator.create(T)`, `allocator.destroy(p)` | `Box.createIn(allocator, value)`; destroyed automatically | the allocator is part of the box's type |\n| `std.ArrayList(T)` with an allocator | `Vector<T, A>` | |\n| `std.heap.ArenaAllocator` | `Arena` | allocations borrow the arena: none survives it |\n| `std.heap.FixedBufferAllocator` | `FixedBufferAllocator` | |\n| `+%`, `+\\|`, `@addWithOverflow` | `wrappingAdd`, `saturatingAdd`, `checkedAdd` | |\n| `@intCast`, `@truncate` | `as` (lossless only), `truncate<T>` | |\n| `comptime` generics | `<T>` generics with interface bounds | no compile-time execution |\n| `@cImport` | `extern \"C\"` declarations; C structs imported by `@header` | |\n| `export fn`, `callconv(.C)` | `@export`, `@callback` | |\n| Debug / ReleaseSafe / ReleaseFast | one mode, always checked | proved checks are removed |\n| `std.debug.print` | `Console.writeLine` | |\n\n## The same program\n\n```zig\n// Zig 0.13\nconst std = @import(\"std\");\n\nfn sumAll(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, n: usize) !u64 {\n    var list = std.ArrayList(u64).init(allocator);\n    defer list.deinit();\n    var i: u64 = 0;\n    while (i < n) : (i += 1) try list.append(i * i);\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (list.items) |x| total += x;\n    return total;\n}\n\npub fn main() !void {\n    const total = try sumAll(std.heap.page_allocator, 10);\n    std.debug.print(\"{}\\n\", .{total});\n}\n```\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sumAll(n: u64) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    var list = Vector<u64>.create();          // freed at the end of the function, by its owner\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        list.push(i * i)?;\n    }\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (x in list) {\n        total += x;\n    }\n    return Ok(total);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(sumAll(10)!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n285\n```\n\nThere is no `deinit`: the vector is destroyed when its owner goes out of scope, on every path, including the early\nreturn of `?`. Another allocator goes in the type, `Vector<u64, A>` for an allocator type `A`. An arena works the Zig\nway instead: `arena.allocArray(n, 0u64)` returns a slice that borrows the arena, and everything is freed with it.\n\n## What changes\n\n**Lifetimes are checked.** Returning a slice of a local array, keeping a pointer into an `ArrayList` across an\n`append`, or using an arena allocation after `arena.deinit()`: each is a compile error in CeeSafe, with a message naming\nthe borrow. In Zig these compile and fail at run time, if they are noticed at all.\n\n**Destruction is automatic.** A struct with a `drop` block, or that owns a box, vector or file, is destroyed exactly\nonce when its owner goes away. Ownership moves on assignment, so there is no double free to avoid by convention.\n\n**Errors carry types.** An error is any type, usually an enum with payloads (`ParseError.BadDigit(c)`), not a global\nerror set. `?` requires the function's error type to match; there are no implicit error-set unions.\n\n**Generics, not `comptime",
    16 =>
        "`.** CeeSafe's generics are type parameters checked once, for every type argument, against the\nbounds you declare. There is no compile-time evaluation of arbitrary code, no `@typeInfo` and no reflection. Constants\nare evaluated at compile time; functions are not.\n\n**Safety is not a build mode.** There is no ReleaseFast. Overflow and bounds checks are always on, and the compiler\nremoves those it proves cannot fail.\n\n## Things you will miss\n\n`comptime`, `@cImport` of whole headers, packed structs and arbitrary-width integers (`u3`), SIMD vectors, inline\nassembly, async, the cross-compiling toolchain in one binary, and Zig's build system. CeeSafe relies on a C compiler for\ncode generation and cross compilation.\n",
    17 =>
        "# Debugging\n\nA CeeSafe program is debugged as the C it compiles to, with the C mapped back to CeeSafe lines, so lldb and gdb work\nwithout knowing anything about CeeSafe.\n\n## A debug build\n\n```sh\nceesafec build -g main.cee -o program\nlldb ./program          # or: gdb ./program\n```\n\n`-g` writes `#line` directives into the C and compiles it with `-g -O0`. Breakpoints then go on CeeSafe lines, and\nstepping moves through CeeSafe statements.\n\n| | lldb | gdb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop at a line | `b main.cee:12` | `break main.cee:12` |\n| Stop when a check fails, before the program exits | `b ceesafe_panic` | `break ceesafe_panic` |\n| Where am I | `bt` | `bt` |\n\n## Names\n\nThe C names follow fixed rules, so a CeeSafe name is easy to find:\n\n| CeeSafe | C |\n|---|---|\n| function `length` | `ceeF_length` |\n| method `Counter.bump` | `ceeM_7Counter_bump` (the type's name, prefixed by its length) |\n| local or parameter `sum` | `v_sum_1` |\n| a temporary the compiler made | `t_3` |\n| field `x` | `f_x` |\n| struct `Point` | `ceeT_Point` |\n| an item of a dependency `geometry` | `ceeF_geometry_area`, `ceeT_geometry_Point` |\n\n## CeeSafe names in the debugger\n\n`editors/debug` adds two commands that print with CeeSafe names:\n\n```sh\nlldb ./program -o \"command script import editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py\"\ngdb ./program -ex \"source editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py\"\n```\n\n- `ceesafe-locals`: the current frame's locals and parameters, without the compiler's temporaries, with struct fields by\n  their CeeSafe names: `p = { x: 3, y: 4 }`.\n- `ceesafe-bt`: the backtrace as `Counter.bump at main.cee:6`.\n\n```text\n(lldb) b main.cee:12\n(lldb) run\n(lldb) ceesafe-locals\np = { x: 3, y: 4 }\nsum = 7\n(lldb) ceesafe-bt\nlength at main.cee:12\nmain at main.cee:18\n```\n\n## When the program panics\n\nA failed check prints the reason and the CeeSafe location, and exits with status 134. Under a debugger, a breakpoint on\n`ceesafe_panic` stops at the moment of the panic, with the whole call stack to inspect:\n\n```text\n(lldb) b ceesafe_panic\n(lldb) run\n(lldb) ceesafe-bt\nceesafe_panic at ceesafe_rt_core.c:207\ncee_add_u8 at rt.h:190\ngrow at main.cee:2\nmain at main.cee:7\n```\n\nFor memory errors in `unsafe` code, `ceesafec run --interp` is often faster: the interpreter stops at the first invalid\naccess and names it.\n",
    18 =>
        "# Editors\n\n## VS Code\n\nThe extension in `editors/vscode` gives `.cee` files syntax highlighting, and through the language server: errors as\nyou type, the type or signature on hover, go to definition (F12), the outline, and formatting (Shift+Alt+F).\n\nBuild and install it with [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/):\n\n```sh\ncd editors/vscode\nvp install\nvp test                 # the grammar, and ceesafec lsp through VS Code's own client library\nvp run package          # makes ceesafe-language-0.1.0.vsix\ncode --install-extension ceesafe-language-0.1.0.vsix\n```\n\nThe extension starts `ceesafec lsp`. It finds `ceesafec` on your `PATH`, or where the setting `ceesafe.server.path` says.\n\n## Other editors\n\n`ceesafec lsp` is a standard Language Server Protocol server on standard input and output, so any editor with an LSP\nclient can use it: Neovim, Helix, Emacs, Zed, Sublime Text. Configure it to run `ceesafec lsp` for `.cee` files. The\nTextMate grammar in `editors/vscode/syntaxes/ceesafe.tmLanguage.json` works in editors that accept TextMate grammars.\n\n## What the server does\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Diagnostics | every error and note of `ceesafec check`, as you type |\n| Hover | the type of an expression; `let`/`var` and the type of a variable; a function's signature; a field's type |\n| Go to definition | locals, parameters, functions, methods, fields, structs, enums and interfaces in the file |\n| Document symbols | the outline: functions, structs with their fields and methods, enums, interfaces, constants |\n| Formatting | the whole file, in `ceesafec fmt`'s style |\n\nA file that belongs to a package is checked with the package and its locked dependencies; any other file is checked\non its own. The details are in [Tooling §2](../spec/tooling.md#_2-language-server-t3).\n",
    19 =>
        "# Errors\n\nCeeSafe has two kinds of failure, and keeps them apart:\n\n- **Recoverable errors** are values: a function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`, and the caller decides what to\n  do. Allocation failure and I/O errors are of this kind.\n- **Bugs** stop the program: an index out of bounds, an integer overflow, dividing by zero, unwrapping an error or a\n  `null`. This is a **panic**: the program prints the reason and where it happened, and exits at once. There is no\n  unwinding and no catching.\n\n## `Result`\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError {\n    Empty,\n    BadDigit(u8),\n    TooLarge,\n}\n\nfn parse(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length == 0) {\n        return Err(ParseError.Empty);\n    }\n    var value: u32 = 0;\n    for (c in text.bytes()) {\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n            return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c));\n        }\n        let next = value.checkedMul(10);\n        if (next == null) {\n            return Err(ParseError.TooLarge);\n        }\n        value = next! + (c - '0') as u32;\n    }\n    return Ok(value);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (parse(\"2026\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)) => {\n            Console.write(\"not a digit: \");\n            Console.writeLine(c);\n        }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"no number\"); }\n    }\n    match (parse(\"20x6\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)) => {\n            Console.write(\"not a digit: \");\n            Console.writeLine(c);\n        }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"no number\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2026\nnot a digit: 120\n```\n\n`Result<T, E>` is an enum with two variants, `Ok(T)` and `Err(E)`, matched without a type prefix. `T` may be `void`\n(`Result<void, E>`, with `Ok()`); `E` is usually an enum of the ways the function can fail.\n\n## `?`: pass the error on\n\nIn a function that returns a `Result` with the same error type, `e?` is the value inside `Ok`, or returns the `Err`\nto the caller at once. Every local is destroyed on the way out, as for any `return`.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError {\n    Empty,\n    BadDigit(u8),\n}\n\nfn digit(c: u8) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n        return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c));\n    }\n    return Ok((c - '0') as u32);\n}\n\nfn twoDigits(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length != 2) {\n        return Err(ParseError.Empty);\n    }\n    return Ok(digit(text[0])? * 10 + digit(text[1])?);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(twoDigits(\"42\")!);\n    match (twoDigits(\"4x\")) {\n        Ok(n) => { Console.writeLine(n); }\n        Err(_) => { Console.writeLine(\"not two digits\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\nnot two digits\n```\n\nThe same works on optionals: `o?` returns `null` from a function that returns an optional.\n\n## `!`: this cannot fail\n\n`e!` is the value inside `Ok` (or a present optional's value). If it is an `Err` (or `null`), the program panics. Use\nit where failure would be a bug, or in small programs and tests where stopping is the right answer:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var names = Vector<StringView>.create();\n    names.push(\"ada\")!;                  // allocation failure stops this program\n    names.push(\"grace\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(names.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\n## Allocation failure is a value\n\nEvery operation that allocates returns a `Result<…, AllocError>`: `Box.create`, `Vector.push`, `String.append`,\n`Arena.alloc` and the rest. A program that must survive running out of memory, such as a kernel, handles it; a\nprogram that need not, writes `!` or passes it on with `?`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32;\n    next: Box<Node>?;\n}\n\nfn push(head: Box<Node>?, value: i32) -> Result<Box<Node>, AllocError> =>\n    Box.create(Node { value: value, next: head });\n\nfn build(n: i32) -> Result<Box<Node>?, AllocError> {\n    var head: Box<Node>? = null;\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        head = push(move head, i)?;\n    }\n    return Ok(head);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    match (bui",
    20 =>
        "ld(3)) {\n        Ok(list) => { Console.writeLine(list!.value); }\n        Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory) => { Console.writeLine(\"out of memory\"); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\nThe test suite runs every allocating program again with each allocation failing in turn, to check that every\nfailure path cleans up after itself.\n\n## Panics\n\nA panic prints the reason and the source location, and exits with status 134:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: panics -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let values = [1, 2, 3];\n    var i: usize = 0;\n    while (true) {\n        Console.writeLine(values[i]);\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\nHere the fourth read stops the program with `panic: index out of bounds` and the file and line of `values[i]`.\nPanics never run `drop` blocks or deferred statements: the program is in a state its author did not expect, so\nnothing more of it runs.\n\n## I/O errors\n\nFiles, sockets and processes report `IoError`, a built-in enum (`NotFound`, `PermissionDenied`, `AlreadyExists`, …,\n`Other(code)`), whose `message()` is the C library's text:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: exits 1 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    match (File.open(\"/no/such/file\")) {\n        Ok(file) => {\n            Console.writeLine(\"opened\");\n            return 0;\n        }\n        Err(e) => {\n            Console.writeLine(e.message());\n            return 1;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\nNo such file or directory\n```\n\n## Next\n\n[Memory and allocation](memory.md).\n",
    21 =>
        "# CeeSafe for AI agents\n\nThis page is for AI agents that write CeeSafe, and for the people who set them up. CeeSafe is new, so a model has seen\nlittle of it: it will write C#, TypeScript or Rust that looks like CeeSafe and is not. The compiler is built to correct\nthat quickly. It says exactly what is wrong, fixes what it is sure of, and serves its own specification.\n\nThe guide and the specification are inside `ceesafec`: an agent reads them through `ceesafec explain` and the MCP\nserver's resources (`ceesafe://docs/…`, below), so it needs no network access to read the rules. On the web they are\nalso in machine-readable form: [llms.txt](https://vkuttyp.github.io/CeeSafe/llms.txt) (an index) and\n[llms-full.txt](https://vkuttyp.github.io/CeeSafe/llms-full.txt) (the guide and the specification in one file), and\nevery page is available as Markdown at its address with `.md` added.\n\n## In a new package\n\n`ceesafec new` writes `AGENTS.md`, which most coding agents read: what CeeSafe is, the package's layout, the commands that\ncheck the work, how to use the MCP server, and the common mistakes below. Name your agent to get its own file and its\nMCP configuration too:\n\n```sh\nceesafec new hello --agent claude      # also CLAUDE.md (importing AGENTS.md) and .mcp.json\n```\n\n| `--agent` | Writes |\n|---|---|\n| `agents` (the default) | `AGENTS.md` |\n| `claude` | `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json` |\n| `gemini` | `GEMINI.md`, `.gemini/settings.json` |\n| `copilot` | `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `.vscode/mcp.json` |\n| `cursor` | `.cursor/rules/ceesafe.mdc`, `.cursor/mcp.json` |\n\nCeeSafe's part of `AGENTS.md` sits between `<!--CEESAFE START-->` and `<!--CEESAFE END-->`. After updating `ceesafec`, run\n`ceesafec agents` in the package to refresh that part; whatever you wrote outside it stays as it is.\n\n## Set up the MCP server\n\n`ceesafec mcp` is a [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) server. Add it to your agent once:\n\n```sh\nclaude mcp add ceesafe -- ceesafec mcp          # Claude Code\n```\n\nor, for any MCP client:\n\n```json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"ceesafe\": { \"command\": \"ceesafec\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } }\n```\n\nIts tools:\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_check` | type-checks code and returns its diagnostics as JSON, with fixes |\n| `ceesafe_fix` | applies every fix the compiler is sure of, and returns the files and what remains |\n| `ceesafe_format` | formats a file in the one canonical style |\n| `ceesafe_run` | runs a program in a sandboxed interpreter: no files, sockets or processes, and a step and memory budget |\n| `ceesafe_emit_c` | shows the generated C, and where allocations, bounds checks and overflow checks went |\n| `ceesafe_explain` | what a diagnostic code means |\n| `ceesafe_spec_search`, `ceesafe_spec_section` | search and read the specification, which is embedded in `ceesafec` |\n| `ceesafe_symbol` | the type and declaration of what is at a position |\n\nThe prompt `write-ceesafe` gives an agent this page and the loop below. The specification and this guide are resources\n(`ceesafe://docs/…`). The [tooling reference](../spec/tooling.md) §6 has the details.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Write the code.\n2. Check it: `ceesafe_check`, or `ceesafec check --diagnostics-format=json`.\n3. Apply the fixes: `ceesafe_fix`, or `ceesafec fix`. A fix is offered only where the compiler is sure of it: `mut` before\n   an argument, `let` to `var`, `truncate<T>(…)` for a narrowing cast, a missing `;`.\n4. Fix the rest yourself. Each diagnostic has a code; `ceesafe_explain` (or `ceesafec explain N0249`) says what it means, and\n   the message often names the specification section to read.\n5. Repeat until there are no errors. Then run it with `ceesafe_run`, or build it with `ceesafec build`.\n\nWithout MCP, the same loop works from a shell: `ceesafec check --diagnostics-format=json main.cee` prints one JSON\ndocument with every diagnostic, its position and its fixes, and `ceesafec fix main.cee` applies them.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nThese are the mistakes models m",
    22 =>
        "ake most often in CeeSafe, each with the compiler's answer and what to write instead.\n\n### There is no `~`\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0102 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => ~x;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:29: error[N0102]: expected expression, found '~'\n  |\n1 | fn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => ~x;\n  |                             ^\n```\n\nComplement with an exclusive or of all ones:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn invert(x: u32) -> u32 => x ^ 0xFFFFFFFF;\n```\n\n### There are no type aliases\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0101 -->\n```ceesafe\ntype Count = u32;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:1: error[N0101]: expected 'fn', 'const', 'struct' or 'enum', found 'type'\n  |\n1 | type Count = u32;\n  | ^^^^\n```\n\nWrite the type itself, or wrap it in a struct when it deserves a name of its own.\n\n### There are no tuples\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0100 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    let (a, b) = (1, 2);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:9: error[N0100]: expected identifier, found '('\n  |\n2 |     let (a, b) = (1, 2);\n  |         ^\n```\n\nDeclare two variables, or return a struct with named fields.\n\n### Arrays are not compared with `==`\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0207 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn same(a: [u8; 4], b: [u8; 4]) -> bool => a == b;\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:44: error[N0207]: operator '==' cannot be applied to type '[u8; 4]'\n  |\n1 | fn same(a: [u8; 4], b: [u8; 4]) -> bool => a == b;\n  |                                            ^^^^^^\n```\n\nCompare element by element:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn same(a: [u8; 4], b: [u8; 4]) -> bool {\n    for (i in 0..a.length) {\n        if (a[i] != b[i]) { return false; }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### Indexes are `usize`, and a range's type comes from its bounds\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0205 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (i in 0..4) {\n        total += values[i];\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:25: error[N0205]: expected 'usize', found 'i32'\n  |\n4 |         total += values[i];\n  |                         ^\n```\n\n`0..4` is a range of `i32`. Use the length, which is a `usize`, or a typed literal:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        total += values[i];\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n```\n\n### A block is not a value\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0100 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn sign(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    let s = if (x < 0) { -1 } else { 1 };\n    return s;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:28: error[N0100]: expected ';', found '}'\n  |\n2 |     let s = if (x < 0) { -1 } else { 1 };\n  |                            ^\n\nmain.cee:2:39: error[N0100]: expected ';', found '}'\n  |\n2 |     let s = if (x < 0) { -1 } else { 1 };\n  |                                       ^\n```\n\nAn `if` or `match` expression's branches are expressions, written without braces:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sign(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    let s = if (x < 0) -1 else 1;\n    return s;\n}\n```\n\n### A temporary is not sliced or measured\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0216 -->\n```ceesafe\nconst TABLE: [u8; 4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(TABLE.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:23: error[N0216]: 'length' needs an array variable or field; bind the value to a local first\n  |\n4 |     Console.writeLine(TABLE.length);\n  |                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nBind it to a local first, as the message says:\n\n```ceesafe\nconst TABLE: [u8; 4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n\nfn main() {\n    let table = TABLE;\n    Console.writeLine(table.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n```\n\n### A function in a field is not called through the field\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0210 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Counter {\n    step: fn(i32) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn advance(c: borrow Counter, n: i32) -> i32 => (c.step)(n);\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:49: error[N0210]: this expression cannot be called\n  |\n5 | fn advance(c: borrow Counter, n: i32) -> i32 => (c.step)(n);\n  |                                                 ^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nPass the function or closure as a parameter, and call the parameter:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn advance(step: fn(i32) -> i32, n: i32) -> i32 => step(n);\n```\n\n### A borrowed string in an enum needs a reg",
    23 =>
        "ion\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0270 -->\n```ceesafe\nenum Failure {\n    Refused(StringView),\n    Io,\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:2:13: error[N0270]: a payload of scoped type 'StringView' needs a region parameter on the enum: 'enum Failure<region r>' (type-system.md §25.4)\n  |\n2 |     Refused(StringView),\n  |             ^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nA `StringView` borrows its text, so a type holding one says from where. For fixed messages, an enum of reasons and a\nfunction that words them is simpler:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Reason {\n    TooLarge,\n    BadMagic,\n}\n\nenum Failure {\n    Refused(Reason),\n    Io,\n}\n\nfn describe(reason: Reason) -> StringView => match (reason) {\n    Reason.TooLarge => \"too large\",\n    Reason.BadMagic => \"bad magic number\",\n};\n```\n\n### A method cannot share a field's name\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0228 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    balance: i64;\n\n    fn balance(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:8: error[N0228]: 'Account' already has a member named 'balance'\n  |\n4 |     fn balance(borrow this) -> i64 => this.balance;\n  |        ^^^^^^^\n```\n\nName the method for what it does (`current`), or make the field `public`.\n\n### What the compiler fixes for you\n\n`ceesafe_fix` and `ceesafec fix` repair these without being asked twice: a `let` that is assigned (`var`), an argument for a\n`borrow mut` parameter without `mut`, a `mut` where the parameter is a shared `borrow`, `as` between integer types that\ncan lose information (`truncate<T>(…)`, which wraps), and a `;` missing after a statement.\n\n## Where to read more\n\nThe [guide](introduction.md) teaches the language; [Coming from another language](coming-from/index.md) maps what you know.\nThe rules themselves are the [specification](../spec/type-system.md), with the [grammar](../spec/grammar.md) and every\n[diagnostic](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n",
    24 =>
        "# Formatting and documentation\n\n## Formatting\n\n`ceesafec fmt` rewrites CeeSafe source in one canonical style. It has no options, so code formatted by anyone looks the\nsame. The style is specified in [Formatting](../spec/formatting.md).\n\n```sh\nceesafec fmt                       # the package's sources (or: ceesafec fmt file.cee dir/)\nceesafec fmt --check               # change nothing; list the files that would change; exit 1 if any\nceesafec fmt --stdout file.cee    # print one file formatted\n```\n\nThe style, briefly:\n\n- 4-space indentation, no tabs, at most one blank line in a row, lines up to 120 columns;\n- a block or list you wrote on one line stays on one line if it fits; one you broke across lines is laid out one\n  element per line, with a trailing comma;\n- a line that is too long is broken at the outermost place that helps: an expression body, then a list, then an\n  initialiser, then an operator;\n- `} else {` on one line, and the blocks of an `if`/`else` chain are broken together;\n- trailing comments on consecutive lines are aligned.\n\nIt is safe by construction: the formatter only changes whitespace and trailing commas, and before returning it parses\nits own output and checks that the program and its comments are unchanged. A file with a syntax error is left alone.\n\n## Documentation\n\n`ceesafec doc` writes one Markdown page for a package's public items: each item's signature, as the formatter prints\nit, and its `///` doc comment, which is Markdown.\n\n```sh\nceesafec doc -o api.md --title \"geometry\"\nceesafec doc --missing             # list the public items without a doc comment; exit 1 if any\n```\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n/// A point in the plane.\n///   - coordinates are exact integers;\n///   - `norm` is the squared length.\npublic struct Point {\n    public x: i64;\n    public y: i64;\n\n    /// The squared distance from the origin.\n    public fn norm(this) -> i64 => this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y;\n}\n```\n\nbecomes a section with `public struct Point { public x: i64; public y: i64; }`, the list, and a subsection for\n`Point.norm`. The [lib/core reference](../spec/api/core.md) on this site is generated this way.\n",
    25 =>
        "# Generics and interfaces\n\n## Generic functions and types\n\nFunctions, structs and enums can take type parameters. Each use with different types compiles to its own copy\n(monomorphisation), so generic code is as fast as hand-written code for each type.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Pair<T> {\n    first: T;\n    second: T;\n    fn swapped(this) -> Pair<T> => Pair { first: this.second, second: this.first };\n}\n\nenum Maybe<T> {\n    Nothing,\n    Just(T),\n}\n\nfn firstOf<T>(pair: Pair<T>) -> T => pair.first;\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pair { first: 1, second: 2 };           // Pair<i32>, inferred\n    let q = p.swapped();\n    Console.writeLine(firstOf(q));                  // T = i32, inferred\n    let names = Pair<StringView> { first: \"x\", second: \"y\" };\n    Console.writeLine(firstOf<StringView>(names));  // written out\n    let m: Maybe<u8> = Maybe.Just(7);\n    match (m) {\n        Maybe.Just(v) => { Console.writeLine(v); }\n        Maybe.Nothing => {}\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\nx\n7\n```\n\nType arguments are inferred from the arguments where possible, and written otherwise: `Pair<u8> { … }`,\n`Vector<i32>.create()`, `firstOf<StringView>(names)`.\n\n**Generic code is checked once,** for every possible type argument. Inside it, a value of type `T` can be moved,\nborrowed, stored and returned, but nothing else is known about it: no arithmetic, no fields, no `==`. What it can do\ncomes from its **bounds**.\n\n## Interfaces\n\nAn interface lists methods. A type implements it with an `impl` block, and a generic function bound by it can call\nthose methods.\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView;\n}\n\nstruct Square {\n    side: u64;\n}\n\nstruct Rect {\n    w: u64;\n    h: u64;\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.side * this.side;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"square\";\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.w * this.h;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"rect\";\n}\n\nfn total<S: Shape>(shapes: Slice<S>) -> u64 {\n    var sum: u64 = 0;\n    for (shape in shapes) {\n        sum += shape.area();\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nfn describe<S: Shape>(shape: borrow S) {\n    Console.write(shape.name());\n    Console.write(\": \");\n    Console.writeLine(shape.area());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let squares = [Square { side: 2 }, Square { side: 3 }];\n    var rects = Vector<Rect>.create();\n    rects.push(Rect { w: 4, h: 5 })!;\n    Console.writeLine(total(squares[..]) + total(rects.view()));\n    describe(squares[1]);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n33\nsquare: 9\n```\n\n- Calls through a bound are resolved at compile time: there is no virtual dispatch and no cost.\n- `Copy` is a built-in bound: a `T: Copy` can be used twice and stored in arrays.\n- Several bounds are joined with `+`: `<T: Shape + Copy>`.\n- An interface can itself be generic: `interface Comparable<T> { fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow T) -> i32; }`.\n\n## Dynamic dispatch\n\nA bound is resolved at compile time, so a `Slice<S>` holds shapes of one type. For a collection of different types\nthat implement the same interface, use `dyn Shape`: a value of some type implementing `Shape`, whose methods are\ncalled through a table at run time. Its size is not known, so it lives behind a pointer: `borrow dyn Shape`,\n`borrow mut dyn Shape`, or an owning `Box<dyn Shape>`.\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView;\n}\n\nstruct Square {\n    side: u64;\n}\n\nstruct Rect {\n    w: u64;\n    h: u64;\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.side * this.side;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"square\";\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> u64 => this.w * this.h;\n    fn name(borrow this) -> StringView => \"rect\";\n}\n\nfn describe(shape: borrow dyn Shape) {\n    Console.write(shape.name());\n    Console.write(\": \");\n    Console.writeLine(shape.area());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var shapes = Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>.create();\n    shapes.push(Box.create(Square { side: 3 }",
    26 =>
        ")!)!; // a Box<Square> becomes a Box<dyn Shape>\n    shapes.push(Box.create(Rect { w: 2, h: 5 })!)!;\n    for (shape in shapes) {\n        describe(shape);\n    }\n    let sq = Square { side: 4 };\n    describe(sq); // a Square, lent as a borrow dyn Shape\n}\n```\n\n```text\nsquare: 9\nrect: 10\nsquare: 16\n```\n\n- A `borrow T` or a `Box<T>` converts to the `dyn` type where one is expected, if `T` implements the interface.\n  There is no way back to `T`.\n- Every method of the interface must take `borrow this` or `borrow mut this`.\n- A call through `dyn` cannot be inlined. Prefer a bound when one type is enough.\n- An interface method may declare its effects, `@effects() fn visit(borrow mut this, x: u64);`, which every `impl`\n  must keep: kernel code in an atomic context can then call it through `dyn`.\n\n## Next\n\n[Functions and closures](closures.md).\n",
    27 =>
        "# Getting started\n\nThis page makes a package, builds and runs it, and shows the commands you will use every day. It assumes `ceesafec` is\n[installed](installation.md) and on your `PATH`.\n\n## A new package\n\n```sh\nceesafec new hello\ncd hello\n```\n\n`ceesafec new` writes a manifest, a first source file, and instructions for AI coding agents:\n\n```text\nhello/\n├── ceesafe.toml\n├── src/\n│   └── main.cee\n├── AGENTS.md\n└── .gitignore\n```\n\n`AGENTS.md` tells an agent working in the package what CeeSafe is and how to check its work. With\n`ceesafec new hello --agent claude` (or `gemini`, `copilot`, `cursor`) it also writes that agent's own file and\nconfigures CeeSafe's MCP server for it; `--no-agent` writes none. See [CeeSafe for AI agents](for-agents.md).\n\n`ceesafe.toml` names the package and says what it builds:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"hello\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nkind = \"executable\"\n\n[dependencies]\n```\n\nand `src/main.cee` is a program:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello from hello\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nHello from hello\n```\n\n## Build and run\n\nInside a package, the commands need no file names:\n\n```sh\nceesafec build            # compiles to C, then to out/hello\n./out/hello\nceesafec run --interp     # runs the program in the reference interpreter instead\nceesafec check            # type and borrow checks only: the fastest way to find errors\nceesafec fmt              # formats the package's sources in the canonical style\n```\n\nEvery `.cee` file under `src/` belongs to the package, and all of them share one scope: a function declared in one\nfile can be called from another without an import.\n\n## A slightly larger program\n\nReplace `src/main.cee` with:\n\n```ceesafe\n/// The number of steps the Collatz sequence takes from `n` to 1.\nfn steps(n: u64) -> u32 {\n    var value = n;\n    var count: u32 = 0;\n    while (value != 1) {\n        value = if (value % 2 == 0) value / 2 else 3 * value + 1;\n        count += 1;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var longest: u64 = 1;\n    for (n in 1..1000u64) {\n        if (steps(n) > steps(longest)) {\n            longest = n;\n        }\n    }\n    Console.write(\"longest below 1000: \");\n    Console.write(longest);\n    Console.write(\", \");\n    Console.write(steps(longest));\n    Console.writeLine(\" steps\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\nlongest below 1000: 871, 178 steps\n```\n\n`3 * value + 1` is checked: if it overflowed a `u64`, the program would stop with a message naming the line, in every\nbuild. CeeSafe never wraps silently.\n\n## When the compiler says no\n\nErrors name a code, the place, and usually the fix. This program keeps a reference to a vector's element and then\ngrows the vector, which could move the element:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = Vector<i32>.create();\n    numbers.push(1)!;\n    let first = borrow numbers[0];\n    numbers.push(2)!;\n    Console.writeLine(first);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:5:5: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'numbers' as mutable while 'numbers[0]' is borrowed\n  |\n5 |     numbers.push(2)!;\n  |     ^^^^^^^\n  = note: 'numbers[0]' is borrowed here (4:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (6:5)\n```\n\nThe fix is to finish with `first` before the push, or to copy the value (`let first = numbers[0];`). Every code is\nexplained in [Diagnostics](../spec/diagnostics.md).\n\n## Files without a package\n\nFor a quick experiment, a package is not needed: give the files to the command.\n\n```sh\nceesafec build tool.cee helpers.cee -o tool\nceesafec run --interp tool.cee\n```\n\n## Next\n\n- [Basics](basics.md): values, types, functions and control flow.\n- [Packages](packages.md): dependencies, the lock file, and sharing code.\n- [The ceesafec command](ceesafec.md): every command and option.\n",
    28 =>
        "# Installation\n\n## Build ceesafec\n\nCeeSafe has no released binaries yet (they are a step of the repository's `ROADMAP.md`, P9). `ceesafec` is written in\nCeeSafe and builds itself; the first build of a checkout starts from novac 0.6.0, the Nova release CeeSafe forked\nfrom, which you download from [Nova's releases](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/releases/tag/v0.6.0) into\n`~/.nova/0.6.0/bin/novac`.\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/vkuttyp/CeeSafe.git\ncd CeeSafe\nsh tools/bootstrap.sh ~/.nova/0.6.0/bin/novac HEAD out/ceesafec\nout/ceesafec --version\n```\n\n`tools/bootstrap.sh` has novac build the last commit that novac can read, and the `ceesafec` that makes build the tree\nyou checked out. After that, `ceesafec` rebuilds itself:\n\n```sh\nout/ceesafec build -o out/ceesafec.new && mv out/ceesafec.new out/ceesafec\n```\n\n`ceesafec` is one native executable: its runtime, its libraries and this specification are inside it, so it can be\ncopied anywhere, for example onto your `PATH` as `~/.local/bin/ceesafec`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n| | For | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| A C compiler: clang, gcc or MSVC | building `ceesafec`, and `ceesafec build` | clang and gcc are fully supported; MSVC is best-effort (`/std:c11`). `check`, `run --interp`, `fmt`, `lsp` and `mcp` need none |\n| novac 0.6.0 | the first build of a checkout | not needed once you have a `ceesafec` |\n| git | dependencies from git repositories | only for [packages](packages.md) that use them |\n| Node.js and [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) | the VS Code extension | optional |\n\nTo check everything, run the contract suite (the repository's `spec/testing.md`; about an hour, since it compiles and\nruns the corpus many times and compares the results with novac 0.6.0's):\n\n```sh\npython3 tools/contract_tests.py --ceesafec out/ceesafec --novac ~/.nova/0.6.0/bin/novac --root <a Nova checkout at v0.6.0>\n```\n\n## Check that it works\n\n```sh\ncat > hello.cee <<'EOF'\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(\"Hello, CeeSafe\");\n}\nEOF\nceesafec build hello.cee -o hello && ./hello      # through C, to a native program\nceesafec run --interp hello.cee                   # or in the reference interpreter\n```\n\n`ceesafec build` uses the C compiler in `$CC`, or `cc`; `--cc clang` picks one. On Windows it finds one itself: clang\n(`winget install LLVM.LLVM`), MinGW's gcc, or Visual Studio's `cl`, and it sets up Visual Studio's headers and\nlibraries for clang and `cl` when you are not in a developer prompt. So with LLVM and Visual Studio's \"Desktop\ndevelopment with C++\" workload (or its Build Tools) installed, `ceesafec build` works from any terminal and makes\n`out\\hello.exe` ([tooling](../spec/tooling.md) §8).\n\n## Editor support\n\nThe repository has a VS Code extension in `editors/vscode`: syntax highlighting, and through `ceesafec lsp` errors as you\ntype, hover, go to definition, the outline and formatting. See [Editors](editors.md) to build and install it, and to\nuse the language server from other editors.\n",
    29 =>
        "# What CeeSafe is\n\nCeeSafe is a memory-safe systems programming language. Its syntax will look familiar if you know C# or TypeScript; its\nsafety comes from ownership and borrowing, as in Rust. It is compiled ahead of time to portable C11, so it runs\nwherever a C compiler does: on Linux, macOS and Windows, on bare metal, and inside an operating system kernel.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n\n    drop {\n        Console.write(\"closing account \");\n        Console.writeLine(this.id);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var account = Account { id: 7, balance: 0 };\n    account.deposit(100);\n    Console.writeLine(account.balance);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n100\nclosing account 7\n```\n\n## What it promises\n\nIn safe code, which is all code outside an `unsafe` block, CeeSafe rules out:\n\n- **use after free and double free:** every value has one owner, and it is destroyed exactly once;\n- **dangling references:** a reference cannot outlive what it refers to, which the compiler checks without lifetime\n  annotations in most code;\n- **data races between a reference and a write:** while something is borrowed it cannot be changed, and while it is\n  borrowed mutably nothing else can read it;\n- **out-of-bounds access and reading uninitialised memory:** indexes are checked, and a variable must be assigned\n  before it is read;\n- **silent integer overflow:** arithmetic that overflows stops the program, in every build. Where wrapping is meant,\n  the code says so (`x.wrappingAdd(y)`).\n\nIt does this **without a garbage collector or reference counting.** Destruction is deterministic: a value is destroyed\nat the end of its scope, or when its owner is, in an order the language defines.\n\n## What makes it different\n\n- **Lifetimes without annotations, mostly.** A function that takes and returns references names the parameters its\n  result borrows from (`-> borrow(a, b) T`). Only types that store references, and functions over them, declare\n  region parameters.\n- **Allocation is explicit and pluggable.** Each allocation chooses its allocator: the system heap, an arena, a pool,\n  a fixed buffer, a scratch frame, or your own. Allocation failure is a value (`Result<T, AllocError>`), handled or\n  propagated with `?`.\n- **The compiler shows its work.** It can explain which boxes it placed on the stack and which bounds and overflow\n  checks it removed, and why.\n- **C you can read.** The generated C is structured and warning-free, maps back to CeeSafe lines for debuggers, and has\n  no undefined behaviour by design. A reference interpreter runs the same programs and stops on any memory error,\n  which makes it a second implementation to test the compiler against.\n- **Built for kernels too.** A freestanding profile without a C library, context effects that reject sleeping while a\n  spin lock is held, C structs imported from the C codebase's own headers, and lock guards and reference-counted\n  kernel objects. All of this was exercised, as Nova, inside [CosmoOS](https://github.com/vkuttyp/CosmoOS), an\n  operating system written in C.\n\n## Status\n\nCeeSafe is **pre-1.0**. It forked from Nova 0.6.0, an equal language at the fork, and its compiler, `ceesafec`, is\nwritten in CeeSafe and compiles itself. The language and its tools work end to end, and are tested against Nova's\ncorpus of about 550 programs and against novac 0.6.0's output. But nobody has used it in production. What is done and\nwhat is next are in the repository's `ROADMAP.md`.\n\nSome things it does not have yet: trigonometry and logarithms, iterators\nbeyond `for` over arrays, slices and vectors. Each is\nplanned, or recorded as an open decision in [Nova's list at the fork](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/open-decisions.md).\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Install the compiler](installation.md) and [write a first program](getting-started.md).\n- Read [the language guide](basics.md), chapter by chapter.\n- Coming f",
    30 =>
        "rom another language? Start with [CeeSafe for Rust, Zig, C, C# and Go programmers](coming-from/index.md).\n- The full rules are in the [specification](../spec/README.md): the grammar, the type system and the ownership rules.\n",
    31 =>
        "# Kernel code\n\nCeeSafe is designed to run inside an operating system kernel as well as in user programs. Its kernel support was\ndeveloped, before the fork, by Nova against [CosmoOS](https://github.com/vkuttyp/CosmoOS), a kernel written in C,\nwhere the language runs as kernel components and as loadable modules on x86-64 and AArch64 (CosmoOS builds those with\nnovac). Nothing in it is specific to CosmoOS: the same mechanisms fit any C kernel or firmware.\n\n## The freestanding profile\n\n`--profile freestanding` (or `profile = \"freestanding\"` in a package's manifest) compiles for an environment without a\nC library or an operating system:\n\n- there is no `main`: the entry points are `@export` functions that the environment calls;\n- everything that needs a hosted runtime is unavailable, and naming it is an error: `Console`, files, sockets,\n  `SystemHeap` and the collections built on it;\n- memory comes from an allocator the environment provides, written in CeeSafe over the kernel's C functions;\n- `--no-float` (or `float = false`) rejects floating point, for code compiled without FP registers.\n\n`ceesafec build --emit obj` then writes one relocatable object, with only the undefined symbols the environment\nprovides, ready for the kernel's own build to link.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->\n```ceesafe\n/// A CRC-32C over `n` bytes at `data`, for C callers.\n/// Safety: `data` points to `n` readable bytes, as the C prototype's contract says.\n@export(\"crc32c_sum\")\nunsafe fn crc32c(data: *u8, n: usize) -> u32 {\n    return checksum(Slice.fromRaw(data, n));\n}\n\nfn checksum(bytes: Slice<u8>) -> u32 {\n    var crc: u32 = 0xFFFFFFFF;\n    for (b in bytes) {\n        crc = crc ^ (b as u32);\n        for (bit in 0..8) {\n            let mask = if ((crc & 1) != 0) 0x82F63B78u32 else 0u32;\n            crc = (crc >> 1) ^ mask;\n        }\n    }\n    return crc ^ 0xFFFFFFFF;\n}\n```\n\nThe unsafe part is confined to the boundary: the exported function turns C's pointer and length into a slice under a\nstated contract, and everything behind it is safe, checked CeeSafe.\n\n## Context effects\n\nKernel code runs in contexts with rules: while a spin lock is held it must not sleep; in an interrupt handler it must\nnot sleep or allocate. CeeSafe checks these rules at compile time, across function calls:\n\n- an operation's **effects** are `sleep` (it may block) and `alloc` (it may allocate), inferred for every function\n  from what it does and calls, its destructors included;\n- `@effects(…)` declares what a C function may do, or bounds what a CeeSafe function may do;\n- `@context(atomic)` on a function forbids `sleep` in it; `@context(irq)` forbids `sleep` and `alloc`;\n- `@context(atomic)` on a **struct** makes holding one an atomic context: a spin-lock guard.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0295 -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/sched.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn cosmo_sleep_ms(ms: u32);\n}\n\n/// Holding a spin-lock guard makes the code that runs meanwhile atomic: it must not sleep.\n@context(atomic)\nstruct SpinGuard { cpu: u32; drop {} }\n\n/// Safety: cosmo_sleep_ms has no preconditions; it may sleep.\nfn backOff() { unsafe { cosmo_sleep_ms(1); } }\n\nfn update(counter: borrow mut u64) {\n    let guard = SpinGuard { cpu: 0 };\n    counter += 1;\n    backOff(); // sleeps while the guard is held\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:17:5: error[N0295]: a 'SpinGuard' is live here, which makes this an atomic context (type-system.md §32.3), but this may sleep: it calls 'backOff', which calls 'cosmo_sleep_ms', declared @effects(sleep)\n   |\n17 |     backOff(); // sleeps while the guard is held\n   |     ^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nThis proves that CeeSafe code does nothing its context forbids, given that the `@effects` of C functions are declared\ncorrectly. It does not prove the absence of deadlocks or lock-order inversions: the kernel's own lockdep stays the\ncheck for those.\n\n## Hardware registers\n\n`lib/core` provides `Volatile<T>`, a cell read and written only by volatile accesses, and `MmioPtr<T>`, the address of\nmemory-",
    32 =>
        "mapped registers. Making an `MmioPtr` is the one unsafe step; register access through it is safe.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct UartRegs {\n    data: Volatile<u32>;\n    status: Volatile<u32>;\n}\n\nstruct Uart {\n    regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>;\n\n    /// Safety: `base` is the mapped address of a UART that no other `Uart` aliases.\n    public unsafe fn attach(base: usize) -> Uart => Uart { regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>.at(base) };\n\n    public fn putByte(borrow mut this, b: u8) {\n        while ((this.regs.get().status.read() & 0x20) != 0) {\n            Cpu.spinHint();\n        }\n        this.regs.getMut().data.write(b as u32);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Kernel allocators, locks and objects\n\nThe CosmoOS integration (the repository's `integrations/cosmoos/`) builds the rest from these pieces:\n\n- `KernelHeap`, an allocator over `kmalloc`/`kfree`, so `Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, value)` allocates kernel memory\n  and returns `Err` when the kernel has none;\n- `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` that hold the data they protect: the data is reachable only through a\n  guard, the guard unlocks when it is destroyed, and a spin guard is an atomic context;\n- `KRef<T>`, a reference-counted kernel object whose final release may sleep, so dropping one under a spin lock does\n  not compile;\n- C kernel state (lists, locks, kernel objects) embedded by value in CeeSafe structs, built in place and never moved;\n- a loadable module whose state, `init` and teardown are CeeSafe.\n\nHow these were built and tested inside CosmoOS, including a stress test under lockdep, is in the\n[CosmoOS reports](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/cosmoos/c7-report.md) and the [profiles](../spec/profiles.md).\n\n## Next\n\n[The ceesafec command](ceesafec.md).\n",
    33 =>
        "# Memory and allocation\n\nCeeSafe has no hidden allocation. Values live on the stack unless the program asks for memory, and every request names\nwhere the memory comes from and returns an error when there is none. This chapter goes from the simplest owner of\nheap memory, `Box`, to arenas, pools and scratch frames, and ends with how the compiler moves boxes to the stack when\nit can prove that is safe.\n\nThe rules are in [Memory model](../spec/memory-model.md) and the type system's sections on\n[boxes](../spec/type-system.md#_18-boxes-and-allocators-m3b) and\n[regions](../spec/type-system.md#_19-regions-m4).\n\n## `Box`\n\n`Box<T>` owns one `T` on the heap. It is one pointer, it moves (never copies), and destroying it destroys the `T`\nand frees the memory. Fields and methods are reached through it as if it were the value.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n    fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> => Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });\n\nfn main() {\n    var account = open(7)!;\n    account.deposit(100);                  // through the box\n    Console.writeLine(account.balance);\n}                                          // the Account is destroyed and its memory freed here\n```\n\n```text\n100\n```\n\nBoxes are how recursive types are built, since a type cannot contain itself directly:\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Tree {\n    Leaf,\n    Node(Box<Tree>, i32, Box<Tree>),\n}\n\nfn sum(t: borrow Tree) -> i32 => match (t) {\n    Tree.Leaf => 0,\n    Tree.Node(left, value, right) => sum(left) + value + sum(right),\n};\n\nfn leaf() -> Box<Tree> => Box.create(Tree.Leaf)!;\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Tree.Node(Box.create(Tree.Node(leaf(), 1, leaf()))!, 2, Box.create(Tree.Node(leaf(), 3, leaf()))!);\n    Console.writeLine(sum(tree));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n```\n\nA long list of boxes is destroyed with a loop, not recursion, so it cannot overflow the stack.\n\n## Allocators\n\n`Box<T>` is `Box<T, SystemHeap>`: memory from the platform's `malloc`. The allocator is part of the type, and\n`Box.createIn(allocator, value)` chooses another one:\n\n| Allocator | Gives | Frees |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SystemHeap` | the platform heap | each value when its owner is destroyed |\n| `Arena` | chunks of one region | everything at once, when the arena is destroyed or reset |\n| `FixedBufferAllocator` | a buffer you provide (an array on the stack, say) | nothing: the buffer is reused when the allocator ends |\n| `Scratch` frames | a runtime stack of chunks, last in first out | everything in a frame when the frame ends |\n| `Pool<T>` | slots for up to *n* values of one type | each slot when its box is destroyed |\n| your own | any struct that implements `Allocator` | as you implement it |\n\n`FailingAllocator` is a `SystemHeap` that can be told to fail after *n* allocations, for testing failure paths.\n\n## Arenas\n\nAn arena hands out memory quickly and frees it all at once. Every allocation **borrows the arena**, so none can\noutlive it: the compiler rejects any attempt to keep one after the arena is reset or destroyed.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn histogram(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap {}, 65536u64);\n    let counts = arena.allocArray(256usize, 0u32)?;   // a SliceMut<u32> in the arena\n    for (b in input) {\n        counts[b as usize] += 1;\n    }\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for (c in counts) {\n        if (c != 0) {\n            distinct += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return Ok(distinct);                               // a number, not arena memory\n}                                                      // the arena releases everything here\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(histogram(\"hello\".bytes())!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n4\n```\n\nValues in an arena must not need destruction (no `drop` blocks, no boxes inside), since the arena never runs their\ndestructors. Returning `counts` itself would be a compile error: it borrows an arena that ends with the function.\n\n## A ",
    34 =>
        "fixed buffer\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Span {\n    start: u32;\n    length: u32;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var storage = [0u8; 1024];\n    let fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);       // borrows `storage` mutably\n    let spans = fba.allocArray(16usize, Span { start: 0, length: 0 })!;\n    spans[3].length = 5;\n    Console.writeLine(spans[3].length);\n    Console.writeLine(spans.length);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5\n16\n```\n\nWhen the buffer is full, an allocation returns `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`; nothing is ever taken from the heap.\n\n## Scratch frames\n\nScratch memory is a stack of frames: a function enters a frame, allocates temporaries from it, and they are all\nreleased when the frame ends. Nested frames borrow their parent, so frames always end in order.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn render(n: usize) -> Result<usize, AllocError> {\n    using frame = Scratch.enter();\n    let buffer = frame.allocArray(n, 0u8)?;\n    {\n        using inner = frame.enter();\n        let temporary = inner.allocArray(n * 2, 0u16)?;\n        temporary[0] = 1;\n    }                                                  // `inner`'s memory is released here\n    return Ok(buffer.length);\n}                                                      // `frame`'s memory is released here\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(render(64)!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n64\n```\n\n## Pools\n\nA pool holds up to a fixed number of values of one type. `take` returns a box-like `PoolBox` that returns its slot\nwhen destroyed, so pool values may have `drop` blocks.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Particle {\n    x: i32;\n    v: i32;\n}\n\nfn simulate() -> Result<i32, AllocError> {\n    using pool: Pool<Particle> = Pool.create(SystemHeap {}, 64u32);\n    var a = pool.take(Particle { x: 0, v: 2 })?;\n    let b = pool.take(Particle { x: 10, v: -1 })?;\n    a.x += a.v;\n    return Ok(a.x + b.x);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(simulate()!);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n```\n\n## Your own allocator\n\nAn allocator is a struct with no fields that implements `lib/core`'s `Allocator` interface: `allocate` returns memory\nfor a `Layout` (size and alignment) or an `AllocError`, and `deallocate`, which is `unsafe` because it must be given\nexactly what `allocate` returned, gives it back. `Box.createIn(MyAllocator {}, value)` then uses it. The CosmoOS\nkernel's `KernelHeap` over `kmalloc` is written this way; see [Kernel code](kernel.md) and the\n[lib/core reference](../spec/api/core.md).\n\n## Placement: boxes on the stack\n\nThe compiler looks for boxes that provably never outlive their function, or one loop iteration, and puts them on the\nstack instead. The program cannot tell the difference: destruction happens at the same points and in the same\norder, and allocation failure can no longer happen for those boxes. `ceesafec build --explain-allocations` lists each\nallocation and the decision; `--no-placement` turns it off. The rules are in\n[Optimisations](../spec/optimisations.md).\n\n## Next\n\n[Arrays, slices and collections](collections.md).\n",
    35 =>
        "# Ownership and borrowing\n\nCeeSafe manages memory without a garbage collector by tracking, at compile time, who owns each value and who is\nlooking at it. This chapter explains the rules, why they hold, and what the compiler says when a program breaks\nthem. The formal rules are in [Ownership](../spec/ownership.md) and\n[References and the borrow checker](../spec/lifetimes.md).\n\n## Copy types and move types\n\nIntegers, floats, `bool`, references and string views are **Copy**: assigning one copies it, and both copies stay\nusable. A struct or enum is Copy when everything in it is Copy and it has no `drop` block.\n\nEverything else is a **move type**: boxes, vectors, strings, files, and any struct that owns one or has a `drop`\nblock. Assigning a move-type value, passing it, returning it or storing it **moves** it. The old place is then empty,\nand using it is a compile error.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0250 -->\n```ceesafe\nstruct Buffer {\n    size: u32;\n    drop { Console.writeLine(\"freed\"); }\n}\n\nfn consume(b: Buffer) {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Buffer { size: 64 };\n    consume(b);\n    Console.writeLine(b.size);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:11:5: error[N0250]: 'b.size' is used here after it was moved (on at least one path)\n   |\n11 |     Console.writeLine(b.size);\n   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\nMoves are what make destruction safe: a value has exactly one owner, so it is destroyed exactly once.\n\n## Destruction\n\nA value is destroyed when its owner goes away: at the end of the variable's scope, when a temporary's statement\nends, or when an assignment overwrites it. Destroying a value runs its `drop` block, if it has one, and then destroys\nits fields. Locals are destroyed in the reverse order of their declaration.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Noisy {\n    id: u32;\n    drop {\n        Console.write(\"drop \");\n        Console.writeLine(this.id);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let first = Noisy { id: 1 };\n    let second = Noisy { id: 2 };\n    {\n        let inner = Noisy { id: 3 };\n    }                                   // inner is destroyed here\n    var replaced = Noisy { id: 4 };\n    replaced = Noisy { id: 5 };         // 4 is destroyed by the assignment\n    let moved = first;                  // first is moved: it is destroyed once, as `moved`\n    Console.writeLine(\"end of main\");\n}\n```\n\n```text\ndrop 3\ndrop 4\nend of main\ndrop 1\ndrop 5\ndrop 2\n```\n\nAt the end of `main`, the locals are destroyed in reverse order of declaration: `moved` (holding 1), `replaced`\n(holding 5), then `second`. `first` was moved, so it is not destroyed again. A value moved on only some paths gets a\nhidden flag, so it is still destroyed exactly once.\n\nA **panic** or `Process.exit` does not run `drop` blocks: a panic stops the program at once (there is no unwinding).\n\n### `defer` and `using`\n\n`defer s;` runs `s` when the enclosing block exits by any path: its end, `break`, `continue` or `return`.\n`using x = e;` declares a local that cannot be moved away: it lives exactly as long as its block, which documents\nthat its lifetime *is* the scope (a lock guard, an arena, a file).\n\n```ceesafe\nfn work(fail: bool) -> i32 {\n    defer Console.writeLine(\"cleanup\");\n    if (fail) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(\"working\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(work(false));\n    Console.writeLine(work(true));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nworking\ncleanup\n0\ncleanup\n1\n```\n\n## Borrowing\n\nMost code does not want to take ownership; it wants to look at a value or change it in place. A **reference** does\nthat without moving:\n\n- `borrow T` is a shared reference: it can read, and any number can exist at once;\n- `borrow mut T` is an exclusive reference: it can read and write, and while it is in use nothing else can touch\n  the value.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Counter {\n    hits: u64;\n}\n\nfn show(c: borrow Counter) {               // looks at the counter\n    Console.writeLine(c.hits);\n}\n\nfn record(c: borrow mut Counter) {         // changes it in place\n    c.hits += 1;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var counter = Counter",
    36 =>
        " { hits: 0 };\n    record(mut counter);                   // `mut` at the call: this call changes `counter`\n    record(mut counter);\n    show(counter);                         // borrowed for the call; no marker needed\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\n```\n\nA `borrow T` parameter accepts the value itself, borrowed implicitly. A `borrow mut T` parameter needs `mut` before\nthe argument, so that every place where a value may change is visible where it is called, as C#'s `ref` is.\n\n### The rule\n\nWhile a borrow is **live**, meaning the reference or anything made from it is still used later, the borrowed value\nmay not be:\n\n- changed, mutably borrowed or destroyed, if the borrow is shared;\n- used in any way, if the borrow is mutable.\n\nThe compiler checks this within each function, on every path. A borrow ends at its last use, not at the end of its\nscope.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0240 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var total = 0;\n    let r = borrow total;\n    total += 1;\n    Console.writeLine(r);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:5: error[N0240]: cannot assign to 'total' while 'total' is borrowed\n  |\n4 |     total += 1;\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^\n  = note: 'total' is borrowed here (3:5)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (5:5)\n```\n\nMoving the `Console.writeLine(r)` above the `+=` fixes it: the borrow is then dead before the write. The same rule\nis what stops a vector from growing while an element is borrowed, since growing can move its elements.\n\n### Returning references\n\nA function that returns a reference must return something borrowed from its parameters, never from its own locals.\nWith one reference parameter, that is assumed. With several, the result names the ones it may borrow from:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn larger(a: borrow i64, b: borrow i64) -> borrow(a, b) i64 => if (a > b) a else b;\n\nfn firstPositive(values: Slice<i64>) -> borrow i64 {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        if (values[i] > 0) {\n            return borrow values[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return borrow values[0];\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x: i64 = 3;\n    let y: i64 = 7;\n    Console.writeLine(larger(x, y));\n    let numbers = [-1i64, 4, 9];\n    Console.writeLine(firstPositive(numbers[..]));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n4\n```\n\nThe caller then knows how long the result stays valid: as long as the arguments it borrows from. Returning a\nreference to a local is rejected, since the local is gone when the function returns:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0244 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn pick(a: borrow i32) -> borrow i32 {\n    let copy = a + 1;\n    return borrow copy;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(pick(1));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:3:5: error[N0244]: cannot return a reference to 'copy', which is local to 'pick'\n  |\n3 |     return borrow copy;\n  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  = note: borrowed here (3:12)\n```\n\n### Two mutable borrows\n\nTwo `borrow mut` references to one value can never be live together, even as two arguments of one call:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn addTo(target: borrow mut i32, amount: borrow mut i32) {\n    target += amount;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    var x = 1;\n    addTo(mut x, mut x);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:7:18: error[N0241]: cannot borrow 'x' as mutable while 'x' is mutably borrowed\n  |\n7 |     addTo(mut x, mut x);\n  |                  ^^^^^\n  = note: 'x' is mutably borrowed here (7:11)\n  = note: the borrow is used later here (7:5)\n```\n\n## References in structs: regions\n\nA struct that holds a reference or a view must say so, with a **region parameter**: the region is the lifetime of\nwhat the reference points into. Values of such a type cannot outlive that region.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Word<region src> {\n    text: StringView<src>;\n    start: usize;\n}\n\nfn firstWord<region src>(line: StringView<src>) -> Word<src> {\n    var end: usize = 0;\n    while (end < line.length && line[end] != ' ') {\n        end += 1;\n    }\n    return Word { text: line[0..end], start: 0 };\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let line = \"hello wide world\";\n    let word = firstWord(line);\n    Console.writeLine(word.text);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nhello\n```\n\nPlain functions that",
    37 =>
        " take and return references never need region parameters; only types that *store* references,\nand functions that pass such types through, do. Regions are a compile-time idea: they cost nothing at run time.\n\n## What the checker does not do\n\nThe borrow checker works within one function at a time, using the signatures of the functions it calls. It is\ndeliberately simpler than Rust's: some correct programs are rejected. When that happens, the usual fixes are to end\na borrow earlier, copy a value out, split a function, or use an index or a [slot map handle](collections.md) instead\nof a stored reference.\n\n## Next\n\n[Errors](errors.md): `Result`, `?`, `!` and panics.\n",
    38 =>
        "# Packages\n\nA package is a directory with a `ceesafe.toml`: a program or a library, its sources, and the packages it depends on.\nDependencies come from local paths or git repositories, pinned to exact commits in `ceesafe.lock`. The full rules are in\n[Tooling §4](../spec/tooling.md#_4-packages-t6-adr-0013) and [ADR 0013](../spec/decisions/0013-m10-modules-and-packages.md).\n\n## The manifest\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"app\"                 # an identifier: what other packages call this one\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nkind = \"executable\"          # \"executable\", \"library\", or \"object\" (one .o for a kernel's build)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float\nsources = [\"src\"]            # the default\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C files built with an executable\n\n[dependencies]\ngeometry = { path = \"../geometry\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }      # or rev = \"<commit>\", or branch = \"main\"\n```\n\nOnly `name` is required. A dependency's key must be the name its own manifest gives it, and a dependency must be a\nlibrary.\n\n## Using a dependency\n\nAll files of one package share one scope. Another package's items are reached through its name, and only its\n`public` items are visible:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// geometry/src/lib.cee\npublic struct Point {\n    public x: i64;\n    public y: i64;\n\n    public fn create(x: i64, y: i64) -> Point => Point { x: x, y: y };\n    public fn norm(this) -> i64 => this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y;\n}\n\npublic fn origin() -> Point => Point.create(0, 0);\n\nfn helper() -> i64 => 1;          // internal: only geometry can call it\n```\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n// app/src/main.cee\nimport geometry.Point;            // one item; `import geometry;` brings all its public items\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point.create(3, 4);   // imported\n    let q = geometry.origin();    // qualified\n    Console.writeLine(p.norm() + q.norm());\n}\n```\n\n- `geometry.X` names a dependency's public item, in types, expressions and patterns.\n- `import geometry.X;` brings one item into the file's scope, and `import geometry;` all its public items. Imports\n  apply to their file only. A name two imports provide is an error where it is used.\n- Within a package, the name of a dependency is reserved: nothing else in the package may be called `geometry`.\n- A public function may not expose a type its users cannot see (a public function returning an internal struct).\n- Two packages may declare the same name: in the generated C, a dependency's items carry its package's name.\n\n## The lock file\n\n```sh\nceesafec fetch          # resolves dependencies and writes ceesafe.lock\nceesafec build          # uses ceesafe.lock; fails if it does not match ceesafe.toml\n```\n\n`ceesafe.lock` records every dependency, direct and indirect: where it comes from, and for a git dependency the exact\ncommit and a SHA-256 of its files. Commit it with `ceesafe.toml`, so that every build of the package uses the same code.\n\n- A `tag` or `branch` is resolved to a commit by `ceesafec fetch` and stays that commit until the next fetch, even if the\n  branch moves.\n- Every build checks each git dependency's files against the lock's hash, so a checkout changed behind your back is\n  an error.\n- Git repositories and checkouts are cached in `~/.ceesafe` (or `$CEESAFE_HOME`). `--offline` builds never touch the network.\n- Nothing runs during a fetch or a build except `git` and the C compiler: packages have no build scripts.\n- Two path or git dependencies that need the same package must agree on its source and commit.\n\n## The registry\n\nA dependency can also be a version requirement, looked up in the package index:\n\n```toml\n[dependencies]\nshapes = \"2.1\"                # any 2.x from 2.1.0 on\ngeometry = \"=1.4.0\"           # exactly\njson = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }\n```\n\n`ceesafec fetch` chooses the newest version of each package that satisfies every requirement on it,",
    39 =>
        " and records its\ncommit and hash in `ceesafe.lock`, so builds stay pinned until the next fetch. A version already locked is kept while it\nstill fits; `ceesafec fetch --update` (or `--update shapes`) chooses afresh. When no choice works, the error says which\nrequirements clash and where each came from.\n\nTo publish a library, give its manifest a `version`, commit and push it, and run `ceesafec publish`. It checks the package\nand prints the entry to add to the index by pull request.\n\n## Building\n\n```sh\nceesafec check          # the package and its dependencies, checked together\nceesafec build          # out/<name>\nceesafec run --interp\nceesafec fmt            # the package's own sources\nceesafec doc -o api.md  # its public items\n```\n\nA build compiles the package and all its dependencies together into one C program, so generic code from a dependency\nis specialised for its users and checked like the rest.\n\n## Files without a package\n\nEverything also works on files: `ceesafec build a.cee b.cee -o tool`. Files given together are one package with no\ndependencies.\n",
    40 =>
        "# Structs, enums and patterns\n\n## Structs\n\nA struct groups named fields. Fields end with `;`. Functions declared inside are its methods (with a `this`\nreceiver) or static functions (without one).\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64;\n    y: i64;\n\n    fn origin() -> Point => Point { x: 0, y: 0 };            // static: Point.origin()\n\n    fn manhattan(this) -> i64 {                               // `this` by value (Point is Copy)\n        return abs(this.x) + abs(this.y);\n    }\n\n    fn moveBy(borrow mut this, dx: i64, dy: i64) {            // changes the point in place\n        this.x += dx;\n        this.y += dy;\n    }\n}\n\nfn abs(v: i64) -> i64 => if (v < 0) -v else v;\n\nfn main() {\n    var p = Point.origin();\n    p.moveBy(3, -4);\n    Console.writeLine(p.manhattan());\n    let q = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };       // every field, in any order\n    Console.writeLine(q.x + q.y);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n7\n3\n```\n\n- A **struct literal** names every field exactly once.\n- The **receiver** says how a method gets its struct: `this` (a copy, or a move), `borrow this` (read-only access) or\n  `borrow mut this` (changes the caller's value). Calling a `borrow mut this` method needs a `var` (or a mutable\n  reference); the call borrows it for the duration of the call.\n- A struct is **Copy**, copied on assignment, when every field is Copy and it has no `drop` block. Otherwise it\n  moves: see [Ownership](ownership.md).\n- Fields are laid out in declaration order with C's rules, so a struct is the same in C.\n\n### Visibility\n\nItems and members are `internal` by default: visible throughout their package. `public` makes them visible to other\npackages (see [Packages](packages.md)); `private` on a field limits it to the file that declares the struct, which\nlets a type guard an invariant.\n\n## Enums\n\nAn enum is one of several variants, each with zero or more positional payloads. Variants are written with the enum's\nname.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Shape {\n    Empty,\n    Circle(i64),\n    Rect(i64, i64),\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> i64 => match (s) {\n    Shape.Empty => 0,\n    Shape.Circle(r) => 3 * r * r,\n    Shape.Rect(w, h) => w * h,\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Circle(2)));\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Rect(3, 4)));\n    Console.writeLine(area(Shape.Empty));\n}\n```\n\n```text\n12\n12\n0\n```\n\n## `match`\n\n`match` tries its arms in order. It must be **exhaustive**: every possible value matches some arm, or the compiler\nnames one that does not. An arm that can never be reached is an error too.\n\nAs a statement, each arm is a block; as an expression (above), each arm is a value, separated by commas.\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Command {\n    Move(i32, i32),\n    Wait(u32),\n    Quit,\n}\n\nfn run(command: Command) {\n    match (command) {\n        Command.Move(0, 0) => { Console.writeLine(\"stay\"); }\n        Command.Move(x, _) => {\n            Console.write(\"move x by \");\n            Console.writeLine(x);\n        }\n        Command.Wait(seconds) => {\n            Console.write(\"wait \");\n            Console.writeLine(seconds);\n        }\n        Command.Quit => {}\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    run(Command.Move(0, 0));\n    run(Command.Move(5, 1));\n    run(Command.Wait(3));\n    run(Command.Quit);\n}\n```\n\n```text\nstay\nmove x by 5\nwait 3\n```\n\nPatterns can be nested: a variant inside a variant (`Ok(Shape.Circle(r))`), a literal in a payload's position\n(`Command.Move(0, 0)`), `null` or `Some(…)` for optionals, and `_` for anything. Integers and booleans can be matched\ndirectly, with `_` for the rest.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn kind(n: u8) -> StringView => match (n) {\n    0 => \"zero\",\n    'a' => \"the letter a\",\n    _ => \"something else\",\n};\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(kind(97));\n    Console.writeLine(kind(3));\n}\n```\n\n```text\nthe letter a\nsomething else\n```\n\n## Optionals\n\n`T?` is a `T` or `null`. A plain `T` converts to `T?` wherever one is expected.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn find(values: Slice<i32>, wanted: i32) -> usize? {\n    for (i in 0..values.length) {\n        if (values[i] == wanted) {\n            return i;              ",
    41 =>
        "    // converts to usize?\n        }\n    }\n    return null;\n}\n\nfn firstOfTwo(values: Slice<i32>) -> usize? {\n    let a = find(values, 2)?;           // `?`: if null, return null\n    return a;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let numbers = [5, 2, 9];\n    match (find(numbers[..], 9)) {\n        Some(i) => { Console.writeLine(i); }\n        null => { Console.writeLine(\"absent\"); }\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(find(numbers[..], 7) == null);\n    Console.writeLine(firstOfTwo(numbers[..])!);   // `!`: the value, or stop the program if null\n}\n```\n\n```text\n2\ntrue\n1\n```\n\n- `o == null` and `o != null` test for absence.\n- `o!` is the value; on `null` it stops the program with \"unwrapped null\".\n- `o?` is the value; on `null` the enclosing function returns `null` (it must return an optional).\n- A reference can be optional (`borrow T?`), and so can a box (`Box<T>?`); both are one pointer.\n\n## Results\n\n`Result<T, E>` is the other built-in enum: `Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`. It is how functions report failure; see\n[Errors](errors.md).\n\n## Next\n\n[Ownership and borrowing](ownership.md): what happens when a value is assigned, passed and destroyed.\n",
    42 =>
        "# Threads\n\nA program can run work on several threads. The compiler checks that safe code never has a data race: a value moves to\nanother thread only if it is safe there, and threads share memory only through types made for it.\n\n## Spawning and joining\n\n`Thread.spawn` starts a thread running a `move` closure, which owns what it captures. It returns a `Thread<T>`, and\n`join` waits for the thread and gives back its result.\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var numbers = Vector<u64>.create();\n    for (i in 1..101u64) {\n        numbers.push(i)!;\n    }\n    let worker = Thread.spawn(move () => {\n        var sum: u64 = 0;\n        for (n in numbers[..]) {\n            sum += n;\n        }\n        return sum;\n    })!;\n    Console.writeLine(worker.join());\n}\n```\n\n```text\n5050\n```\n\n- The vector is moved into the thread. The spawner cannot use it afterwards, so nothing is shared.\n- Spawning can fail (no memory, no thread): `Thread.spawn` returns a `Result`, here unwrapped with `!`.\n- A `Thread<T>` that is dropped without `join` is joined then: every thread ends before `main` returns.\n- A panic in any thread ends the whole program.\n\n## What may cross: `Send`\n\nA value may move to another thread if it is `Send`. Plain data, strings, vectors and boxes are. References are not,\nnor anything that holds one, nor raw pointers. The compiler infers it, and reports N0334 where it fails:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0334 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn main() {\n    var n: u64 = 5;\n    let address = borrow n as *u64;\n    let t = Thread.spawn(move () => address.isNull())!;\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:4:26: error[N0334]: this thread's closure captures 'address', a '*u64', which is not Send: it cannot move to another thread (type-system.md §38.1)\n  |\n4 |     let t = Thread.spawn(move () => address.isNull())!;\n  |                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n```\n\n`Send` and `Share` can also bound a generic function: `fn run<T: Send>(value: T)`.\n\n## Sharing: `Arc`, `Mutex` and atomics\n\nTo share a value, give each thread an owner of it: `Arc<T>` counts its owners, and the last one to go frees it. An\n`Arc` gives only shared access, so a value that threads change goes in a `Mutex`, whose guard lends it to one thread\nat a time.\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Tally {\n    count: u64;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tally = Arc<Mutex<Tally>>.create(Mutex<Tally>.create(Tally { count: 0 })!)!;\n    var threads = Vector<Thread<void>>.create();\n    for (t in 0..4u64) {\n        let mine = tally.clone();\n        threads.push(Thread.spawn(move () => {\n            for (k in 0..250u64) {\n                var guard = mine.get().lock();\n                let data = guard.data();\n                data.count += 1;\n            }\n        })!)!;\n    }\n    threads.clear(); // dropping the threads joins them\n    var guard = tally.get().lock();\n    Console.writeLine(guard.data().count);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1000\n```\n\nFor a counter or a flag, an atomic is simpler: `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize` and `AtomicBool` change\nthrough a shared reference with `load`, `store`, `swap`, `compareExchange`, `fetchAdd` and `fetchSub`.\n\nThose are sequentially consistent: every thread sees every atomic operation in one order. That is the simple rule,\nand on x86-64 it costs a full barrier on each store. When one thread publishes data and another reads it, release\nand acquire are enough, and `storeWith` and `loadWith` say so:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Message {\n    data: AtomicU64;\n    ready: AtomicBool;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Message { data: AtomicU64.create(0), ready: AtomicBool.create(false) };\n    var got: u64 = 0;\n    Thread.both(() => {\n        while (!m.ready.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Acquire)) {}\n        got = m.data.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Relaxed);\n    }, () => {\n        m.data.storeWith(42, StoreOrdering.Relaxed);\n        m.ready.storeWith(true, StoreOrdering.Release);\n    });\n    Console.writeLine(got);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n42\n```\n\nThe release store makes the data written before it visible to a thread whose acquire load reads `true`. The\n`…With` method",
    43 =>
        "s take C11's orderings (`Relaxed`, `Acquire`, `Release`, `AcquireRelease`, `SequentiallyConsistent`). A\nweaker ordering never makes a data race, but a wrong one can make a thread read a stale value, and neither\n`ceesafec run --interp` nor ThreadSanitizer will notice. Use the plain methods unless a measurement says otherwise.\n`benchmarks/atomics` has one: a ring between two threads, 5 to 9 times faster on x86-64 with release and acquire.\n\n## Borrowing: `Thread.both`\n\nA spawned thread cannot borrow, since it may outlive what it borrowed. `Thread.both(a, b)` can: it runs `a` on a new\nthread and `b` on this one, and returns only when both have finished. So the two closures may use the caller's data,\nfor example the two halves of a slice:\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (v in values) {\n        total = total + v;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values: [u64; 6] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    let halves = values[..].splitAt(3);\n    var low: u64 = 0;\n    var high: u64 = 0;\n    Thread.both(() => { low = sum(halves.left); }, () => { high = sum(halves.right); });\n    Console.writeLine(low + high);\n}\n```\n\n```text\n21\n```\n\nThe usual borrowing rules hold between the two closures: both may read a value, but if one changes it the other may\nnot use it. A value both read must be `Share`, and one that a closure changes must be `Send`; a reference counts as\nits referent here. `splitAtMut` gives two halves that each closure may change. If a thread cannot be started, `both`\nruns `a` and then `b` on this thread, so it never fails.\n\n## Where threads run\n\nThreads are part of the hosted profile: Linux, macOS, Windows and CosmoOS user programs. Kernel code uses the\nkernel's own locks instead ([Kernel code](kernel.md)). The interpreter (`ceesafec run --interp`) runs threads one at a\ntime, in a fixed order, so its output is always the same.\n\n## Next\n\n[Unsafe code and C](unsafe-and-c.md).\n",
    44 =>
        "# Unsafe code and C\n\nSafe CeeSafe cannot express everything a systems program needs: talking to hardware, calling C, implementing an\nallocator. For those there is `unsafe`, kept small and visible. The contract unsafe code must keep is in\n[Unsafe code](../spec/unsafe.md), and the C boundary in the type system's\n[C interoperability](../spec/type-system.md#_28-c-interoperability-m8b) section.\n\n## Raw pointers\n\n`*T` and `*mut T` are raw pointers: plain addresses, Copy, with no borrow attached. **Making** a pointer is safe;\n**using** one (`read`, `write`, `add`, turning it back into a reference) is allowed only inside `unsafe`.\n\n```ceesafe\n/// Safety: `p` points to `n` initialised `u32`s that nothing else writes while this runs.\nunsafe fn sum(p: *u32, n: usize) -> u64 {\n    var total: u64 = 0;\n    for (i in 0..n) {\n        total += p.add(i).read() as u64;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the pointer comes from a live array and stays inside it.\nfn main() {\n    var values = [1u32, 2, 3, 4];\n    let p = borrow mut values[0] as *mut u32;\n    unsafe {\n        p.add(3).write(10);\n    }\n    Console.writeLine(unsafe { sum(p, values.length) });\n}\n```\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## The `Safety:` rule\n\nEvery `unsafe fn`, and every function that contains an `unsafe` block, must say in its doc comment why it is sound, on\na line starting with `Safety:`. The compiler enforces it:\n\n<!-- ceesafe: fail N0283 -->\n```ceesafe\nfn first(p: *u8) -> u8 {\n    return unsafe { p.read() };\n}\n```\n\n```text\nmain.cee:1:4: error[N0283]: function 'first' uses 'unsafe' and needs a '/// Safety:' doc comment saying why it is sound (type-system.md §27.4)\n  |\n1 | fn first(p: *u8) -> u8 {\n  |    ^^^^^\n```\n\nFor an `unsafe fn`, the line states what callers must guarantee; for a safe function with an `unsafe` block inside,\nwhy the block cannot go wrong. Reviewers read these lines; the compiler checks that they exist.\n\n`unsafe` permits only the pointer operations, calls of unsafe functions and C functions, and volatile accesses.\nEverything else in an unsafe block is checked exactly as in safe code.\n\n## Calling C\n\nC functions are declared in an `extern \"C\"` block and called inside `unsafe`, since CeeSafe cannot check what C does.\nTheir types are C's: integers, `bool`, raw pointers, `*CChar` for C strings, and `@repr(C)` structs.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn strlen(s: *CChar) -> usize;\n    fn puts(s: *CChar) -> i32;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the literal ends in a zero byte, as `puts` and `strlen` need.\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let text = \"hello from C\\0\";\n    let p = text.ptr as *CChar;\n    unsafe {\n        puts(p);\n    }\n    return truncate<i32>(unsafe { strlen(p) });\n}\n```\n\nC files are built with the program: `ceesafec build main.cee helpers.c -o program`, or the `c` list of a package's\nmanifest.\n\n## Being called from C\n\n`@export` makes a CeeSafe function callable from C, and `@repr(C)` gives a struct C's layout. `ceesafec` writes\n`ceesafe_exports.h` with their declarations for the C side to include.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32;\n    y: i32;\n}\n\n/// The Manhattan length of a point, callable from C as `geo_length(struct Point)`.\n@export(\"geo_length\")\nfn length(p: Point) -> i32 => abs(p.x) + abs(p.y);\n\nfn abs(v: i32) -> i32 => if (v < 0) -v else v;\n\nfn main() {\n    Console.writeLine(length(Point { x: 3, y: -4 }));\n}\n```\n\n`@callback` gives a function a C address without exporting a symbol, for C APIs that take a function pointer and a\n`void *` argument, as kernels' timer and thread APIs do. `extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` is the type of such a pointer.\n\n## C structs from the C code's headers\n\nA C codebase's own structs are not re-declared in CeeSafe, where the two copies could drift apart. They are imported from\nits headers: CeeSafe uses them by pointer or embedded by value, names only the fields it uses, and the C compiler checks\neach one at build time.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: skip -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/timer.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @ct",
    45 =>
        "ype(\"struct timer\")\n    struct timer;\n    @effects()\n    fn timer_start(t: *mut timer, delay_ns: u64);\n}\n```\n\nSee [C structs from headers](../spec/decisions/0008-c-struct-bindings-from-headers.md) and\n[Kernel code](kernel.md).\n\n### Arrays, keyword names and tables\n\nA C array member is declared as a CeeSafe array (`uint8_t uuid[16]` is `uuid: [u8; 16]`) and used like one: indexed with\nbounds checks, sliced, copied. A member whose name is a CeeSafe keyword takes `@cname`. An array of C structs is indexed in\nplace. A constant table can be exported to C as data with `@export`.\n\n<!-- ceesafe: check -->\n```ceesafe\n@header(\"kernel/gpt.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct gpt_part {\n        first_lba: u64;\n        @cname(\"type\")\n        kind: [u8; 16];\n        uuid: [u8; 16];\n    }\n    struct gpt_table {\n        nparts: u32;\n        parts: [gpt_part; 32];\n    }\n}\n\n/// `const uint8_t gpt_type_esp[16]` in C.\n@export(\"gpt_type_esp\")\nconst TYPE_ESP: [u8; 16] = [0x28, 0x73, 0x2a, 0xc1, 0x1f, 0xf8, 0xd2, 0x11, 0xba, 0x4b, 0x00, 0xa0, 0xc9, 0x3e, 0xc9, 0x3b];\n\n/// Is partition `i` an EFI system partition?\nfn isEsp(table: borrow gpt_table, i: usize) -> bool {\n    let kind = table.parts[i].kind; // a copy of the C array\n    for (k in 0..kind.length) {\n        if (kind[k] != TYPE_ESP[k]) { return false; }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\nThis is how CosmoOS's GPT parser is written in Nova, the language CeeSafe forked from ([the C9 report](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/cosmoos/c9-report.md); the rules are\n[ADR 0014](../spec/decisions/0014-c9-c-arrays-and-exported-constants.md)).\n\n## Next\n\n[Kernel code](kernel.md).\n",
    46 =>
        "# The CeeSafe Specification\n\nCeeSafe is a memory-safe systems programming language with C#/TypeScript-style syntax, compiled ahead of time to\nportable C. This directory is its specification. `ceesafec` embeds it: `ceesafec explain` and the MCP server\n(`ceesafe_spec_search`, `ceesafe_spec_section`, the `ceesafe://docs/…` resources) serve this text.\n\nCeeSafe forked from Nova at Nova `v0.6.0`, when the two were the same language (CeeSafe's Decisions 0003 and 0006 in\nthe repository's `docs/decisions/`). This text began as Nova's specification at that tag, renamed and changed where the\nfork changed something (§3). From the fork on it is CeeSafe's own: Nova's later changes do not apply to it, and its\nchanges do not apply to Nova.\n\n## 1. Documents\n\nNormative, in order of authority after the ADRs:\n\n| Document | What it specifies |\n|---|---|\n| [`decisions/`](decisions/) | the ADRs: accepted decisions override the other documents' proposals (§2) |\n| [`ownership.md`](ownership.md) | the safety invariants (O*, B*, E*, R*, H*, U*) and their formal design |\n| [`language-spec.md`](language-spec.md) | the language: goals, syntax overview, semantics |\n| [`grammar.md`](grammar.md) | the grammar |\n| [`type-system.md`](type-system.md) | types and static semantics, the built-in types and libraries, chapter by chapter |\n| [`lifetimes.md`](lifetimes.md) | references and the borrow checker |\n| [`memory-model.md`](memory-model.md) | allocation, regions, placement |\n| [`unsafe.md`](unsafe.md) | `unsafe`, raw pointers and their contract |\n| [`c-backend.md`](c-backend.md) | the generated C: its rules, names, ABI version, structure |\n| [`optimisations.md`](optimisations.md) | each optimisation's preconditions and checks |\n| [`profiles.md`](profiles.md) | the hosted and freestanding profiles and their runtimes |\n| [`diagnostics.md`](diagnostics.md) | every diagnostic code |\n| [`tooling.md`](tooling.md) | the command line, `doc`, `lsp`, debugging, packages, fixes, `explain`, `mcp`, agent files |\n| [`formatting.md`](formatting.md) | the canonical style `ceesafec fmt` writes |\n| [`api/core.md`](api/core.md) | `lib/core`'s API, generated by `ceesafec doc` |\n\nDescriptive: [`architecture.md`](architecture.md) (how `ceesafec` is built),\n[`intermediate-representation.md`](intermediate-representation.md) (the AST, HIR and NIR as implemented) and\n[`testing.md`](testing.md) (how CeeSafe is tested).\n\nThe [guide](../guide/introduction.md) teaches the language; every example in it is compiled by `tools/guide_check.py`.\nBoth are published at [vkuttyp.github.io/CeeSafe](https://vkuttyp.github.io/CeeSafe/) (the repository's `site/`).\n\n## 2. Decisions\n\n`decisions/0001`–`0030` are Nova's ADRs, inherited at the fork. Their text is Nova's record, unchanged, under a header\nthat says so. CeeSafe's own language decisions continue the series from ADR 0031 in the same directory. The port's\ndecisions (how CeeSafe was made from Nova: names, bootstrap, the fork point) are \"Decisions\" in the repository's\n`docs/decisions/`, a separate series.\n\n## 3. What the fork changed\n\nAt the fork the language is Nova `v0.6.0`'s; what differs is how it is named and built:\n\n- the compiler is `ceesafec`; source files are `.cee`; packages are `ceesafe.toml` and `ceesafe.lock`, the cache is\n  `$CEESAFE_HOME` (default `~/.ceesafe`), and the default registry index is `github.com/vkuttyp/CeeSafe-index`;\n- the command line reads only `.cee` and `.c` files (`tooling.md`);\n- the generated C and the runtime use CeeSafe's names: `cee_`, `ceeF_`, `ceeMk_`, … for generated names, and\n  `ceesafe_*`, `CEE_*`, `CEESAFE_*`, `ceesafe/rt.h` for the runtime; programs may not declare C names with those\n  prefixes (N0292); and `ceesafe/rt.h` defines `CEESAFE_ABI_VERSION`, which every generated unit checks\n  (`c-backend.md` §1.1);\n- messages say CeeSafe: `ceesafe panic: …`.\n\n## 4. Reading this text\n\n- **Milestone tags** (M1–M15, C0–C9, K1–K7, G7b, …) name the Nova milestone that introduced a rule. They ar",
    47 =>
        "e kept so a\n  rule can be traced to Nova's history; they say nothing about CeeSafe's plan.\n- **Links to Nova.** A document CeeSafe did not fork (Nova's milestones, roadmap, open decisions, reports, research)\n  is linked in Nova's repository at `v0.6.0`.\n- **CosmoOS.** The kernel profile, `lib/kernel` and the CosmoOS bindings were developed by Nova inside CosmoOS, and\n  CosmoOS builds its Nova code with novac. Where this text reports what was run in CosmoOS, it was run with Nova.\n- **Paths.** `src/…` is `ceesafec`'s source. `lib/…` and `runtime/…` name the library and runtime sources ceesafec\n  embeds, generated at the fork from Nova's (`architecture.md` §6). `tests/corpus/…`, `benchmarks/…` and `programs/…`\n  are CeeSafe's copies under `tests/reference/` (Nova's at the fork, as `.cee` files). `integrations/cosmoos/…` is\n  CeeSafe's own adaptation of Nova's CosmoOS checks (`testing.md` §1).\n- **Tests.** A test named like a C# class (`FixTests`, `PlacementTests`) is Nova's; where CeeSafe has its own check,\n  the text names its tool (`testing.md`).\n\n## 5. Changing it\n\nSpec before code: a change to the language changes this specification first, with an ADR when it is a decision, and\nthen the compiler, its tests and the guide. Each change that makes CeeSafe differ from novac 0.6.0 is recorded as an\nexception in the comparisons (`testing.md` §3).\n",
    48 =>
        "# lib/core\n\n- [struct PhysAddr](#struct-physaddr)\n- [struct VirtAddr](#struct-virtaddr)\n- [struct Layout](#struct-layout)\n- [interface Allocator](#interface-allocator)\n- [enum LoadOrdering](#enum-loadordering)\n- [enum StoreOrdering](#enum-storeordering)\n- [enum Ordering](#enum-ordering)\n- [struct AtomicU64](#struct-atomicu64)\n- [struct AtomicU32](#struct-atomicu32)\n- [struct AtomicUSize](#struct-atomicusize)\n- [struct AtomicBool](#struct-atomicbool)\n- [struct Volatile](#struct-volatile)\n- [struct MmioPtr](#struct-mmioptr)\n- [struct SliceHalves](#struct-slicehalves)\n- [struct SliceHalvesMut](#struct-slicehalvesmut)\n- [struct SliceSplitting](#struct-slicesplitting)\n- [struct SliceAlgorithms](#struct-slicealgorithms)\n\n## struct PhysAddr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct PhysAddr\n```\n\nA physical address.\n\n### PhysAddr.of\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### PhysAddr.bits\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### PhysAddr.offset\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct VirtAddr\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct VirtAddr\n```\n\nA virtual address.\n\n### VirtAddr.of\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### VirtAddr.bits\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### VirtAddr.offset\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct Layout\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct Layout\n```\n\nThe size in bytes and the alignment (a power of two) of a block of memory.\n\n## interface Allocator\n\n```ceesafe\npublic interface Allocator {\n    fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, p: *mut u8, layout: Layout);\n}\n```\n\nAn allocator a `Box<T, A>` can use: a struct without fields that implements this (type-system.md §30.2).\n\n## enum LoadOrdering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum LoadOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic load (ADR 0021): C11's memory_order_relaxed, _acquire and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum StoreOrdering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum StoreOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Release,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic store: C11's memory_order_relaxed, _release and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum Ordering\n\n```ceesafe\npublic enum Ordering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    Release,\n    AcquireRelease,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic read-modify-write (swap, compare-exchange, add, subtract): C11's memory_order_relaxed,\n_acquire, _release, _acq_rel and _seq_cst. A compare-exchange that fails orders as a load: AcquireRelease as\nAcquire, Release as Relaxed, the others as themselves.\n\n## struct AtomicU64\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU64\n```\n\nA u64 that threads may read and change at once: every operation is atomic and sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicU64.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU64.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU64.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: the runtime accesses `value` only atomically, and safe code reaches it only through these methods.\n\n### AtomicU64.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u64)\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### Atom",
    49 =>
        "icU64.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u64\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64, order: Ordering) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicU32\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU32\n```\n\nA u32 that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64.\n\n### AtomicU32.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU32.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU32.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u32)\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u32\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32, order: Ordering) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicUSize\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA usize that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64 (usize is 64 bits on every target, §29.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-syste",
    50 =>
        "m.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicUSize.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> usize\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: usize)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: usize, desired: usize) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAdd\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSub\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(\n    borrow this,\n    expected: usize,\n    desired: usize,\n    order: Ordering,\n) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAddWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchAdd, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSubWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchSub, with `order`.\n\n## struct AtomicBool\n\n```ceesafe\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicBool\n```\n\nA bool that threads may read and change at once.\n\n### AtomicBool.create\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn create(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicBool.constant\n\n```ceesafe\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicBool.load\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> bool\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.store\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: bool)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.swap\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: bool) -> bool\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchange\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicBool.loadWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.storeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.swapWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: Ordering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchangeWith\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool, order: Ordering) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n## struct Volatile\n\n```ceesafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct Volatile<T: Copy>\n```\n\nA cell read and written only by volatile accesses: a hardware register, or memory shared with a device.\nNot Copy: a copy would read the register with an ordinary access, once, instead of each time it is used.\n\n### Volatile.read\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn read(borrow this) -> T\n```\n\nSafety: `this.value` is a live, aligned `T` (it is a field), and the read is a single volatil",
    51 =>
        "e access.\n\n### Volatile.write\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn write(borrow mut this, v: T)\n```\n\nSafety: as `read`; `borrow mut this` makes the write exclusive.\n\n## struct MmioPtr\n\n```ceesafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct MmioPtr<T>\n```\n\nThe address of a memory-mapped `T`, typically a `@repr(C)` struct of `Volatile` registers. Not Copy: two\ncopies would be two owners of the same registers.\n\n### MmioPtr.at\n\n```ceesafe\npublic unsafe fn at(address: usize) -> MmioPtr<T>\n```\n\nSafety: `address` is mapped, aligned for `T`, and no other `MmioPtr` or reference aliases the registers.\n\n### MmioPtr.get\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T\n```\n\nSafety: `at`'s contract makes `base` a live `T` reached only through this value; the result borrows `this`.\n\n### MmioPtr.getMut\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T\n```\n\nSafety: as `get`; `borrow mut this` makes the access exclusive.\n\n### MmioPtr.address\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn address(borrow this) -> usize\n```\n\nThe address of the registers, as given to `at`.\n\n## struct SliceHalves\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct SliceHalves<region r, T> {\n    public left: Slice<r, T>;\n    public right: Slice<r, T>;\n}\n```\n\nThe two parts of a slice that `splitAt` divides (type-system.md §16.5): the elements before the split, and from it on.\n\n## struct SliceHalvesMut\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct SliceHalvesMut<region r, T> {\n    public left: SliceMut<r, T>;\n    public right: SliceMut<r, T>;\n}\n```\n\nThe two parts of a mutable slice that `splitAtMut` divides: disjoint, so both may be written at once.\n\n## struct SliceSplitting\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct SliceSplitting<region r, T>\n```\n\nSplitting a slice in two (type-system.md §16.5): `s.splitAt(mid)` is `SliceSplitting<T>.splitAt(s, mid)`. The\nstruct's region ties the halves to the slice they come from; it has no fields and is never made.\n\n### SliceSplitting.splitAt\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn splitAt(s: Slice<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalves<r, T>\n```\n\nThe elements before `mid`, and from `mid` on; panics if `mid` is past the end.\n\n### SliceSplitting.splitAtMut\n\n```ceesafe\npublic fn splitAtMut(s: SliceMut<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalvesMut<r, T>\n```\n\nAs splitAt, for a mutable slice: two mutable slices that do not overlap.\nSafety: slicing `s[mid..]` checks `mid` against the length (it panics past the end), so `[0, mid)` and\n`[mid, length)` are inside `s` and disjoint, and each is lent for as long as `s` is.\n\n## struct SliceAlgorithms\n\n```ceesafe\npublic struct SliceAlgorithms<T>\n```\n\nThe algorithms over a slice of `T`, as static functions: the struct has no fields and is never made.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.sortBy\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(cmp)\npublic fn sortBy(s: SliceMut<T>, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32)\n```\n\nOrders `s` so that `cmp` of each element and the next is at most zero, for a consistent `cmp`. Not stable.\nAn introsort: quicksort with a median-of-three pivot, insertion sort for short runs, and heapsort past a\ndepth of 2·log₂ n, so O(n log n) calls of `cmp` in the worst case. Allocates nothing; the recursion is on\nthe shorter side, so the stack is O(log n). An inconsistent `cmp` leaves some permutation of `s`.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.forEach\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(visit)\npublic fn forEach(s: Slice<T>, visit: borrow mut fn(borrow T))\n```\n\nCalls `visit` on each element, in index order.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.any\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(test)\npublic fn any(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool\n```\n\nDoes `test` return true for some element? Stops at the first that does.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.all\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(test)\npublic fn all(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool\n```\n\nDoes `test` return true for every element? Stops at the first that does not.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.retain\n\n```ceesafe\n@effectsOf(keep)\npublic fn retain(s: SliceMut<T>, keep: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> usize\n```\n\nMoves the elements for which `keep` returns true to the front, in order, calling it once on each element in\nindex orde",
    52 => "r; returns how many there are. `v.retain(keep)` then truncates the vector to that length.\n",
    53 =>
        "# CeeSafe Compiler Architecture\n\nStatus: describes `ceesafec` as it is at the fork (CeeSafe's Decision 0006). Normative semantics: `ownership.md`,\n`language-spec.md`, `type-system.md`. `ceesafec` is written in CeeSafe (about 70,600 lines in `src/`); it was ported\nfrom Nova's C# compiler (novac 0.6.0) pass by pass, and at the fork it emits the same C as novac 0.6.0 with CeeSafe's\nnames (the repository's `docs/fork-point.md`).\n\n## 1. Pipeline\n\n```text\n source files (.cee; the embedded libraries first)\n     │  Lexer                 src/syntax/lexer.cee      → tokens, interned symbols\n     │  Parser                src/syntax/parser.cee     → AST (spans; recovery at declarations and statements)\n     │  Package names         src/check/packages.cee    → names across packages rewritten (ADR 0013)\n     ▼\n   AST ── Type checker        src/check/                → HIR: every expression typed, names resolved,\n     │                                                    generic code checked once (type-system.md §20)\n     ▼\n   HIR ── NIR builder         src/nir/nir_builder.cee   → NIR: one control-flow graph per function\n     ▼\n   NIR    src/nir/passes.cee (analyzeProgram), in order:\n     │   1. in-place check                  (Box.create built in place, type-system.md §22)\n     │   2. move check                      (O2–O5)       src/nir/move_check.cee, move_paths.cee\n     │   3. borrow check                    (B1–B5, E*)   src/nir/borrow.cee\n     │      (1–3 run on generic versions, not on instances; an instance that does not check is N0222)\n     │   4. drop elaboration                (O4)          src/nir/drops.cee      only if no error so far\n     │   5. effect check                    (§32)         src/nir/effect_check.cee\n     ▼\n   C backend  src/backend/c_backend.cee (generateC):\n     │   closure-call specialisation      (optimisations.md §5)    src/nir/closure_specialisation.cee\n     │   check elimination                (optimisations.md §2–§3) src/backend/check_elimination.cee\n     │   placement                        (memory-model.md §5)     src/backend/placement.cee\n     │   structuring                      (c-backend.md §3)        src/backend/c_structurer.cee\n     │   emission                         (c-backend.md)           src/backend/c_emitter.cee, c_body.cee, …\n     ▼\n   C files: main.c (or one file per function group with --split, ADR 0022), ceesafe/rt.h and the runtime's .c files\n     │  src/host/build.cee, toolchain.cee, compilers.cee\n     ▼\n   C compiler (clang, gcc, MSVC's cl, or a cross toolchain) → .o → link\n```\n\nSafety checking is complete before the C backend runs. No later pass may introduce a borrow, a move or a drop that the\ncheckers have not seen. The optimisations decide what C is written; they do not change what the checkers accepted\n(check elimination and placement record facts the emitter reads; closure-call specialisation adds functions whose\nbodies are rewritten copies of checked ones).\n\n## 2. Intermediate representations\n\n### 2.1 AST\n\nConcrete syntax with spans, kept in tables (`src/syntax/ast.cee`) indexed by node ids. Used for diagnostics, the\nformatter (`src/formatting/`), `doc` (`src/documentation/`) and the language server's index\n(`src/tooling/semantic_index.cee`). `ceesafec parse --dump-ast` prints it (`ast_printer.cee`).\n\n### 2.2 HIR — typed high-level IR\n\n- Every expression carries a resolved type (`src/types/types.cee`); every name its declaration.\n- Desugared: `for`, `?`, `using`/`defer`, method calls with their receiver mode, automatic borrows and reborrows,\n  `if`/`match` expressions (§15.3).\n- `ceesafec check --dump-hir` prints it (`src/hir/hir_printer.cee`).\n\n### 2.3 NIR — the ownership IR\n\nA place-based control-flow graph per function, modelled on Rust's MIR (`intermediate-representation.md`). Every move,\ncopy, borrow, storage start and end and drop is an explicit statement at a point in the graph, so ini",
    54 =>
        "tialisation and\nloan liveness are dataflow over blocks, and places make field-level disjointness (B1) and partial moves (O2) exact.\n`ceesafec build --dump-nir` prints it (`src/nir/nir_printer.cee`).\n\n## 3. Tables, not pointers\n\nThe compiler's data is held in vectors indexed by 32-bit ids (`src/support/`: the interner, maps, bit sets), not in\ngraphs of references. That keeps it within the borrow rules of `lifetimes.md` §4 and §8 (a non-constant index aliases\nthe whole vector), makes every walk ordered, and keeps the output a pure function of the input: no hash-order\niteration in an output path, no timestamps, no absolute host paths.\n\n## 4. Diagnostics\n\n- Codes `N0001…` (`src/diagnostics/catalog.cee`); every code has a row in `diagnostics.md` or another document of this\n  specification, which `ceesafec explain N0123` prints (`src/tooling/explain.cee`).\n- Borrow errors show the loan, the conflicting access and the later use.\n- Recovery: the parser recovers at statement and declaration boundaries; a type error suppresses cascades; the NIR\n  checkers run only on programs that type-check.\n- `--diagnostics-format=json` for editors and CI; `ceesafec fix` applies the fixes diagnostics carry\n  (`src/tooling/fixes.cee`, `tooling.md` §5).\n\n## 5. Tools\n\nThe same front end serves the other commands (`tooling.md`): `fmt`, `doc`, `explain`, `fix`, `run --interp` (the NIR\ninterpreter, `src/interpreter/`), `lsp` and `mcp` (`src/tooling/language_server.cee`, `mcp_server.cee`, which compile\nin memory through `src/tooling/compile.cee`), and the package commands (`src/packages/`: `ceesafe.toml`,\n`ceesafe.lock`, the registry).\n\n## 6. Repository structure\n\n```text\nCeeSafe/\n  ceesafe.toml                 # ceesafec is a CeeSafe package\n  src/                         # the compiler (the directories named above)\n    library/                   # GENERATED: the libraries, runtime and documents ceesafec embeds\n  spec/                        # this specification; spec/decisions/ holds the ADRs\n  guide/                       # the guide; every `ceesafe` example is checked (tools/guide_check.py)\n  templates/AGENTS.md          # what `ceesafec new` and `ceesafec agents` write\n  editors/                     # the VS Code extension; lldb and gdb scripts\n  integrations/cosmoos/        # ceesafec's C held to CosmoOS's build, tests and boots (C1–C9, K3)\n  tests/reference/             # CeeSafe's corpus, benchmarks, programs and libraries at the fork\n  tools/                       # generators, comparisons with the novac 0.6.0 baseline, the contract suite\n  bootstrap/                   # the stage-0 pin and the fixed-point records\n  docs/                        # the port's plans, evidence and decisions\n```\n\nThe compiler, the runtime C and the libraries are versioned together: the runtime ABI between them is internal, and\n`CEESAFE_ABI_VERSION` names it (`c-backend.md` §2). At the fork the libraries (`lib/core`, `lib/std`, `lib/kernel`) and\nthe runtime (`runtime/`) are generated into `src/library/` from Nova `v0.6.0` under CeeSafe's names\n(`tools/gen_library.py`, `tools/gen_runtime.py`); `tests/reference/lib/` holds the libraries as `.cee` files. A path\n`lib/…` or `runtime/…` in this specification names one of those sources.\n",
    55 =>
        "# C Backend Design (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. Applies from M1. CosmoOS-specific flags are covered in\n`cosmoos/abi.md` and `cosmoos/build-integration.md`.\n\n## 1. Target dialect\n\n- **C11** (ISO/IEC 9899:2011), compiled with `-std=c11` (or `gnu11` where the\n  host project requires it). Generated code must also compile under C17.\n- Headers used by generated code: **only** freestanding headers (`<stdint.h>`,\n  `<stddef.h>`, `<stdbool.h>`, `<stdalign.h>`, `<limits.h>`, `<stdnoreturn.h>`)\n  plus `ceesafe/rt.h`. Everything hosted lives behind runtime functions.\n- Compiler-specific features are allowed only through macros in `ceesafe/rt.h`\n  with a portable fallback: `__builtin_*_overflow`, `__builtin_expect`,\n  `__attribute__((noreturn, cold))`, `_Static_assert`.\n- Supported compilers: clang ≥ 15 (CosmoOS's floor; its CI uses Debian clang 19.1.7)\n  and gcc ≥ 11 for hosted builds. **CosmoOS profiles are clang/lld only**\n  (`research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md` §3). MSVC is best-effort for the hosted profile\n  (C11 mode, VS 2022), without the kernel profile.\n- The generated C must compile warning-free under\n  `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Werror`\n  **and** under CosmoOS's own warning set (`-Wcast-qual -Wmissing-prototypes\n  -Wstrict-prototypes -Wvla -Wundef -Wimplicit-fallthrough`, `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`\n  for the kernel; see `cosmoos/abi.md`). All non-exported functions are `static`, and every\n  exported one has a prototype in the generated header.\n- Correct both with and without `-fno-strict-aliasing`. CosmoOS compiles with it,\n  but CeeSafe does not rely on it.\n- **No floating-point contraction or fast-math** (M11 G7, type-system.md §35.6). `rt.h` turns contraction off where\n  a pragma can (clang, MSVC) and requires `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0`, in programs that use floats (`CEE_FLOATS`; a\n  program without floats compiles under `-mgeneral-regs-only`); builds pass `-ffp-contract=off` to clang and gcc\n  and never `-ffast-math` or its parts, so each float operation rounds once, as in the interpreter.\n- **No compiler-rt / libgcc.** CosmoOS links no builtins library, so generated code\n  must never need one. That rules out `__int128` division, `long double` arithmetic,\n  16-byte atomics and, in AArch64 userland, outline-atomic helpers (to be verified in C1;\n  pass `-mno-outline-atomics` if needed). The runtime header supplies portable\n  fallbacks instead of builtins when a builtin would lower to a libcall.\n- `-nostdinc` builds: freestanding headers come from `-isystem $(clang\n  -print-resource-dir)/include` (CosmoOS invariant B2). Generated code must not include libc headers\n  directly; only `ceesafe/rt*.h` does, and only in hosted and userland profiles.\n- **Runtime per profile (M8c, `profiles.md` §4.2).** Hosted programs get `ceesafe/rt.h`, the\n  core runtime `ceesafe_rt_core.c`, and the platform layer `ceesafe_rt_hosted.c` / `ceesafe_rt_io.c` (C library\n  calls). A hosted program that prints a float also gets `ceesafe_rt_float.c`, the float formatter and its table\n  (type-system.md §35.7; M11 G7b). Freestanding programs get `ceesafe/rt.h` and `ceesafe_rt_core.c` compiled with `CEE_FREESTANDING`\n  (a `#define` at its top), which leaves out everything that needs a platform service; the\n  environment defines `ceesafe_panic`. Freestanding builds add `-ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector\n  -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables` (the stack protector's `__stack_chk_*` symbols\n  come from the C library; unwind tables are useless with panic = abort and have no unwinder to read\n  them in a kernel).\n- Plain C `char` is **never** used for CeeSafe data (`char` is signed on x86-64 and\n  unsigned on AArch64; CosmoOS's `limits.h` is also wrong about it on AArch64). Bytes are `uint8_t`.\n\n## 1.1 MSVC, best effort (M7e)\n\n`ceesafec build --cc cl` uses it, and so does `ceesafec build` on Windows when no clang or gcc is found (`tooling.md` §8).\nThe hosted profile also builds with Microsoft's C c",
    56 =>
        "ompiler: `cl /std:c11 /W3 /WX`, checked on\nx64 (CI) and ARM64 (a Windows 11 VM). Programs are linked with an 8 MiB stack\n(`/link /STACK:8388608`), as Linux and macOS give the main thread. Windows' default is 1 MiB, which\n`run-pass/deep-recursion.cee` overflows. This is\n**best effort**: no CosmoOS profile uses MSVC, and the strict warning set (§1) is checked with clang and\nGCC only. What differs:\n\n- **`rt.h`** uses compiler builtins only behind a test for GCC or clang, with portable code otherwise:\n  - checked arithmetic falls back to the portable overflow checks, as with `CEE_NO_BUILTIN_OVERFLOW`;\n  - counting leading zeros (stable vectors) uses `cee_clz64`, a shift loop off GCC and clang;\n  - `CEE_UNUSED` and `CEE_COLD` are empty;\n  - `cee_max_align_t` stands for C11's `max_align_t`, which MSVC's `<stddef.h>` lacks in C mode.\n    There it is `double`. The generated alignment assertions use it.\n- **Process behaviour** (`ceesafe_rt_hosted.c`, under `_WIN32`):\n  - standard output and standard error are switched to binary mode at the first write, so\n    `\"\\n\"` stays one byte;\n  - a panic ends with exit status 134 (`_exit(134)` after flushing), the status `abort()` gives\n    on Linux and macOS. Windows' own `abort()` would give 3.\n- **I/O** (`ceesafe_rt_io.c`, under `_WIN32`):\n  - program arguments work as elsewhere;\n  - files use the C runtime's `_open`, `_read`, `_write`, `_close` and `_unlink` in binary mode;\n  - `Process.run` uses `_spawnvp(_P_WAIT, …)`;\n  - **TCP is not supported**: after the same address check as elsewhere, every socket call fails\n    with `ENOSYS` (`IoError.Other`).\n- **Test.** Nova's `MsvcTests` checks three things (CeeSafe's Windows checks are `tools/host_c_check.py` and the run corpus under `--cc cl`):\n  - every run-pass and run-fail program builds with MSVC and gives the corpus expectations;\n  - `nwc` reads files and standard input;\n  - a program runs `cmd /c exit 7` with `Process.run`, sees a missing program fail, and sees TCP\n    reported as unsupported.\n\n  No corpus program needs an exception: `io-errors.cee` passes only invalid addresses, which fail\n  the address check first. The `test-windows` CI job runs the tests on x64. Locally, a Windows 11\n  ARM64 VM (MSVC 19.51) runs them. On other hosts they return early.\n\n## 1.2 The runtime ABI version\n\nThe runtime and the C that `ceesafec` generates are versioned together, and nothing between them is stable across\nversions. `ceesafe/rt.h` defines the version right after its include guard:\n\n```c\n#define CEESAFE_ABI_VERSION 1\n```\n\nand every generated unit that includes it (`main.c`, and each unit of the split form, §2.4) checks it on the three\nlines after the `#include`:\n\n```c\n#if CEESAFE_ABI_VERSION != 1\n#error \"this C was generated for CeeSafe's runtime ABI version 1\"\n#endif\n```\n\nso C generated by one `ceesafec` and compiled against another's runtime fails to compile instead of linking wrong\ncode. A change to the runtime's interface (a helper's name, signature or meaning, a layout the generated code\nrelies on) raises the version. Version 1 is the fork's (Nova `v0.6.0`'s runtime under CeeSafe's names).\n\nNova's runtime and generated names (`nova_*`, `nv*`, `NV_*`, `NOVA_*`) and CeeSafe's (§2.1) are disjoint, so one\nprogram may link C generated by novac and by `ceesafec`, each with its own runtime.\n\n## 2. Lowering of types\n\n| CeeSafe | C |\n|---|---|\n| `i8..i64`, `u8..u64` | `int8_t..int64_t`, `uint8_t..uint64_t` |\n| `isize`/`usize` | the fixed-width type of the target's pointer width (`int64_t`/`uint64_t` on LP64), plus `_Static_assert(sizeof(void *) == 8)`. `ptrdiff_t`/`size_t` are used at FFI boundaries (M8) |\n| `bool` | `bool` (`_Bool`) |\n| `char` | `uint32_t` |\n| `borrow fn(T…) -> R` (M11 G8b) | `const ceeCK_<mangle> *`, a pointer to the first member, the code pointer `R (*)(const void *, T…)`, of an environment `ceeCE_<id>` that holds a pointer per capture; the body is `ceeCB_<id>`, which converts `env` back through `uintptr_t`; a call is `(*f)(",
    57 =>
        "f, args…)` (type-system.md §36.2) |\n| `borrow dyn I`, `borrow mut dyn I`, `Box<dyn I, A>` (M13 G2) | `ceeDR_<D…>` = `struct { void *data; const ceeDT_<D…> *vt; }` (16 bytes; the dyn type mangles as `D<length><interface><arguments>`, `dyn Shape` → `D5Shape0`), typedef'd before every struct, so any struct may hold one. Not a niche optional: `Box<dyn I>?` has a tag. The table type `ceeDT_…` is defined after the structs: `drop` (NULL if `T` needs none), `size`, `align`, then `m0…` in the interface's method order, each taking `void *` for the receiver. Per (`T`, `I`) pair that a coercion names: `static const ceeDT_… ceeVT_<T>_<D…>`, with adapters `ceeVT_…_<slot>` that convert the address back through `uintptr_t` and call `T`'s method, and `ceeVT_…_drop` over `T`'s drop glue. A coercion is `((ceeDR_…){ .data = (void *)(uintptr_t)p, .vt = &ceeVT_… })`; a call through `x` is `x.vt->m<slot>(x.data, args…)`; borrowing `*x` is `x` itself. `Box<dyn I, A>`'s drop glue calls `vt->drop` and frees `data` with `A` (a user allocator's `deallocate` gets the table's size and alignment) (type-system.md §37.4). **`dyn fn`** (M13 G3, §36.5) mangles as `DF` (`DG` for `dyn mut fn`), its effect list and its signature; its table's one entry `m0` is a move literal's body, whose `self` is the boxed environment `ceeCE_<id>` (the code pointer, then the captured values; drop glue `ceeD_CE<id>_`) |\n| `fn(T…) -> R` (M11 G8a) | a typedef `ceeN_<mangle>` of a pointer to the functions' C signature; a value is a function's address, a call an indirect call (type-system.md §36.1) |\n| `f32`/`f64` | `float`/`double` (`rt.h` checks the IEEE formats; literals by their bits through `cee_f32_from_bits`/`cee_f64_from_bits`; float→integer conversions through range-checked helpers; type-system.md §35). The CosmoOS kernel forbids floats (`profiles.md`; `--no-float` in G7c) |\n| `struct S` | `typedef struct ceeT_S ceeT_S;` then `struct ceeT_S { … }` with fields `f_<name>` in declaration order, structs em