        "ime files:\n  - `ceesafe/rt.h`: freestanding checked helpers `cee_add_i32`, `cee_div_u64`, `cee_shl_u8`,\n    `cee_shift_amount_s/u`, `cee_as_iN`, …;\n  - `ceesafe_rt_core.c`: output formatting and the panic message, with no libc;\n  - `ceesafe_rt_hosted.c`: the platform layer over stdio.\n\n  `ceesafec build --emit c` writes all of them next to `main.c`. Build with\n  `cc -I <dir> <dir>/*.c`.\n- Panic output: `ceesafe panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:<column>`. Stdout is flushed first, then\n  `abort()` (exit status 134).\n- `main() -> i32` becomes `int main(void) { return (int)ceeF_main(); }` (M7d renamed `cee_` to `ceeF_`, §2.1). `Process.exit(c)` calls\n  `exit(c)`, which flushes stdio.\n\n## 8. Validation\n\n- Golden-file tests for representative C output (mainly to review diffs).\n- Every `run-pass` test is compiled with clang and gcc (host) at `-O0` and `-O2`,\n  with and without `-fsanitize=address,undefined`. Outputs must agree\n  (differential testing).\n- M7a: every run-corpus program is also built with `#line` directives (clang `-O0 -g`) and must\n  produce the same results. Nova's `SourceMapTests` compiles programs with `-g` and checks the\n  debug line table: it names the `.cee` file and the lines of the statements.\n- M7: a Csmith-style random program generator for CeeSafe; three-way\n  differential testing (clang/gcc × opt levels), plus a reference\n  interpreter on NIR (`ceesafec run --interp`) as an oracle (`milestone-7.md`).\n",
    69 =>
        "# ADR 0001 — Compiler implementation language\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0001, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** 2026-10-22 (OD-1; see ADR 0002).\n- Date: 2026-10-22\n- Scope: the language of the *host compiler* (`novac`) only. Generated Nova programs never depend on it.\n\n## Context\n\nThe master prompt (§17) asks for a comparison of C++20, Rust and C#/.NET, with a stated preference for C# if it is technically suitable. The host environment has .NET SDK 10.0.401, clang and gcc available. The compiler:\n\n- parses, type-checks and borrow-checks Nova, lowers through two IRs and emits C text;\n- must be **deterministic** (bit-identical C output for identical input and flags), because CosmoOS builds should be reproducible;\n- must compute **target** layouts (x86-64 and AArch64, LP64) independently of the host;\n- must run on macOS and Linux dev hosts and in CosmoOS CI;\n- will not be self-hosted for a long time (master prompt §17).\n\n## Comparison\n\n| Criterion | C# / .NET 10 | Rust | C++20 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Implementation productivity for a compiler | High: pattern matching, records, discriminated-union-style hierarchies, `Span<T>`, LINQ for analyses | High once fluent; borrow checker friction for graph-shaped IRs (usually solved with arenas plus indices) | Medium: a lot of boilerplate (visitors, ownership of AST nodes) and slow iteration |\n| Memory usage | GC heap; manageable with struct-of-arrays token and IR storage and pooling. Peak memory probably 2–4× an equivalent Rust compiler (estimate, not measured) | Lowest | Low |\n| Compiler throughput | Good with RyuJIT and tiered PGO; NativeAOT available for startup. Roslyn shows that C# can host an industrial compiler | Best | Best |\n| Startup latency (matters for per-file `make` invocations) | JIT startup ~50–100 ms; **NativeAOT** removes most of it (estimate, to be measured in M1) | Native | Native |\n| Build times of the compiler itself | Fast (seconds) | Slow incremental builds as the project grows | Slow |\n| Correctness tooling | Nullable reference types, analyzers, xUnit, FsCheck/CsCheck property testing, SharpFuzz (libFuzzer/AFL bridge) | Excellent (cargo test, proptest, cargo-fuzz, miri) | Sanitizers, but the compiler itself can have memory bugs |\n| Cross-platform | macOS, Linux and Windows first-class; NativeAOT single binaries per RID | Excellent | Excellent, but build-system pain |\n| Bootstrap requirements | .NET SDK on the dev host (not in CosmoOS) | rustup | A C++ toolchain, which every host already has |\n| Maintainability by the owner | **Owner prefers and knows C#** (many .NET projects in `~/dev`) | Unknown | Unknown |\n| Risk of host leakage into target semantics | Real: `sizeof`, `Marshal`, `nint` and host endianness must **never** be used for target layout. Mitigated by a `TargetInfo` abstraction and a banned-API analyzer | Same risk, same mitigation | Same risk |\n| Self-hosting path | Rewrite in Nova eventually. C# is not on the path, but neither is Rust or C++ | Same | Same |\n\n## Decision\n\n**C# on .NET 10**, structured for performance from the start:\n\n1. Tokens as a `struct` array, plus a separate trivia array. Identifiers are interned into a `Symbol` table (`int` IDs).\n2. AST stored as immutable class hierarchies at first (clarity matters more in M1). The later IRs (HIR, NIR) are index-based: functions own arrays of blocks, statements and locals, referenced by `int` IDs, as in rustc's `IndexVec`. Ownership dataflow uses `BitArray`/`ulong[]` bitsets.\n3. All output goes through a deterministic emitter: no `Dictionary` enumeration order in output paths (use ordered collections), no hash-seeded naming, and a stable mangli",
    70 =>
        "ng scheme.\n4. All target layout queries go through `TargetInfo` (pointer size, alignment, endianness, C type widths). A Roslyn analyzer bans `System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal.SizeOf`, `Unsafe.SizeOf` and `sizeof` in `Nova.Compiler.*` outside tests.\n5. Ship as a NativeAOT single-file binary for CI and for CosmoOS's build. Development uses `dotnet run`.\n6. Testing: xUnit plus a file-based test runner (`tests/compile-pass`, `tests/compile-fail` with expected diagnostics, `tests/run-pass` with expected stdout). Property and fuzz tests come later (CsCheck, SharpFuzz).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- C# is technically suitable. Compilers with the same structure (Roslyn, F#, Bflat/NativeAOT tooling) exist and perform well. The main costs are GC pauses and memory use on very large inputs, which do not matter at Nova's scale for several years.\n- The CosmoOS build must have `novac` available on the host. **CosmoOS itself gains no .NET dependency**: only generated C files (and optionally a checked-in generated C snapshot) enter its build. See `docs/cosmoos/build-integration.md`.\n- Revisit if (a) per-file startup measured in M1 exceeds 150 ms under NativeAOT, or (b) peak memory compiling a 100 kLOC Nova workspace exceeds 2 GB.\n",
    71 =>
        "# ADR 0002 — Milestone 0 decision set\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0002, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-22: \"approve all the open decisions\")\n- Scope: every proposal listed in `docs/open-decisions.md` at the end of M0/C0.\n\nEach decision below adopts the **proposed** answer from `open-decisions.md`, with the\nrationale given in the referenced document. Later changes need a new ADR that\nsupersedes the relevant item.\n\n## Language and compiler\n\n| ID | Decision | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| OD-1 | Compiler in **C# / .NET 10**; NativeAOT for distribution | ADR 0001 |\n| OD-2 | **Hybrid memory architecture**: ownership + borrowing as the sole safety mechanism; regions/arenas as loans; escape analysis optimisation-only | `memory-model.md` §1–2 |\n| OD-3 | No references in user aggregates in v0 (E3); lifetime-parameterised structs designed for **M6** | `ownership.md` §4 |\n| OD-4 | `class` reserved; future sugar for always-boxed non-Copy types | `language-spec.md` Ex4 |\n| OD-5 | **Panic = abort**; no unwinding; destructors do not run on panic | `ownership.md` §0 |\n| OD-6 | **Integer overflow panics in all builds**; explicit `wrapping*`/`checked*`/`saturating*` | `ownership.md` §0 |\n| OD-7 | Structs are **implicitly Copy** iff all fields are Copy and no `drop`; `@noncopy` opts out | `ownership.md` §1 |\n| OD-8 | v0 arena contents must have no `drop` (R2); drop lists later | `memory-model.md` §3.3 |\n| OD-9 | No closures in v0 (non-capturing `fn` pointers only); scoped closures M6+ | `language-spec.md` §6 |\n| OD-10 | Static generics only in v0; `dyn Interface` future | `language-spec.md` Ex12 |\n| OD-11 | Package manifest **`nova.toml`** (name, version, profile, path-only dependencies in v0); exact schema specified in M1 | `milestone-1.md` |\n| OD-12 | No growable scratch arrays in v0 | `memory-model.md` §3.4 |\n| OD-13 | `Rc<T>`/`Weak<T>` post-M6, explicit, single-threaded | `memory-model.md` §6 |\n| OD-14 | `@flat` layouts: post-M9 research only | `memory-model.md` §7 |\n| OD-17 | Call sites: implicit shared borrow; `mut x` for exclusive; `borrow x`/`borrow mut x` elsewhere | `language-spec.md` §3 |\n| OD-18 | Reference types spelled `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` | `language-spec.md` §2 |\n\n## CosmoOS integration\n\n| ID | Decision | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| OD-15 | Nova userland runtime uses **public libc functions only** (no raw `cosmo_*` syscall wrappers) | `profiles.md` §2.2 |\n| OD-16 | Native personality is the default; musl/Linux personality profile later, opt-in | `profiles.md` §2.3 |\n| OD-19 | In-tree route S1 uses **checked-in, deterministic generated C** with a provenance header (no .NET in CosmoOS CI) | `cosmoos/build-integration.md` §3 |\n| OD-20 | Prove C1 out of tree (S0) first, then propose a Nova unit for CosmoOS | `cosmoos/feasibility.md` |\n| OD-21 | Missing libc pieces are proposed as CosmoOS libc units; Nova works around them meanwhile | `profiles.md` §2.2 |\n\n## Kernel questions (KQ)\n\nAccepted where the answer is a **Nova-side policy**:\n\n| ID | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| KQ-1 | IRQ-context Nova code is `@noalloc` (follow the `interrupt.h` policy) |\n| KQ-4 | `@nosleep` scopes may allocate via `kmalloc`; the permission lives in the kmalloc binding and is revisited if kmalloc gains `might_sleep` |\n| KQ-8 | Address typing stays Nova-side; no CosmoOS header changes requested |\n\n**Not decidable from Nova alone.** Each needs a CosmoOS code change or a CosmoOS policy\nruling made as an owner-chosen unit. Until then, Nova adopts the conservative default shown:\n\n| ID | Question | Nova default until resolved |\n|---|---|---|\n| KQ-2 | Export `preempt_enable` / ",
    72 =>
        "out-of-line quiesce read lock for modules | Modules may not use `Quiesce.read()` |\n| KQ-3 | `call_quiesce` barrier at module unload | Modules may not `deferDrop` into module text |\n| KQ-5 | Built-in Nova code calling non-exported internals | Only exported symbols, even for built-in code, until ruled |\n| KQ-6 | `kobject_put` from IRQ context for non-blocking releases | Forbidden |\n| KQ-7 | FP-register check for `.ko` files | Nova's C4 acceptance runs the disassembly check itself |\n",
    73 =>
        "# ADR 0003 — M3 heap types are built in, ahead of general generics\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0003, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-22, when starting M3: \"Built-in types\")\n- Scope: how M3 provides `Result`, `Box` and allocators before generics (M6), interfaces\n  (M6) and raw pointers / `unsafe` (M8) exist.\n\n## Context\n\nThe M3 acceptance examples (`language-spec.md` Ex4, Ex5, Ex11, Ex14) use `Result<T, E>`,\n`Box<T, A>`, `T?`, a generic function with an interface bound (`createUser<A: Allocator>`),\n`StringView` and integer methods such as `checkedMul`. The `Allocator` interface in\n`memory-model.md` §3.2 uses `unsafe fn` and `*mut u8`. Generics and interfaces are scheduled\nfor M6 and raw pointers for M8 (`roadmap.md`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. `Result<T, E>` and `Box<T, A>` are **compiler-known generic types**, like `Slice<T>` in M2.\n   Each instantiation is monomorphised in C. User-declared generic types and functions remain\n   M6.\n2. Allocators are **built-in, free-standing types**: `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator`. Their\n   values are zero-sized. `AllocError` is a built-in enum.\n3. The `Allocator` interface stays as specified in `memory-model.md` §3.2. User-defined\n   allocators, and generic functions over allocators (Ex5 as written), come with interfaces in\n   M6. M3 runs Ex5 with a concrete allocator type.\n4. Optionals (`T?`, `null`, `?` on optionals) stay in M6 with the other library types. Ex11 is\n   run in an adapted form without `StringView`, `checkedMul` and `okOr`.\n5. When M6 adds generics, `Result` and `Box` should become library types with the same surface\n   syntax and the same C layout, so programs written for M3 keep compiling.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M3 stays small: no generic type checking and no interface resolution.\n- The acceptance criteria are met with Ex4, Ex14 and adapted Ex5/Ex11 (`milestone-3.md`); the\n  deferred parts of Ex5 and Ex11 are tracked for M6.\n- The built-in surface (`Ok`, `Err`, `Box.create`, `Box.createIn`, `?`, `!`) is the one M6 must\n  preserve.\n",
    74 =>
        "# ADR 0004 — M4 regions: built-in region types, scoped results, LIFO scratch frames\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0004, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (2026-10-22, by the agent under the owner's instruction \"start M4\", following\n  the pattern the owner chose in ADR 0003; open to revision by the owner)\n- Scope: how M4 provides `Arena`, `FixedBufferAllocator`, `Scratch` and `Pool` before generics,\n  interfaces and `unsafe` exist (M6, M8).\n\n## Context\n\n`memory-model.md` §3.3–3.5 and `ownership.md` R1–R4 describe region allocators whose allocations\nare **loans of the region**. The examples (`language-spec.md` Ex15, Ex16) use generic methods\n(`arena.allocArray<u32>(256)`), named arguments and allocations returned inside `Result`. v0 has\nnone of: generic methods, named arguments, references inside `Result` (§17.1 forbids them).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Built-in region types**, as ADR 0003 did for `Box`. `Arena`, `FixedBufferAllocator`,\n   `ScratchFrame`, `Pool<T>` and `PoolBox<T>` are compiler-known, and their operations are\n   built-in methods. M6 may turn them into library types with the same surface.\n2. **Scoped results.** A `Result<T, E>` whose `T` or `E` is scoped (a reference, a slice, or\n   another scoped value) is itself scoped. It follows the reference rules (§13.1): only in `let`\n   locals, parameters and return types. The borrow checker treats every local of a scoped type as\n   holding loans, not only reference locals (`lifetimes.md` §3).\n3. **R1 as specified.** Allocation methods take `borrow this` and return values that carry a shared\n   loan of the region. An allocation may still be a `borrow mut T`: its memory is fresh and\n   disjoint from every other allocation, so exclusive access to it does not conflict with the\n   shared loan of the region.\n4. **No generic methods.** `allocArray(n, fill)` takes the fill value (like `[v; N]`), and `T`\n   comes from `fill`. `Pool<T>`'s `T` comes from the expected type, as for `Ok`/`Err`.\n5. **`Arena.create` cannot fail.** Chunks are allocated on demand, so it returns an `Arena`, not a\n   `Result`.\n6. **Scratch frames are LIFO by borrowing (corrects `memory-model.md` §3.4).**\n   - The claim that `using` alone keeps frames LIFO is wrong. With frames `a`, then `b`, an\n     allocation from `a` while `b` is live lands above `b`'s watermark, and `b`'s release then\n     frees memory that `a`'s allocation still uses.\n   - Fixed by: a nested frame is made with `parent.enter()`, which holds a **mutable loan** of the\n     parent. The parent cannot allocate while the child lives (B1).\n   - A root frame is made with `Scratch.enter()`, and only one root frame per thread may be live:\n     entering a second one panics (\"a scratch frame is already active\").\n   - Scratch memory is a runtime-owned stack of heap chunks, not the native stack.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- R1 needs no special case in the borrow checker beyond decision 2: loans flow from the region\n  into the allocation through the method's receiver, like any `borrow(this)` result.\n- Ex15 and Ex16 run in adapted forms (`milestone-4.md`).\n- `memory-model.md` §3.4 is amended to point here.\n",
    75 =>
        "# ADR 0005 — M6: user generics and interfaces; built-in collections; floats deferred\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0005, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-22, when starting M6: \"Built-in types\" for collections,\n  \"Adapt Ex13\" for floats)\n- Scope: what M6 implements in the language, and what stays built in.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **User generics and interfaces are real language features in M6.** This covers generic\n   functions, structs and enums, monomorphisation, interfaces, `impl` blocks and bounds\n   (`language-spec.md` Ex12, Ex13, OD-10: static dispatch only).\n2. **Generic bodies are checked once, generically** (`type-system.md` §20):\n   - a type parameter is an opaque type: a move type that may need drop, supporting only what its\n     bounds allow;\n   - move and borrow checking also run on the generic version;\n   - each instantiation is then monomorphised for C. Errors are reported at the definition, not\n     per instantiation.\n3. **Collections are built-in generic types**, like `Box` (ADR 0003) and the regions (ADR 0004):\n   `Vector<T, A>`, `String<A>`, `StringView`, `SlotMap<T>` with `Handle<T>`, and\n   `StableVector<T>`. Their memory is managed by the C runtime. The user-visible API is the one\n   `memory-model.md` §4 describes. A Nova core library implementation waits for `unsafe` (M8).\n4. **Library conversion is deferred to M8.** `Result`, `Box`, the regions and the collections\n   become Nova library types once raw pointers and `unsafe` exist. The surface syntax stays.\n5. **Floats stay out.** Ex13 runs with integer geometry. Floats (`f32`/`f64` arithmetic,\n   formatting, the kernel-profile ban) are an open item to schedule separately (OD-23 in `open-decisions.md`).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M6 adds the generic machinery (type parameters, instantiation, inference, interfaces) that M8's\n  library will need. The collections' semantics (invalidation through borrows, H2) are checked\n  by the same borrow checker.\n- Ex13 and Ex12 run in adapted forms. Ex12's `return if (…) a else b` is written as an `if`\n  statement, since `if` expressions do not exist in v0.\n",
    76 =>
        "# ADR 0006 — OD-3: region parameters on aggregates and functions\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0006, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-23: \"implement OD-3 first\", before C3). The two\n  remaining questions of `od3-design.md` §10 were not answered explicitly; this ADR takes the\n  design's defaults, which the owner may revisit.\n- Scope: references and other scoped values inside user structs and enums, generic instances and\n  built-in collections. Implemented as milestone **M6x** (`milestone-6x.md`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Implement OD-3 now**, as designed in `od3-design.md`, before C3.\n2. **Syntax (default of §10 question 2):** region parameters `<region r>` on structs, enums and\n   functions; region arguments first in a type's argument list (`Token<src>`, `StringView<r>`,\n   `Slice<r, T>`); `borrow(r) T` for references; `static` is the built-in region of static data.\n3. **Precision (default of §10 question 3):** one loan set per value, the union over its regions.\n4. **Regions are erased after checking.** Two types that differ only in region arguments are the\n   same type. Regions only determine, in signatures, which arguments' loans flow into the result\n   and into `borrow mut` arguments (`type-system.md` §25.6).\n5. **Type parameters act as regions** in signatures, and values whose type mentions a type\n   parameter carry loans in generic bodies, so a generic checked once stays sound when\n   instantiated with a scoped type.\n6. Destroying a value that both holds loans and needs drop is a use of its loans (drop check). It\n   reports the existing N0243 rather than a new code; N0271 stays unassigned.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- E3 and E4 are relaxed exactly as `type-system.md` §25 says. `Box`, statics, constants, arrays,\n  slices and pools still reject scoped values (N0246).\n- v0 programs keep their meaning: a v0 signature has no region names, and its flows equal its v0\n  return sources.\n- New diagnostics: N0270, N0272, N0273, N0274.\n",
    77 =>
        "# ADR 0007 — M8: hybrid core library, raw pointers, unsafe contexts\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0007, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24, when starting M8: \"Hybrid\" for the core\n  library). Decisions 2–5 are the agent's defaults under that instruction, following the pattern of\n  ADR 0004; the owner may revise them.\n- Scope: how M8 provides the core library, raw pointers and unsafe code (`milestone-8.md`). FFI,\n  profiles and MMIO details are specified with their increments (8b–8d).\n\n## Context\n\nMaster prompt Milestone 8 asks for a core library, freestanding compilation, a C FFI, raw-pointer\nunsafe operations, custom entry points and target capabilities. Every library type today (Option,\nResult, Box, Vector, Console, File, …) is built into the compiler and the C runtime (ADR 0003,\n0005); Nova has no library source files. The grammar reserves `unsafe` and `extern`, and\n`ownership.md` U1–U3 sketch the rules, but nothing is specified or implemented.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Core library: hybrid** (owner). The existing built-in types stay built in. The \"split\" is\n   a per-profile capability table (hosted-only names such as `Console`, `File` and `SystemHeap` are\n   rejected in freestanding code) plus the runtime split into a freestanding core and a hosted part.\n   New systems types that unsafe Nova can express (`Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, `PhysAddr`, memory\n   helpers) are written in Nova, in a `lib/core` bundled with the compiler (8c, 8d).\n   *Rejected:* everything built in (`lib/core` would stay empty, and safe wrappers over unsafe code\n   would be exercised only by tests); porting the M3–M6 built-ins to Nova (a large rewrite with\n   regression risk, not needed for C4).\n2. **Raw pointers are `*T` and `*mut T`**, Copy, with no loans (U3). Making and converting them is\n   safe; using them is unsafe. Conversions use `as`, so no new operator is needed; the operations\n   are methods (`p.read()`, `p.write(v)`, `p.add(n)`) and `Ptr.*` / `Slice.fromRaw` built-ins.\n   There is no `*p` dereference operator, since Nova has no prefix dereference at all (references\n   and boxes auto-dereference). *Rejected:* a `*p` place operator (new syntax, and `*` is already\n   multiplication); `null` as a pointer literal (`null` is the optional's empty value, §22.1); the null pointer is\n   `0 as *T`.\n3. **Pointer reads and writes need a Copy pointee** in v0. Moving a value out of or into raw memory\n   would need the drop elaborator to track raw places. It can come later, with the allocator\n   interface.\n4. **Unsafe contexts** are `unsafe { }` blocks, `unsafe { e }` expressions (Ex17: `let n =\n   unsafe { write(…) };`, since Nova has no block expressions) and `unsafe fn` bodies. The `Safety:`\n   doc comment (U2) is an **error** when missing, not a warning: Nova has no warnings, and the rule\n   is cheap to satisfy.\n5. **Generated C has no undefined pointer arithmetic**: raw pointers are plain `T *` (no `const`),\n   and arithmetic and casts go through `uintptr_t` (`unsafe.md` §4).\n\n6. **FFI (8b)**:\n   - Extern declarations name C types exactly, through the mapping of `type-system.md` §28.3\n     (`usize` is `size_t`, `*T` is `const T *`, and the pointee-only `CChar` is `char`), and calls go\n     through generated wrappers. Calling a C function through an incompatible prototype is undefined\n     in C11, and clang and GCC reject redeclared library functions with other types.\n   - `@export` alone makes a function C-callable. Ex17's `public extern \"C\" fn` spelling is not\n     used: the attribute already says everything, and an `extern` definition would read like a\n     declaration.\n ",
    78 =>
        "  - `@repr(C)` structs keep their Nova names in C, so C code using `nova_exports.h` has the same\n     struct type. Arrays and other non-FFI-safe types are not allowed in them yet.\n   - The interpreter models a few C library functions and faults on any other C call.\n   - Function pointers move to 8d: a C callback needs a C-typed wrapper, which exports provide.\n7. **Systems capabilities (8d)**:\n   - `lib/core` is Nova source embedded in `novac` and part of every package; unused parts are\n     not emitted, so programs that do not use it compile to the same C as before.\n   - `Volatile<T>` is a cell written through `borrow mut this`, not an interior-mutable cell: writing a\n     register needs exclusive access to the register block, which `MmioPtr<T>.getMut()` gives.\n   - Only C function pointers exist (`extern \"C\" fn(…)`); their values are extern and exported\n     functions, whose C addresses already exist. Calling one is unsafe.\n   - `Target.isX86_64()` and friends are decided by the C compiler, because one generated C file\n     serves every LP64 target; there is no conditional compilation in Nova yet.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `ownership.md` U3's `borrowFromRaw(p)` is spelled `Ptr.toBorrow(p)` / `Ptr.toBorrowMut(p)` (`borrow` is a\n  keyword, so `Ptr.borrow` would not parse).\n- The interpreter becomes the dynamic oracle for unsafe code too (null, dead, out-of-bounds,\n  misaligned, uninitialised accesses), but it does not track loans.\n- M5's rule 1c (address exposed to unsafe) is met conservatively: a function that turns references\n  into pointers keeps its boxes on the heap.\n- Pointee types excluded type parameters in 8a. 8d lifts this (`lib/core`'s `Volatile<T>` needs\n  `*T`); a scoped type argument for such a `T` is N0274, as for boxes.\n",
    79 =>
        "# ADR 0008 — C struct types are imported from the codebase's headers\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0008, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24: \"take the freedom of modifying [profiles.md §3.2] to\n  suit a better implementation. Nova in fact is multi-platform; it should work with the Linux codebase\n  also\"). The design below is the agent's under that instruction; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: how Nova code uses struct types that a C codebase defines: CosmoOS kernel and boot-protocol\n  structs, Linux kernel structs, C library structs. It replaces the rule of `profiles.md` §3.2\n  that kernel structs are \"never re-declared\" and are seen only as opaque types \"with size and alignment\n  queried at build time\".\n\n## Context\n\nThe old rule protected against a real hazard: a struct's layout in a kernel depends on the kernel's\nconfiguration (`CONFIG_*` adds and removes fields, e.g. lockdep's `class` in `spinlock_t`), on the\narchitecture, and on the ABI version (CosmoOS module ABI 11). A layout written down in Nova would be\nright for one build and silently wrong for another. But the rule also left Nova unable to *use* such a\nstruct: an opaque type can be passed along, not read. C6's survey found that three of its five\ncandidates (the framebuffer validator, the module ELF validator, GPT) are blocked for exactly this\nreason, and every driver or subsystem Nova will touch later takes kernel structs. Nova is also meant to\nwork with other C codebases than CosmoOS, the Linux kernel above all, where the same hazard holds at a\nmuch larger scale (layouts change with every `.config`).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Imported C types.** Inside an `extern \"C\"` block, `struct name { field: Type; … }` declares a view of\n   a C type that a header defines, and `@header(\"path.h\")` on the block names the headers that define\n   it. The C spelling is `struct name`, or the `@ctype(\"name_t\")` attribute's for a typedef. A\n   declaration without fields (`struct name;`) imports the type opaquely.\n2. **The layout is the header's, never Nova's.** Nova knows no size, alignment or offset of an imported\n   type. Generated C includes the headers and uses the real type, so every field access is the C\n   compiler's member access, compiled under the codebase's own flags. One Nova source works for every\n   configuration and architecture that has the fields it names.\n3. **Only the fields Nova uses are declared, with their C names, and each is checked.** Generated C\n   asserts at compile time that every declared field exists and has exactly the declared type\n   (`_Static_assert` on `_Generic` of the member and on its size). A configuration that removes or changes\n   a field fails to compile, naming the field; it never miscompiles.\n4. **Imported types live behind pointers and references.** Since Nova does not know their size, values of\n   them are not Nova values: no locals, parameters or fields of the type itself, no copies; `*T`,\n   `*mut T`, `borrow T` and `borrow mut T` only. Field places through them are read and written like any\n   field (embedded imported structs are places too). Ownership of such objects stays with the C API that\n   provides them (kernel objects, boot data), which is what the kernel-memory design wants anyway.\n5. **Nothing is platform-specific.** The same mechanism imports CosmoOS's `struct cosmoboot_info`, Linux's\n   `struct file`, or the C library's `struct stat`, each from that platform's headers under that\n   platform's flags. A profile only decides which headers and flags a build uses.\n6. **`@repr(C)` mirrors stay** for types Nova owns and exports (type-system §28.5), where Nova *define",
    80 =>
        "s*\n   the layout and C code includes `nova_exports.h`.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `profiles.md` §3.2, `cosmoos/abi.md` and `risks.md` P-3 change accordingly; the research audit\n  (`research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md`, implication 26) is left as written, since this satisfies it: types\n  still come from the headers of the exact ABI.\n- The interpreter cannot run code that touches an imported type's fields (it has no layout); such code is\n  an interpreter fault, as C calls are (type-system §28.6).\n- Field types must be types C and Nova both spell (integers, `bool`, raw pointers, imported types, and\n  `@repr(C)` structs); C arrays, bit-fields, unions and anonymous members need later additions.\n- It is specified in `type-system.md` §31 and implemented with the first component that needs it (C6b,\n  2026-10-24). The first implementation leaves out embedded imported structs (a field whose type is\n  an imported type itself): fields are integers, `bool` and raw pointers. Embedded members are a later\n  addition, with arrays and the rest above.\n",
    81 =>
        "# ADR 0009 — C7: context effects, and C state embedded by value\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0009, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-24, choosing \"embed by value\" for kernel locks and objects\n  over out-of-line C state). The rest is the agent's design under that choice, following\n  `cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §4–§7; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: C7 (`cosmoos/c7-report.md`). It amends ADR 0008 point 4 (imported types only behind pointers).\n\n## Context\n\nC7 wraps CosmoOS's locks, kernel objects and quiescence (audit in `cosmoos/c7-report.md`). Three facts from\nthe code decide the design:\n\n1. The kernel's synchronisation objects are C state that lives **inside** the data it protects. `spinlock_t` is 24\n   bytes (`kernel/include/kernel/spinlock.h:31-37`). `struct mutex` embeds a spinlock, a wait queue and list\n   links (`mutex.h:20-35`). A `struct kobject` is embedded in its owner and found again with `container_of`.\n2. Once initialised, some of this state **points into itself**. `mutex_init` runs `list_init`, which sets\n   `head->next = head` (`kernel/scheduler/mutex.c:21-22`, `list.h:24-28`). Moving such a value bitwise\n   corrupts it.\n3. Atomic versus sleepable is a runtime property: `might_sleep()` (`lockdep.h:239`) panics on sleeping with\n   preemption disabled. Nothing checks it statically, and nothing checks at all that a final `kobject_put`,\n   whose release may block, is not made under a spinlock (`object.c:74-81` has no `might_sleep`).\n\n## Decision\n\n### 1. Context effects (C7a)\n- Two effects: **`sleep`** (may block) and **`alloc`** (may allocate). A function's effects are those of\n  everything it calls and every value it drops, inferred over the whole package. Built-in operations\n  declare theirs (heap allocation is `alloc`; hosted I/O is `sleep`).\n- `@effects(sleep, alloc)` (any subset; `@effects()` for none) **declares** them: on an `extern` function it\n  is what Nova assumes, and on a Nova function it is an upper bound that the inferred set must fit. An extern\n  function without `@effects` is assumed to have both, the most conservative choice. A call through a C\n  function pointer has both.\n- `@context(atomic)` (no `sleep`) and `@context(irq)` (no `sleep`, no `alloc`; KQ-1's Nova default) on a\n  function forbid effects in its body. On a **struct**, `@context(atomic)` makes every point where a value\n  of it is live (maybe initialised and not yet dropped) atomic: holding a spin-lock guard is an atomic\n  context, with no annotation at the use.\n- Effects are checked in **every profile**. They create obligations only where a context or an `@effects`\n  bound is declared, so hosted programs without them are unaffected.\n- The checker proves only that Nova code respects the declared effects of what it calls. CosmoOS's runtime\n  checks (`might_sleep`, lockdep) stay the backstop. Nova generates no `might_sleep()` calls: the debug half of\n  the macro calls `lockdep_might_sleep`, which modules cannot link to (it is not exported), and every sleeping\n  primitive Nova calls has its own.\n\n### 2. C state embedded by value (C7b)\n- A Nova struct may hold an imported C type by value, and so may an imported struct (an embedded member).\n  Such a type, and every type that contains one by value, is **C-laid-out**. Like an imported type, it has no\n  Nova layout. Generated C defines it with the real C member types, so the C compiler lays it out, and it\n  gets no layout assertions. The interpreter faults on it.\n- C-laid-out values are **immovable**. They are made **in place** and never moved:\n  - the only way to make one is a struct literal given directly to",
    82 =>
        " `Box.createIn` (or nested in such a\n    literal);\n  - imported fields are left out of the literal and **zero-filled** (C's `{0}`), which is the only imported\n    value Nova can make;\n  - the contents of such a box are never moved out;\n  - a C-laid-out type cannot be a local, parameter, result, array element, enum payload, optional or\n    type argument (a reference to one can be).\n  `static` items come later (§4).\n- **`@placed` methods** (`fn m(borrow mut this)`) run C's initialisers at the final address. After\n  `Box.createIn` places the value, the compiler calls the `@placed` methods of its fields' types, innermost\n  first in field order, then the type's own. They run before anything else can borrow the value, so code\n  never sees uninitialised C state. A lock's `@placed` method calls `spinlock_init` with its name.\n- `Ptr.containerOf` (unsafe) recovers a struct from a pointer to one of its fields, using C's `offsetof`.\n  Kernel callbacks need it, since they receive the embedded `struct kobject *` or `struct quiesce_head *`.\n  (Implementation note, C7b: it moved to C7d, whose release and quiescence callbacks are generated by the\n  compiler and are its only users so far.)\n\n### 3. CosmoOS bindings (C7c–C7e, `integrations/cosmoos/c7/`)\n- `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` hold their C lock and their data. `lock()` returns a guard\n  that borrows the lock and gives `borrow mut T` access. Spin guards are `@context(atomic)`. `Mutex.lock` is\n  `sleep`. Dropping a guard unlocks. Data is reachable only through a guard.\n- `KRef<T>`, a counted reference to a Nova-defined kernel object (it embeds `kobject`). Its drop is\n  `kobject_put`, with the `sleep` effect unless the type is `@nonblockingRelease`. A final put under a spin\n  guard is then a compile error, the hole of fact 3.\n- `Quiesce.synchronize()` (`sleep`). `Quiesce.deferDrop(box)` uses `call_quiesce` with an embedded\n  `quiesce_head`. It is `unsafe` in modules, because module unload does not wait for pending callbacks\n  (`module.c:627-631`; KQ-3). Its contract is that the module stays loaded until the callback has run.\n- Not in C7: `QuiesceGuard` read sections in modules (`quiesce_read_unlock` inlines the unexported\n  `preempt_enable`, `quiesce.h`; KQ-2; spin guards are read sections already), `PerCpu<T>` (CosmoOS exports\n  no general per-CPU allocation), and statics (§4).\n\n### 4. Deferred\n`static` items (locks and caches with a lifetime of the module), and with them `ObjectCache<T>` (C5). Lock-order\nchecking stays lockdep's job.\n\n## Consequences\n- ADR 0008 point 4 now reads: imported types appear by value only in C-laid-out types, which are immovable and\n  made in place. Everywhere else they appear behind pointers and references.\n- `type-system.md` gains §32 (effects) and §33 (embedded C state). `profiles.md` §1 changes the effects row to\n  checked everywhere. `kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §5–§6 point to them.\n- The proposals of `kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` that C7 leaves out stay proposals: `PerCpu`, statics,\n  `Unlinked<T>`, `@scopedOnly`, and the irq-state ordering of nested `IrqSpinGuard`s beyond `using`'s LIFO.\n",
    83 =>
        "# ADR 0010 — M9c: writes through pointers made from references end with the loan\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0010, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, choosing to add the rule so that bounds checks on vectors can be\n  eliminated, over limiting M9c to arrays and slices). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: M9c (`milestone-9.md`, `optimisations.md` §2). It amends `unsafe.md` §2 (the U2 contract).\n\n## Context\n\nM9c removes bounds checks that an earlier check or a loop's condition already proves (`optimisations.md` §2). The\nproof is about values held in a function's locals: an index, a slice's length, a vector's length. It stays true\nuntil the local changes, and the analysis has to see every change.\n\nA local changes through an assignment, which the analysis sees, or through a reference to it. A slice or scalar\nlocal whose address is never taken cannot change any other way, and needs no new rule. A vector is different:\n`push`, `pop` and `clear` take `borrow mut` of it, so every vector has its address taken. The analysis can still\nfollow it, because a mutable loan is the only way to change the vector's length, and while that loan is live the\nborrow checker rejects every other use of the vector (B1–B2). So a length read directly from the vector stays\nvalid until the next `borrow mut` of it.\n\nThat argument fails if the vector can be changed with no live loan. Today's U2 contract allows that: a pointer made\nfrom a reference (`(borrow mut v) as *mut Vector<u8>`; casts are safe, `type-system.md` §27.2) can be kept, by this\nfunction or another, and used later through `Ptr.toBorrowMut(p)` in an `unsafe` block, after the loan it came from\nhas ended. The write is valid under §2 as written: the storage is live and nothing borrows it at that moment.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. Add a rule to U2 (`unsafe.md` §2): a pointer made from a reference may be used to **write** its target, directly\n   or through a reference made from it, only while the reference's loan could still be live. That is, only before the\n   target is next used by anything other than that pointer: read, written, borrowed, moved, or its storage ended.\n   Pointers that did not come from a reference are not affected: those from the C side, `Slice.fromRaw` of foreign\n   memory, MMIO addresses.\n2. This is the rule of Rust's raw pointers derived from `&mut` (inferred: Stacked Borrows makes such a later write\n   undefined), reduced to writes, which is all M9c relies on.\n3. The interpreter does not track loans (`unsafe.md` §5), so the rule is not checked, like the rest of U2's\n   \"as observed by safe code\".\n4. Unsafe code in this repository was audited for writes that the rule forbids (`optimisations.md` §2.6).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- M9c may treat a local's value, and a vector local's length, as fixed between two mutable loans of it. So it removes\n  `v[i]` checks in loops, and keeps facts about an index that is also passed as `mut i` to a helper (LZ4's\n  decompressor).\n- Unsafe code that kept a pointer to a local's storage and wrote through it after using the local directly becomes\n  invalid. The safe pattern is unchanged: use the pointer within the loan, or keep the data behind the pointer (a\n  raw buffer, a box made into a raw pointer) rather than in a local.\n",
    84 =>
        "# ADR 0011 — M11 G7: floating-point types\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0011, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's;\n  the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions are written into `type-system.md` (a new §35),\n  `grammar.md`, `c-backend.md` and `profiles.md` before any code (§6).\n- Scope: M11 G7 (`milestone-11.md`), closing OD-23 (`open-decisions.md`). It amends `type-system.md` §1 (N0216 for\n  floats), §5 (flexible literals) and §7 (conversions).\n\n## 1. Context\n\nFacts this design rests on, from the code and the docs as they are today:\n\n- **What already exists.** The lexer reads float literals with `f32`/`f64` suffixes (`Syntax/Lexer.cs:236-239`,\n  `grammar.md` §1: `1.` and `.5` are not floats). The checker rejects them: \"floating-point numbers are not supported\n  in M1\" (N0216, `TypeChecker.cs` `Synth`, `FloatLiteralExpr`), and `f64` as a type is unknown (N0204). `language-spec.md` §2 and Ex2/Ex13 already use\n  `f64` (IEEE 754, C `float`/`double`), and `c-backend.md`'s type table maps `f32`/`f64` to `float`/`double`.\n- **Profiles.** `profiles.md` §1: floats are *yes* in hosted and CosmoOS user programs, **forbidden** in the\n  CosmoOS kernel, and *target-defined* in the generic freestanding profile. CosmoOS builds its kernel and loader with\n  `-mgeneral-regs-only` on both arches (CosmoOS `build/arch/x86_64.mk:6,14`, `aarch64.mk:19,28`), and\n  `scripts/check-fpregs.sh` rejects objects that touch FP registers. Nova runs that script in `c4.py` and `c5.py`\n  (`c4.py:204-206`, `c5.py:104`); `c6.py` and `c7.py` reuse C4's other checks but not this one.\n- **No libm, no exact libc formatting.** CosmoOS's libc has no `<math.h>` (`libc/include/`), and its `printf`\n  float conversions are \"deliberately small … not exactly rounded in the last digit for every input\"\n  (`libc/src/printf.c:9-14`). Formatting therefore cannot go through libc if output is to be deterministic and equal\n  to the interpreter's.\n- **No compiler-rt** (`c-backend.md` §1). On the two 64-bit targets Nova supports, float arithmetic and\n  `int64 ↔ double` conversions compile to instructions. `fmod`, `sqrt` and the like are libm calls unless they become\n  builtins that lower to an instruction.\n- **The interpreter is an oracle** (M7c). Every program must print the same output compiled and interpreted, and the\n  fuzzer compares them (`tests/Nova.Fuzz`). .NET's `double` and `float` are IEEE binary64/binary32 with\n  round-to-nearest-even. The JIT does not fuse `a * b + c` into an FMA unless asked (`Math.FusedMultiplyAdd`)\n  (inferred, to verify in G7).\n- **C compilers may contract.** clang contracts `a * b + c` into an FMA within an expression by default\n  (`-ffp-contract=on`). gcc does in GNU modes (`-ffp-contract=fast`), but not under `-std=c11`. An FMA rounds once\n  instead of twice, so the C result could differ from the interpreter's and between compilers (inferred from\n  compiler documentation, to verify in G7).\n- **Undefined behaviour.** In C, converting a float to an integer type that cannot hold its truncated value is\n  undefined (C11 6.3.1.4). Generated C must have none (`c-backend.md`).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. IEEE floats, deterministic (proposed) | B. Floats as in C | C. No floats; fixed-point library |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Semantics | IEEE 754 binary32/64, round-to-nearest-even, no contraction, no traps; same result compiled and interpreted | whatever the C compiler does (contraction, `-ffast-math` allowed) | integers only |\n| Float → int | checked: panics on NaN and out of range | C's cast (UB out of ran",
    85 =>
        "ge) | — |\n| Printing | Nova's own shortest round-trip formatter | libc `printf(\"%g\")` | — |\n| Cost | a formatter in the runtime (~1–2k lines with tables, inferred) | small | a library |\n| Safety | no UB; deterministic | UB on conversion; results vary by compiler | — |\n\nB breaks two rules (no UB; the interpreter as oracle). C leaves Ex2/Ex13 and every numeric program out. **A is\nproposed.**\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Types.** `f32` and `f64` are IEEE 754 binary32 and binary64, laid out per `TargetInfo` (4/4 and 8/8 on both\n   targets). They are Copy, need no drop, hold no loans, and are FFI-safe (C `float`/`double`). The FP environment is\n   the default one, never changed by Nova code: round to nearest even, no traps, and no flags read.\n2. **Literals.** `1.5`, `1e9`, `2.5e-3`, with an optional `f32`/`f64` suffix. An unsuffixed float literal is\n   *flexible* like an integer one (§5): it takes an expected `f32` or `f64`, and defaults to `f64`. The literal's\n   value is the decimal rounded to nearest (even) in its type, exactly once: `0.1f32` is the `f32` nearest 0.1, not the\n   `f64` nearest 0.1 rounded again. An integer literal is not a float (`let x: f64 = 1;` is N0205; write `1.0`), as\n   Nova has no implicit conversions. A literal that rounds to infinity is an error (N0206).\n3. **Operators.** `+ - * /` and unary `-` on two operands of the same float type give that type. They **never\n   panic**: overflow gives ±infinity, `0.0 / 0.0` gives NaN, as IEEE says. This is the one exception to \"arithmetic\n   panics on failure\" (OD-6), and the spec states it. `%` is not defined on floats in G7 (N0207), since C's `fmod`\n   is a libm call. Comparisons `== != < <= > >=` are IEEE's: NaN is unequal to everything, itself included, and\n   `-0.0 == 0.0`. Bitwise and shift operators are N0207.\n4. **No contraction, no excess precision.** Each operation rounds to its type. The generated C enforces this:\n   - `rt.h` checks `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0` (`<float.h>` is a freestanding header) and emits\n     `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF`;\n   - novac's builds pass `-ffp-contract=off`, and `c-backend.md`'s checklist requires it of integrations, since\n     gcc ignores the pragma;\n   - `-ffast-math` and its parts are unsupported, as `-fwrapv` already is not relied on.\n\n   The interpreter computes each operation in .NET's `float` or `double`. Constant folding in the compiler uses the\n   same .NET arithmetic.\n5. **Conversions** (`type-system.md` §7).\n   - `as` stays lossless only: `f32 as f64`; `i8`, `i16`, `u8` and `u16 as f32`; and `i8`–`i32`, `u8`–`u32 as f64`.\n     Every value of the source type is exactly representable in the target type.\n   - `round<T>(e)` (new, free-standing like `truncate`): any integer or float to a float `T`, **rounded to nearest\n     even**. It is always defined: `round<f64>(x)` for `x: i64`, `round<f32>(d)` for `d: f64` (overflow gives\n     infinity).\n   - `truncate<T>(e)` extended: a float to an integer `T`, rounding **toward zero**. It **panics** if `e` is NaN or the\n     truncated value does not fit in `T` (\"float out of range\", a new panic kind). The C code compares `e` against the\n     exactly representable bounds before converting, so there is no UB.\n   - `checkedTruncate<T>(e)` returns `T?` instead of panicking. `saturatingTruncate<T>(e)` clamps, with NaN giving 0,\n     as Rust's `as` does.\n   - `f.bits()` and `f64.fromBits(u)` (`f32` likewise) reinterpret the representation, for hashing and serialising.\n     Literals are emitted to C by their bits, so their values never depend on the C compiler's decimal parsing.\n6. **Printing.** `Console.write`, `writeLine` and `String.appendNumber` accept `f32` and `f64`. The text is the\n   **shortest decimal that rounds back to the same value**, laid out as ECMAScript's `Number::toString` (ECMA-262\n   §6.1.6.1.20):\n   - plain digits for 1e-6 ≤ |x| < 1e21 (`0.1`, `1.5`, `100`, `123456.789`);\n   - an exponent otherwise (`1e21`, `1.5e-7`);\n   - `NaN`, `Infinity` and `-In",
    86 =>
        "finity`, and `0` for both zeros.\n\n   ECMAScript's rule is fully specified and widely implemented, so test vectors exist. The C runtime implements\n   it with its own shortest-digits algorithm (Ryu or Schubfach, §5 Q4), using 64×64→128-bit multiplication in\n   portable 32-bit halves (no `__int128`, which MSVC lacks). The interpreter gets the shortest digits from .NET's\n   `ToString(\"R\")` (shortest round-trip since .NET Core 3.0) and lays them out by the same rule.\n7. **Profiles.** Floats are allowed in hosted programs and CosmoOS user programs. In the freestanding profile, a\n   build option `--no-float` (CosmoOS kernel builds pass it, from `c4.py`–`c8.py`) makes any float type, literal or\n   operation an error, with a new code. `check-fpregs.sh` stays the backstop, and G7 adds it to `c6.py`–`c8.py`. Without the option, freestanding code\n   may use floats, as `profiles.md` §1's \"target-defined\" says. Printing floats is hosted-only (it uses the console).\n8. **Not in G7.** `%`, a math library (`sqrt`, `floor`, `sin`, …), parsing floats from text, `f16`/`f128`,\n   SIMD, rounding modes and FP exceptions, floats as `match` patterns, and floats in `const` expressions beyond\n   literals and the four operators. The first two come next (§5 Q5).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Ex2 and Ex13 of `language-spec.md` become runnable, and numeric code can be written. The README's \"What Nova does\n  not have yet\" loses \"floating-point types\".\n- A float result is the same compiled with clang, gcc or MSVC and interpreted, so the fuzzer can generate float code\n  and compare outputs. The cost is the forbidden contraction: FMA-heavy kernels are slower than C compiled with\n  contraction on. A future explicit `f.mulAdd(a, b)` (an FMA builtin) would restore it where wanted. Any performance\n  claim needs a benchmark first (a float workload in `benchmarks/suite`, e.g. n-body).\n- The C runtime grows by a formatter and its tables (inferred: about 10 KiB of tables for Ryu's `double` path; less\n  for Schubfach). It is linked only into programs that print floats.\n- `truncate` on floats can panic, unlike `truncate` on integers. The name says what happens to the fraction, and the\n  panic is the out-of-range case. Q3 offers a different name.\n- The CosmoOS kernel profile is unchanged in effect: floats were forbidden, and stay so through `--no-float`.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-25: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Approach A** (IEEE, deterministic, no contraction)? Recommended: yes.\n2. **Never-panicking float arithmetic** (infinity and NaN) as an explicit exception to OD-6? Recommended: yes. The\n   alternative, panicking on non-finite results, is expensive (a check after every operation) and unlike every\n   other language.\n3. **Conversion names.** `round<T>` for to-float, and `truncate<T>` (panicking), `checkedTruncate<T>` and\n   `saturatingTruncate<T>` for to-integer? Alternative: Rust's saturating `as` for float → int. Recommended: the\n   proposal, since an unexpected NaN or huge value then fails loudly.\n4. **Formatter.** A port of Ryu (Apache-2.0 or Boost licence; its licence would sit in `runtime/`), or a new\n   implementation of Schubfach from its paper (no licence question; more of our own code to test)? Recommended:\n   Schubfach, tested against .NET's shortest output on several million random and boundary values.\n5. **Math library scope after G7.** Recommended: next, `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor`, `ceil`, `min` and `max`, which lower\n   to single instructions on both targets (`__builtin_sqrt` with `-fno-math-errno`, `__builtin_floor`, …). Without\n   libm, everything else would be a Nova implementation with its own accuracy tests.\n6. **`--no-float`** as an option rather than a separate profile? Recommended: an option. Profiles stay as\n   `profiles.md` defines them, and a Linux kernel build would use the same option.\n\n## 5a. Amendments during implementation (2026-10-25)\n\n- **G7a.** `rt.h`'s IEEE checks, contraction pragmas and float helpers apply o",
    87 =>
        "nly in programs that use floats\n  (`NV_FLOATS`).\n  - Without that guard, the check `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0` broke every CosmoOS kernel object on x86-64.\n  - CosmoOS builds those objects with `-mgeneral-regs-only`, which leaves no SSE there (`c4.py`).\n- **G7b.**\n  - **Exponent sign.** §3.6's examples wrote `1e21`. ECMAScript writes `1e+21`, and Nova follows ECMAScript exactly,\n    so its test vectors apply (type-system.md §35.7).\n  - **The interpreter's digits** come from an exact search over rationals, not from .NET's `ToString(\"R\")`. .NET 10\n    prints 2^-25 as `2.980232238769531E-08`, which does not read back as 2^-25.\n  - **One-digit results.** Schubfach is used without the Java code's two-digit minimum, so `5e-324` prints as\n    `5e-324`.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`type-system.md` §35, `grammar.md`, `c-backend.md`, `profiles.md`), then:\n\n- **G7a.** Types, literals, operators, comparisons, `as`, `round`, `truncate` and its variants, `bits`/`fromBits`, C\n  emission and the interpreter.\n- **G7b.** The formatter and printing, in the C runtime and the interpreter.\n- **G7c.** `--no-float`, the fuzzer generating float expressions, an MSVC run, and a float benchmark.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass for every operator at ±0, ±infinity, NaN, subnormals and the boundaries of each conversion;\n- run-fail for `truncate` of NaN and out-of-range values;\n- check-fail for mixed types, `%`, bitwise operators and integer literals where a float is expected;\n- a formatter test against .NET on random bits and on ECMAScript's test cases;\n- C output compiled with clang, gcc and MSVC, and compared with the interpreter.\n",
    88 =>
        "# ADR 0012 — M11 G8: function values and scoped closures\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0012, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-25, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-9a and OD-9b record the\n  deferred options). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions are\n  written into `type-system.md` (a new §36), `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md`, `intermediate-representation.md` and\n  `c-backend.md` before any code.\n- Scope: M11 G8 (`milestone-11.md`), closing OD-9 (`open-decisions.md`: \"Non-capturing `fn` pointers only. Scoped\n  capturing closures in M6+\"). It amends `type-system.md` §8 (\"Functions are not values in M1\") and §29.5.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **No Nova function values today.** A callee must be a package function or a built-in (`type-system.md` §8). The\n  only function-typed values are C function pointers, `extern \"C\" fn(…) -> R` (§29.5). Calls through them are\n  unsafe and limited to FFI-safe types. `@callback` gives a Nova function a C address, with the same limits (§34.1).\n  Probing `fn` in an argument position gives `expected ')', found ':'` (N0100) (`milestone-11.md`, G8).\n- **No dynamic dispatch.** Interfaces are bounds, not types (§21; `dyn` is OD-10). Generics are monomorphised and\n  \"checked once, generically\" (§20).\n- **Loans already flow through values.** A scoped value carries the loans it was made from: references, views,\n  slices, and since M6x aggregates with region parameters (§25, ADR 0006). The NIR borrow checker propagates them\n  through moves, copies and calls, and a signature summarises a callee completely (OD-3 goal 3).\n- **Kernel constraints.** The kernel profile has no implicit allocation and a 2 KiB frame limit\n  (`profiles.md` §1, §3). CosmoOS kernel APIs take callbacks as a C function plus a `void *` argument, for\n  example the thread and timer bindings in `integrations/cosmoos/c8/`.\n- **Effects.** A call through a C function pointer is assumed to have both effects, `sleep` and `alloc` (§32.1).\n\nWhat programs need, from M9's suite and the README tour: comparators for sorting, `forEach`/`map`/`filter`-style\nhelpers over slices and vectors, retain-if on vectors, visitors over trees, and callbacks that capture a little local\nstate (a counter, a threshold). None of these needs the closure to outlive the function that made it.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Scoped closures with one type per signature (proposed) | B. Monomorphised closures with an `Fn` interface | C. Owning, heap-allocated closures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A closure value is | `{ code, env }`, with `env` pointing at the captures in the maker's frame | an anonymous struct, one type per literal | a box holding the captures, with a vtable |\n| Captures | by reference; the value carries their loans | by reference or by move | by move |\n| Can outlive its maker | no (scoped, like a reference) | by move, through generics only | yes |\n| Call | indirect | direct, inlinable | indirect |\n| Needs | function types, a literal syntax | anonymous types, an `Fn` bound with function-type syntax, inference of closure types in generics | `dyn` (OD-10), allocation (not in the kernel) |\n| Fits the kernel | yes (no allocation, a small env) | yes | no |\n\nA covers every use in §1 with the least new machinery: the closure is a scoped value, and scoped values are already\nchecked. B is faster at a call (inlining), but every function that takes a closure becomes generic, and a field\nholding one needs a type parameter. C needs `dyn` and the heap. **A is proposed now. B and C stay open (§5 Q5)**,\nand A does not block them.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Functi",
    89 =>
        "on types.** `fn(T1, …, Tn) -> R` is the type of a **plain function value**: one code address, Copy, no\n   loans, and not FFI-safe. The name of any package function (generic ones with type arguments) is a value of that\n   type where one is expected, and so is a closure literal that captures nothing. `f(args)` calls it, safely. It is\n   distinct from `extern \"C\" fn(…)` (§29.5), which stays the FFI type.\n2. **Closure types.** `borrow fn(T…) -> R` and `borrow mut fn(T…) -> R` are **closures**. The value is a code address\n   and a reference to an environment. As with references (§13), the shared form is Copy and is called through a shared\n   loan of the environment. The `mut` form is unique: each call reborrows it, and it may change what it captured. A\n   plain `fn(…) -> R` converts implicitly to either closure type. Closure types are scoped (§19.1): they live within\n   the loans of their captures, follow the reference rules for where they may appear (§13.1: parameters, `let`\n   locals, results through a signature), and may be fields of aggregates with region parameters (§25).\n   `borrow(r) fn(…) -> R` names the region.\n3. **Literals.** TypeScript-style arrow functions:\n\n   ```nova\n   (x: i32, y: i32) => x + y\n   (x) => x > limit\n   (item) => { total += item; }\n   ```\n\n   - The parameter types may be left out where a function or closure type is expected, and are taken from it.\n   - The body is an expression, or a block with `return`. It may not use `break` or `continue` out of the closure,\n     nor `?` and `return` for the enclosing function: `return` returns from the closure.\n   - The parser reads `(` … `)` `=>` as a closure when the parentheses hold only names, each with an optional `: T`.\n     A parenthesised expression cannot be followed by `=>`, so the two cannot be confused.\n4. **Captures.** A literal captures the enclosing locals (and parameters) that it names. Each is captured **by\n   reference**: shared if the body only reads it, mutable if the body assigns to it or borrows it mutably. Any\n   mutable capture makes the literal a `borrow mut fn`, so assigning it to a `borrow fn` is an error (a new code).\n   Copy captures that are only read may be copied into the environment instead, as an optimisation. The borrow checker\n   cannot tell the difference, since nothing may write them while the closure lives. A closure value carries the\n   loans of all its captures, so the existing rules (B1–B5) forbid, for example, changing `total` while a closure that\n   reads it is live. Moving a capture out (`take(x)` inside the body) is an error (a new code). Owning captures are\n   option B or C.\n5. **Environment.** The captures live in a hidden local of the block that holds the literal, not in a statement\n   temporary, so `let f = (x) => x + k;` works. The environment is a C struct of pointers (and copied values) in the\n   maker's frame, so there is no allocation, and its size counts against the kernel profile's frame limit.\n6. **C representation.**\n   - A plain function value is a function pointer, `R (*)(A…)`.\n   - A closure is `struct { R (*code)(void *env, A…); void *env; }`, with one `static` function per literal.\n   - A plain function converted to a closure gets a small generated adapter, which ignores `env`.\n   - Calls are indirect calls, which a C compiler may inline when it sees the target (inferred).\n   - No casts between function pointer types are needed, so there is no UB from calling through a mismatched type.\n7. **Effects** (§32). A call through a function or closure value has the effects of its type. A function or closure\n   type may carry an effect list, `@effects() fn(i32) -> bool`, which every value assigned to it must satisfy (the\n   checker knows a literal's effects, and a named function's). Without the list, a call is assumed to `sleep` and\n   `alloc`, as for C function pointers. Code in `@context(atomic)` therefore calls only closures whose type declares\n   no `sleep`.\n8. **Interpreter and NIR",
    90 =>
        ".** NIR gains a function-address operand (it exists for C pointers), a closure aggregate\n   `{ code, env }`, and an indirect call. Captures lower to references to the captured places, so moves, drops and\n   loans need no new analysis, only the closure value's loan set. The interpreter executes closure bodies as NIR\n   functions with an environment argument.\n9. **Library.** With closures in place, these may follow in a separate increment, each spec-first:\n   - `Slice.sortBy(cmp: borrow fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32)`, implemented in Nova over the slice;\n   - `forEach`, `any` and `all` on slices and vectors;\n   - `Vector.retain(keep: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool)`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Callbacks, comparators and small visitors are written directly, with checked captures, and without `unsafe` or\n  `@callback` plumbing. The C-callback pattern (`fn` plus `void *`) also gets a safe Nova form: a later binding may\n  pass a closure's `code` and `env` to C APIs that take exactly that pair (to be designed with the kernel bindings).\n- A closure cannot be returned to a caller unless its captures come from the caller (named regions), nor stored past\n  its captures, nor sent to another thread. Those need owning captures (B or C).\n- Calls through closures are indirect. Hot loops may still prefer a generic function with an interface bound, which\n  is monomorphised. No performance claim is made without a benchmark; a comparator sort against C's `qsort` would\n  be the first.\n- The parser gains one lookahead (arrow functions), and type syntax gains `fn(…) -> R`, also inside `borrow`.\n  `borrow fn(…)` contains no `(` right after `borrow`, so it does not touch G6's source-list rule.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-25: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Approach A**, scoped closures with one type per signature, now? Recommended: yes.\n2. **Literal syntax:** TypeScript/C# arrows `(x) => x + 1`, or a keyword form `fn(x) => x + 1`? Recommended: arrows,\n   matching the language's TypeScript/C# style. `=>` already marks expression bodies in Nova (`fn f() => e`) and in\n   match arms.\n3. **Capture mode inference:** shared or mutable per variable, inferred from the body, as Swift and Rust do? Or\n   explicit capture lists (`[mut total](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/decisions/x) => …`, C++-style)? Recommended: inferred. The borrow checker reports any\n   conflict at the use that causes it, and both kinds of closure type are visible in signatures.\n4. **Effects on function types** (§3.7) in G8, or calls through closures always assumed to `sleep` and `alloc` until\n   later? Recommended: in G8. Without it, kernel code in atomic contexts cannot call closures at all.\n5. **Owning closures later:** B (monomorphised, `F: Fn(i32) -> i32` bounds) when performance needs it, and C only\n   with `dyn` (OD-10)? Recommended: record both as open decisions (OD-9a, OD-9b) and decide when a use appears.\n\n## 5a. Amendments during implementation (2026-10-26)\n\n- **One pointer, not two** (§3.6). A closure's value points at its environment, whose first member is the code\n  pointer (C11 6.7.2.1p15). So `borrow fn(…)` is an ordinary reference type, whose pointee is the callable object, and\n  every reference rule applies unchanged (type-system.md §36.2). A reference to a plain function value is a different\n  type, so the two representations cannot be confused.\n- **Closures never leave their block** (§4 said a closure could be returned when its captures come from the caller).\n  The environment is a local of the block that makes the closure, so returning it is N0244 even then. Returning\n  closures needs owning captures: OD-9a or OD-9b.\n- **A moved capture** is N0251 (a move out of a reference), not a new code.\n- **Effect lists** (§3.7) come in their own step after closures (G8b′, type-system.md §36.3), with these choices:\n  - the list goes before `fn`, also inside `borrow` (`borrow @effects() fn(i32)`), since it describes the callable;\n  - infe",
    91 =>
        "rence does not narrow a value's type: a named function or literal gets the type without a list unless one\n    is expected;\n  - a value whose type's list is too wide is N0299 (the wrong kind of value), not a new code. A named function's own\n    effects, known only after inference, are N0295 where it is named.\n- **The library** (§3.9, G8c, type-system.md §36.4):\n  - every method's closure is a `borrow mut fn`, `sortBy`'s too (§3.9 proposed `borrow fn`): a shared closure converts\n    to it, and a comparator may count its calls;\n  - `sortBy` is an unstable introsort, which needs no allocation and runs on move-type elements through a new\n    `swap`;\n  - `retain` is lib/core code over the elements followed by a new `truncate`, since generic code cannot name a\n    `Vector<T, A>` over an unknown allocator;\n  - the methods have the effects of a closure without an effect list. Taking a closure argument's effects instead is\n    OD-9c.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`type-system.md` §36, `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md` for closure loans, `intermediate-representation.md`,\n`c-backend.md`), then:\n\n- **G8a.** Plain function values: types, named functions as values, calls, C emission and the interpreter.\n- **G8b.** Closure literals, captures and loans, environments, `borrow fn`/`borrow mut fn`, and effects on function\n  types.\n- **G8c.** `sortBy`, `forEach`/`any`/`all` and `retain`, a sort benchmark against `qsort`, and the fuzzer generating\n  closures.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass: captures read and written, a closure passed down two calls, a generic higher-order function, a closure\n  in a struct with a region parameter, effects, compiled and interpreted;\n- check-fail:\n  - a write to a captured variable while a closure that reads it is live;\n  - a `mut` closure where a shared one is expected;\n  - a closure escaping its captures (returned, stored in a box);\n  - a moved capture;\n  - `break` out of a closure;\n  - an atomic context calling a closure without `@effects()`;\n- M2's mutation suite extended with closure loans.\n",
    92 =>
        "# ADR 0013 — M10 T5–T6: modules and packages\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0013, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-26). The owner took every recommendation of §4: package-level names\n  (option A) with `namespace` retired, `src/` as the default sources, git `rev`, `tag` or `branch` pinned in the lock,\n  and dependencies' C names prefixed with their package's name. The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it.\n  Not yet implemented: the decisions go into `language-spec.md` §5, `type-system.md` §1 (scopes), `grammar.md`,\n  `c-backend.md` §2.1 and `tooling.md` (a new §4) before any code.\n- Scope: M10 T5 (modules) and T6 (packages and building), `milestone-10.md`. It settles OD-11 (`open-decisions.md`:\n  \"`nova.toml` with name, version, profile, dependencies (path-only in v0)\"). The owner has already chosen git\n  dependencies as well as path ones, with a lock file (2026-10-26).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **One compilation is one package.** All its files share one item scope; names are simple. `namespace X;` is parsed\n  and recorded but qualifies nothing, and two items of the same name are N0201 even in different namespaces\n  (`type-system.md` §1). `import` is a reserved keyword, but the grammar has no import declaration.\n- **Visibility today:** `public`, `internal` (the default) and `private` are parsed. Only `private` on struct fields\n  has an effect, and it means visible within the file (§12, C7c).\n- **`lib/core`** is part of every package: its items are in scope unqualified, with no import (§29.2).\n- **The compiler is whole-program.** Generics are monomorphised (§20), and the ownership analyses, placement and the\n  optimisations (`optimisations.md`) run over the whole NIR. The C backend writes one `main.c` per program, with\n  names such as `nvF_area` and `nvT_Point` (`c-backend.md` §2.1), unique only within one package.\n- **Programs that would use packages:** CosmoOS integration code that today shares bindings by copying files\n  (`integrations/cosmoos/c5/kernel.nova` into c7 and c8), the benchmark programs, `nwc`, and code outside this\n  repository (ADR 0008: Nova also serves Linux and other C codebases, not only CosmoOS).\n- **The kernel profile** needs nothing to run at build time that is not Nova source or C, and builds must be\n  reproducible.\n\n## 2. Decisions proposed\n\n### D1. A package is a directory with a manifest\n\n`nova.toml` at the package's root; its sources are every `.nova` file under the directory `src/` (or the\ndirectories the manifest's `sources` lists), in ordinal path order. The manifest is a small subset of TOML: tables,\nstrings, booleans, arrays of strings and inline tables. `novac` parses it itself, with no new dependency.\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"geometry\"            # an identifier: what dependents call the package\nversion = \"0.3.1\"            # recorded; not used to choose versions (D6)\nkind = \"library\"             # \"executable\" (has main), \"library\", or \"object\" (one .o, profiles.md §4.3)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C sources built with it (type-system.md §28.6)\n\n[dependencies]\nshapes = { path = \"../shapes\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\", rev = \"3f2a9c0d…\" }    # a full commit id\nutil = { git = \"https://example.org/util.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }          # resolved to a commit in nova.lock\n```\n\n### D2. Names: one scope per package, qualified across packages\n\nRecommended (option **A** in §3):\n- Within a package nothing changes: its files share one item scope, and names are simple.\n- A dependency's **publi",
    93 =>
        "c** items are reached through the dependency's name as the manifest writes it:\n  `geometry.Point`, `geometry.area(p)`, as types and in expressions.\n- `import geometry;` brings every public name of `geometry` into the file's scope; `import geometry.Point;` brings\n  one. An imported name never hides a name of the package itself or of `lib/core`. Two imports giving the same name\n  are an error where the name is used, not where it is imported.\n- `namespace` declarations are retired: N0106 for any `namespace` after a grace period in which it is accepted and\n  ignored. (Alternatively, kept as documentation with no effect.)\n- `lib/core` stays unqualified, in every package.\n\n### D3. Visibility between packages\n\n- Only `public` items, and a public struct's `public` fields and methods, are visible to dependents. `internal`, the\n  default, stays within the package; `private` stays within the file (fields: §12).\n- A public item's signature must not name a type its dependents cannot see (a public function returning an internal\n  struct): a new error. Implementations of an interface (`impl I for T`) are visible wherever both `I` and `T` are.\n\n### D4. Building stays whole-program\n\n`novac build` (no file arguments, in a package directory) checks and compiles the package with all its\ndependencies, as one program, as today. Generated C names of items from a package other than the one being built\ngain the package's name (`nvF_geometry_area`, `nvT_geometry_Point`), so two packages may declare the same name. No\nseparate compilation and no cache in this ADR: they would need generic code checked once per package and a stable\ninterface format, and would be decided after measuring build times (the M10 plan's \"cache of generated C per\npackage\" is deferred until then).\n\n### D5. Sources: path and git dependencies\n\n- `path` is relative to the manifest. `git` takes `rev` (a full commit id), or `tag` or `branch`, which `novac fetch`\n  resolves to a commit and records.\n- Git sources are fetched with the `git` executable into a cache (`$NOVA_HOME/git`, default `~/.nova/git`), one\n  checkout per commit.\n- **Nothing runs at fetch or build time** but `git` and the C compiler: a package has no build scripts.\n\n### D6. The lock file\n\n- `nova.lock`, written by `novac fetch` and committed with the package. Each dependency, transitively: its name,\n  source, commit for git, and a SHA-256 of its manifest and sources.\n- `novac build` uses the lock and fails if a package's sources no longer match their hash, or if `nova.toml` names a\n  dependency the lock lacks (run `novac fetch`). `--offline` never runs `git`.\n- **No version solving.** Two packages that depend on a package of the same name must agree on its source and commit;\n  otherwise it is an error that names both. `version` is recorded but not compared. *Amended by\n  [ADR 0017](0017-m13-package-registry.md) (2026-10-28): registry dependencies are version requirements solved\n  against an index; path and git dependencies keep this rule.*\n\n### D7. Tools follow the package\n\n`check`, `build`, `run`, `doc` and `fmt` without file arguments act on the package of the current directory. The\nlanguage server checks a document with its package when there is one (`tooling.md` §2.1). `novac new <name>`\nwrites a manifest and `src/main.nova`.\n\n## 3. Options for names (D2)\n\n| | A. Package-level names (proposed) | B. Hierarchical namespaces | C. File modules |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Inside a package | one scope, as today | `namespace X.Y;` qualifies every item | each file its own module |\n| Across packages | `geometry.Point`, `import geometry;` | `import geometry.shapes;` | `import geometry/shapes;` |\n| Existing code | unchanged | every file needs a namespace or moves | every multi-file package needs imports |\n| Compiler work | qualified names through dependencies, imports, visibility | the above, plus namespaces within a package | the above, plus per-file scopes |\n\nA keeps everything written so far valid, and qualifies only what cross",
    94 =>
        "es a package boundary, where names can collide.\nB and C give finer structure inside a large package; nothing in this repository needs it yet, and A does not prevent\nadding namespaces later.\n\n## 4. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Names: A (recommended), B or C (§3)? And under A, retire `namespace` (recommended) or keep it as a comment?\n2. Sources: `src/` by default (recommended), or every `.nova` file under the package directory?\n3. Git references: `rev`, `tag` and `branch`, each resolved to a commit in the lock (recommended), or `rev` only?\n4. C names: prefix items of dependencies with the package name (recommended, D4)?\n5. `novac new`: wanted now, or later?\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n- New: a manifest parser, a dependency resolver and fetcher (`git`), the lock file, qualified names and imports in\n  the type checker, visibility errors, package-prefixed C names, and package-aware commands and language server.\n- New diagnostics (codes assigned when specified): manifest errors, an unknown dependency, an unresolved or\n  ambiguous imported name, a non-public item used from another package, a public signature naming a non-public\n  type, a hash mismatch, a dependency conflict.\n- Tests: packages in `tests/packages/` (path dependencies, a diamond, a conflict, visibility, imports); git sources\n  from a local bare repository made by the test, so the suite needs no network.\n- Risks: R-12 (new) for dependencies: a fetched package is code the build trusts. Mitigated by commits pinned in the\n  lock, verified hashes, and no build scripts; not by review, which stays the user's.\n",
    95 =>
        "# ADR 0014 — C arrays in imported structs, C member names, and exported constants\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0014, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-26: \"start C9 with the GPT parser\", then \"You can go ahead with any\n  integration you need\"). The design below is the agent's under that instruction; the owner may revise it.\n- Scope: the additions to C interoperability that C9 needs (`docs/cosmoos/c9-report.md`): CosmoOS's GPT\n  parser (`kernel/block/gpt.c`) cannot be written in Nova with the API of `kernel/include/kernel/gpt.h`\n  without them. ADR 0008 named C arrays as a \"later addition\"; this is it.\n\n## Context\n\n`gpt.h` is an ordinary C header, and its shapes are common in kernel and library headers:\n\n- `struct gpt_table` holds `uint8_t disk_guid[16]` and `struct gpt_part parts[32]`; `struct gpt_part` and\n  `struct gpt_build_part` hold `uint8_t type[16], uuid[16]`. An imported struct's fields could only be\n  integers, `bool`, raw pointers, function pointers and embedded imported structs (type-system §31.1, §33.1).\n- One member is named `type`, a Nova keyword. A field had to be declared with its C name.\n- `gpt_build` takes `const struct gpt_build_part *parts, unsigned nparts`: an array of imported structs behind\n  a pointer. `Ptr.add` needs the pointee's size, which Nova does not know for an imported type (§31.2).\n- `gpt.h` declares data: `extern const uint8_t gpt_type_esp[16]` and `gpt_type_cosmo_root[16]`. Nova could\n  export functions only, and had no constant arrays at all (`const T: [u8; 2]` was N0216).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Array fields of imported structs.** A field of an imported struct may have type `[E; N]`, where `E` is an\n   integer, `bool`, or an imported type. Its C member must be `CE name[N]`: the generated C asserts\n   `_Generic(&member, CE (*)[N]: 1, default: 0)`, which is exact, and that the member has the size of Nova's\n   array. Inside Nova the field is an ordinary array place: index it (bounds-checked against `N`), slice it,\n   borrow it, and, when `E` is Copy, read and assign it whole.\n2. **Arrays of imported types are C-laid-out.** `[T; N]` with `T` imported (or C-laid-out) is itself C-laid-out\n   (§33.1): allowed where an imported type by value is (a field, an embedded member, a box's contents), never a\n   value. Indexing it gives a place of `T`, used through its fields or borrowed.\n3. **Lowering by the same representation.** Nova's `[E; N]` is `struct { E e[N]; }` in C (`c-backend.md` §2),\n   with the size and alignment of `E[N]`, and an access through the struct type is an access through an\n   aggregate that contains the member's type (C11 6.5p7). An array field place is `(*(nvA… *)&p->member)`: no\n   copy, and every operation on arrays works unchanged. The generated C asserts the sizes are equal.\n4. **`@cname(\"name\")` on a field of an imported struct** gives its C member name when that name is not a usable\n   Nova identifier (a keyword: `type`, `match`, `in`). Nova code uses the Nova name.\n5. **Pointer arithmetic on pointers to imported types.** `add`, `sub` and `offset` on `*T` / `*mut T` with `T`\n   imported are allowed (still unsafe, §27.3): C scales by its own `sizeof`. `read`, `write` and `fromRaw` stay\n   N0294, since they would copy a value or make a slice of one.\n6. **Constant arrays.** `const NAME: [E; N] = [ … ];` (or `[v; N]`), with `E` an integer or `bool` and every\n   element a constant expression (§10). A use is the array's value, as for any constant.\n7. **Exported constants.** `@export(\"symbol\")` (or `@export`) on a constant of integer, `bool` or such array type\n   defines C data: `const E symbol[N] = { … }",
    96 =>
        ";` (or `const T symbol = v;`), declared in `nova_exports.h` as\n   `extern const E symbol[N];`. It goes in read-only data, which C4's object checks allow.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- type-system §10 (constant arrays), §28.1 and §28.4 (exported constants, `@cname`), §31.1 to §31.3 (array\n  fields, `@cname`, pointer arithmetic) and §33.1 (arrays of imported types) change; `c-backend.md` §2.3\n  describes the lowering.\n- The interpreter has no layout for imported types: accessing an array field, or `add` on a pointer to an\n  imported type, is an interpreter fault, as other imported-field accesses are (§31.4). Constant arrays and\n  exported constants work in the interpreter.\n- A header's `char name[N]` (plain `char`) cannot be declared: `CChar` is pointee-only (§28.3). Bit-fields,\n  unions, anonymous members and flexible array members remain later additions.\n- An exported function whose C prototype in the header uses an `enum` type (`enum gpt_result gpt_parse(…)`)\n  is defined by Nova with the enum's underlying integer type. GCC and Clang make an enum without negative\n  values `unsigned int`, so the two declarations are compatible and the header can be included; a compiler\n  that chose another type would reject the build (it could not miscompile). This is recorded in the C9 report\n  as a property of those compilers, not a Nova rule.\n",
    97 =>
        "# ADR 0015 — M13 G2–G3: dynamic dispatch and owning closures\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0015, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's;\n  the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into `type-system.md` (§21, §36, and a new §37),\n  `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md`, `intermediate-representation.md` and `c-backend.md` before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G2 and G3 (`milestone-13.md`). It closes OD-10 (`dyn Interface`) and OD-9b (owning, heap-allocated\n  closures), which ADR 0012 deferred \"only together with `dyn`\". It amends `type-system.md` §21.1 (\"Interfaces are not\n  types\").\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Interfaces are bounds only.** `type-system.md` §21.1: \"there is no `dyn Shape` value in v0 (OD-10)\". Generics are\n  monomorphised (§21.2). A list of shapes of different types cannot be written. Probe at `b9fa451`:\n  `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>` is `expected '>', found identifier` (N0100).\n- **Interface methods are dispatchable.** Every method has a receiver: `this`, `borrow this` or `borrow mut this`.\n  Methods take no type parameters, and v0 has no `Self` type (§21.1). So every method that takes a reference receiver\n  could be called through a table of function pointers. A method that takes `this` by value could not, since the\n  caller does not know the value's size.\n- **Closures are scoped.** A capturing closure is a reference to an environment in its maker's block. So it cannot be\n  returned, stored past its block, or sent to another thread (§36.2; ADR 0012 §5a). Probe: returning\n  `(x: u64) => x + n` as `fn(u64) -> u64` is N0299 (\"this closure captures 'n', so it is a 'borrow fn'\").\n- **Allocation is explicit and fallible.** `Box.create` returns `Result<Box<T, A>, AllocError>` (§18). The kernel\n  profile allows allocation only through an explicit allocator (`KernelHeap`, C5). So an owning closure must not\n  allocate behind the programmer's back.\n- **No C-level UB from function pointers.** ADR 0012 §3.6 generates an adapter instead of casting function pointer\n  types. The same rule applies to tables of methods.\n- **Threads (G4) will need both features.** A thread's body is an owning closure, sent to another thread. That ADR\n  comes after this one. This one should not rule out marking a `dyn` type as safe to send (§3.8).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### Dynamic dispatch\n\n| | A. Fat pointers (proposed) | B. A vtable in the object | C. Enums only |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A `dyn` value is | `{ data, vtable }` behind a reference or box | a thin pointer to an object whose first field is its vtable | no `dyn`: users write a closed `enum` and `match` |\n| Works for existing types | yes (any `T` that implements `I`) | only types declared to carry a header | yes, but the set of types is closed |\n| Cost | two words per pointer; one indirect call | one word; a header in every object | a branch per call |\n| Precedent | Rust, Go's interface values | C++ | — (what Nova has today) |\n\nA works with today's types and values, needs no layout change, and keeps `borrow dyn I` free (no allocation). B\nchanges object layouts. C is the status quo. **A is proposed.**\n\n### Owning closures\n\n| | D. `move` literals boxed as `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R, A>` (proposed) | E. Monomorphised closures with `Fn` bounds (OD-9a) | F. Garbage-collected or reference-counted closures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Storage | one explicit, fallible `Box` allocation | none: the closure is a value of an anonymous type | implicit heap |\n| Can be returned or stored in a field | yes | only through generics | yes |\n| Call | indirect | direct, inlinable | indirect ",
    98 =>
        "|\n| Needs | A (`dyn`), a `move` literal | anonymous types, `Fn` interfaces, bound syntax | a runtime Nova does not have |\n\nD follows from A with little new: a `move` literal's environment is an ordinary value, and the box holds it behind a\n`dyn` function type. E stays open as OD-9a, the faster path for generic code. F conflicts with Nova's memory model.\n**D is proposed.**\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`dyn I` types.** For an interface instantiation `I` (`Shape`, `Comparable<i32>`), `dyn I` is a type whose values\n   are \"some type that implements `I`\". It has no size known to the compiler, so it appears **only behind a pointer**:\n   - `borrow dyn I` and `borrow mut dyn I`: references, scoped like every reference (§13);\n   - `Box<dyn I, A>`: an owning box, a move type that needs drop.\n\n   A `dyn I` anywhere else is a new error: a local, a field, a parameter, an array or `Vector` element, or a type\n   argument. `Vector<Box<dyn I>>` is allowed, since its elements are boxes.\n2. **Dyn-compatible interfaces.** `dyn I` is allowed when every method of `I` takes `borrow this` or\n   `borrow mut this`. A method that takes `this` by value makes `I` incompatible: `dyn I` is a new error, which names\n   that method. A generic interface is compatible once its type arguments are given.\n3. **Coercions** happen only where the expected type asks for them, never through inference:\n   - `borrow T` → `borrow dyn I` and `borrow mut T` → `borrow mut dyn I`, when `T` implements `I`;\n   - `Box<T, A>` → `Box<dyn I, A>` (a move of the box, with no new allocation);\n   - a `T` that does not implement `I` is N0268, as for an unsatisfied bound.\n\n   There is no way back: v1 has no downcasting and no `is`/`as` from `dyn I` to `T`.\n4. **Calls.** `x.area()` on a `borrow dyn Shape`, a `borrow mut dyn Shape` or a `Box<dyn Shape>` calls through the\n   table. The receiver rules are those of a reference to `T`: a `borrow mut this` method needs mutable access. A\n   call's effects are those declared on the interface method. Without a declaration, it may `sleep` and `alloc`, as\n   for C function pointers and closures without a list (§32.1, §36.3). An `impl` method's inferred effects must fit\n   what its interface declares (N0295).\n5. **What `T` may be.** In v1, `T` must not be scoped: no references, no closures and no region parameters, so the\n   `dyn` value carries no loans other than the pointer's own. That keeps `lifetimes.md` unchanged: `borrow dyn I`\n   carries the loan of the reference it came from, and `Box<dyn I>` carries none. A `dyn(r) I` holding scoped values\n   is future work.\n6. **Owning closures** (OD-9b):\n   - **Types.** `dyn fn(T…) -> R` and `dyn mut fn(T…) -> R` are the `dyn` forms of the closure types. Behind a\n     `Box`, they own their environment: `Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>` is an owning closure. The existing\n     `borrow fn(…) -> R` keeps its meaning and representation (§36.2). It is not rewritten as `borrow dyn fn`.\n   - **Literals.** `move (x) => x + n` is a **move literal**. It captures each named local **by move**, or by copy\n     for a Copy type. Its value may appear only as the argument of `Box.create` or `Box<…, A>.createIn` where the\n     expected result is a `Box<dyn fn…, A>` or `Box<dyn mut fn…, A>`. Anywhere else, it is a new error, until OD-9a\n     gives move literals their own types.\n   - **Mutation.** A body that changes a captured value makes the literal a `dyn mut fn`, called through a\n     `borrow mut` of the box. A `dyn fn` is called through a shared one.\n   - **Allocation.** `Box.create(move (x) => x + n)` returns `Result<Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>, AllocError>`.\n     Allocation stays explicit and fallible, and the kernel profile can use it with `KernelHeap`.\n   - **Captures.** v1 allows only non-scoped captures, by the same rule as 5. Capturing a reference is a new error.\n7. **C representation.**\n   - `borrow dyn I` is `struct { void *data; const nvT_<T,I> *vt; }`, two words.\n   - `Box<dyn I, A>` is the same pair",
    99 =>
        " with an owning `data`.\n   - The table is a `static const` struct per (`T`, `I`). It holds the drop glue, the size and alignment (for a user\n     allocator's `deallocate` layout), then one entry per method in declaration order.\n   - Each entry is a generated adapter `R nvTA_…(void *self, A…)`, which converts `self` to `T *` and calls `T`'s\n     method. So no function pointer is ever called through a different type.\n   - A call is `x.vt->m(x.data, args…)`. Dropping a `Box<dyn I, A>` calls `vt->drop(data)`, then frees `data` with `A`\n     and the table's size and alignment.\n   - A boxed move literal's environment is a struct of its captures. Its table holds the body as the call entry and\n     the environment's drop glue.\n8. **Room for threads (G4).** The thread ADR may add marker bounds to `dyn` types (`dyn fn() + Send`). Nothing here\n   depends on that, and nothing here prevents it.\n9. **NIR and the interpreter.** NIR gains a `dyn` pointer aggregate `{ data, vtable }`, a vtable constant per\n   (`T`, `I`), and a call through a table entry. Coercion is building the pair. The borrow checker treats\n   `borrow dyn I` as a reference, so it needs no new rule. The interpreter keeps a table of the implementing method\n   per (`T`, `I`) and calls it.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Open sets of types become possible: `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>`, plugin-style registries, callbacks stored in\n  structs, and functions that return closures (`fn adder(n: u64) -> Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>`).\n- A `dyn` call is indirect and not inlined. Hot code can still use generics. No performance claim without a\n  benchmark: a `dyn` against generic dispatch comparison joins `benchmarks/`.\n- The language gains one keyword in types (`dyn`), one literal prefix (`move`) and one generated table per\n  (`T`, `I`) actually coerced, so code size grows only with use.\n- Scoped `dyn` values (`dyn(r) I`), downcasting, and move literals outside a box remain open: OD-10a, OD-10b and\n  OD-9a.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Fat pointers (A) for `dyn I`, behind references and `Box` only?** Recommended: yes.\n2. **Owning closures as boxed `move` literals (D), `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R>`?** Or wait for monomorphised closures\n   (OD-9a)? Recommended: D now. It is what returning or storing a closure needs. OD-9a can follow for speed.\n3. **Syntax.** `dyn I` and `move (x) => …`, as in Rust? Or a Nova-specific spelling, such as `any Shape` (Swift)\n   or `interface Shape`? Recommended: `dyn` and `move`. They are short, widely known, and say what they mean.\n4. **v1 limits:** no scoped `T` behind `dyn`, and no scoped captures in move literals (§3.5, §3.6). Recommended: yes.\n   They keep the borrow checker unchanged, and `dyn(r) I` can follow once a use needs it.\n5. **No downcasting in v1.** Recommended: yes. Closed sets of types are better served by enums and `match`.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §21 amended, §37 `dyn`, §36.5 move literals; grammar; IR; c-backend), then:\n\n- **G2a.** `dyn I` types, compatibility, coercions, calls through `borrow dyn I`: checker, NIR, C, interpreter.\n- **G2b.** `Box<dyn I, A>`: coercion from `Box<T, A>`, drop through the table, user allocators.\n- **G3.** Move literals, `dyn fn`/`dyn mut fn`, boxing, calls; the fuzzer generating `dyn` calls and boxed closures.\n\nTests to add (none run yet):\n\n- run-pass, compiled and interpreted:\n  - a heterogeneous `Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>`;\n  - `borrow mut dyn` mutation;\n  - a generic interface;\n  - drop order through `Box<dyn I>`;\n  - a returned adder closure;\n  - a counter closure (`dyn mut fn`);\n  - a closure stored in a struct field;\n  - fault injection on `Box.create(move …)`.\n- check-fail:\n  - `dyn I` by value;\n  - an incompatible interface;\n  - a `T` that does not implement `I`;\n  - scoped `T`;\n  - a move literal outside a box;\n  - a scoped capture;\n  - a `dyn mut fn` called through a shared reference;\n  - a `dyn` call in an atomic cont",
    100 => "ext without `@effects()`.\n- The M2 mutation suite extended with `dyn` references.\n",
    101 =>
        "# ADR 0016 — M13 G4: userland threads and data-race freedom\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0016, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-24 and OD-25 record the\n  deferred options). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into\n  `type-system.md` (a new §38), `ownership.md` (the data-race invariants), `c-backend.md`, `profiles.md` and\n  the runtime before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G4 (`milestone-13.md`): threads in user programs (hosted and CosmoOS userland), with a compile-time\n  guarantee that safe code has no data races. The kernel keeps C7's model (ADR 0009: effects, lock guards, `KRef`).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **No threads.** Probe at `b9fa451`: `Thread.spawn(…)` is `cannot find 'Thread' in this scope` (N0200). Nova has\n  no mutable globals: the only top-level data are `const` items (grammar.md §2, `ConstDecl`). So today a program's\n  state is reachable only from its one thread, apart from C state reached through `unsafe` code.\n- **Owning closures exist** (G3, type-system §36.5). A `move` literal owns its captures, which is what a thread body\n  needs: it must not borrow its spawner's stack, which may be gone.\n- **The platforms have what is needed.** CosmoOS userland libc has `cosmo_thread_start`/`join`, `cosmo_mutex_*` and\n  `cosmo_cond_*` over futexes (`libc/include/cosmo/thread.h`). POSIX hosts have pthreads, and Windows has\n  `CreateThread` and SRW locks. C11 `<stdatomic.h>` gives atomics on every compiler Nova supports except MSVC\n  before 17.5. There, the `Interlocked*` intrinsics are used (inferred; to check on the Windows VM).\n- **The kernel already has a model.** C7's lock guards, `KRef<T>` and context effects are kernel-only (§32–§34). A\n  userland design should look the same where it can: a lock returns a guard that lends the data.\n- **What a guarantee needs.** A data race needs two threads reaching the same memory, at least one writing, without\n  synchronisation. Safe Nova already gives one writer or many readers per value, within a thread (B1–B5). Across\n  threads, what remains is controlling **what may cross** to another thread, and **what may be shared** between\n  threads.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Marker properties, owned transfer, `Arc`/`Mutex`/atomics (proposed) | B. Message passing only (channels; no shared memory) | C. Unchecked threads (`unsafe` spawn) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rust's `Send`/`Sync`, simplified | Erlang/Go-style | C's |\n| Shared state | through `Arc<Mutex<T>>`, atomics | none: values are moved through channels | anything |\n| Data-race freedom | checked | checked (by construction) | none |\n| Cost | two inferred properties per type; a few library types | copies through channels; no shared caches | none |\n| Covers | workers, shared counters, caches, pools | pipelines | everything, unsafely |\n\n**A is proposed**, with channels as a later library type over it. B alone cannot express a shared cache or counter\nwithout copying. C contradicts the master prompt's safety rules.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Two inferred properties**, never written by users in v1 (no `unsafe impl`), and usable as bounds like `Copy`\n   (`<T: Send>`):\n   - **`Send`**: a value may be moved to another thread. It holds for integers, floats, `bool`, and owned types made\n     only of `Send` types: structs, enums, arrays, `Box<T, A>`, `Vector`, `String`, `SlotMap`, `StableVector` and\n     resources. It also holds for `Arc<T>` when `T: Share`, `Mutex<T>` when `T: Send`, and the atomics.\n     - It fails for: references and every scoped type; raw pointers; imported C typ",
    102 =>
        "es; closures (`borrow fn`); a box\n       or vector with a user allocator; regions; and `Thread<T>`, whose join must happen on the thread that spawned\n       it.\n     - `Box<dyn I>` and `Box<dyn fn…>` are `Send` only when written `dyn I + Send` or `dyn fn(…) + Send`. Coercing to\n       one requires `T: Send`.\n   - **`Share`**: shared references to it may be used from several threads at once. It holds for every `Send` type\n     with no interior mutability (all of them in v1: Nova has no `Cell`), and for `Mutex<T>` and the atomics.\n2. **Spawning.** `Thread.spawn(move () => body)` starts a thread running the move literal's body. It returns\n   `Result<Thread<T>, SpawnError>`, where `T` is the body's result type.\n   - The literal's captures must be `Send` (new code). Since they are moved in, the spawner keeps nothing it could\n     race with.\n   - `T` must be `Send` (the same code).\n   - Spawning allocates the closure and the thread's stack. It has the `sleep` and `alloc` effects.\n   - `Thread.spawn` takes only a move literal, or a `Box<dyn fn() -> T + Send>`. This is the one place besides\n     `Box.create` where a move literal may appear (amending §36.5).\n3. **Joining.** `t.join()` consumes the handle, waits, and returns the body's `T`. Dropping an unjoined `Thread<T>`\n   joins it and destroys the result. There are no detached threads in v1, so a program ends only after every thread\n   it started. A panic in any thread aborts the process (OD-5).\n4. **Shared state** (library types, safe to use; their implementation is `unsafe` over the runtime):\n   - `Arc<T>` (`T: Share`): shared ownership with an atomic count. `Arc.create(v)` returns a `Result`. `a.clone()`\n     gives another owner. `borrow` of the contents is shared only. The last drop destroys `T`.\n   - `Mutex<T>` (`T: Send`): `m.lock()` returns a guard lending `borrow mut T`, scoped like C7's guards (type-system\n     §32.4), and the unlock is the guard's drop. `Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the usual shared, mutable state.\n   - `AtomicU32`, `AtomicU64`, `AtomicBool`, `AtomicUSize`: `load`, `store`, `swap`, `compareExchange`, `fetchAdd`,\n     `fetchSub`, all sequentially consistent in v1. Weaker orderings stay open (OD-24).\n   - `Condvar` and channels follow as a separate increment, each spec-first.\n5. **Why this is enough.** Every value is reachable from one thread (moved in) or through an `Arc` of a `Share`\n   type. A `Share` type without a `Mutex` or an atomic has no way to be written through a shared reference. So two\n   threads can write the same memory only through a `Mutex` or an atomic. `unsafe` code may break this, as it may\n   break B1–B5. `ownership.md` gains invariants T1–T3 with this argument, and the mutation suite gains thread cases.\n6. **Profiles.** Hosted (pthreads, or Win32 on Windows) and CosmoOS userland (`cosmo_thread_*`, `cosmo_mutex_*`). The\n   kernel and freestanding profiles reject `Thread`, `Arc` and `Mutex` as hosted-only names (N0293). The kernel keeps C7.\n7. **Effects** (§32). `spawn`, `join`, `lock` and a `Thread` drop have `sleep`. Atomic operations have none.\n8. **The interpreter** runs threads deterministically: one at a time, switching only when a thread blocks (`join`,\n   a contended `lock`) or ends, in spawn order. A deadlock, where every thread is blocked, is an interpreter fault.\n   This keeps `novac run --interp` reproducible. It means the interpreter cannot find races, but safe code has none.\n9. **C.**\n   - The runtime gains `nova_thread_start`, `nova_thread_join`, `nova_mutex_*` and atomics over `<stdatomic.h>`, in\n     `runtime/hosted` and as platform hooks for CosmoOS.\n   - A thread's body is the boxed closure, called through its table as for `dyn fn` (§37.4).\n   - `Arc<T>` is `struct { atomic count; T value; } *`. `Mutex<T>` embeds the platform mutex and `T`.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Programs can use several cores safely, with the same ownership rules plus two inferred properties. Nothing changes\n  for single-threaded code: a type's `Send`",
    103 =>
        " and `Share` matter only at `spawn`, `Arc` and `Mutex`.\n- **Not in v1:**\n  - scoped threads that borrow the spawner's locals (`Thread.scope`; OD-25);\n  - user `unsafe impl Send`;\n  - weaker atomic orderings (OD-24);\n  - thread-locals;\n  - detached threads.\n- Benchmarks: a parallel workload (for example `numeric`'s sieve over several threads) joins `benchmarks/` before any\n  performance claim, on the Ryzen box (6 cores).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **Marker properties with `Arc`/`Mutex`/atomics (A)**, rather than message passing only (B)? Recommended: A.\n2. **Names.** Should they be `Send` and `Share` (Rust's `Sync` renamed to say what it means), or `Send` and `Sync`?\n   Recommended: `Send` and `Share`.\n3. **Joining on drop**, with no detached threads in v1? Recommended: yes. Nothing outlives `main`, and no handle\n   leaks a thread.\n4. **Sequentially consistent atomics only in v1?** Recommended: yes. Weaker orderings would wait for a benchmark that\n   needs them (OD-24).\n5. **Scoped threads later** (OD-25)? Recommended: yes. They need a rule for borrows that outlive a closure's call,\n   which the other parts do not.\n6. **CosmoOS userland in v1, with a test in QEMU** (`integrations/cosmoos/`, as for C3)? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §38, ownership.md T1–T3, c-backend, profiles), then:\n\n- **G4a.** `Send` and `Share` inference and bounds, with check-fail tests over every kind of type.\n- **G4b.** `Thread.spawn`/`join`, the hosted runtime, the interpreter's scheduler; run-pass and fault-injection tests.\n- **G4c.** `AtomicU32`/`U64`/`Bool`/`USize`, `Arc<T>`, `Mutex<T>` with guards, run under ThreadSanitizer as an extra\n  C configuration.\n- **G4d.** CosmoOS userland: the platform hooks, and a QEMU test on both arches.\n- A parallel benchmark on the Ryzen box.\n",
    104 =>
        "# ADR 0017 — M13 G6: a package registry and version solving\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0017, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5; OD-26 records the deferred\n  option). The wording is the agent's; the owner may revise it. Not yet implemented: the decisions go into\n  `tooling.md` §4 and `diagnostics.md`, with an amendment to ADR 0013 D6, before any code.\n- Scope: M13 G6 (`milestone-13.md`): finding packages by name and version, and choosing compatible versions, on top\n  of ADR 0013's path and git dependencies, lock file and hashes.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4): a dependency is a path or a git repository pinned to a commit by `nova.lock`\n  with a SHA-256 of its sources. `version` in `nova.toml` \"is recorded but not compared\" (D6). Two packages that need\n  the same dependency must name the same source and commit, or it is N0326. There is no way to find a package by name,\n  and no way to say \"any 1.x\".\n- **What G6 must not lose:** reproducible builds (the lock and its hashes), `--offline`, nothing executed at build\n  time except `git` (D5), and the kernel profile's rule that a build needs only Nova source and C.\n- **Hosting.** A registry needs somewhere to live. Nova has a GitHub organisation and a documentation site on GitHub\n  Pages. It has no server, and the project should not have to run one.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A git index (proposed) | B. A registry server with an HTTP API | C. No registry: a curated list in the docs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What it is | a git repository (`nova-lang/index`) with one small TOML file per package, listing each version's git URL, commit and source hash | a service storing package archives, with publish and download endpoints | links |\n| Hosting | GitHub; anyone can mirror it with `git clone` | a server, storage, accounts, uptime | none |\n| Publishing | a pull request adding a version line, checked by CI (the commit exists, the hash matches, the manifest's name and version agree) | `novac publish` with a token | edit a page |\n| Integrity | the index records each version's hash, and the lock checks it as today | the server's archive hashes | none |\n| Offline and mirrors | the index is a git repository like any dependency | needs a mirror protocol | — |\n| Precedent | crates.io-index, Homebrew taps, Go's sum database (in part) | npm, PyPI | — |\n\n**A is proposed.** It adds names and versions without a service to run. The code still comes from each package's\nown git repository, pinned to a commit and a hash exactly as today. B can come later behind the same manifest\nsyntax, if the index outgrows git.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **The index** is a git repository: by default `https://github.com/vkuttyp/nova-index`, or `$NOVA_INDEX`.\n   - One file per package, `packages/<name>.toml`, with one entry per published version: `version`, `git` (a URL),\n     `commit` (a full id), `hash` (the SHA-256 of `tooling.md` §4.3), and `dependencies` (the version requirements of\n     that version's `nova.toml`, so solving needs no checkouts).\n   - Entries are only appended. A version can be marked `yanked = true`, which keeps old locks working and stops new\n     resolutions from choosing it.\n   - The index's CI checks each pull request: the commit exists, its manifest's name and version match the entry,\n     the hash matches, and the version is new.\n2. **Registry dependencies** in `nova.toml`:\n   ```toml\n   [dependencies]\n   geometry = \"1.4\"           # a version requirement: >=1.4.0, <2.0.0\n   lz4 = \"=0.3.1\"             # exactly\n   json = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://exampl",
    105 =>
        "e.org/my-index.git\" }   # another index\n   ```\n   - Requirements follow Semantic Versioning's caret rule: `\"1.4\"` means `>=1.4.0, <2.0.0`, and `\"0.3\"` means\n     `>=0.3.0, <0.4.0`.\n   - Also `=`, `>=`, `<`, and comma-separated combinations.\n   - Path and git dependencies keep their meaning, and override the index for their name.\n3. **Version solving** (`novac fetch`): the newest version of each package that satisfies every requirement on it,\n   found by a PubGrub-style solver.\n   - A **failure is explained**, as a chain of requirements (`app needs geometry ^1.4; geometry 1.4 needs shapes ^2;\n     plot needs shapes ^1`), as a new code.\n   - **One version per package name** in a build, as today. Allowing several would need distinct C names per version\n     (`c-backend.md` §2.1) and is left open (OD-26).\n   - The **result goes into `nova.lock`**: the source is `registry+<index URL>`, with the version, commit and hash.\n     From then on a build is exactly as reproducible as with git dependencies today. `novac fetch --update [name]`\n     re-solves; otherwise a lock that still satisfies the manifest is kept.\n4. **Publishing** is a pull request to the index.\n   - `novac publish` (no network) checks the package: it builds, the manifest's version is new, and the working tree\n     is a clean, pushed commit.\n   - It then prints the entry to add.\n   - No tokens and no accounts. The index's maintainers review the first version of each new package name, and later\n     versions from the same repository merge automatically after CI.\n5. **Unchanged:**\n   - D5: nothing runs at fetch or build time but `git`.\n   - `--offline`.\n   - The hashes.\n   - N0326, for a name reached through a path or git dependency and the registry at once.\n   - The kernel profile, which needs nothing new.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Packages can be found by name and depended on by a range, and builds stay pinned and hashed.\n- The index is a public repository that someone must review: the first version of each package name, by hand.\n- Solving adds about one module to `Packages/` and new tests:\n  - unit tests of the solver over synthetic indexes, including the failure explanations;\n  - an end-to-end test against a local index repository made in the test, so it needs no network.\n- Not decided here: several versions of one package in a build (OD-26), private-index authentication (plain git\n  credentials work already), and a search command.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner (decided 2026-10-28: each as recommended)\n\n1. **A git index (A)** rather than a registry server? Recommended: A.\n2. **Where it lives:** a new public repository, `vkuttyp/nova-index`? Or an organisation, which would need creating?\n   Recommended: `vkuttyp/nova-index` now, moved later if an organisation is made. Creating it needs your account.\n3. **Caret requirements** (`\"1.4\"` means `^1.4`)? Recommended: yes, as Cargo does.\n4. **One version per package name in a build** (OD-26 for more)? Recommended: yes.\n5. **Review:** should the first version of each package name be reviewed by hand, and later versions merge after CI?\n   Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`tooling.md` §4.6 the index, §4.7 solving, §4.8 publishing; diagnostics), then:\n\n- **G6a.** Version requirements and the index format, with a local test index; the solver and its failure\n  explanations.\n- **G6b.** `novac fetch` with registry dependencies, and the lock entries.\n- **G6c.** `novac publish`'s checks.\n- **G6d.** The index repository and its CI, once the owner has created it.\n",
    106 =>
        "# ADR 0018 — OD-25: scoped threads by fork-join\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0018, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's. The\n  decisions are in `type-system.md` §38.6 and `ownership.md` T4. §38.6 makes one point precise: the arguments must be\n  closure literals or `fn()` values, since the capture rule is about captures the checker can see.\n- Scope: OD-25 (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0016 §5 question 5: threads that borrow their spawner's data.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **What v1 threads cannot do.** `Thread.spawn` takes only a move literal, or a `Box<dyn fn() -> T + Send>`, and\n  every capture must be `Send` (type-system §38.2). References, slices and views are never `Send` (§38.1). So a\n  program cannot hand two threads the two halves of a vector it owns. It must move the data into an `Arc` (an\n  allocation, and the vector is no longer its own), copy it, or, as `benchmarks/parallel` does, give each thread\n  only integers to compute from.\n- **Why references are not `Send` today.** A spawned thread can outlive the block that made it: the `Thread<T>` is a\n  value that can be stored, returned or dropped later. A reference it carried could then outlive its referent.\n- **What the borrow checker already does.** A closure is a reference to an environment in its maker's block, and it\n  carries its captures' loans (§36.2). Two closures passed to one call are both live for the whole call, so their\n  captures must not conflict. Probes, run 2026-10-28 with novac 0.2.0-dev:\n  - two closure arguments that capture the same local mutably are refused (**N0241**, \"cannot borrow 'x' as mutable\n    while 'x' is mutably borrowed\");\n  - one that captures it shared and one mutably are refused too (**N0241**).\n- **Where the gap shows.** Divide-and-conquer over data the caller owns: sort the halves of a slice in parallel,\n  sum the chunks of a vector, process the rows of an image. `splitAtMut` (M13 G5) already gives two disjoint\n  `SliceMut` halves; only the thread is missing.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Fork-join: `Thread.both(a, b)` (proposed) | B. Scopes with handles: `Thread.scope((s) => { s.spawn(…) … })` | C. Library-only: `parallelChunks` over slices |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rayon's `join` | Rust's `std::thread::scope` | a fixed data-parallel helper |\n| What a thread borrows | anything its closure captures, for the call | anything, for the scope | one chunk of one slice |\n| New borrow rule | **none**: two scoped closures used only during one call | closures that leave their maker's block with borrowed captures (heap environments holding references; a move literal with scoped captures, which ADR 0015 §3.6 forbids) | none |\n| New `Send` rule | references are `Send` inside the call, under conditions (§3.2) | the same | the same, for one element type |\n| Number of threads | two per call; more by recursion | any, in a loop | any |\n| Size | small | large | small, narrow |\n\n**A is proposed.** It adds parallelism without adding a borrow rule: everything it needs about lifetimes is the\nexisting rule for closure arguments. B is the more familiar API, but it needs closures that outlive their maker's\nblock while holding references, which is a new kind of value (OD-10a's territory). C covers one pattern and can be\nwritten over A in the library later.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **`Thread.both(a, b)`**, in `lib/std` (hosted and CosmoOS userland, as `Thread`): `a` and `b` are closures with\n   no parameters and a `void` result, `borrow fn()` or `borrow mut fn()` (§36.2). It runs `a` on a new thread and\n   `b` on ",
    107 =>
        "the calling thread, waits for both, and returns. Results travel through the closures' captures:\n\n   ```nova\n   fn sum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 { ... }\n\n   fn parallelSum(values: Slice<u64>) -> u64 {\n       let halves = values.splitAt(values.length / 2);\n       var low: u64 = 0;\n       var high: u64 = 0;\n       Thread.both(() => { low = sum(halves.left); }, () => { high = sum(halves.right); });\n       return low + high;\n   }\n   ```\n\n   Nova has no tuples, so a pair-returning form would need a library struct; it is left out of v1 (question 3).\n   The example checks and runs today (2026-10-28) with a plain two-closure function in place of `Thread.both`: the\n   two closures' captures (`halves` shared by both, `low` and `high` each changed by one) are already accepted.\n2. **What may be captured.** Inside the call, `a` runs on another thread while `b` runs here, so each capture of\n   `a` and of `b` must be safe to use from another thread at the same time:\n   - a capture used shared needs its type to be **`Share`**, where, for this rule only, `borrow T`, `Slice<T>` and\n     `StringView` are `Share` when `T` is;\n   - a capture used mutably needs its type to be **`Send`**, where, for this rule only, `borrow mut T` and\n     `SliceMut<T>` are `Send` when `T` is;\n   - a captured closure is refused in v1 (question 4);\n   - otherwise the error is **N0334**, as for `Thread.spawn`, naming the capture and the type.\n   The conflicts between `a`'s and `b`'s captures are the borrow checker's, unchanged (§1): two closures cannot both\n   change a value, or one read what the other changes.\n3. **Why this is sound.** Both closures and every loan they carry are live for the whole call (§36.2), and the call\n   returns only after the thread running `a` has ended. So nothing `a` reaches can be ended, moved or changed by\n   the caller while `a` runs, and the two closures cannot reach one value in conflicting ways. With the capture\n   rule, a value reachable from both threads is either read by both (and `Share`) or reached by one only (and\n   `Send`). This is T1–T3 of `ownership.md` with \"moved in\" widened to \"borrowed for a `Thread.both` call\".\n4. **If a thread cannot be started** (the runtime's record cannot be allocated, or the platform refuses), `both`\n   runs `a` and then `b` on the calling thread. The result is the same, since each closure's effects are its own, so\n   `both` cannot fail and returns `void`. A program cannot tell from its output whether `a` ran in parallel.\n5. **Panics.** A panic in either closure aborts the process (OD-5), as in any thread.\n6. **Effects** (§32): `sleep` and `alloc`, those of `spawn` and `join` together.\n7. **The interpreter** runs `a` as a thread under its baton (`Interpreter/Threads.cs`), then `b`, then waits, so\n   `novac run --interp` stays deterministic.\n8. **C.** A scoped closure is a pointer to an environment whose first member is its code (c-backend §2). `both`\n   starts a thread on a runtime helper that calls `a`'s code with `a`'s environment (`nova_thread_start`), calls `b`\n   directly, then `nova_thread_join`s. No allocation beyond the runtime's thread record.\n9. **More than two.** Recursion: split, `both`, split again. A data-parallel helper (option C) may follow in\n   `lib/std` over `both`, spec first.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Programs can process data they own on several cores without `Arc`, copies or `unsafe`.\n- The `Send` and `Share` rules gain a scoped extension used only by `Thread.both`. Outside it, references stay\n  neither, and `Thread.spawn` is unchanged.\n- One OS thread per call: `both` suits coarse splits (a few per core). A thread pool, so fine-grained recursion is\n  cheap, is not in this decision.\n- **Not in this decision:** handles and loops of spawns (option B); results returned as a pair; captured closures;\n  a thread pool.\n- Benchmarks: `benchmarks/parallel` gains a variant that sums a vector the program owns, split by `both`, against\n  C with pthreads over the same split, on the Ryze",
    108 =>
        "n box (6 cores), before any performance claim.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Fork-join (A)**, rather than Rust-style scopes (B) or a slice helper only (C)? Recommended: A.\n2. **The name.** `Thread.both(a, b)`, or `Thread.join(a, b)` (Rayon's name, but `t.join()` already means joining one\n   thread), or `Thread.parallel(a, b)`? Recommended: `both`.\n3. **Results through captures only in v1**, with no pair-returning form until Nova has tuples or a library pair?\n   Recommended: yes.\n4. **No captured closures in v1** (a closure captured by `a` or `b`)? Recommended: yes. Allowing them needs the\n   capture rule applied through a closure's own captures, which can follow when a use needs it.\n5. **Sequential fallback when a thread cannot start**, so `both` cannot fail? Recommended: yes.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (type-system §38.6, ownership.md T1–T3, c-backend §2, the runtime), then:\n\n- the capture rule (N0334 cases) with check-fail tests: each kind of capture, shared and mutable, accepted and refused;\n- `Thread.both` in the checker, C and the interpreter; run-pass tests, including the sequential fallback under fault\n  injection;\n- the guide's threads page (`guide/threads.md`) and `novac explain` text;\n- the fuzzer generating `both` calls;\n- the `benchmarks/parallel` variant on the Ryzen box;\n- a CosmoOS userland run in QEMU on both arches (`c2.py`, as for M13 G4).\n\n## 7. Implementation and results (2026-10-28)\n\n- Spec: type-system §38.6, ownership T4, c-backend (`nvTF_`, `nvBOTH_`), N0334's row (`4e4576d`).\n- Code (`ab9c95c`): `CheckThreadBoth` and `TypeTable.IsForkSafe` in the checker; `BuiltinFunction.ThreadBoth` through NIR\n  and the effect checker (`sleep`, `alloc`); the C helpers; the interpreter's `ThreadBoth` under its baton, mirroring\n  `nova_thread_start`'s one allocation, so the fault-injection sweep exercises the sequential fallback. No new borrow\n  rule: the probes of §1 hold for `Thread.both` too (`check-fail/thread-both-borrows.nova`).\n- Found on the way, and fixed first (`e6b1a67`): a `SliceMut` field (`halves.left` of `splitAtMut`) was moved by a\n  use instead of reborrowed (type-system §13.3), so a closure could not pass it on (N0251), and a second use outside\n  one was N0250.\n- Tests: `run-pass/thread-both.nova` (halves read and changed in parallel, recursion to four threads, a `Mutex`, an\n  `fn()` value), `check-fail/thread-both.nova` (each refused capture, a closure variable, a move literal) and\n  `thread-both-borrows.nova`; the guide's threads page; the fuzzer (26 of the first 200 seeds). Run 2026-10-28: the\n  full suite (3,424) passes; the fuzzer's seeds 1–300 give no finding; `c2.py` runs the corpus, `thread-both`\n  included, in CosmoOS on x86-64 (188 programs, the fault sweep 43 programs and 381 runs) and AArch64 (the same: 188, and 43 and 381).\n- Benchmark (`benchmarks/parallel`, the `both` pair; the Ryzen box, a Ryzen 5 PRO 3600 with 6 cores, clang 18.1.8 -O2, 7 runs\n  interleaved, at normal priority, load average\n  about 1.2 on the shared box): 16 million values in a vector the program owns, split in halves.\n\n  | Threads | Nova `Thread.both` | C, recursive pthreads | Nova/C | Speed-up Nova / C |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 1 | 277.5 ms | 268.8 ms | 1.03 | 1.00 / 1.00 |\n  | 2 | 155.1 ms | 149.2 ms | 1.04 | 1.79 / 1.80 |\n  | 4 | 89.4 ms | 82.6 ms | 1.08 | 3.10 / 3.26 |\n\n  `Thread.both` scales as the same split does in C. Both stay below linear by the same margin; filling the vector,\n  which is serial, is inside the times (inferred).\n",
    109 =>
        "# ADR 0019 — OD-9a: direct closure calls by specialisation\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0019, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-28, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's. The\n  decision is in `optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md` §3 and `c-backend.md` §2.1. A remains open\n  (OD-9a), as a language question.\n- Scope: OD-9a (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0012 §5 question 5 \"until a performance need appears, with a\n  benchmark\": monomorphised closures, so a call through a closure can be a direct call the C compiler inlines.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **How a closure call compiles today.** A closure is a pointer to an environment whose first member is its code\n  (type-system §36.2). A call through one is `(*c)(c, a, b)` in C (c-backend §2). A library method such as `sortBy`\n  takes `borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32`, one type for every comparator of that signature, so its body cannot\n  know which function it calls. The C compiler does not specialise lib/core's introsort for the one comparator either.\n- **The need, measured** (2026-10-28, the Ryzen box: Ryzen 5 PRO 3600, Debian 12; `benchmarks/sort`, 10 rounds of\n  sorting 1,000,000 values; sort time with the data generation subtracted; median of 7 runs, interleaved; load average\n  1.0–2.1 on the shared box). \"Direct\" is novac's C for the benchmark with the seven calls through the comparator\n  replaced by direct calls to its function, `nvCB_92(t, a, b)`, by hand: what a specialisation would produce. Every\n  program printed the same checksum.\n\n  | Compiler | Nova today | Nova, direct calls | C `qsort` | the same introsort in C | the same, comparator opaque |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | clang 18.1.8 -O2 | 1.062 s | **0.671 s** | 1.171 s | 0.617 s | 0.907 s |\n  | gcc 12.2.0 -O2 | 1.076 s | **0.797 s** | 1.195 s | 0.659 s | 0.941 s |\n\n  Direct calls take 37% (clang) and 26% (gcc) off the sort, bringing Nova from 1.72× the C introsort to 1.09× under\n  clang. The rest is Nova's checks (M11 G8c found the same split).\n- **Where the closures come from.** Every call of lib/core's closure-taking methods (`sortBy`, `retain`, `forEach`,\n  `any`, `all`) in the corpus, the benchmarks and the guide passes a closure literal written at the call, or a named\n  function adapted to a closure (§36.2: `sortBy(compare)` in `closure-methods.nova`), and the methods pass it on\n  unchanged to their helpers (checked 2026-10-28 by searching for the calls). So at those calls the code that will\n  run is known. User functions with closure parameters were not surveyed.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Closure types and `Fn` bounds (OD-9a as written) | B. Specialisation in the compiler (proposed) | C. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Rust's: each literal its own type; `fn sortBy<F: Fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32>(…, cmp: borrow mut F)` | the call graph: a callee cloned per closure code it is called with | — |\n| Language change | yes: closure types, an `Fn` bound family, inference of `F`, its effects and loans in bounds; lib/core's signatures change | **none**: `sortBy` and every user function keep their signatures | none |\n| Which calls become direct | only through functions written with an `F` bound | every call through a parameter whose closure is known at the call, in any function | none |\n| Cost | a large language feature | a NIR-to-NIR pass, like M9's; code size: one clone per (function, closure) pair | the measured 26–37% stays |\n\n**B is proposed.** It gives the measured gain to code as it is written, lib/core's included, without adding to the\nlanguage. A remains open as a langua",
    110 =>
        "ge question (expressiveness: owning closures in generic code without a box),\nnot as a performance one.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision\n\n1. **Closure-call specialisation**, an optimisation pass on NIR before C (`optimisations.md` §5), on by default with\n   `--no-closure-specialisation`, as M9's are. It changes no program's behaviour, only which C function is called.\n2. **What is known.** At a call `f(…, c, …)`, an argument `c` is *known* when it is a closure made in the caller from a\n   literal, or an `fn` value's adapter, or a known parameter of a function being specialised, or a reborrow or copy\n   of a known value. Its *code* is that literal's or adapter's function.\n3. **What is cloned.** When `f`'s parameter `p` has a closure type and the argument is known with code `k`, the call\n   goes to `f[p := k]`: a copy of `f` in which every call through `p` (or a reborrow or copy of `p`) is a direct call\n   to `k`, and every call that passes `p` on is specialised the same way. Clones are made once per (function,\n   parameter, code), so recursion (`introsort` calling itself) calls its own clone. A function with several closure\n   parameters is specialised for each known one.\n4. **What is not.** A closure stored in a field, a `dyn fn` (§37), a `Box<dyn fn…>`, or one whose code depends on a\n   branch is called as today. Generic functions are specialised after monomorphisation, as one more instance.\n5. **Equivalence.** The clone is the same NIR with a call target fixed, so its checks, effects and loans are the\n   original's; borrow and effect checking run before the pass and are not repeated. The interpreter runs the program\n   unspecialised, and the corpus already compares it with native runs. The pass is checked by: the run-pass corpus with\n   and without it (outputs equal), the fuzzer's configurations (one more: `--no-closure-specialisation`), a test that\n   counts direct and indirect closure calls in the C of `benchmarks/sort`, and `--explain-closure-calls`, which lists\n   each call through a closure and whether it became direct.\n6. **Kernel and freestanding profiles** get it too: it adds no allocation and changes no frame size (a clone's frame\n   is the original's).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `sortBy`, `retain`, the closure-taking slice methods (§36.4) and user functions with closure parameters call\n  their closures directly, so the C compiler can inline them. No source changes.\n- Code size grows by one function per (function, closure) pair actually reached; `--explain-closure-calls` reports\n  the clones.\n- A remains open, as a language question.\n- **Not in this decision:** closure types or `Fn` bounds (A); specialising `dyn` calls (devirtualisation); inlining\n  in novac itself (the C compiler does it once the call is direct).\n- Benchmarks: `benchmarks/sort` is measured again on the Ryzen box after the pass, with clang and gcc, before any\n  claim, and `benchmarks/suite`'s closure workload (`nbody`) with it.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Specialisation in the compiler (B)**, rather than closure types and `Fn` bounds (A) now? Recommended: B, with\n   A left open as a language question.\n2. **On by default**, with `--no-closure-specialisation` and `--explain-closure-calls`? Recommended: yes, as for M9's\n   bounds-check and overflow-check elimination.\n3. **No limit on clones** in v1, the count reported by `--explain-closure-calls`? Recommended: yes; the count is\n   bounded by the closure literals a program writes times the functions they reach.\n\n## 6. Implementation plan (once accepted)\n\nSpec first (`optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md`, `c-backend.md`), then:\n\n- the pass (`Optimisation/ClosureSpecialisation.cs`): known closures, the clone worklist, the switch and the\n  explanation;\n- tests: the corpus with and without it, a C-shape test on `benchmarks/sort` (no indirect comparator calls remain),\n  explanation snapshots, the fuzzer configuration;\n- `benchmarks/sort` and `benchmarks/suite` re-measured on the Ryzen ",
    111 =>
        "box.\n\n## 7. Implementation and results (2026-10-28)\n\n- Spec: `optimisations.md` §5, `intermediate-representation.md` §3, `c-backend.md` §2.1 (`5bae3d6`).\n- Code (`9ec6aa1`): `Nir/ClosureSpecialisation.cs`; `NirFunction.Variant` and `NirCall.Variant`; the C names\n  `nvXS<n>_<name>`; the emitter's reachability now follows variants, so a library function that only its variants\n  replace is not emitted; the interpreter runs the specialised NIR; `--no-closure-specialisation` and\n  `--explain-closure-calls`.\n- In `benchmarks/sort` every one of the seven calls through the comparator is direct, through six variants of lib/core's\n  introsort (`sortBy`, `introsort`, `insertion`, `heapsort`, `partition`, `siftDown`).\n- Found on the way: `novac run` gave the interpreter the C backend's NIR, so with the pass on it was specialised twice,\n  which replaced originals by variants (an interpreter fault on the first variant call). The pass now leaves a\n  specialised program unchanged, and `run` passes its switch to the backend.\n- Tests, run 2026-10-28: the full suite passes (3,431), with the run corpus built in one more C configuration\n  (`clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation`); `ClosureSpecialisationTests` (the `--explain-closure-calls` snapshot of\n  `closure-calls/known.nova`, whose closure chosen by a branch stays indirect; its output with and without the pass;\n  no indirect comparator call in `benchmarks/sort`'s C, seven without the pass); the fuzzer's seeds 1–300 give no\n  finding in six configurations and the interpreter; `c2.py` runs the corpus in CosmoOS on x86-64 and AArch64 (188 programs each; the fault sweep 43 programs and 381 runs each).\n- Measured on the Ryzen box (clang 18.1.8 and gcc 12.2.0, -O2, 7 runs, the same session; load average 1.3–2.8 on the\n  shared box, normal priority). `benchmarks/sort`, sort time:\n\n  | Compiler | Nova, pass on | Nova, `--no-closure-specialisation` | the same introsort in C | C `qsort` |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | clang | **0.691 s** | 1.048 s | 0.610 s | 1.162 s |\n  | gcc | **0.791 s** | 1.100 s | 0.657 s | 1.188 s |\n\n  The pass takes 34% (clang) and 28% (gcc) off the sort, which is what §1's hand edit showed (0.671 s and 0.797 s).\n  Nova goes from 1.72× and 1.68× the C introsort to 1.13× and 1.20×.\n- `benchmarks/suite` with the pass on and off: `nbody`, `parsing` and `data-oriented` move within ±8% in both\n  directions, which is noise on that box. None of them calls through a closure: `nbody` has no closure literal any\n  more, so §4's reference to it as the suite's closure workload was out of date. The sort is the measured case.\n",
    112 =>
        "# ADR 0020 — OD-9c: functions whose effects are their closure arguments'\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0020, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-9c (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by M11 G8c (type-system §36.4): a function whose effects are its own plus\n  those of a closure argument, so that `sortBy` with an `@effects()` comparator may run in an atomic context.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Effects today** (type-system §32, §36.3). Each function's effects (`sleep`, `alloc`) are inferred from its body. A\n  call through a closure or function value has its *type's* effect list, and a type without one has both effects.\n  `@context(atomic)` forbids `sleep`, and `@context(irq)` forbids both.\n- **The gap.** lib/core's closure-taking methods (`sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain`; §36.4) take\n  `borrow mut fn(…)` with no list, so each of them has both effects, whatever closure it is given. None of them can be\n  used in atomic or interrupt code, even with a comparator that does nothing but compare. Giving the parameter a list\n  (`borrow mut @effects() fn(…)`) would forbid every comparator that allocates, everywhere. What is wanted is Swift's\n  `rethrows`: the method has the effects of what it is given.\n- **Who needs it.** CosmoOS kernel code holds spinlocks (atomic context) and runs interrupt handlers. Sorting or\n  scanning a small table under a lock, with `sortBy` or `any`, is today impossible in safe Nova. It must be written out by\n  hand (inferred: no CosmoOS port does this yet; `kernel/block/gpt.nova` and the A5 ports have no closures).\n- **What compiles the same either way.** Nothing in C or the interpreter changes. Effects are only checked.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `@effectsOf(p)` on a function (proposed) | B. Effect variables (`fn sortBy<effects E>(…, cmp: … @effects(E) fn …) @effects(E)`) | C. Check effects after closure specialisation (ADR 0019) | D. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Model | Swift's `rethrows`, for two effects | Koka's effect polymorphism | the variant `sortBy` for a known comparator has that comparator's effects | — |\n| Syntax | one attribute naming parameters | effect parameters, bounds and arguments | none | none |\n| Inference at calls | the argument's own effects (a literal's body, a named function's inference) | unification of effect variables | precise, per variant | — |\n| Soundness argument | short (§3.3) | the general one | ties acceptance to an optimisation (`--no-closure-specialisation` would change which programs compile) | — |\n| Cost | the checker, one attribute | a new kind of generic parameter | none in the checker, but wrong in principle | the gap stays |\n\nC is ruled out because a program's acceptance must not depend on an optimisation. B can say more, for example a\nfunction whose effects are the union of two closures stored in a struct. But nothing needs that, and it is a new kind\nof generics. A covers the library methods and any user function of the same shape.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The attribute\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(cmp)\npublic fn sortBy(s: SliceMut<T>, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) { … }\n\n@context(atomic)\nfn sortUnderLock(lock: *mut spinlock_t, table: SliceMut<i32>) {\n    unsafe { spin_lock(lock); }\n    table.sortBy((a, b) => a - b);   // allowed: sortBy's own effects are none, and the comparator's are none\n    unsafe { spin_unlock(lock); }\n}\n```\n\n- `@effectsOf(p, …)` on a function or method names one or more of its parameters. Each must be a function or closure\n  type: `fn(…)`, `borrow fn(…)` ",
    113 =>
        "or `borrow mut fn(…)`, with or without an effect list. Otherwise it is a new error code\n  (N0336). It may be combined with `@effects(…)`, which then bounds the function's *own* effects (§3.2).\n- It changes no type. The function's type and value are as before (§3.4).\n\n### 3.2 Inside the function\n\n- A call whose callee is the parameter `p` itself is not one of the function's own effects: the caller accounts for it.\n- Every other use is checked as today:\n  - a call through a copy or a reborrow of `p` (`let c = p; c(x)`) has its type's list;\n  - `p` passed to a function that is not `@effectsOf` for it: that function's inferred effects, which include\n    calls through its parameter by type;\n  - `p` stored in a field or captured by a closure: the type's list wherever it is called.\n- `p` passed directly as the argument for another function's `@effectsOf` parameter contributes nothing either:\n  `sortBy` may hand `cmp` to an `@effectsOf` helper.\n- The function's own effects are inferred from everything else. They are bounded by `@effects(…)` if given, and checked\n  against `@context` as today.\n\n### 3.3 At a call\n\nThe effects of a call to an `@effectsOf` function are its own effects, plus, for each named parameter, the effects of\nthe argument:\n\n| Argument | Its effects |\n|---|---|\n| a closure literal | its body's inferred effects |\n| a named function (adapted as in §36.1–§36.2) | that function's inferred effects |\n| the caller's own `@effectsOf` parameter, passed on directly | none (the caller's caller accounts for it) |\n| anything else | its type's effect list (both effects without one) |\n\nA diagnostic for such a call names the argument as the witness, for example \"calls sortBy with a closure that does\nconsole I/O (line 12)\", following the chain as N0295 does today.\n\n**Why it is sound.** A parameter is immutable (N0211), and closures and function values cannot be `var` (N0246). So\nduring the call, every call through `p` itself runs exactly the argument the caller passed, and that argument's effects\nare counted at the call. Calls through anything else made from `p` are counted by their types, as before. Effects are a\nproperty of a call's dynamic extent: a closure stored by the function and called after it returns is counted where it\nis called, by its type.\n\n### 3.4 As a value\n\nNaming an `@effectsOf` function as a value (`let f: @effects() fn(…) = sortBy`) gives it no special treatment. Its\neffects as a value are its own effects plus each named parameter's *type* list. The check of §36.3 then applies.\n\n### 3.5 lib/core\n\n- `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any` and `all` become `@effectsOf` their closure parameter, and so does `retain`. `retain` still\n  has the effects of destroying a `T` (its own, inferred per instantiation).\n- The spec's sentence \"these methods cannot be used in `@context(atomic)` … code\" (§36.4) changes accordingly.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Kernel code** can use the library's algorithms under a lock with a comparator that neither sleeps nor allocates.\n- **One new attribute** and one diagnostic code (N0336 for a misplaced or misnamed parameter). N0295 gains a witness\n  kind (the argument of a call).\n- **Nothing changes** for code that does not use the attribute, or in C, the interpreter, or the fuzzer's programs.\n- **Tests (planned):**\n  - check-pass: `sortBy`/`any` under `@context(atomic)` with literal and named comparators, and an `@effectsOf` helper\n    that passes its parameter on;\n  - check-fail: a sleeping comparator in atomic code (N0295 naming the argument), a copy of `p` called inside (counted\n    by type), a misnamed parameter or one that is not a closure (N0336), and `@effectsOf` with an `@effects()` bound\n    violated by the function's own body.\n- **Not decided here:** effect variables (B), which stay open in OD-9c if a need appears.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A, `@effectsOf(p)`, as above? (Recommended.)\n2. The name: `@effectsOf(p)` (proposed), or `@effects(…, of p)` inside the existin",
    114 =>
        "g attribute?\n3. lib/core's five methods change in the same step (§3.5)? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\nThe decision is in type-system §32.5, with §36.2 and §36.4 changed to match. Three points refine §3 without changing\nwhat it decides. Each follows from the same argument (§3.3: a parameter is never assigned):\n\n- **Copies of `p`.** §3.2 counted a copy of `p` by its type. A local that is assigned nothing but copies, casts or\n  reborrows of `p` holds the same argument, so a call through it is exempt as well. Anything else still counts by type.\n- **Arguments.** Besides a literal written in the call, a local *known* to hold a literal counts as that literal. That\n  is closure specialisation's analysis (optimisations.md §5.1), which the effect checker runs whatever the\n  optimisation switches are, so which programs compile does not depend on them. So does a local holding the caller's\n  own `@effectsOf` parameter.\n- **Named functions.** A named function adapted to a closure (§36.2) used to be held in the adapter's environment and\n  called through. Its effects were then the closure type's, both effects without a list, whatever the function did.\n  The adapter now calls the function directly. Its effects are the function's own, and the C has one indirect call\n  fewer.\n\nThe diagnostic code is N0336: N0335 was already `novac publish`'s. `EffectChecker.CallEffects` computes a call's\neffects, and `OriginsOf` finds what each local holds. Tests: `check-pass/effects-of.nova`, `check-fail/effects-of.nova`\n(N0295 for a sleeping literal, a sleeping named function, an argument without an effect list, a parameter handed to a\nfunction without the attribute, and the function as a value), `check-fail/effects-of-attribute.nova` (N0336), and\n`check-fail/closure-method-effects.nova` (lib/core's methods with a comparator that does I/O).\n",
    115 =>
        "# ADR 0021 — OD-24: weaker orderings for lib/std's atomics\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0021, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-24 (`open-decisions.md`), deferred by ADR 0016 \"sequentially consistent only in v1, until a benchmark needs\n  more\": acquire, release and relaxed orderings for `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize` and `AtomicBool`.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (type-system §38.3, `lib/std/sync.nova`): every atomic operation is sequentially consistent. Each is a call\n  into `runtime/hosted/nova_rt_thread.c`, which uses `__atomic_*_n(…, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)`, or `Interlocked*` under\n  MSVC, where even a load is a compare-exchange.\n- **The benchmark.** `benchmarks/atomics` passes 50 million values through a single-producer, single-consumer ring of\n  1,024 slots between the two threads of `Thread.both`. It is the shape of a work queue or a log buffer. Four C builds\n  of the same ring separate the cost of the ordering from the cost of the call:\n\n  | Ring (median of 9, interleaved) | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Nova today (seq_cst, runtime calls) | 2.683 s | 2.629 s |\n  | C, seq_cst inline | 2.761 s | 2.170 s |\n  | C, seq_cst through calls | 1.607 s | 1.529 s |\n  | **C, acquire/release (slots relaxed) through calls** | **0.289 s** | **0.291 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.158 s | 0.157 s |\n\n  Ryzen 5 PRO 3600 (x86-64), Debian 12, 2026-10-29, threads pinned to two cores sharing an L3 (CPUs 1 and 2), under\n  `nice`, load average 1.0–2.3 on the shared box. Every program printed the same checksum. A seq_cst run varies by up\n  to 40% between runs: it is dominated by the ring's two cache lines moving between cores. The acquire/release runs\n  vary by 1–2%.\n\n  On the Mac (Apple M-series, clang 21, 20 million values, 5 runs, not pinned), the orderings make no difference: 0.718\n  s and 0.712 s for acquire/release, against 0.573 s for seq_cst through calls. arm64's seq_cst loads and stores are\n  `ldar`/`stlr`, which acquire/release also uses. x86's seq_cst store is an `xchg`, a full barrier, which is what the\n  orderings remove.\n- **The need, then:** on x86-64, the commonest server target, the orderings alone make this ring 5–9 times faster,\n  without changing how atomics are called.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. An ordering argument on new methods (proposed) | B. Inline atomics in generated C | C. Both | D. Nothing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Change | `loadWith(Acquire)` and friends in lib/std; the runtime gains ordered entry points | atomics become built-in operations the emitter writes as `__atomic_*` | — | — |\n| Gain measured | 5–9× on the ring (x86) | none alone: inline seq_cst was no faster than calls (table) | a further 1.8× over A (0.158 s against 0.289 s) | — |\n| Cost | library and runtime; no compiler change | the emitter, the interpreter, MSVC's intrinsics, `Send`/`Share` reasoning unchanged | both | — |\n\nB gains nothing without A. C's extra step is a compiler change with its own decision, left open (§4).\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The library\n\n```nova\npublic enum LoadOrdering { Relaxed, Acquire, SequentiallyConsistent }\npublic enum StoreOrdering { Relaxed, Release, SequentiallyConsistent }\npublic enum Ordering { Relaxed, Acquire, Release, AcquireRelease, SequentiallyConsistent }\n\n// On each of AtomicU64, AtomicU32, AtomicUSize, AtomicBool (where the operation exists today):\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u64;\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: StoreOrdering);\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, va",
    116 =>
        "lue: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64, order: Ordering) -> …;   // as compareExchange\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64;\n```\n\n- The existing methods are unchanged and stay sequentially consistent, so no program changes meaning.\n- **No undefined orderings.** C11 leaves a store with acquire ordering, and a load with release ordering, undefined.\n  Separate enums for loads and stores make those impossible to write. A compare-exchange takes one ordering, and its\n  failure ordering is derived as C11 requires: `AcquireRelease` fails with `Acquire`, `Release` fails with `Relaxed`,\n  and the others fail with themselves.\n- **The meaning** is C11's (the C standard §7.17.3), which the documentation states for each enum value. Nova adds no\n  memory model of its own.\n\n### 3.2 The runtime and the C\n\n- `nova_rt_thread.c` gains `nova_atomic_<op>_<width>_ord` entry points taking the ordering as an `int`. Each switches on\n  it, with constant orderings in the `__atomic_*` builtins, so the C compiler emits each case's own instructions.\n- Under MSVC, every `Interlocked*` function is a full barrier, which is a correct (stronger) implementation of every\n  ordering. So MSVC changes nothing.\n\n### 3.3 Safety\n\n- **Memory safety and data-race freedom are unchanged.** Every access to an atomic is still atomic, and `Send`/`Share`\n  and T1–T3 (`ownership.md`) are untouched. A weaker ordering changes which values a load may return, never whether an\n  access is a race.\n- **What it does allow is logic bugs.** A program that publishes data with `Relaxed` instead of `Release` may read\n  stale values. That was OD-24's stated safety impact, and the API keeps it opt-in.\n- **Testing limits.** The interpreter runs threads one at a time, in sequentially consistent order, which is one legal\n  execution of a weaker program. So it cannot find an ordering bug, and neither can ThreadSanitizer, which sees no race.\n  The guide says so.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- lib/std gains three enums and six methods per atomic type. The runtime gains the ordered entry points. Neither the\n  compiler nor the language changes.\n- `benchmarks/atomics` gains a Nova build with acquire/release, expected near the \"through calls\" row.\n- **Not decided here:**\n  - inline atomics (B), whose further 1.8× is its own question;\n  - `fence(order)`;\n  - weaker orderings inside `Arc`'s count, which are a change to `unsafe` library code with its own argument.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A as above: ordered `…With` methods, existing methods unchanged? (Recommended.)\n2. Separate `LoadOrdering`/`StoreOrdering` enums, so undefined orderings cannot be written? (Recommended; the alternative\n   is one enum and a panic on an invalid ordering.)\n3. Record inline atomics (B) as a new open decision, OD-27? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- lib/std (`sync.nova`) has the three enums and the `…With` methods. The runtime has `nova_atomic_<op>_ord_<width>`\n  (`nova_rt_thread.c`, declared in `nova/rt.h`), which switch to constant orderings. The interpreter treats each as\n  sequentially consistent (`CLibrary.cs`). Type-system §38.3 and the threads guide describe them.\n- Tests: `run-pass/atomic-orderings.nova` checks each method's result and a release/acquire message between the two\n  threads of `Thread.both`, in every C configuration, ThreadSanitizer included, and in the interpreter.\n- **Results.** `benchmarks/atomics` gained `spsc-ordered.nova`, the same ring with acquire/release indices and relaxed\n  slots. On the Ryzen box (as in §1: pinned to CPUs 1 and 2, median of 9, interleaved, load average 1.1–2.9):\n\n  | | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Nova, sequentially consistent | 1.663 s | 2.634 s |\n  | **Nova, acquire/release** | **0.366 s** | **0.442 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release through cal",
    117 =>
        "ls | 0.302 s | 0.293 s |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.161 s | 0.156 s |\n\n  Nova's ring is 4.5× (clang) and 6.0× (gcc) faster with orderings, within 1.2–1.5× of C's through calls. The rest of\n  the gap to inline C is OD-27.\n- **The interpreter and spinning.** The interpreter switches threads only when one blocks or ends (ADR 0016 §3.8). So a\n  thread that spins on an atomic, waiting for a thread that has not yet run, spins forever under `novac run --interp`.\n  This was already true with sequentially consistent atomics. The test puts its spinning receiver on the new thread\n  for that reason.\n",
    118 =>
        "# ADR 0022 — M14 K2a: generated C in several translation units\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0022, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K2a (`milestone-14.md`), the first step of OD-29: novac stays whole-program, but its C is split so that\n  a C compiler can build it in parallel, and rebuild only what changed.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (c-backend §2.1): `novac build --emit c` writes one `main.c` holding every function of the program,\n  lib/core's included, each with internal linkage (`static`), plus the runtime files (`nova_rt_core.c`, …) and, with\n  `@export`, `nova_exports.h`. Internal linkage is what lets CosmoOS link several separately generated programs (its\n  four ports) into one kernel: each has its own copy of the lib/core functions it uses, and the copies never collide.\n- **The cost at kernel scale** (M14, measured 2026-10-29, Mac M-series): a synthetic 202,004-line package became one\n  1,076,433-line `main.c`. novac took 13.1 s, and `clang -O2 -c` took 33.8 s and 1.25 GB on one core. A kernel\n  written in Nova would pay both on every change. CosmoOS's C kernel compiles each file separately, in parallel, and\n  only what changed.\n- **What does not change here.** Checking stays whole-program (ADR 0013 D4), and so does novac's own time. Separate\n  compilation of Nova packages is OD-29's second step, decided later with K2a's measurements.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Split by source file (proposed) | B. Split into N equal parts | C. One file, as today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit of rebuild | the Nova file that changed, if no other unit's declarations changed | any change can move functions between parts | everything |\n| Determinism | each function's unit is a function of its source file | needs a stable partition | — |\n| Readable | `kernel/net/tcp.nova` → `tcp.c` | no | — |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A, behind a switch\n\n### 3.1 The layout\n\n`novac build --emit c --split -o DIR` writes:\n\n- `nova_program.h`, the program's declarations: every type, every function prototype, the runtime's header, and the\n  `@export` prototypes (the content of `nova_exports.h`, which is still written as before);\n- one `<name>.c` per Nova source file, holding the functions declared in it, the closures written in them, and their\n  string literals and source locations. Each includes `nova_program.h`;\n- `nova_instances.c`, holding the instantiations of generic code, lib/core's functions and the drop glue: everything\n  that belongs to no single user file. One file, so that two units never define the same instance;\n- the runtime files, as today.\n\nA unit's name is its source file's name, with the shortest distinguishing directory prefix when two files share a\nname (`net_tcp.c`, `vfs_tcp.c`), so the layout is a pure function of the package's file list.\n\n### 3.2 Linkage and names\n\n- A function called from another unit has external linkage. Every other function stays `static`, as today.\n- **External names carry the program's prefix**, so two programs linked into one image cannot collide: `nvF_` becomes\n  `nvF_<program>_`. The program's name is the package's (`nova.toml`), or `--program NAME` for a file build. The\n  prefix also applies to the instances in `nova_instances.c`, which every program has a copy of.\n- Small helpers that the emitter generates per use (`nvVP_`, `nvVA_`, the check helpers) become `static inline` in\n  `nova_program.h`, so every unit can inline them. A call across units to a Nova function cannot be inlined without\n  link-time optimisation. §4 says what that may cost.\n- `@export` names ",
    119 =>
        "are unchanged: they are the C names the user chose.\n\n### 3.3 Determinism\n\nThe output stays a pure function of the inputs: the units, their order, each function's placement and the prefix are\nderived from the sources and the switch, never from hash order or time. A test builds each `emit-c` corpus program\nwith `--split` twice and compares, and builds the result in every C configuration.\n\n### 3.4 Without `--split`\n\nNothing changes: one `main.c`, internal linkage, today's names. CosmoOS's four ports, and every snapshot, stay\nbyte-identical. CosmoOS opts in per program when its build moves to Nova (its roadmap 2.0's SA1).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Parallel and incremental C builds.** The acceptance measures them on the 202,004-line program: `make -j` over\n  the units, and a rebuild after a one-file change. A change to a function's signature changes `nova_program.h`, which\n  every unit includes, so it rebuilds them all. A body-only change rebuilds one unit, plus `nova_instances.c` if it\n  changes an instance.\n- **Performance.** Calls across units are no longer inlinable at `-O2` without LTO. The ports' benchmarks\n  (`benchmarks/lz4`, `fbvalid`, `gpt`) are run with and without `--split` to measure it. Kernel code built with LTO\n  regains it.\n- **Tools.** The debugger helpers (`editors/debug/nova_names.py`) learn the prefix. Source maps (`#line`) are\n  unchanged, because each unit's `#line` directives name its own Nova file.\n- **Not decided here:** separate checking of packages, the rest of OD-29.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Split by source file, behind `--split`, with one-file output unchanged by default? (Recommended.)\n2. External names prefixed with the program's name (`nvF_<program>_`)? (Recommended: the alternative, hidden\n   visibility, does not prevent duplicate symbols in a static kernel link.)\n3. Generic instances and lib/core in one `nova_instances.c`? (Recommended; the alternative is one unit per instance.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- `novac build --split [--program NAME]` (`CEmitOptions.Split`, `CEmitter.EmitSplit`), specified in c-backend §2.4.\n  The run corpus builds every program split as a C configuration of its own (`clang -O2 split`), with the strict\n  warnings. The debugger helpers read the renamed functions when `NOVA_PROGRAM` is set (tooling §3.3).\n- **As §3.2 says, the per-type helpers belong in the header.** A first version put them in `nova_instances.c` with\n  external linkage. Every vector push and drop then became a call across units, and `vector-growth` and\n  `string-building` ran 1.7–1.9× slower. In the header, `static inline`, both run as fast as one file.\n- **Measurements** (Mac M-series, Apple clang 21, `-O2`; the synthetic program of `milestone-14.md` spread over 21\n  Nova files): novac 14.2 s. The C:\n\n  | Build | Wall | CPU |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | one file, as before | 33.8 s | 33.8 s |\n  | `--split`, `make -j10` | 14.0 s | 80.3 s |\n  | `--split`, one unit changed | 2.4 s | 2.3 s |\n\n  The program printed the same value either way. CPU time grows because every unit parses the 99,484-line header. A\n  smaller header per unit, holding only what that unit uses, would cut it, and is left for when kernel builds show the\n  need.\n- Two `--split` builds of the same sources are byte-identical.\n\n",
    120 =>
        "# ADR 0023 — M14 K4: building a Nova kernel where novac does not run\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0023, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29, taking every recommendation in §5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K4, OD-31. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 makes two choices that conflict once its kernel is Nova. Its rule 7\n  stops committing generated C, so every build runs novac. Its SW0 makes CosmoOS build itself. novac does not run on\n  CosmoOS.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **novac** is a .NET program, distributed as NativeAOT binaries for Linux, macOS and Windows on x64 and arm64\n  (`release.md`). NativeAOT needs a .NET runtime port for each operating system, and CosmoOS has none. It runs Linux\n  programs through its personality, but not the .NET runtime's full use of Linux: threads, `mmap` and signal-based GC\n  suspension (inferred; untested).\n- **Today** CosmoOS commits the generated C beside each `.nova` file, so any machine with a C compiler builds the\n  kernel. That is what makes a self-hosted build possible now.\n- **Generated C is a pure function** of the sources and novac's version (c-backend §1; CosmoOS checks it in CI).\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Source releases carry the generated C (proposed) | B. novac on CosmoOS | C. A Nova compiler in Nova | D. Keep committing generated C |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Self-hosted build | from a release tarball, no novac | yes | yes | yes |\n| Changing a `.nova` file on CosmoOS | needs a host with novac | yes | yes | needs a host with novac |\n| Cost | a `make dist` target and a CI check | a .NET runtime port, or the Linux personality running .NET | years | the tree doubles as the kernel becomes Nova |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n- The repository holds `.nova` sources only, as roadmap 2.0's rule 7 says. `make dist` (CosmoOS) writes a source\n  tarball with the generated C beside each `.nova` file, made by the pinned novac. A build from the tarball runs no\n  novac.\n- CI checks that the tarball builds without novac on both architectures, and that its C matches a fresh generation\n  (the reproducibility check rule 7 already plans).\n- A self-hosted CosmoOS builds its kernel from a release tarball. Developing the kernel's Nova code on CosmoOS itself\n  needs a machine with novac until B or C exists. Both stay open as later decisions; C is the long-term direction for a\n  self-hosting Nova.\n- Nova needs nothing new for A: `novac build --emit c` already writes the files, with ADR 0022's `--split` layout when\n  the kernel uses it.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SW0's self-hosting acceptance builds the kernel from the release tarball.\n- CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 and `docs/build/nova.md` record the choice (a CosmoOS PR once this is accepted).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A? (Recommended.)\n2. Record B (novac on CosmoOS) and C (a Nova compiler written in Nova) as open decisions for later? (Recommended.)\n",
    121 =>
        "# ADR 0024 — M14 K1: static items (global state)\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0024, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), with `static mut` added at the owner's request (§3.7). The\n  wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K1, OD-28. Global state for a kernel written in Nova (CosmoOS roadmap 2.0's SA2 and SA3), and for hosted\n  programs.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** Nova has only compile-time `const` items (type-system §10). `static var count: u64 = 0;` and\n  `var count: u64 = 0;` at top level are N0101. CosmoOS's four Nova ports keep all their state in C, and C7's kernel\n  demonstrations (`integrations/cosmoos/c7`) get every lock from a function argument or a `KernelHeap` box made at run\n  time.\n- **A kernel needs** tables and singletons protected by locks (the handle table, the module list, lockdep's graph),\n  counters, and per-CPU data. CosmoOS keeps per-CPU data in arrays indexed by CPU id (`[CONFIG_MAX_CPUS]`, beside\n  `struct percpu`).\n- **Kernel locks are C state** (R-11, type-system §33). A spinlock may not be zero-initialised: a zeroed\n  `spinlock_t`'s owner is CPU 0. CosmoOS writes a static lock with a C initialiser macro,\n  `SPINLOCK_INIT(name)`, which sets the owner to `SPINLOCK_NO_OWNER`. C7's `SpinLock<T>` instead initialises its lock at\n  run time, in place (`@placed fn init`).\n- **Thread-safety already has a vocabulary.** `Share` (§38.1) says a value may be used from several threads at once.\n  Lock and atomic types are `Share` by the library's `@threadSafe` declaration.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Immutable statics of `Share` types, built by compile-time constructors (proposed) | B. `static mut` with `unsafe` access (Rust's) | C. Lazily initialised statics (a run-time constructor on first use) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safe access | `borrow NAME`, like any shared reference; mutation only through the type's own synchronisation | every access is `unsafe` | safe, but needs a once-guard on every access |\n| Data races | impossible: the type is `Share` | the user's argument | impossible |\n| Initialisation | at compile time, as C static initialisers: no constructor runs, no order to get wrong | the same | at run time, in an order the program must not depend on |\n| Kernel fit | yes: a `SpinLock<Table>` in a static, built with `SPINLOCK_INIT` | yes, unsafely | no: a once-guard in interrupt context needs a lock of its own |\n\nA is Rust's `static` with interior mutability, and Linux's `DEFINE_SPINLOCK`. The owner chose A and B together: B\nas `static mut`, every use of which is `unsafe` (§3.7), for the floor and for state whose synchronisation Nova cannot\nexpress. C is not taken.\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 The item\n\n```nova\nstatic TABLE: SpinLock<HandleTable> = SpinLock.constant(\"handles\", HandleTable.empty());\nstatic OPENED: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64.constant(0);\n\nfn openOne() -> u64 => OPENED.fetchAdd(1) + 1;\n```\n\n- `static NAME: T = e;` at package level, `public` or not. `T` must be `Share` (§38.1), or it is a new error,\n  N0337: \"a static is shared by every thread\". This also covers a static used only from one thread, because the\n  checker cannot know that.\n- A static is **never assigned or moved** (N0337), and `NAME` in an expression is a place of type `T` that only shared\n  borrows and copies (for a `Copy` `T`) may use. So all mutation goes through `T`'s own methods taking `borrow this`:\n  a lock's guard or an atomic's operation.\n- **Region.** `borrow NAME` has the static region, which outlives every other region. A reference to a static may be\n  stored and returned anywhere a reference may (E1–E4 hold trivially), and passed to another thread when ",
    122 =>
        "`T` is\n  `Share`.\n- A static is never dropped. A `T` with a `drop` is allowed, and its drop never runs: a kernel never exits, and a\n  hosted program's statics end with the process, as in C.\n- Generic statics, and statics inside functions, are not in v1 (N0337).\n\n### 3.2 Compile-time constructors\n\n`e` must be a **static initialiser**, evaluated by the compiler to a C constant initialiser. It may be:\n\n- a constant expression (§10), a string literal (as `*CChar`), or a struct, enum or array literal of static\n  initialisers (fields private to `T` are allowed only inside `T`'s own `@constant` constructor);\n- a call of a `@constant` function: one whose body is a single expression that is a static initialiser of its\n  parameters, the parameters being static initialisers at each call. `@constant` functions are checked as ordinary\n  functions too, so they can be called at run time;\n- a call of a **C constant macro**: an `extern \"C\"` function declared `@constant`, whose name is a C macro that expands\n  to a constant initialiser (`@constant fn SPINLOCK_INIT(name: *CChar) -> spinlock_t;`). Nova trusts the declaration,\n  as it trusts `@effects` on an extern (R-2).\n\nA type whose values are made at their final address (`@placed init`, §33.3) may be in a static only through a\n`@constant` constructor, whose initialiser must leave it as `init` would. For CosmoOS's locks that is the C macro's\njob, so `SpinLock.constant` uses `SPINLOCK_INIT` and does not call `init`.\n\n### 3.3 Per-CPU data is a library type\n\nThere is no `@percpu` in the language. The kernel library (K3) provides `PerCpu<T>`, an array of `T` per CPU behind a\n`Share` type. `PerCpu<T>.local(guard)` lends the calling CPU's element, for as long as a guard that disables\npreemption (or interrupts, for data an interrupt handler shares) is borrowed. Its `unsafe` core argues the rest: the\nguard pins the CPU, so only this CPU's code, and not its interrupts when the guard masks them, uses the element. So:\n\n```nova\nstatic TICKS: PerCpu<u64> = PerCpu.constant(0);\n\n@context(atomic)\nfn tick(preempt: borrow PreemptGuard) { *TICKS.local(preempt) += 1; }\n```\n\nThat keeps the language change to statics, and puts the CPU model where CosmoOS's own API is (OD-30).\n\n### 3.4 C\n\nA static is a C object definition with internal linkage, or external with `@export` (or across ADR 0022's units),\nnamed `nvS_<name>`. Its initialiser is the evaluated C initialiser: the `@constant` calls are expanded, and the C macros\nare written as calls. It is `const` in C only when `T` contains no C state, no atomic and no lock, so that its methods\ncan write through the pointers they derive, in their own `unsafe` code.\n\n### 3.5 Unsafe access at the floor\n\n`unsafe` code may still take `borrow NAME as *T as *mut T`, as for any shared reference (`unsafe.md`). That is how\nthe library types implement their synchronisation. It is not a `static mut`.\n\n### 3.7 `static mut`\n\n```nova\nstatic mut BOOT_INFO: BootInfo = BootInfo.empty();\n\n/// Safety: runs once on the boot CPU, before any other CPU or interrupt can read BOOT_INFO.\nunsafe fn recordBoot(info: BootInfo) { BOOT_INFO = info; }\n```\n\n- `static mut NAME: T = e;` takes the same static initialisers (§3.2). `T` need not be `Share`.\n- **Every use is an `unsafe` operation**: reading, writing, assigning to a field, borrowing, or taking its address. U1\n  (`unsafe.md`) gains \"use a `static mut`\", so outside `unsafe` code each use is N0280, and a function using one needs\n  its `/// Safety:` comment (N0283).\n- A borrow of a `static mut` has the static region, as for a `static`. Nothing checks that two such borrows do not\n  alias, or that two threads do not race: that is the `/// Safety:` argument (U3's rule for references made from\n  pointers).\n- In C it is a non-`const` object (`nvS_<name>`), like a static whose type holds C state.\n- The interpreter checks accesses through it as it checks raw pointers: an access out of bounds or after the end of a\n  value is a fault (`UnsafeTests`).\n\n### 3.6",
    123 =>
        " Hosted programs\n\nThe same `static` works in hosted programs for atomics (`AtomicU64.constant`, in lib/std) and any `@threadSafe` type\nwith a `@constant` constructor. lib/std's `Mutex<T>` allocates its platform lock and so has none in v1. A static mutex\nis a later library change: `PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER` and `SRWLOCK_INIT` are C constant macros.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **The language:** `static` and `static mut` items, the static region, `@constant` functions and C constant macros.\n  Diagnostics: N0337 for a static that is not `Share`, is assigned or moved, or is generic or local; N0338 for an\n  initialiser that is not a static initialiser; N0280 for a use of a `static mut` outside `unsafe`.\n- **The interpreter** evaluates static initialisers once, before `main`, into its memory. C macros it cannot evaluate\n  (it has no C) fault, so kernel statics are tested natively.\n- **Tests (planned):**\n  - run-pass: atomics and a lock in statics, from two threads (`Thread.both`) under TSan;\n  - check-fail: each N0337/N0338 case;\n  - a CosmoOS kernel object table in a static `SpinLock`, as M14 K1's acceptance says.\n- **`@threadSafe` outside Nova's libraries.** A static's type must be `Share`, and kernel lock types are `Share` only\n  by `@threadSafe`, which today is accepted only in Nova's own library files (N0290). The kernel library (K3, OD-30)\n  needs the same permission. ADR 0025 decides it with the library's home: for example, a package that declares itself\n  a trusted library in its `nova.toml`.\n- **Not decided here:** lazily initialised statics, generic statics.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A: immutable statics of `Share` types, built at compile time? (Recommended.)\n2. `@constant` functions and `@constant` C macros as the way to build them? (Recommended. The alternative is to allow\n   only literals and constants, which cannot build a private-field lock or call `SPINLOCK_INIT`.)\n3. Per-CPU data as the kernel library's `PerCpu<T>`, not a language attribute? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\nSpecified in type-system §39, grammar `StaticDecl`, diagnostics N0337 and N0338.\n\n- **Uses** need no new borrow rule. A function that uses a static gets a hidden local holding its address, a\n  `borrow(static) T` (`borrow(static) mut T` for a `static mut`) made by `HirStaticAddress`/`NirStaticAddress`, which\n  holds no loans. `NAME` is `*local`. The existing rules then apply: through a shared reference nothing is changed\n  (N0211, N0248) or moved out (N0251), and returned references need a return type that names `static` (N0245\n  otherwise). Diagnostics call the place \"static 'NAME'\". So assigning or moving a static is N0211 or N0251, not N0337\n  as §4 planned. N0337 is for a static whose type is not `Share`, and N0338 for an initialiser.\n- **One exception to `Share`:** a `StringView`, the only reference a static may hold, viewing static string data.\n- **Initialisers** are type-checked before the function bodies, so the instances their `@constant` calls create are\n  checked with them. They are folded after the bodies, by substituting arguments into `@constant` bodies\n  (`TypeChecker.StaticFold`). A `@constant` body is checked once with its parameters symbolic.\n- **C** (`CEmitter.EmitStatics`): `static NV_UNUSED T nvS_NAME = { … };`. With `--split`, it is `extern` in the\n  header, defined in `nova_instances.c` and renamed like the functions. A C constant macro gets no prototype and no\n  wrapper. A run-time call of one, in a `@constant` function called at run time, is a compound literal\n  `((T)MACRO(args))`.\n- **Interpreter**: each static is a writable allocation, filled before `main` and not counted as a heap allocation; a\n  C macro in an initialiser is a fault.\n- lib/std's atomics have `@constant` constructors (`AtomicU64.constant`, …).\n- **Tests:**\n  - `run-pass/statics.nova`, in every C configuration (split and TSan included) and the interpreter: atomics changed\n    from two threads, constants, struct, array an",
    124 =>
        "d `StringView` statics, a `@constant` constructor setting private\n    fields, `static mut`, and references returned as `borrow(static)`;\n  - check-fail `statics.nova`, `static-moves.nova` and `static-initializers.nova`;\n  - `parse-pass/statics.nova`.\n- **Not yet:** `@export` statics, enum values in initialisers, and a C constant macro compiled natively. The macro path\n  is checked and emitted, but its C needs a header defining the macro: M14 K3's kernel library, with CosmoOS's\n  `SPINLOCK_INIT`, is its first real use and test. Per-CPU data (`PerCpu<T>`) is K3 too.\n",
    125 =>
        "# ADR 0025 — M14 K3: the kernel library\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0025, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), with option **B**, the library in Nova's repository, instead of the\n  recommended A. `trusted` packages (§3.2) and the v1 API (§3.3) were accepted as recommended. §6 records what B\n  changes. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: M14 K3, OD-30. The types CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 needs for SA2 (allocation and object lifetimes) and SA3 (locks,\n  waits and quiescence): where they live, who may declare them thread-safe, and their API.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **What exists.** These were written as demonstrations, each with its own C glue and tests:\n\n  | Type | Where | Status |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `KernelHeap` | C5, `integrations/cosmoos/c5/kernel.nova` | an `Allocator` over `kmalloc`/`kfree` |\n  | `KRef<T>` | C7, `c7/kobject.nova` | a counted kobject whose drop is the release |\n  | `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>`, `Mutex<T>` and their guards | C7, `c7/sync.nova` | the data only through the guard; a spin guard is an atomic context |\n  | `Quiesce` | C7, `c7/quiesce.nova` | quiescence guards |\n  | `ObjectCache<T>` | — | not built |\n  | `PerCpu<T>`, `PreemptGuard` | — | not built (ADR 0024 §3.3) |\n\n  Each lock is made at run time, in a `KernelHeap` box (`@placed init`). None can be a static yet.\n- **Statics** (ADR 0024) now let a lock live in a static, built by a `@constant` constructor and a C constant macro.\n  CosmoOS has `SPINLOCK_INIT(name)`. It has no static initialiser for `struct mutex`, only `mutex_init`.\n- **`@threadSafe`**, which makes a type `Send` and `Share` by declaration, is accepted only in Nova's own library files\n  (N0290). A static's type must be `Share`, and a kernel lock is `Share` only by that declaration.\n- **How CosmoOS builds Nova today:** each `.nova` file is compiled alone (`scripts/nova-gen.py`), so files share no Nova\n  types.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. A Nova package in CosmoOS's tree (proposed) | B. A library in Nova's repository (`lib/kernel`) | C. Keep demonstrations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Owner | CosmoOS: it binds CosmoOS's C API | Nova, but it binds one kernel's API | — |\n| Changes with the kernel | in the same PR as the C it binds | needs a Nova release for each change | — |\n| `@threadSafe` | needs a way to trust a package | already allowed | — |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\n### 3.1 Home and build\n\n- `kernel/nova/core/` in CosmoOS is a Nova package (`nova.toml`: `name = \"kcore\"`, `kind = \"library\"`,\n  `profile = \"freestanding\"`, `float = false`).\n- CosmoOS's kernel code written in Nova becomes packages that depend on it by path. `nova-gen.py` builds each package\n  with `novac build --emit c --split --program <package>` (ADR 0022), so the kernel's Nova packages share Nova types\n  through `kcore`. Packages replace today's one-file ports as each is touched; the four existing ports keep working as\n  they are.\n\n### 3.2 Trusted packages\n\n- A package may declare in its manifest that it holds `unsafe` synchronisation code: `trusted = true`. In such a\n  package `@threadSafe` is accepted, as in Nova's libraries. Elsewhere it stays N0290. A dependency that is trusted is\n  listed in `novac build`'s output, so a reviewer sees which packages carry the argument.\n- `trusted` permits nothing else: `unsafe` already works in any package, with its `/// Safety:` comments (U2). What it\n  adds is that a type's thread-safety becomes the package's claim.\n\n### 3.3 The API (v1)\n\n- **`KernelHeap`**, from C5, unchanged.\n- **`ObjectCache<T>`**, a typed slab cache over `kmem_cache`. A static or boxed cache made by `ObjectCache<T>.create(name)`\n  (`kmem",
    126 =>
        "_cache_create` with `T`'s size and alignment). `cache.alloc(value)` returns `Result<CacheBox<T>, AllocError>`.\n  A `CacheBox<T>` is one pointer whose drop drops the value and returns the memory to the cache. Because `Box<T, A>`\n  needs a stateless allocator (C5), `CacheBox<T>` is its own type, scoped to the cache it came from.\n- **`SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>`, `Mutex<T>` and their guards**, from C7, plus `@constant` constructors:\n  `SpinLock.constant(name, data)` with `SPINLOCK_INIT`, and `Mutex.constant(name, data)` with a new `MUTEX_INIT(name)`\n  macro, which CosmoOS adds to `mutex.h` in the same change.\n- **`PreemptGuard`** (preemption off until dropped; an atomic context) and **`IrqGuard`** (interrupts off; also\n  atomic), and **`PerCpu<T>`**: `CONFIG_MAX_CPUS` values, `PerCpu.constant(v)`, and `p.local(borrow guard)`, which\n  lends this CPU's value while the guard is borrowed. Its `unsafe` core is argued once: a guard pins the CPU, and an\n  `IrqGuard` also keeps this CPU's interrupt handlers out.\n- **`KRef<T>`** and **`Quiesce`**, from C7.\n\n### 3.4 Tests\n\nThe C5 and C7 self-tests and stress tests move into CosmoOS as kernel self-tests of `kcore`. They run in every debug\nboot, on both architectures, under lockdep. Nova's `c5.py` and `c7.py` stay as they are, against their own copies, until\n`kcore` replaces them.\n\n### 3.5 What Nova changes\n\n- `trusted = true` in the manifest, and `@threadSafe` in trusted packages (tooling §4, type-system §38.1).\n- Nothing else. The API is Nova code in CosmoOS.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- **Order:**\n  1. Nova: `trusted`, then the 0.2.0 release with statics and `--split`.\n  2. CosmoOS: the pin moves to 0.2.0, `kcore` lands with its self-tests, and `MUTEX_INIT` is added.\n  3. M14 K1's acceptance, a kernel object table in a static `SpinLock`, is then CosmoOS's SA2 proving conversion.\n- `kcore`'s `unsafe` code is a new place unsoundness can hide (R-2, R-13). Its `/// Safety:` arguments go in the\n  safety-audit pack (`safety-audit.md` §3) for the outside audit (K6).\n- **Not decided here:** a stateful `Box` allocator, which would let `ObjectCache` be an `Allocator`, and wait queues and\n  completions (SA3's own design, on top of these types).\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. The library as a Nova package in CosmoOS's tree, `kernel/nova/core/` (\"kcore\")? (Recommended.)\n2. `trusted = true` in `nova.toml` to allow `@threadSafe` there? (Recommended; the alternative is to keep the kernel\n   library's thread-safe types in Nova's repository.)\n3. The v1 API in §3.3, including the new `MUTEX_INIT` macro in CosmoOS? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. The decision: B, and what it changes\n\nThe owner chose B: the kernel library is part of Nova's repository and ships inside `novac`, like lib/std.\n\n- **`lib/kernel`**, a library for a kernel with CosmoOS's C API. It is not compiled into every freestanding build, as\n  lib/core is, because it names CosmoOS's headers. A build selects it with `kernel = \"cosmoos\"` in `nova.toml`'s\n  `[package]`, or `novac --kernel cosmoos` for a build of files. That is valid only with the freestanding profile\n  (N0320 otherwise). Its names are global in the build, as lib/std's are in a hosted one (§38.5).\n- **`@threadSafe`** is accepted in lib/kernel as in Nova's other libraries. `trusted = true` (§3.2) is still accepted,\n  for packages outside Nova that hold synchronisation code of their own, such as a CosmoOS driver's.\n- **A change to the library is a Nova change**, released and then picked up by CosmoOS's pin. §3.1's \"in the same PR as\n  the C it binds\" becomes two changes, Nova's first. `MUTEX_INIT` was to be added to CosmoOS's `mutex.h` (not\n  done; §7).\n- **Testing.**\n  - Nova's suite type-checks programs that use lib/kernel (`--kernel cosmoos`, freestanding) and the misuses it\n    refuses. It does not compile their C, because CI has no CosmoOS headers.\n  - `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` builds and runs the library's tests natively against a CosmoOS checkout, as `c7.py`\n    does (its ho",
    127 =>
        "st harness and shims).\n  - Once the pin moves, CosmoOS's own self-tests, in debug boots on both architectures, are the library's acceptance.\n\n## 7. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\n- **`lib/kernel`** (`heap.nova`, `sync.nova`, `kobject.nova`, `quiesce.nova`), embedded in novac. It is selected by\n  `--kernel cosmoos`, `kernel = \"cosmoos\"` in `nova.toml`, or `// option: kernel cosmoos` in a corpus file.\n  - From C5 and C7: `KernelHeap`, `SpinLock`/`IrqSpinLock`/`Mutex` and their guards, now `@threadSafe` with `T: Send`;\n    `KRef<T>`; `Quiesce`, `Deferred<T>`, `deferDrop`.\n  - New: the `@constant` constructors (`SpinLock<T>.constant` and `IrqSpinLock<T>.constant`, with `SPINLOCK_INIT`);\n    `PreemptGuard`; `IrqGuard`; and `PerCpu<T: Copy + Send>`, whose `local(IrqGuard)` returns a\n    `PerCpuRef` that owns the guard and lends the value, a second loan on one CPU panicking.\n- **What this needed in the compiler** (type-system §39.4, \"C state at compile time\"):\n  - C state allowed in a static's type, a C constant macro's result and a `@constant` constructor's literal;\n  - `@constant` functions whose result holds C state compile-time only;\n  - each static C string emitted as a `char` array of its own, because CosmoOS builds with `-Wwrite-strings`;\n  - the emitter now counts a box's construction (allocator, `@placed` methods) and a taken function address as\n    reaching library code, which lib/kernel's boxed locks and `KRef` need;\n  - lib/kernel's own composite types are emitted only when used, as lib/std's are.\n- **Trusted packages:** `trusted = true` in `nova.toml` (`PackageInfo.Trusted`) admits `@threadSafe`.\n- **Found in the implementation:**\n  - CosmoOS exports to modules neither `arch_irq_save`/`restore` (they inline to `arch_irq_*_hw`) nor\n    `preempt_enable`. So `IrqGuard`, `PreemptGuard` and `PerCpu` are for code built into the kernel. Locks, kernel\n    objects and the heap work in modules too.\n  - `ObjectCache<T>` is **not built**. Moving a value that is not `Copy` into raw memory has no primitive (`p.write`\n    needs `Copy`, §27.3), so a cache box cannot be filled. It needs a language addition (a move into raw memory, or a\n    stateful `Box` allocator) and is left open. **Resolved by [ADR 0027](0027-m14-static-mutex-and-object-cache.md)**, with a static `Mutex` below.\n  - **No static `Mutex`.** A CosmoOS mutex holds list heads that point to themselves. CosmoOS's static initialisers\n    take the variable's own name for that (`WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)`, `LIST_HEAD_INIT(name)`), and a C constant macro\n    is not given it. So §3.3's `MUTEX_INIT(name)` could not initialise one correctly, and it was not added to\n    CosmoOS. A static mutex needs initialisers that know their own address (a §39 extension), or a lock made at\n    start-up. It is left open. Static spinlocks are not affected: `SPINLOCK_INIT` has no self-pointers.\n- **Tests:**\n  - Nova's suite: `check-pass/kernel-library.nova`, `check-fail/kernel-library.nova` (sleeping under a static spin\n    guard, with interrupts off), `check-fail/kernel-library-constant.nova` (N0338), and `KernelLibraryTests` (trusted\n    packages, the manifest keys, the library absent unless selected).\n  - `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` (2026-10-29, both architectures, one file and split): the module subset\n    (`k3/module.nova`) meets C4's module checks against CosmoOS's headers and exports. The whole library compiles as\n    kernel code; its only kernel-internal references are `arch_irq_save_hw`/`arch_irq_restore_hw`.\n  - **In a booted kernel** (2026-10-29, CosmoOS #489): the self-test `nova-kcore` (CosmoOS\n    `kernel/nova/kcore_test.nova`, built with novac 0.2.0) uses every type but `ObjectCache` as code built into the\n    kernel: a table under a static `SpinLock`, a static `IrqSpinLock`, a `PerCpu` counter, boxed locks, a `KRef`'s\n    clone and release, a grace period and a deferred free. It passes in debug boots (lockdep on) on AArch64 and\n    x86-64. It needed CosmoOS's lockdep class table raised ",
    128 =>
        "from 512 to 640: main was at about 500, and the test's\n    classes overflowed it later in an x86-64 boot.\n  - **Static analysis.** CosmoOS's analyzer gate (`make analyze`, clang `--analyze`) reports \"Potential memory leak\"\n    for each `Box.create` in a `KernelHeap`. They are false: each box frees its block on drop. CosmoOS #489 baselines\n    the four the test makes. The cause, reduced in plain C (2026-10-29, Apple clang 21): `KernelHeap.allocate`\n    returns `Ok(p)`, which the emitter writes as a compound literal with a union member (`(R){ .tag = 0,\n    .u.v_Ok = { .f__0 = p } }`), and the analyzer loses the pointer read back from that union. The same value built\n    by member stores (`r.u.v_Ok.f__0 = p; return r;`) is tracked, and so is a plain struct.\n  - **Fixed in the emitter** (2026-10-29, after 0.2.0; c-backend §2): a variant whose payload may hold a pointer is\n    built by a `static inline` constructor of member stores (`nvMk_<E>_v_<variant>`); scalar payloads keep the\n    compound literal. CosmoOS's `kcore_test.nova` analyzes clean on both architectures with it (4 reports with\n    0.2.0). A first version used constructors for every payload: gcc then ran `string-building` 1.8× slower, with\n    a constructor only on its cold growth path (inferred: inlining). With constructors for pointer payloads only,\n    the suite on the Ryzen box (Nova, both compilers, interleaved, two rounds) moves within its noise (±6% where the\n    C did not change at all), except memory-copy under clang, 1.10×, whose only change runs once outside the loop\n    and which is 1.03× with `-falign-loops=32` (code placement, inferred). CosmoOS's baseline entries can go once\n    its pin moves past 0.2.0.\n\n",
    129 =>
        "# ADR 0026 — M14 K5: inline atomics, and atomics in every profile\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0026, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), both questions as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: OD-27 (M14 K5's first item). How the atomic types' operations reach the machine, and which profiles have\n  them.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n- **Today** (ADR 0016, ADR 0021): `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool` and `Ordering` are in\n  `lib/std`, a hosted-only library. Each operation calls a runtime function in `runtime/hosted/nova_rt_thread.c`\n  (`nova_atomic_add_u64`, `nova_atomic_load_ord_u32`, …). The ordered ones take the ordering as an `int` and switch\n  on it.\n- **The cost of the call** (ADR 0021 §1, `benchmarks/atomics`, Ryzen box, x86-64): the ring with acquire/release takes\n  0.289 s through the runtime calls and 0.158 s written in C with inline `__atomic_*`, a further 1.8×. Sequentially\n  consistent inline C was no faster than the calls (2.761 s against 2.170 s), because the fence dominates.\n- **The kernel has no Nova atomics.** `lib/std` is not in a freestanding build, and `lib/kernel` (ADR 0025) has none.\n  Roadmap 2.0's SA track converts CosmoOS's C, which uses the GCC builtins directly: 1,233 `__atomic_*` calls in 98\n  files (`__atomic_load_n` 593, `__atomic_store_n` 393, `__atomic_fetch_add` 141, …; counted 2026-10-29 in\n  `kernel/` and `drivers/`). A converted subsystem needs atomics of its own.\n- **No library calls.** CosmoOS builds AArch64 code with `-mno-outline-atomics`, and so does Nova's freestanding\n  profile (`profiles.md` §4.3). With it, gcc and clang expand every 8-, 16-, 32- and 64-bit `__atomic_*` on\n  both architectures inline, with no `libatomic` or `__aarch64_*` helper (inferred from CosmoOS's own use; checked\n  per architecture by the implementation, below). MSVC has the `_Interlocked*` intrinsics, already used by the\n  runtime.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Keep the calls | B. Inline in the runtime header | C. Built-in operations in the emitter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| How | as today | the `nova_atomic_*` functions become `static inline` in `rt.h`; the ordering switch folds away where the ordering is a constant | the emitter writes `__atomic_*` with the ordering constant at each call site |\n| Compiler change | none | none: the runtime and its header only | a new intrinsic kind in NIR and the emitter |\n| An ordering not known at compile time | works | works: the switch stays | needs a fallback call |\n| `-O0` | a call | a call to the inline function, with its switch | inline |\n| Gain at `-O2` | — | as C (expected; to be measured) | as C |\n\nB gives what C gives at `-O2`, where the ordering at a call site is a constant (it is in every `…With` call written\nwith an enum constant, after inlining), with no change to the compiler.\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n1. **B: inline atomics in the runtime header.** `rt.h` defines the `nova_atomic_*` functions as `static inline`, with\n   `__atomic_*` (gcc and clang) or `_Interlocked*` (MSVC), and `nova_rt_thread.c` loses them. Nothing else in the\n   generated C changes.\n2. **Atomics in every profile.** `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool` and `Ordering` move from\n   `lib/std` to `lib/core`, so freestanding and kernel code has them, with the same API (type-system §38.3). Their\n   `Send`/`Share` argument (`@threadSafe`) is unchanged. `Arc`, `Mutex` and `Thread` stay in `lib/std`: they need\n   the platform's threads and allocator.\n3. **The freestanding profile's rule is checked.** A test compiles a program using every atomic operation with the\n   freestanding profile's flags ",
    130 =>
        "for both CosmoOS architectures and requires no undefined symbol (as C4's checks do,\n   `c4.py`).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- `lib/core`'s names are reserved in every package: a freestanding package that declares its own `AtomicU64` stops\n  compiling (none of CosmoOS's four ports does).\n- The kernel's Nova code uses the same atomics as userland Nova code, compiled as CosmoOS's C compiles its own.\n  `lib/kernel`'s future reference counts and flags use them.\n- `benchmarks/atomics` is rerun on the Ryzen box after the change: the Nova acquire/release row against the C inline\n  row, and the sequentially consistent rows, under clang and gcc. The result goes into §6 and `docs/benchmarks.md`.\n  If the gap to C does not close, C (built-in operations) is reconsidered.\n- **Not decided here:** atomics on pointers and on 8- and 16-bit integers (add as converted code needs them), and\n  fences as a library function (`atomicFence(order)`), which CosmoOS's C uses 22 times.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Inline atomics through the runtime header (B)? (Recommended.)\n2. Move the atomic types and `Ordering` to `lib/core`, so kernel code has them? (Recommended.)\n\n## 6. Implementation and results (2026-10-29)\n\n- `nova/rt.h` defines the `nova_atomic_*` functions as `static inline`: `__atomic_*` with constant orderings in a\n  switch (gcc, clang), `_Interlocked*` intrinsics (MSVC). `nova_rt_thread.c` no longer has them, and a program that\n  uses only atomics no longer links it. The interpreter is unchanged (`CLibrary.cs`).\n- `lib/core/atomics.nova` has `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool`, `LoadOrdering`, `StoreOrdering`\n  and `Ordering`, moved from `lib/std/sync.nova` unchanged (doc comments added where lib/core requires them).\n  Type-system §38.3 and §38.5 say so.\n- The atomics' `Result` types would have been emitted in every program, because a generic instance made by lib/core's\n  own code counted as the program's. Now an instance made only by a library's code (lib/core, as lib/std and\n  lib/kernel already were) is emitted only where the program uses it. The `emit-c` snapshots did not change.\n- **Freestanding** (§3.3): `integrations/cosmoos/k3/atomics.nova` calls every operation of every type, with every\n  ordering kind, from statics. `k3.py` compiles it with CosmoOS's module flags on both architectures, one file and\n  split: the only undefined symbol is the kernel's `panic`, so no operation calls a helper. On AArch64 the object\n  holds the expected instructions: `ldaxr`/`stlxr` for acquire-release and sequentially consistent read-modify-writes,\n  `ldxr`/`stxr` for relaxed ones, `ldar` and `stlr` for loads and stores.\n- **Results.** `benchmarks/atomics` on the Ryzen box (as ADR 0021 §6: pinned to CPUs 1 and 2, median of 9,\n  interleaved; load average 0.7–2.7):\n\n  | | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Nova, acquire/release, through calls (ADR 0021 §6) | 0.366 s | 0.442 s |\n  | **Nova, acquire/release, inline** | **0.215 s** | **0.195 s** |\n  | C, acquire/release through calls | 0.296 s | 0.297 s |\n  | C, acquire/release inline | 0.160 s | 0.157 s |\n  | Nova, sequentially consistent | 2.508 s | 2.584 s |\n  | C, sequentially consistent inline | 2.816 s | 2.116 s |\n\n  The ordered ring is 1.7× (clang) and 2.3× (gcc) faster than through calls, and within 1.34× and 1.24× of inline C.\n  Nova's ring is now faster than C's through calls. Sequentially consistent rings are dominated by their fences, as\n  before, and vary by tens of percent between runs. Option C (built-in operations) is not needed for this gap: what\n  remains is the ring's own code (inferred: the indexing and its checks around the atomics), not the atomics.\n\n",
    131 =>
        "# ADR 0027 — M14: a static `Mutex`, and `ObjectCache<T>`\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0027, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-29), both questions as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: the two parts of ADR 0025's v1 API (§3.3) that §7 left open: a `Mutex<T>` in a `static` item, and\n  `ObjectCache<T>`, a typed slab cache. Both need a language addition. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 needs both for SA2\n  (object caches) and SA3 (locks and waits in statics).\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 A static mutex\n\n- **The C.** `struct mutex` (CosmoOS `kernel/include/kernel/mutex.h:20`) holds a spinlock, an owner, a\n  `struct waitqueue`, a name, a lockdep class, `pi_active`, a `struct list_node pi_link` and `handoff`. `mutex_init`\n  (`kernel/scheduler/mutex.c:13`) makes `pi_link` and the wait queue's two lists point to themselves\n  (`list_init`, `waitqueue_init`).\n- **CosmoOS's own static initialisers** for such state take the object's own name: `LIST_HEAD_INIT(name)` is\n  `{ &(name), &(name) }` and `WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)` uses `(name).waiters` (`list.h:21`, `wait.h:58`). There is no\n  `MUTEX_INIT`.\n- **Nova's statics** (type-system §39.4) build C state only through a C constant macro whose arguments are static\n  initialisers. None of those can name the static being defined, so a self-pointing list cannot be written. Static\n  spin locks work because `SPINLOCK_INIT` has no self-pointers (ADR 0025 §7).\n- **SA3 needs more of the same:** wait queues, completions and list heads in statics all point to themselves.\n\n### 1.2 An object cache\n\n- **The C.** `kmem_cache_create(name, object_size, align)` allocates and returns a cache, or NULL;\n  `kmem_cache_alloc(cache, flags)` and `kmem_cache_free(cache, obj)` (`kernel/include/kernel/kmalloc.h:47-53`).\n  CosmoOS creates its caches at start-up into globals (`kernel-services/network/mbuf.c:26-27`, `kmalloc.c:43`).\n- **What Nova lacks:**\n  - **Moving a value into raw memory.** `p.write(v)` and `p.read()` are defined only for `Copy` types (§27.3, N0282).\n    A cache's objects are generally not `Copy` (they own boxes, or have a `drop`), so a cache box cannot be filled\n    or emptied.\n  - **A type's size and alignment as a value.** `kmem_cache_create` needs them. The compiler knows them\n    (`TargetInfo`), and `Box` passes them to an allocator as a `Layout`, but Nova code cannot ask for one.\n- **Why not `Box<T, A>`:** a box's allocator is stateless (C5, §30), and a cache is state. A stateful allocator would\n  make every box carry its allocator: two pointers instead of one, through placement, niche optionals and drop glue.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### 2.1 Static mutex\n\n| | A. `here`: a constant initialiser may take the address it initialises | B. Lazy: a `StaticMutex<T>` that runs `mutex_init` at its first lock | C. None: mutexes made at start-up |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | one new static-initialiser form | none | none |\n| CosmoOS | a `MUTEX_INIT_AT(p, name)` macro in `mutex.h` | none | none |\n| Run-time cost | none | an acquire load per lock, and a spinlock for the first | an allocation and a pointer per lock |\n| Also serves | wait queues, completions, list heads (SA3) | mutexes only | — |\n\n### 2.2 Object cache\n\n| | A. Moves through raw pointers, `Layout.of<T>()`, and a library cache | B. Stateful `Box` allocators | C. Wait |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | lift `Copy` from `read`/`write` (unsafe); one built-in | `Box<T, A>` carries `A`'s state | — |\n| Size of a cache box | two pointers (object, cache) | two pointers for every box with a stateful allocator | — |\n| Reach | any unsafe container code (rings, intrusive lists) | caches",
    132 =>
        " and other stateful allocators | — |\n\n## 3. Decision\n\n### 3.1 `here` (option A for the mutex)\n\n- **`here`** is a static initialiser (§39.4) valid only as an argument of a C constant macro whose result\n  initialises a place. Its value is that place's address, and its type is `*mut T` for the place's type `T`. In\n  ```nova\n  @constant\n  public fn constant(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Mutex<T> => Mutex { raw: MUTEX_INIT_AT(here, name), data: data };\n  ```\n  `here` is the address of the field `raw` in whichever static the constructor builds. Through nested `@constant`\n  constructors the place is the full path (`&nvS_TABLE.f_lock.f_raw`).\n- **C.** It lowers to an address constant (C11 §6.6p9: `&`, `.`, `->` and `*` may form one, and no object is read),\n  so the macro may build self-pointers from it: `LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.waiters)` expands to\n  `{ &((p)->wq.waiters), &((p)->wq.waiters) }`.\n- **Rules.** `here` elsewhere is N0338. A `@constant` function that uses it is compile-time only (§39.4, as for C\n  state), so it is never called at run time, where there is no fixed place. The interpreter cannot evaluate C\n  constant macros already (§39.5), so nothing changes there.\n- **lib/kernel.** `Mutex<T>.constant(name, data)` with CosmoOS's new macro:\n  ```c\n  /* A mutex in static storage, as mutex_init leaves one; p is its own address. */\n  #define MUTEX_INIT_AT(p, n) { .lock = SPINLOCK_INIT(n), .owner = NULL, \\\n      .wq = { .lock = SPINLOCK_INIT(n), .waiters = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.waiters), \\\n              .callbacks = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->wq.callbacks) }, \\\n      .name = (n), .class = 0, .pi_active = 0, .pi_link = LIST_HEAD_INIT((p)->pi_link), .handoff = NULL }\n  ```\n  A CosmoOS self-test compares a static mutex made by it with one made by `mutex_init`, member by member, and locks\n  it under lockdep. A change to `struct mutex` must keep the two equal, as for `SPINLOCK_INIT`.\n\n### 3.2 Moves through raw pointers, `Layout.of<T>()`, `ObjectCache<T>` (option A for the cache)\n\n- **`p.write(v)` and `p.read()` for any `T`** (§27.3; unsafe, as now):\n  - `p.write(v)` moves `v` to `p`, without reading or destroying what was there, for `p: *mut T`. A `Copy` `v` is\n    copied, as now. For a type with a `@placed init` (§33.3) the store is followed by `init` at `p`, as\n    `Box.create` does.\n  - `p.read()` moves the `T` at `p` out. The memory then holds no value: the unsafe code must not read it again,\n    drop it or let anything else do so before writing it (U-rules, `unsafe.md`). For `Copy` `T`, as now. For a type\n    with C state (§33), `read` is N0282: C state never moves.\n- **`Layout.of<T>()`**, a `@constant` built-in: `T`'s size and alignment from `TargetInfo` (in C,\n  `sizeof`/`_Alignof` of its C type). It is a static initialiser too.\n- **lib/kernel** (ADR 0025's API, §3.3 there):\n  - `ObjectCache<T: Send>`, `@threadSafe`: `constant()` (empty, for a static), `setUp(name) -> Result<void,\n    AllocError>` (once, at start-up: `kmem_cache_create(name, Layout.of<T>().size, Layout.of<T>().align)`, stored\n    atomically), and `alloc(value) -> Result<CacheBox<T>, AllocError>` (`kmem_cache_alloc`, then `p.write(value)`;\n    on failure the value is dropped, as `Box.create` does). `alloc` before `setUp` is `Err(OutOfMemory)`.\n  - `CacheBox<T>`, `@noncopy`, two pointers: `get`, `getMut`, and a `drop` that reads the value out, drops it and\n    returns the memory (`kmem_cache_free`). It is `Send` when `T` is.\n  - A cache in a box, not a static, would let its boxes outlive it. So v1 has static caches only, the form CosmoOS\n    uses, and a cache is never destroyed.\n\n### 3.3 What changes elsewhere\n\n- Type-system §27.3 (`read`/`write`), §30.1 (`Layout.of<T>()` beside `Layout`), §39.4 (`here`, and `Layout.of` as\n  a static initialiser), `unsafe.md` (the moved-out state) and ownership.md's U-rules.\n- Diagnostics: no new codes. `here` misused is N0338, and `read` of C state is N0282.\n- Tests: corpus check-fail for `here` outside a macro argument, a run",
    133 =>
        "-time call of a function using it, and `read`\n  of C state; run-pass for `read`/`write` of a type with a drop (each dropped once, under ASan and the interpreter);\n  `k3.py` for a static `Mutex` and an `ObjectCache`; CosmoOS self-tests `nova-kcore` extended (in a later pin bump).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Static mutexes, and later static wait queues and completions, cost nothing at run time and are made as CosmoOS's\n  own C makes them, with lockdep's classes from their names.\n- `read` and `write` of non-`Copy` values are new ways for unsafe code to duplicate or leak ownership. They are\n  unsafe, and each use needs its `/// Safety:` argument (U2). The safety-audit pack (`safety-audit.md`) gains them\n  for K6.\n- `ObjectCache<T>` objects are not `Box`es: code that takes a `Box<T, A>` does not take a `CacheBox<T>`.\n- **Not decided here:** destroying a cache (needs boxes scoped to their cache), stateful `Box` allocators, and\n  per-CPU object caches.\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-29)\n\n- **Moves through raw pointers** (type-system §27.3, `unsafe.md` U2): `p.read()` and `p.write(v)` take any `T`; the\n  volatile forms still copy. The NIR move checker lets a value leave `*p` for a raw `p` (`NirPlace.IsRawDeref`), the\n  drop elaborator does not drop the old contents of `*p`, and a write of a type with `@placed` methods is followed\n  by `PtrPlace`, emitted as its `nvPL_` helper (the in-place checker accepts the literal there, as for `Box.create`).\n  `p.read()` of a Nova struct holding C state is N0282.\n- **`layoutOf<T>()`** (§30.1), not `Layout.of<T>()`: Nova has no type arguments on a member call, so it is a function,\n  like `truncate<T>`. Folded from `TargetInfo` to a `Layout` literal; for a C-laid-out `T`, C's `sizeof`/`_Alignof`.\n- **`here`** (§39.4–§39.5): typed by the macro parameter it is given to, checked against the macro's result type,\n  emitted as the address of the place being initialised. lib/kernel's `Mutex<T>.constant` uses CosmoOS's\n  `MUTEX_INIT_AT`, which `integrations/cosmoos/k3/mutex_init_at.h` supplies to `k3.py` until CosmoOS's `mutex.h`\n  defines it.\n- **`ObjectCache<T>`** and **`CacheBox<T>`** (`lib/kernel/cache.nova`) as §3.2 says, with two differences found in\n  the implementation:\n  - `T` holds no C state in v1: a cache box moves its value out to drop it, and C state never moves. A drop in place\n    would lift this.\n  - `CacheBox<T>` is not `Send`, like a `KernelHeap` box: raw pointers are not `Send` (§38.1), and a `@threadSafe`\n    declaration would claim `Share` for any `T`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - run-pass `raw-moves.nova` and `layout-of.nova`, every C configuration and the interpreter;\n  - check-fail `raw-moves-c-state.nova`, `layout-of.nova` and `static-here.nova`; the changed\n    `raw-pointer-types.nova`, whose non-`Copy` `read`/`write` are now valid;\n  - emit-c `static-here.nova`: `MUTEX_INIT_AT((&nvS_TABLE.f_raw), …)`;\n  - `k3.py` (`k3/statics.nova`: a static `Mutex` and an `ObjectCache`), both architectures, one file and split.\n- **Found in a boot** (2026-10-29, novac 0.3.0, CosmoOS branch `novac-0.3.0`): `MUTEX_INIT_AT` matches `mutex_init`\n  member by member, but `ObjectCache.setUp` failed for `T` aligned below 16: CosmoOS's `kmem_cache_create` refuses an\n  alignment below `KMALLOC_MIN_ALIGN` (`slab.c`, `cache_setup`), and asks for that minimum with 0. lib/kernel now\n  passes 0 for alignments up to 16; `nova-kcore` then passes. `k3.py` only compiles, so it could not see this.\n- **Not yet run (superseded by the above):** a static mutex compared with `mutex_init`'s, and the cache, in a booted kernel. That is CosmoOS's\n  side, once a novac release carries this: `MUTEX_INIT_AT` in `mutex.h` and `nova-kcore` extended.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. A static mutex through `here` and a CosmoOS `MUTEX_INIT_AT` macro (A)? (Recommended; B needs no language change but\n   costs a check per lock and covers mutexes only.)\n2. Lift `Copy` from unsafe `p.read()`/`p.write(v)` and add `Layout.of<T>()`, for a lib/ker",
    134 => "nel `ObjectCache<T>` with\n   static caches only (A)? (Recommended.)\n",
    135 =>
        "# ADR 0028 — M15 S7: the host services a compiler needs\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0028, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), every question as recommended (§5). The wording is the agent's. Implemented the same day (§6).\n- Scope: M15 S7 (OD-35). These are the operating-system services `novac build` and its package commands use, which\n  Nova's hosted library (type-system §26) lacks: a compiler written in Nova (OD-33) cannot do without them.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nThe services novac itself uses (an audit of `src/`, 2026-10-30):\n\n| Service | Where novac uses it | Nova today (§26) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run a program and capture its stdout and stderr, optionally with stdin | the C compiler and linker (`Backend.C/CBackend.cs:252-290`); tool discovery (`CCompilers.cs:165`) | `Process.run`: inherited stdio, exit status only |\n| Read an environment variable | `CC`, `PATH`, `NOVA_HOME`, `NOVA_INDEX`, `INCLUDE` | none |\n| Create and list directories | `-o DIR` for `--emit c` (`Program.cs:586`, `:603`, `CBackend.cs:42`); `src/` of a package (`Manifest.cs:271`) | none |\n| Whether a path exists, and what it is | manifests, the cache, `fmt` on a directory | none (`File.open` and look at the error) |\n| A fresh temporary directory, removed afterwards | every `build` (`Program.cs:614`) | none |\n| The working directory | package lookup (`Manifest.FindDirectory`), source paths (`SourceText.SourcePath`) | none |\n\nThe CeeSafe port (P1, `host-process` probe) confirmed the rest works: writing a C file, running `cc` with an argument\nvector, its exit status, and reading the object back.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. Built-in library API in `lib/std` and §26 (proposed) | B. C adapters in each program | C. A generic `extern \"C\"` POSIX binding |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safety | safe API; the runtime owns the unsafe parts | every program writes unsafe FFI | unsafe everywhere, and POSIX only |\n| Portability | the runtime selects the platform (macOS, Linux, Windows, CosmoOS), as `Process.run` does (§26.6) | per program | none on Windows or CosmoOS |\n| Interpreter | modelled, and refused in the sandbox | runs no C (§28.6) | runs no C |\n\n## 3. Proposed decision: A\n\nHosted-only functions (N0293 in freestanding code), blocking, with errors as `IoError`:\n\n- `Process.capture(program, arguments, input) -> Result<ProcessOutput, IoError>`.\n  - `program: StringView` is found on `PATH`; `arguments: borrow Vector<StringView>`; `input: Slice<u8>?` is written to\n    the child's stdin, which is then closed (`null`: an empty stdin).\n  - `ProcessOutput` is a built-in struct with `status: i32`, as `Process.run` reports it, plus `stdout: Vector<u8>`\n    and `stderr: Vector<u8>`.\n  - The runtime reads both pipes and writes stdin concurrently (`poll` on POSIX, overlapped pipes on Windows), so a\n    child that fills one pipe cannot deadlock it.\n  - Failures: allocation is `OutOfMemory`; the program not found is `NotFound`; arguments as for `Process.run`.\n- `Environment.variable(name: StringView) -> StringView?`: the value as static text, like `Process.argument` (§26.1).\n  It is `null` if absent or not valid UTF-8. Nova has no way to set a variable, so the text never changes under the\n  program.\n- `Directory.create(path) -> Result<void, IoError>` (one level; `AlreadyExists` if it exists) and\n  `Directory.remove(path)` (an empty directory).\n- `Directory.list(path) -> Result<Vector<String>, IoError>`: the entries' names, without `.` and `..`, **sorted by\n  byte value**, so programs stay deterministic (c-backend §1's rule applied to I/O).\n- `Path.kind(path) -> Result<PathKind?, IoError>`: `File`, `Directory` or `Other`, or `",
    136 =>
        "null` if nothing is there. It\n  follows symbolic links.\n- `Directory.temporary() -> Result<String, IoError>`: a new, empty, private directory, created atomically (`mkdtemp`)\n  under the platform's temporary directory. The caller removes it.\n- `Directory.current() -> Result<String, IoError>`.\n\nPaths are `StringView`s, under the same rules as `File.open`: zero bytes and paths over 4,095 bytes are `InvalidInput`.\nThe interpreter models these with .NET's APIs on macOS and Linux, and the sandbox (`novac mcp`, `nova_run`) refuses\nthem all, as it refuses processes today.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- Spec: type-system §26.7–§26.10, diagnostics (none new expected), and the runtime in `runtime/hosted/nova_rt_io.c`\n  (POSIX and Windows) with CosmoOS's variants selected at compile time.\n- Tests:\n  - run-pass and run-fail programs for each function (a child writing more than a pipe's buffer to both streams;\n    absent variables; sorted listing; temporary-directory cleanup);\n  - `InterpreterTests` and `McpTests` for the sandbox;\n  - the MSVC configuration for Windows;\n  - `c3.py` in a CosmoOS guest for the spawn path.\n- A compiler written in Nova (the CeeSafe port) can then implement `build` and package mode without C adapters.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. Option A, with this API? (Recommended.)\n2. `Environment.variable` as a `StringView?` of static text, rather than a copied `String`? (Recommended: it matches\n   `Process.argument`, and nothing can change the environment.)\n3. `Directory.list`: should a name that is not valid UTF-8 make the call fail with `InvalidInput`, or be skipped?\n   (Recommended: fail. Skipping could make a compiler silently miss a source file.)\n4. CosmoOS: implement `Process.capture` there in 0.4.0 (its spawn API needs pipe redirection; to be audited in\n   CosmoOS's code), or report `Other` until a later release? (Recommended: audit first, and decide with the evidence.)\n5. Windows: full parity in the same release? (Recommended: yes. novac's own Windows builds depend on these services.)\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\n- **Q4 (CosmoOS), from the audit.** CosmoOS's libc (`libc/include/spawn.h`, `unistd.h`, `dirent.h`, `sys/stat.h`,\n  `stdlib.h`) has `spawnvp` with `spawn_handle` mappings, plus `pipe`, `getenv`, `getcwd`, `opendir`/`readdir`, `stat`,\n  `mkdir` and `rmdir`, but no `poll` and no `mkdtemp`. So `Process.capture` is implemented there in this release.\n  On every platform it routes the child's streams through files in a private temporary directory, not pipes, so\n  nothing needs `poll` or threads to avoid a deadlock. The temporary directory is made by trying names until `mkdir`\n  succeeds, which is atomic on every platform.\n- **One name changed.** `Path.kind` is `File.kind`. A lib/std `Path` would break programs that declare their own:\n  lib/std names cannot be redeclared in a hosted package (§38.5), and the corpus's `run-pass/struct-nested.nova` has\n  one. `Environment`, `Directory`, `PathKind`, `ProcessOutput` and `HostServices` matched nothing in the repository,\n  CeeSafe or the site's guide.\n- **Where it lives:**\n  - `lib/std/host.nova`, with its unsafe code behind documented contracts;\n  - ten `nova_host_*` functions in `runtime/hosted/nova_rt_io.c`, for POSIX, CosmoOS and Windows, declared in `rt.h`;\n  - two checker hooks, so `Process.capture` and `File.kind` call lib/std's `HostServices`;\n  - the interpreter's models in `Interpreter/HostServices.cs`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `run-pass/host-services.nova`, run in every C configuration and in the interpreter;\n  - `check-fail/host-services.nova`;\n  - `HostServicesTests`, where capture runs natively and in the interpreter with a child writing 300,000 bytes to\n    each stream, and the sandbox refuses.\n- **CosmoOS, in a guest.** `c3.py --ssh` builds `integrations/cosmoos/c3/c3-host.nova` for CosmoOS and runs it.\n  The first run found that CosmoOS's `S_ISDIR`/`S_ISREG` test the stat record's `st_type` (a `DT_*` value), not\n  `st_mode` (its `sys/stat.h`). The run",
    137 =>
        "time selects `st_type` there.\n- **Not yet run:** the Windows `CreateProcess` path. The CI's Windows job builds and runs the run corpus with MSVC,\n  which covers everything but capture; `HostServicesTests` covers capture, but not on Windows.\n\n",
    138 =>
        "# ADR 0029 — M14: exported structs that hold C state\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0029, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), both questions as recommended (§5): option A, with arrays in this ADR. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: a struct defined in Nova and used by C by value, whose fields include C state (a `spinlock_t`) and fixed\n  arrays. CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 SA2 needs it for the handle table; the owner chose Nova-owned types for SA2\n  (2026-10-30), and SA3 and later conversions need it for every C struct that embeds a lock.\n\n## 1. Context\n\n### 1.1 The handle table\n\n- **The C.** `struct handle_table` (CosmoOS `kernel/include/kernel/handle.h:86`) is a `spinlock_t`, an array of 64\n  `struct handle_entry { struct kobject *obj; unsigned rights; unsigned flags; }`, two counts and a `bool`.\n  `handle.c` (266 lines) is its only code apart from three writes of `limit` in `process.c` (`:965`, `:1211`,\n  `:2415`).\n- **C makes the values.** The table is embedded by value in `struct process` (`process.h:180`), and self-tests put\n  it in statics and on the stack (`epolltest.c:112`, `:447`, `:530`, `:627`, `:660`; `memtest.c:994`). It is\n  initialised in place by `handle_table_init(t)` and torn down by `handle_table_destroy(t)`. Nova code would only\n  ever see it through a `*mut handle_table` from C.\n- **SA2 so far.** The kobject layer is Nova over C's `struct kobject` (CosmoOS #493): an *imported* struct, which\n  works today. The owner's choice for SA2 was that the types themselves become Nova's, so the definition\n  `struct handle_table { … }` must come from Nova's generated header, not from `handle.h`.\n\n### 1.2 What Nova allows today\n\n- **`@repr(C)` structs** (type-system §28.5) cross to C and are defined in `nova_exports.h`, but their fields may only\n  be integers, `bool`, raw pointers and other `@repr(C)` structs. A `spinlock_t` field is N0291 (checked with novac\n  0.4.0, 2026-10-30: \"a @repr(C) struct cannot hold a 'spinlock_t'\"), and so is an array field (§28.3: arrays do not\n  cross).\n- **C state by value** (§33, C7b) is allowed in a *Nova* struct, which then becomes C-laid-out (§33.1): the C\n  compiler lays it out, it is never moved (§33.2), and it is made only by `Box.create` with a `@placed`\n  initialiser (§33.3). Such a struct is `struct nvT_S` in `main.c` only: C code cannot name it.\n- **Arrays in imported structs** are allowed (ADR 0014, §31): `f: [E; N]` is seen as Nova's array struct over the C\n  member, with no copy.\n\nSo each half exists: C-laid-out structs inside Nova, and C-visible structs without C state. Nothing joins them.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n| | A. `@repr(C)` may hold C state and arrays | B. A new `@cstruct` kind | C. Keep the type C's |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Language | lift two N0291 cases of §28.5; §33's rules apply as they stand | a third struct kind with its own rules | none |\n| Who defines `struct handle_table` | Nova (`nova_exports.h`) | Nova | C (`handle.h`), imported by Nova |\n| SA2's \"Nova-owned types\" | yes | yes | no: the code is Nova, the type C's |\n| New rules to learn | none: a `@repr(C)` struct with C state is C-laid-out, as any struct with C state is | a kind that is both | none |\n| Later conversions (SA3: `struct mutex`, wait queues, `struct process`) | the same rule | the same | each type stays C's |\n\n## 3. Decision: A\n\n### 3.1 Fields\n\nA `@repr(C)` struct's fields may also be:\n\n- **an imported C type by value** (§31), such as `spinlock_t`. The struct is then C-laid-out (§33.1);\n- **an array `[E; N]`** whose element is an integer, `bool`, a raw pointer as in §28.3, a `@repr(C)` struct, or an\n  imported type. Inside ",
    139 =>
        "Nova the field is Nova's array; in C it is the member `CE f[N]`.\n\nEvery other N0291 case stays: references, slices, boxes, vectors, optionals, results, non-`@repr` structs and\nenums, and `@repr` enums as fields. None of the allowed field types needs drop; in addition, a `@repr(C)` struct\nwith C state may not declare a `drop` method (N0291, new): C makes, embeds and discards these values without calling\nNova, so a drop would silently not run.\n\n### 3.2 A `@repr(C)` struct with C state\n\nIt is C-laid-out (§33.1), with every rule of §33 unchanged:\n\n- it is never moved, copied or assigned in Nova (N0294), and crosses to C only behind a pointer (`*T`, `*mut T`;\n  §28.3's by-value crossing is for `@repr(C)` structs without C state);\n- Nova makes one only through `Box.create` with its `@placed` initialiser (§33.2–§33.3), or in a static (§39.4);\n- C makes one wherever C makes a struct (embedded, static, on the stack). Nova code that receives a pointer to\n  one from C relies on C having initialised it, as with any pointer from C: the exported function that takes it\n  says so in its `/// Safety:` comment (§27.4).\n\n### 3.3 C\n\n- **`nova_exports.h`** defines the struct as `struct S { … }` with members named as in Nova, after the `@header`\n  includes that declare its imported member types (it includes them already, c-backend §2.3), and after the\n  `@repr(C)` structs it contains. There is no layout assertion, as for every C-laid-out type: the C compiler lays\n  it out, and `layoutOf<T>()` gives C's `sizeof`/`_Alignof` (ADR 0027).\n- **Array members** are `CE f[N];` with `N` written as a number. A C header that also names the length (CosmoOS's\n  `HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE`) checks it with a `_Static_assert` beside its `#include`. In `main.c` a field place is the\n  member seen as Nova's array struct, `(*(nvA<N>_<E> *)(uintptr_t)&p->f)`, as for ADR 0014's imported arrays, after\n  an assertion that the member and the array struct have one size and alignment (the member's type needs no\n  `_Generic` check: `main.c` defines it).\n- **`main.c`** uses `struct S` for it, as for every `@repr(C)` struct, so both translation units name one type\n  (C11 6.2.7).\n\n### 3.4 The interpreter\n\nC-laid-out types are already outside the interpreter (§33.4), and a `@repr(C)` struct with C state is one of\nthem. Arrays of `@repr(C)` elements in a `@repr(C)` struct without C state run in the interpreter as Nova arrays.\n\n### 3.5 For the handle table (CosmoOS, after a release)\n\n```nova\n@header(\"kernel/spinlock.h\", \"kernel/object.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @ctype(\"spinlock_t\")\n    struct spinlock_t;\n    struct kobject;\n}\n\nconst HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE: usize = 64;\n\n@repr(C)\nstruct handle_entry {\n    obj: *mut kobject; // null: free\n    rights: u32;\n    flags: u32;\n}\n\n@repr(C)\nstruct handle_table {\n    lock: spinlock_t;\n    entries: [handle_entry; 64];\n    count: u32;\n    limit: u32;\n    exiting: bool;\n}\n\n/// Safety: `t` points to storage for a table that nothing uses yet.\n@export(\"handle_table_init\")\nfn tableInit(t: *mut handle_table) { … }\n```\n\n`make nova` writes the generated header (`kernel/include/kernel/handle_types.h`, say), and `handle.h` includes it\nin place of its own definitions, with `_Static_assert(HANDLE_TABLE_SIZE == 64)`. `process.c` and the self-tests are\nunchanged: `unsigned` and `uint32_t` are the same type on both of CosmoOS's targets, and the C sees the same\nmembers. The entries hold `*mut kobject` and count their references by hand, in unsafe code, as the C does: a\n`KRef<kobject>` field would need drop glue, which C-made values never run (§3.1).\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SA2's handle table, and later SA3's locks and wait queues and `struct process` itself, can be Nova's types\n  without changing how C embeds them.\n- C code may write a Nova-owned struct's fields (`process.c` writes `limit`). Such a struct is plain data with C\n  state: Nova keeps no invariant in it that C could break without `unsafe` on the Nova side noticing, which is\n  already the rule for anything",
    140 =>
        " behind a pointer from C (unsafe.md U2).\n- No layout is checked for these structs in Nova; the C compiler is the authority, as for every C-laid-out type.\n  The member types are checked in both translation units by the shared definition.\n- **Tests.**\n  - The run corpus gets a C file that embeds a C-state `@repr(C)` struct by value in a C struct, initialises it\n    through an export, and uses its array.\n  - Compile-fail tests cover a move of one (N0294), a by-value parameter (N0294), a `drop` method on one (N0291),\n    and an array of a non-FFI type (N0291).\n  - `k3.py` compiles CosmoOS's header against the generated one.\n- **Release.** It needs a novac release (0.5.0) and a CosmoOS pin move before SA2's third PR.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **Which option?** A (recommended), B or C (§2).\n2. **Arrays in the same ADR?** Recommended: yes, since the handle table needs both. The alternative is a separate\n   ADR for array fields of `@repr(C)` structs.\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\n- **Checker** (`TypeChecker.IsFfiSafe`, the `@repr(C)` field loop): a field may be an imported type or an array of an\n  allowed element; a `drop` on a C-laid-out `@repr(C)` struct is N0291.\n- **C** (`CEmitter`): a `@repr(C)` struct's array field is the C array member in `main.c` and `nova_exports.h`, with\n  an assertion that it has the layout of Nova's array struct; places use it through the cast that ADR 0014's imported\n  arrays use (`IsCArrayMember`); a struct literal assigns its arrays after the compound literal\n  (`EmitConstruction`); a static's initialiser writes them as plain braces (`CArrayInitializer`). The header names an\n  imported member by its C spelling and defines no imported struct.\n- **Tests.**\n  - `ffi/exported-c-state.nova` with `.c` and `.types.h`, under every C configuration: C embeds `xs_table` (a lock, a\n    self-pointing list head, an array of `@repr(C)` entries) in its own struct, a static and a stack frame, writes a\n    field, and uses it through Nova's exports. `Grid`, a plain `@repr(C)` struct with nested arrays, is a value and a\n    static in Nova. The reviewed header is `ffi/exported-c-state.h`.\n  - `check-fail/c-state-types.nova`: a `drop` (N0291) and a by-value parameter (N0294).\n    `check-fail/ffi-declarations.nova`: array fields are allowed, and arrays of a non-`@repr` struct or a `@repr`\n    enum are N0291.\n",
    141 =>
        "# ADR 0030 — M14: what SA3 needs from Nova (one definition, `offsetOf`, the caller's address)\n\n> **Inherited from Nova.** This is Nova's ADR 0030, which CeeSafe inherited at the fork (Nova `v0.6.0`; CeeSafe's\n> Decision 0006). Its text is Nova's record and is not changed: read Nova and `novac` as the language and compiler\n> CeeSafe forked from, `.nova` as `.cee`, and Nova's C names as CeeSafe's (`spec/c-backend.md`). Links to documents\n> CeeSafe did not fork go to Nova's repository.\n\n- Status: **Accepted** (project owner, 2026-10-30), every question as recommended (§5): A, A, A. The wording is the agent's.\n- Scope: what CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 SA3 needs to rewrite `wait.c`, `completion.c`, `semaphore.c`, `mutex.c` and\n  `quiesce.c` in Nova with Nova-owned types (the owner's choice, 2026-10-30). SA3's host test, `tests/host/test_sync.c`\n  (CosmoOS #496), records the behaviour the Nova versions must reproduce.\n\n## 1. Context\n\nWhat the C uses, and where Nova stands. Each item was checked against novac 0.5.0 on 2026-10-30.\n\n### 1.1 The same struct, defined twice in one translation unit (blocks SA3)\n\n- **The include graph.** `thread.h` includes `completion.h`, which includes `wait.h`; `mutex.h`, `completion.h` and\n  `semaphore.h` include `wait.h`. Once `struct waitqueue` is Nova's (ADR 0029), `wait.h` gets it by including the\n  generated header, as `handle.h` does since SA2 (CosmoOS #494).\n- **The collision.** A Nova port that reads a thread's fields (`wait.nova` sets `thread->waiting_on`; the mutex reads\n  priorities) names `kernel/thread.h` in its `@header`. Its `main.c` then sees `struct waitqueue` twice: once through\n  `thread.h` → `completion.h` → `wait.h` → the generated header, and once in its own definition (c-backend §2.3:\n  `main.c` defines every `@repr(C)` struct). A second definition of a struct in one translation unit is an error\n  (C11 §6.7.2.3p1).\n- **Why SA2 did not meet it.** `handle.nova` names `spinlock.h` and `object.h`, and neither includes `handle.h`.\n- **The guard.** CosmoOS's `nova-gen.py` renames each generated header's guard `NOVA_EXPORTS_H` after the file's path,\n  so that two generated headers in one file do not hide each other. The guard is not the problem; the second\n  definition in `main.c` is.\n\n### 1.2 A struct from a pointer to its member (`container_of`)\n\n- **The C.** `list_for_each_entry(e, head, link)` and `container_of` recover a struct from its embedded `list_node`:\n  a wait entry from its `link`, and a mutex from its `pi_link` in the owner's `pi_held` list. The second member is not\n  at offset 0.\n- **Nova.** ADR 0009 §2 proposed `Ptr.containerOf` (unsafe). Its implementation note moved it to C7d, and C7d's\n  generated callbacks never needed it, so **it does not exist** (no match in `src/` or `lib/`). Nothing gives a field's\n  offset as a value either. `layoutOf<T>()` (ADR 0027) gives only size and alignment.\n\n### 1.3 The caller's address (lockdep)\n\n- **The C.** `mutex_lock`, `mutex_trylock`, `mutex_unlock`, `complete` and the completion waits pass\n  `(uintptr_t)__builtin_return_address(0)` to lockdep, which names the acquiring and releasing call sites in its\n  reports (`docs/kernel/lockdep/`, CosmoOS L21).\n- **Nova.** Nothing gives it. A Nova function's C is `static`, and the C compiler may inline it into its exported\n  wrapper, so even a raw `__builtin_return_address` in its body would name different frames depending on the\n  optimiser.\n\n### 1.4 What is not a gap (checked)\n\n- **Static inline functions of a header.** An `extern \"C\"` declaration of `list_init`, which `list.h` defines\n  `static inline`, compiles warning-free under CosmoOS's flags. The generated prototype `void list_init(struct\n  list_node *);` follows the inline definition, has no storage class, and so takes the definition's internal linkage\n  (C11 §6.2.2p4–5). `raw_this_cpu`, `list_*`, `wait_entry_init` and `thread_current` are all callable already. The\n  spec does not say so; §3.4 adds it.\n- **Function pointers i",
    142 =>
        "n a `@repr(C)` struct** (`wait_entry.fn`) and calls through them: type-system §29.5. The\n  generated typedef and C's `wait_callback_fn` name compatible types.\n- **Macros that are code** (`might_sleep`, `list_for_each_entry`, `wait_event`): `might_sleep` is three lines over\n  `raw_this_cpu()` and `lockdep_might_sleep`, which a port writes in Nova. A list walk is a loop over `next` once\n  §1.2 is solved. `wait_event` stays a C macro for C callers, over the exported functions.\n- **`panic`'s format** stays in a floor, as in SA2.\n\n## 2. Options\n\n### 2.1 One definition (§1.1)\n\n| | A. `main.c` includes its exports header | B. `@cdefined` structs | C. No Nova-owned types where the graph loops |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What changes | a `@repr(C)` struct is defined only in the exports header, which `main.c` includes; `--exports-header <path>` names the header (the `#include` and the guard) | an attribute marks a struct whose definition a `@header` brings, and `main.c` asserts its members instead of defining it | the type stays C's, imported (§31) |\n| The definition | one, generated, used by both | Nova's, but written in C's header by hand or by a script | C's |\n| `nova-gen.py` | passes `--exports-header kernel/include/kernel/wait_types.h`; no guard renaming | unchanged | unchanged |\n| SA3's choice of Nova-owned types | kept | kept, with two copies to keep in step | given up for waitqueue, completion and mutex |\n\n### 2.2 `container_of` (§1.2)\n\n| | A. `offsetOf(T, field)` | B. `Ptr.containerOf(p, T.field)` only | C. Keep list walks in a C floor |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Form | a built-in, `usize`, C's `offsetof` | an unsafe built-in for the one use | none |\n| Reach | `container_of`, and any other offset (an embedded member's address from its struct's) | `container_of` | — |\n\n### 2.3 The caller's address (§1.3)\n\n| | A. `callerAddress()` in an export | B. Floor wrappers | C. Report no address |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Form | a built-in, valid only in an `@export` function: the exported symbol's return address | each exported lock function is C that captures the address and calls a Nova function with it | lockdep gets 0 |\n| Lockdep reports | as today | as today | lose the call sites |\n| C per primitive | none | one wrapper per entry point (about a dozen) | none |\n\n## 3. Decision: A, A, A\n\n### 3.1 One definition\n\n- `novac build --exports-header <path>` names the exports header: `<path>` is the name C code includes, and the\n  guard is derived from it (`kernel/include/kernel/wait_types.h` → `KERNEL_INCLUDE_KERNEL_WAIT_TYPES_H`). Without it\n  the header is `nova_exports.h`, as today.\n- `main.c` (and, with `--split`, every unit) includes the exports header, after the `@header`s, and does not define\n  the `@repr(C)` structs itself. Their layout assertions follow the include, in `main.c`. A `@repr(C)` struct is then\n  defined once in every translation unit that sees it, however many headers bring it in, so a kernel header may\n  include the generated header and a Nova port may include that kernel header.\n- The header's `@header` includes come before its own definitions, as today (c-backend §2.3).\n\n### 3.2 `offsetOf(T, field)`\n\n- `offsetOf(T, f)` is a built-in whose value is the offset in bytes of field `f` of the struct type `T`, as `usize`.\n  `T` is a struct with a field `f` (a non-struct `T` or a missing field is N0339, new). For a C-laid-out `T`, or an\n  imported one, it is C's `offsetof`, which the C compiler computes (§33.1); otherwise it is the layout's offset, a\n  constant.\n- It is a static initialiser (§39.4), like `layoutOf<T>()`.\n- It is not `unsafe`: it reads no memory. Using it to recover a struct, as `(p as *mut u8).sub(offsetOf(T, f)) as\n  *mut T`, is raw-pointer arithmetic, which is (`unsafe.md` U2). lib/kernel adds `containerOf` and a list walk over\n  `list_node` on it (§3.5).\n\n### 3.3 `callerAddress()`\n\n- `callerAddress()` is a built-in of type `usize`, valid only in the body of an `@export` function (N0340 elsewhere,\n  new). Its ",
    143 =>
        "value is the address the exported symbol's call returns to: C's `__builtin_return_address(0)` taken in\n  the export's wrapper, which passes it to the function's body as a hidden argument. So it does not depend on\n  inlining. Nova calls of the same function get 0, since no C frame called them.\n- MSVC: `_ReturnAddress()` (c-backend §1.1); the interpreter gives 0.\n- It is not `unsafe`: it is a number, not a pointer one may follow.\n\n### 3.4 Static inline functions (spec only)\n\ntype-system §28.2 says what §1.4 found: an `extern \"C\"` function that a `@header` defines `static inline` may be\ndeclared and called. The generated prototype takes the definition's internal linkage (C11 §6.2.2p4).\n\n### 3.5 lib/kernel\n\n`lib/kernel/list.nova` binds CosmoOS's `struct list_node` and its inline helpers (`list_init`, `list_push_back`,\n`list_remove`, `list_empty`) and adds `containerOf` over `offsetOf`, with unsafe iteration that may remove the\ncurrent entry, as `list_for_each_entry_safe` does. `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` checks it against CosmoOS's headers\nwith the rest.\n\n## 4. Consequences\n\n- SA3's ports can be written with Nova-owned types and only the format of `panic` in C. Each primitive's exported\n  functions keep their C names and types, so the 669 call sites and the `*_INIT` macros are unchanged.\n- **`main.c` no longer defines `@repr(C)` structs.** A build with `--emit c` must keep the exports header beside\n  `main.c`, as it already writes it. FFI tests check that every C configuration still compiles.\n- **Tests.**\n  - The run corpus gets a program whose `@header` includes its own exports header, which used to be a redefinition\n    error.\n  - `offsetOf` gets tests on a Nova struct, a C-laid-out one and an imported one, against C's `offsetof`.\n  - `callerAddress()` gets a test in an export called from C, comparing against the C caller's own\n    `__builtin_return_address`.\n  - Check-fail tests cover N0339 and N0340.\n- **Release.** It needs a novac release (0.6.0) before SA3's conversion PRs. The host test (#496) does not wait.\n\n## 5. Questions for the owner\n\n1. **One definition (§2.1):** A (recommended), B or C?\n2. **Offsets (§2.2):** A, `offsetOf(T, field)` (recommended), B or C?\n3. **The caller's address (§2.3):** A, `callerAddress()` (recommended), B or C?\n\n## 6. Implementation (2026-10-30)\n\nTwo forms differ from §3, for the reason `layoutOf<T>()` differs from ADR 0027's `Layout.of<T>()`:\n- **`offsetOf<T>(field)`**, not `offsetOf(T, field)`: a type is no expression in Nova, so `T` is a type argument.\n- **`containerOf(node, offset)` returns `*mut void`**, which the caller casts, and is not generic: the struct that\n  embeds a node is usually C-laid-out, and no type argument may be (type-system §33.1). It is a top-level function of\n  lib/kernel, since Nova has no type arguments on a member call.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **One definition** (`CEmitter`): `HeaderStructs` is the set the exports header defines (the `@repr(C)` structs and the\n  Nova structs they hold); `main.c` includes the header after the `@header`s and skips their definitions, keeping the\n  layout assertions; `--exports-header` (`CEmitOptions.ExportsHeader`) names the header and its guard.\n- **`offsetOf`**: `TypeChecker.CheckOffsetOf`, a literal for a Nova layout, `BuiltinFunction.OffsetOf` (C's\n  `offsetof`) for a C-laid-out or imported type, in static initialisers too; an interpreter fault for the latter.\n- **`callerAddress()`**: `TypeChecker.CheckCallerAddress` marks the export (`HirFunction.UsesCallerAddress`); its C\n  gets a hidden last parameter `nv_caller`, which the export's wrapper fills with `nv_return_address()` (rt.h) and\n  Nova's calls and callback wrappers with 0. The interpreter gives 0.\n- **lib/kernel/list.nova**: `list_node`, `List.init`/`isEmpty`/`pushBack`/`pushFront`/`remove`/`first`/`next` over\n  list.h's inline helpers, and `containerOf`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `ffi/includes-exports.nova` (a `@header` that includes the program's own exports header; `callerAddre",
    144 =>
        "ss()` from\n    two C call sites at `-O0` and `-O2`, and 0 from Nova);\n  - `ffi/exported-c-state.nova` (`offsetOf` on a Nova layout, a C-laid-out struct, an imported one and in a static,\n    each equal to C's `offsetof`);\n  - `check-fail/offset-of.nova` and `check-fail/caller-address.nova`.\n\n  A walk over a list with removals was checked against CosmoOS's `list.h` on the host (2026-10-30: sum 15, the odd\n  nodes unlinked).\n",
    145 =>
        "# Diagnostics\n\nStatus: M1 increment 1a. Lists every diagnostic code the compiler can emit. Codes are stable:\na retired code is never reused. Source of truth in code: `DiagnosticCodes`\n(`src/diagnostics/catalog.cee`).\n\n## Format\n\n```text\n<path>:<line>:<column>: error[N0100]: expected ';', found 'let'\n  |\n2 |     let x = 1\n  |              ^\n```\n\n- `<path>` is the path as given on the command line (never made absolute), so output is reproducible.\n- Line and column are 1-based. Columns count UTF-16 code units (grammar.md §1.1).\n- Diagnostics are emitted in the order they are found. The lexer finishes before the parser starts,\n  so all lexical errors of a file come before its syntax errors.\n- `--diagnostics-format=json` gives the same diagnostics as one JSON document, with fixes, and `ceesafec explain\n  <code>` prints a code's row (M12; `tooling.md` §5).\n\n## Recovery policy\n\nThe parser reports **one** diagnostic per broken statement or item, then re-synchronises\n(grammar.md §3). The parse-fail corpus enforces this: each test lists exactly the expected\ndiagnostics, so any cascade fails the test.\n\n## Codes\n\n### Lexer (N0001–N0099)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0001 | Unexpected character (including non-ASCII outside literals and comments) | `let a = 1 # 2;` |\n| N0002 | *(unassigned)* | |\n| N0003 | Unterminated block comment | `/* ...` at EOF |\n| N0004 | Integer literal does not fit in 64 bits | `18446744073709551616` |\n| N0005 | Unterminated string literal (newline or EOF before `\"`) | `\"abc` |\n| N0006 | Invalid escape sequence, or `\\u{...}` that is not a Unicode scalar value | `\"\\q\"`, `\"\\u{D800}\"` |\n| N0007 | Invalid char literal: empty, more than one character, or unterminated | `''`, `'ab'` |\n| N0008 | Invalid digit for the radix, or no digits after a radix prefix | `0b102`, `0x` |\n| N0009 | Unknown literal suffix | `12abc`, `1f32` (float suffix needs a float literal) |\n| N0010 | Misplaced digit separator `_` (must sit between two digits) | `1__0`, `0x_FF`, `1_.5` |\n\n### Parser (N0100–N0199)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0100 | Expected a specific token | missing `;`, `(` after `if`, `}` before `fn` |\n| N0101 | Expected an item (`fn` or `const`) at the top level | `let x = 1;`, `struct S {}` (not in M1) |\n| N0102 | Expected an expression | `let a = ;` |\n| N0103 | Comparison operators chained | `a < b < c` |\n| N0104 | Invalid assignment target | `1 = 2;`, `f() = 3;` |\n| N0105 | Expected a type | `fn f(x: ) {}` |\n| N0106 | Namespace declaration not first in the file | `fn a() {}` then `namespace X;` |\n| N0107 | Expected a field or `fn` inside a struct body | `struct A { 42; }` |\n| N0108 | Expected a `match` pattern (`_`, a literal, or `E.V(…)`) | `match (n) { + => { } }` |\n| N0109 | An `import` declaration after an item | `fn a() {}` then `import geometry;` |\n| N0110 | Nested more than 1,000 levels deep (grammar.md §2.2: blocks, statements, types, patterns, unary operands, and each link of an operator, call or member chain). The rest of the file is not parsed | 1,000 nested parentheses; a sum of 1,001 terms |\n\n### Names, types and flow (N0200–N0299)\n\nSemantic checks run only when every file parsed without errors.\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0200 | Name not found (variable, function, or built-in member) | `missing`, `Console.print(1)` |\n| N0201 | Item defined twice in the package | two `fn twice()` |\n| N0202 | Parameter declared twice | `fn f(a: i32, a: i32)` |\n| N0203 | Local redeclares a local or parameter in scope (no shadowing of locals) | `let x = 1; let x = 2;` |\n| N0204 | Unknown type name | `fn f(a: int)` |\n| N0205 | Type mismatch against the expected type | `let a: bool = 1;`, `if (n) {}` |\n| N0206 | Integer literal out of range for its type | `256u8`, `let c: i8 = 128;` |\n| N0207 | Operator not applicable to the operand types (includes mixed types, signed bitwise ops, negating unsigned values) | `a + b` with `u8`/`i32`, `s & s` with `i32` |\n| N0208 | `as` con",
    146 =>
        "version that can lose information | `a as u32` with `a: i32` |\n| N0209 | Wrong number of arguments | `two(1)` |\n| N0210 | Not callable, or a function or built-in used as a value | `local()`, `let f = two;` |\n| N0211 | Assignment to an immutable binding (`let`, parameter, constant, function) | `param = 3;` |\n| N0212 | Local read before it is definitely assigned | `var x: i32; return x;` |\n| N0213 | End of a non-`void` function is reachable | `fn f() -> i32 { }` |\n| N0214 | `break`/`continue` outside a loop | `break;` at function level |\n| N0215 | `return` with or without a value contradicts the return type | `return 1;` in a `void` function |\n| N0216 | Construct not supported in M1 (string values, member access, qualified types, generic arguments other than `truncate<T>`; floats and `char` until M11) | `truncate<i32, u8>(x)` |\n| N0217 | Not a constant expression, or constant evaluation would panic | `const C: u8 = 200 + 100;` |\n| N0218 | Constant depends on itself | `const A: i32 = A + 1;` |\n| N0219 | Invalid `main` signature | `fn main(args: i32)` |\n| N0220 | `let` without initializer | `let a: i32;` |\n| N0221 | `var` without type annotation or initializer | `var c;` |\n| N0222 | A generic's substituted copy does not check for its type arguments, and no other error explains why (type-system §20.4). Today: `void` for a type parameter whose payload the body binds or builds, since `Result<void, E>.Ok` has none | `fn same<T>(r: Result<T, u8>) -> Result<T, u8> => match (r) { Ok(v) => Ok(v), Err(e) => Err(e) };` used with `Result<void, u8>` |\n| N0223 | *(unassigned)* | |\n| N0224 | Value of type `void` used as a value | `let d = nothing();` |\n| N0225 | Expression statement that is not a call | `1 + 2;` |\n| N0226 | `void` used other than as a return type | `var e: void;` |\n| N0227 | Struct contains itself by value (infinite size); reported at the field that closes the cycle | `struct Node { next: Node; }` |\n| N0228 | Duplicate member (field or function) in a struct | `struct D { a: i32; a: i64; }` |\n| N0229 | Struct literal does not initialise every field | `Point { x: 1 }` |\n| N0230 | Unknown field (in a literal or an access), or a function named where a field was expected | `p.z`, `p.sum` |\n| N0231 | Field initialised twice in a literal | `Point { x: 1, x: 2, y: 3 }` |\n| N0232 | `this` outside a method, or a `this` parameter outside a struct | `fn f() -> i32 => this;` |\n| N0233 | Method called as a static function, or a static function called on a value | `Point.sum()`, `p.origin()` |\n| N0234 | Field access, method call or literal on something that is not a struct | `n.x` with `n: i32` |\n| N0235 | A block branch of an `if` or `match` expression can complete, so it has no value (type-system.md §15.3) | `let a = if (c) { f(); } else 2;` |\n| N0236 | A closure whose type cannot be inferred: a parameter without a type, or a block body returning a value, where no function or closure type is expected (type-system.md §36.2) | `let f = (x) => x + 1;` |\n\n### References and borrows (N0240–N0249; `lifetimes.md`)\n\nN0240–N0245 come from the NIR borrow checker. It runs only on programs that type-check, and its\nerrors carry notes (\"borrowed here\", \"the borrow is used later here\").\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0240 | Assignment to a place while it is borrowed (shared or mutable) | `let r = borrow x; x = 2; use(r)` |\n| N0241 | Borrow conflicting with a live borrow | two live `borrow mut x`, or `borrow mut x` while `borrow x` is live |\n| N0242 | Read of a place while it is mutably borrowed | `let m = borrow mut x; print(x); m = 1;` |\n| N0243 | A place or temporary goes out of scope while borrowed | `let r = same(five()); use(r)` |\n| N0244 | Returning a reference to a local or temporary (E1) | `return borrow x;` with `x` local |\n| N0245 | Return-source errors (E2): missing or invalid `borrow(...)` list, or a result derived from an unlisted parameter | `fn f(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow T` |\n| N0246 | Reference in a disall",
    147 =>
        "owed position: struct field, constant, `var` local, reference to reference, source list outside a return type. Since M4a this covers every scoped type, scoped results included (`type-system.md` §19.1) | `struct S { r: borrow i64; }` |\n| N0247 | `borrow` of something that is not a place | `borrow 5`, `take(mut five())` |\n| N0248 | `borrow mut` (or a `borrow mut this` call) on an immutable place | `borrow mut param`, `shared.grow()` |\n| N0249 | `mut` argument marker missing or misplaced | `take(x)` for `borrow mut`, `look(mut x)` for `borrow` |\n\n### Moves and destruction (N0250–N0259; `type-system.md` §14)\n\nN0212 and N0250–N0253 come from the NIR move checker (since M2c), which also runs only on\nprograms that type-check. A move while borrowed is reported by the borrow checker as N0240.\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0250 | Use of a value that was moved (on at least one path), including reading a partially moved struct as a whole | `consume(r); consume(r);` |\n| N0251 | Move out of a place reached through a reference | `let a = r.a;` with `r: borrow Pair` and `Pair.a` a move type |\n| N0252 | Move of a field out of a value whose type has a `drop` block | `consume(o.res)` with `struct Owner { res: Res; drop {} }` |\n| N0253 | Move out of a `using` binding | `using u = make(); consume(u);` |\n| N0254 | `return`, `break`, `continue` or `defer` inside a deferred statement | `defer return 1;` |\n| N0256 | `match` that does not cover every value (add the missing variants or `_`) | `match (s) { Shape.Empty => { } }` |\n| N0257 | Unreachable `match` arm: a duplicate pattern, or any arm after `_` | `1 => { } 1 => { }` |\n| N0258 | *Retired in M3c:* a non-Copy payload bound from a value scrutinee now moves out, and the match consumes the scrutinee (`type-system.md` §18.4) | — |\n| N0259 | Invalid array (`type-system.md` §16): a length that is not an integer literal from 1 to 2^48 − 1, an element type that is not Copy, or an array larger than 2^31 − 1 bytes; since M9b also `copyFrom` or `fill` on a slice whose element type is not Copy (§16.5) | `[Res; 2]`, `[i32; 0]` |\n\nArrays and slices otherwise reuse codes: indexing or slicing a non-array, a non-`usize` index,\nor a bad `for` sequence or range is N0205; writing an element of a `let` array or through a\n`Slice` is N0211; a slice in a field, a `var` local or an array element is N0246;\n`borrow mut a[..]` of an immutable array is N0248; slicing a non-place is N0247. Since M9b, `copyFrom` or\n`fill` on a `Slice` is N0211, on a slice of an immutable array N0248, and a source that is not a slice of the\nelement type N0205. Slices are\nreferences, so the borrow checker reports their conflicts as N0240–N0245.\n\n### Region parameters (N0270–N0279; `type-system.md` §25, M6x)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0270 | A scoped field or payload in a struct or enum without region parameters | `struct S { v: StringView; }` |\n| N0272 | Storing a value through a `borrow mut` parameter whose loans the signature does not let flow into it | `fn put(v: borrow mut Vector<StringView>, s: StringView) { v.push(s)!; }` |\n| N0273 | An unknown region name, region arguments on a type without region parameters, or the wrong number of them | `StringView<nowhere>`, `Plain<r>` |\n| N0274 | A generic used with a scoped type argument where it needs an unscoped one (it boxes `T`, makes an array of `T`, …) | `boxed<StringView>(s)` with `fn boxed<T>(v: T) -> Box<T>` |\n\nN0271 is unassigned: destroying a value that holds loans while their referents are gone reports N0243.\n\n### Unsafe and raw pointers (N0280–N0289; `type-system.md` §27, `unsafe.md`, M8a)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0280 | An unsafe operation outside an unsafe context: a read, write or arithmetic through a raw pointer, `Ptr.toBorrow`/`toBorrowMut`, `Slice.fromRaw`/`SliceMut.fromRaw`, a call to an `unsafe fn`, or a use of a `static mut` (U1, type-system.md §39.3) | `let v = p.read();` in a safe function |\n|",
