        " N0281 | An invalid raw-pointer pointee: a reference, slice, `StringView` or other scoped type, or `never` (a type parameter is allowed since M8d; a scoped argument for it is N0274) | `*borrow i32`, `*Slice<u8>` |\n| N0282 | A pointer operation that the pointer or its pointee does not allow: reading or writing a non-Copy type, writing through a `*T`, `toBorrowMut` or `SliceMut.fromRaw` from a `*T`, any access through `*void` | `p.write(1)` with `p: *i32`; `q.read()` with `q: *void` |\n| N0283 | An `unsafe fn`, or a function containing `unsafe`, without a doc comment with a `Safety:` line (U2) | `unsafe fn f() {}` with no `/// Safety: …` above it |\n\n### C interoperability and profiles (N0290–N0299; `type-system.md` §28, M8b; `profiles.md` §4, M8c)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0290 | An unknown attribute, an attribute in the wrong place or with wrong arguments, a repeated attribute, an ABI other than `\"C\"`, or an extern declaration with a body, receiver or type parameters; `@export` on `main` | `@inline fn f() {}`, `@repr(C) enum E { A }`, `extern \"Rust\" { … }` |\n| N0291 | A type that cannot cross the C boundary in an extern declaration, an export, or a `@repr(C)` struct's field; a `drop` on a `@repr(C)` struct that holds C state (ADR 0029) | `fn f(s: Slice<u8>);` in `extern \"C\"`, `@export fn g() -> Box<i32>` |\n| N0292 | A C name that is not a valid C identifier, is a C keyword (or `bool`, `true`, `false`, `NULL`, `main`), is reserved by C, or starts with `ceesafe_`, `cee_`, `CEE_` or `cee` and an uppercase letter | `@export(\"int\")`, `fn ceesafe_alloc();` in `extern \"C\"`, `@repr(C) struct S { default: i32; }` |\n| N0293 | A hosted-only name (`Console`, `Process`, `File`, the heap allocators and every collection or box that needs them, …) or a `main` function in a package built with `--profile freestanding` (`profiles.md` §4.1) | `Console.writeLine(1);`, `let b = Box.create(5)!;` |\n| N0294 | An imported C type (type-system.md §31), or a C-laid-out type (§33), used where CeeSafe would need its size or would move it: as a local, parameter, result, field, element, optional or type argument, in a struct literal, or with `read`, `write`, `add`, `sub`, `offset` or `fromRaw` through a pointer; or a borrow of a pointer, `usize` or `isize` field of one | `fn f(i: cosmoboot_info) {}`, `p.add(1)` with `p: *cosmoboot_info` |\n| N0295 | An operation whose effect (`sleep`, `alloc`) the context forbids: in an `@context(atomic)` or `@context(irq)` function, while an `@context(atomic)` value (a spin-lock guard) is live, or beyond a CeeSafe function's `@effects` bound (type-system.md §32); a closure body beyond its type's effect list, or a named function with effects its expected function type's list lacks (§36.3) | `mutex_lock(m)` while a `SpinGuard` is live |\n| N0296 | A `private` field named outside the source file that declares its struct: read, assigned, borrowed or given in a literal (type-system.md §12) | `lock.data` outside `sync.cee` |\n| N0297 | A float type, literal or `fromBits` in a build with `--no-float` (type-system.md §35.9) | `let x = 1.5;` under `ceesafec build --no-float` |\n| N0298 | A function that cannot be a function value: an `extern` or `unsafe` function, one with region parameters, or `main` (type-system.md §36.1) | `let f = puts;` with `puts` declared in an `extern \"C\"` block |\n| N0299 | A closure of the wrong kind: a `borrow mut fn` where a `borrow fn` is expected, or a capturing closure where a plain `fn` is expected (type-system.md §36.2); a function or closure value whose type's effects are not in the expected type's effect list (§36.3) | `let f: fn(i32) -> i32 = (x) => x + k;` |\n\nCalling an extern function outside an unsafe context is N0280; two extern declarations or exports\nwith one C name are N0201; `CChar` as a value type is N0281.\n\nRaw pointers reuse codes: an invalid pointer cast is N0208, ordering comparisons on pointers are\nN0207, a constant of\npointer type is N0216, and an `",
    149 =>
        "unsafe main` is N0219. (An `unsafe` interface method was N0216 until C5.)\n\n### Results and the heap (N0260–N0269; `type-system.md` §17–§18)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0260 | `Ok(...)` / `Err(...)` (or, since M4c, `Pool.create(...)`; since M6c, `null`) without an expected type that fixes its type arguments, or where another type is expected | `let a = Ok(1);`, `let p = Pool.create(SystemHeap {}, 4u32);`, `let n = null;` |\n| N0261 | `?` in a function that does not return a `Result` with the same error type; `?` on an optional in a function that does not return an optional (M6c) | `?` on `Result<i32, F>` in a function returning `Result<i32, E>` |\n| N0262 | `?` or `!` on something that is not a `Result` or optional value (a reference to one included) | `n?` with `n: i32` |\n| N0263 | An allocator argument that is not an allocator type (`SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator`, or for `Box` since C5 a struct without fields implementing `Allocator`) | `Box<i32, Point>`, `Box.createIn(point, 1)` |\n| N0265 | Type arguments that cannot be inferred: a generic call, struct literal or enum value whose arguments, fields or expected type do not determine every type parameter | `let d = Maybe.Nothing;`, `let e = make();` |\n| N0266 | Generic instantiation nested more than 64 deep (polymorphic recursion) | `fn deep<T>(x: Maybe<T>) { deep(Maybe.Just(x)); }` |\n| N0267 | An `impl` that does not match its interface (receiver, parameter or return type, a missing or extra method), a duplicate `impl`, an `impl` for a generic or scoped type, an interface method without a receiver, or a method call that two bounds both provide | `impl Shape for Square { fn area(this) -> i64 … }` |\n| N0268 | A type argument that does not satisfy a bound of its type parameter (an interface it does not implement, or `Copy`) | `total(plain)` with `fn total<S: Shape>(…)` |\n| N0264 | A type a region cannot hold (R2): one that needs drop, a scoped type, or a non-Copy fill value for `allocArray` | `arena.alloc(Res { id: 1 })`, `arena.alloc(someReference)` |\n\nGeneric code (M6a) reuses codes:\n- an operator on a type parameter is N0207, a field or method on one is N0234, and a literal for\n  one is N0205;\n- a generic type without its arguments, or with the wrong number of them, is N0204;\n- a duplicate or shadowing type parameter is N0201;\n- a method with type parameters of its own is N0216;\n- a generic `main` is N0219;\n- naming something that is not an interface as a bound or in an `impl` is N0204;\n- a method on a type parameter that no bound declares is N0234;\n- an `impl` method clashing with a type's own method is N0228.\n\nRegion values reuse codes:\n- assigning a `using` binding as a whole is N0211;\n- nesting a frame that is not a `using` binding is N0248;\n- a method call on a frame that is not a local is N0247;\n- a storage that is not `[u8; N]` or `SliceMut<u8>` is N0205;\n- returning a frame or fixed-buffer allocator without sources is N0245.\n\n`?` inside a deferred statement is N0254, and `Ok`/`Err` with the wrong number of values is\nN0209. The names `Result`, `Ok`, `Err`, `AllocError`, `Box`, `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator`\ncannot be declared (N0201). Moving out of a box's contents is N0251 until M3c.\n\n### Build (N0300–N0399)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0300 | `ceesafec build --emit exe` without a `main` function | a library-only file |\n\nNames across packages (N0310–N0319; `type-system.md` §2, ADR 0013):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0310 | The package named in an import, or in a struct literal `p.T { … }`, is not a dependency of the package (elsewhere an unknown `p` in `p.x` is N0200) | `import geometri;` |\n| N0311 | A dependency has no item of that name | `geometry.Pointt` |\n| N0312 | The item exists but is not `public`, or a field or method of a public struct is not | `geometry.helper()` with `fn helper` internal |\n| N0313 | A simple name is provided by two imports | `import a; import b;` both with `Point`, ",
    150 =>
        "then `Point` |\n| N0314 | A public item's signature names a type that other packages cannot see | `public fn make() -> Hidden` with `struct Hidden` internal |\n| N0315 | An item, parameter, local, loop variable or pattern binding has the name of one of the package's dependencies | `let geometry = 1;` |\n\nPackages (N0320–N0329; `tooling.md` §4, ADR 0013):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0320 | `ceesafe.toml` or `ceesafe.lock` is not valid in the TOML subset | `name = app` (no quotes) |\n| N0321 | A manifest field is missing, unknown or invalid, or a dependency is an executable | `kind = \"binary\"` |\n| N0322 | A path dependency's directory or manifest is missing, or its name differs from its key | `geometry = { path = \"../geo\" }` with no `../geo/ceesafe.toml` |\n| N0323 | A git dependency cannot be fetched or resolved, or is absent with `--offline` | an unknown tag |\n| N0324 | The lock file is missing or does not match the manifest | a dependency added without `ceesafec fetch` |\n| N0325 | A git checkout's manifest and sources do not match the lock's hash | a file edited in the cache |\n| N0326 | One package name reached through two different sources | two paths to two `geometry` packages |\n| N0327 | A dependency cycle | `a` depends on `b`, `b` on `a` |\n| N0328 | An invalid version, version requirement, or index entry (`tooling.md` §4.6) | `shapes = \"1.x\"` |\n| N0329 | No versions satisfy the requirements: the conflicting requirements and where each came from (§4.7) | `^2.1 (from geometry 1.4.0) and ^1.0 (from plot 0.3.0)` |\n| N0335 | `ceesafec publish`: the package cannot be published as it is (§4.8) | a path dependency, or uncommitted changes |\n\nDynamic dispatch and owning closures (N0330–N0339; `type-system.md` §37, §36.5, ADR 0015):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0330 | `dyn I` where it is not behind a reference or a `Box`: a local, parameter, field, element, optional or type argument | `fn f(s: dyn Shape)`, `Vector<dyn Shape>` |\n| N0331 | `dyn I` for an interface that is not dyn-compatible: one of its methods takes `this` by value | `interface Sink { fn close(this); }` then `borrow dyn Sink` |\n| N0332 | A `move` literal captures a scoped value: a reference, view, slice, closure, or a value with region parameters (`type-system.md` §36.5) | `let v = s.view(); Box.create(move () => v.length)` |\n| N0333 | A `move` literal anywhere but as the value of `Box.create`/`createIn` with an expected `Box<dyn fn…>` | `let f = move (x: u64) => x;` |\n| N0334 | A value that is not `Send` where it must cross to another thread: a spawned closure's capture or result, a `dyn … + Send` coercion's source, a `Send` bound's argument, or, for `Thread.both`, a capture that may not be used from two threads at once or an argument that is not a closure literal or `fn()` value (`type-system.md` §38, §38.6) | `Thread.spawn(move () => view.length)` with `view: StringView` |\n| N0336 | `@effectsOf` that names no parameter, or a parameter that is not of a function or closure type, or has no names (`type-system.md` §32.5) | `@effectsOf(n)` with `n: i32`; `@effectsOf()` |\n| N0337 | A static that cannot be one: a `static` whose type is not `Share`, or a generic or local one (`type-system.md` §39) | `static C: Cell = …` with `Cell` not `Share` |\n| N0338 | Not a static initialiser: an initialiser or `@constant` body outside §39.4, or a C constant macro called elsewhere | `static N: u64 = compute();` |\n| N0339 | `offsetOf<T>(field)` whose `T` is not a struct, or has no field of that name (`type-system.md` §30.1, ADR 0030) | `offsetOf<u32>(x)`; `offsetOf<Pair>(c)` |\n| N0340 | `callerAddress()` outside the body of an `@export` function, including in a closure inside one (`type-system.md` §28.4, ADR 0030) | `fn f() -> usize => callerAddress();` |\n",
    151 =>
        "# Formatting (M10 T1)\n\n`ceesafec fmt` rewrites CeeSafe source into one canonical style. It has no options: two programmers who run it get the same\nspacing, indentation, blank lines, brace placement and comment alignment. Where the style leaves a choice to the author\n(§3), it is a choice of line breaks, which the formatter keeps.\n\nThe formatter changes only whitespace, and the trailing commas of §3.3. A formatted file parses to the same tree as\nthe original (the same AST, apart from spans) and keeps every comment, in order. Formatting a formatted file changes\nnothing.\n\n## 1. Command\n\n```sh\nceesafec fmt file.cee...          # rewrite each file in place (only if it changes)\nceesafec fmt --check file.cee...  # change nothing; list the files that would change, exit 1 if any\nceesafec fmt --stdout file.cee    # print the formatted text of one file\n```\n\nA file with a syntax error is not formatted: its diagnostics are printed, the file is left as it is, and the exit\nstatus is 1. A directory argument means every `.cee` file under it.\n\n## 2. Lines and indentation\n\n- The text is UTF-8 with LF line endings; it ends with exactly one newline. No line has trailing whitespace.\n- Indentation is 4 spaces per level; there are no tabs.\n- A level is opened by a `{` that ends its line (a block, a struct, enum, interface or impl body, a match, a\n  multi-line struct or array literal) and by a broken list (§3.3). Its contents are indented one level, and its\n  closing delimiter is on its own line at the opening line's indentation.\n- **Blank lines:** at most one in a row. None directly after an opening `{` or before a closing `}`, and none at the\n  start or end of the file. Elsewhere, between two items, two members or two statements, a blank line is kept if\n  the source has one or more, and not added if it has none.\n- **Line width:** 120 columns, counting the code and not a trailing comment. A line longer than that is broken by §3.4\n  where it can be. A line that cannot be broken (a long string literal, a long name) stays long.\n\n## 3. Line breaks\n\n### 3.1 What the author decides\n\nThe formatter keeps these choices and only lays out the result:\n- whether a block, struct, enum, interface or impl body, or match is written on one line (`{ return x; }`) or across\n  lines (anywhere between its braces);\n- whether a list (arguments, parameters, struct fields, array elements) is broken, and, in a broken list, which\n  elements start a new line (so a literal can keep several fields per line);\n- whether an expression body (`=> expr`) starts on the line of the `=>` or on the next one;\n- where a long binary expression or a chain of member calls (a `.` after a call, an index, `!` or `?`) is broken;\n- whether an attribute is followed by a line break.\n\nAny other line break in the source is removed: two tokens on different lines that no rule here separates are joined\nwith their §4 spacing. In particular `else` always follows the `}` before it on the same line.\n\nA one-line form is kept only if it fits in 120 columns and holds no line comment; otherwise it is broken, as if the\nauthor had written it across lines.\n\n### 3.2 One-line forms\n\n- A one-line block has one space inside each brace: `{ x += 1; }`, `{ break; }`. An empty block is `{}`.\n- A one-line struct body is `struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }`; a one-line enum is `enum E { A, B(i32) }`.\n- A one-line match is `match (v) { 0 => \"zero\", _ => \"many\" }`, and as a statement\n  `match (o) { Some(v) => { use(v); } null => {} }`.\n- A one-line struct literal is `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; an array literal is `[1, 2, 3]`; argument and parameter lists\n  are `f(a, b)` and `fn f(a: i32, b: i32)`. One-line lists have no trailing comma.\n- **An if/else chain is laid out as a whole:** if one of its blocks is broken, or the author started an `else` on a new\n  line, every block of the chain is broken.\n\n```ceesafe\nif (c) { a(); } else { b(); }      // fits: kept on one line\nif (c) {                           // otherwise: every block bro",
    152 =>
        "ken\n    a();\n} else {\n    b();\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Broken lists\n\nStatements, members, enum variants and match arms of a broken body are one per line. A list that is broken (by its\nauthor, or by §3.4) is laid out as a block:\n- the opening delimiter ends its line;\n- the elements are indented one level; each element that the author started on a new line starts a new line, and\n  the others follow the element before them after `, `; a list broken by §3.4 has one element per line;\n- the closing delimiter starts its own line at the opening line's indentation;\n- the last element is followed by a comma, where the grammar allows one (grammar.md §2: parameters, arguments, struct\n  field initialisers, array elements, enum variants, and the arms of a match expression, except an arm whose value is\n  a block). The commas between the arms of a match statement are left as written. Type parameters, generic arguments,\n  attribute arguments, closure parameters, function-type parameters and interface method parameters are never broken.\n\n```ceesafe\nlet bodies = [\n    Body { x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, vx: 0.0, vy: 0.0, vz: 0.0, mass: SOLAR_MASS },\n    Body {\n        x: 4.84143144246472090e+00, y: -1.16032004402742839e+00, z: -1.03622044471123109e-01,\n        vx: 1.66007664274403694e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR, vy: 7.69901118419740425e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR,\n    },\n];\n```\n\n### 3.4 Breaking a line that is too long\n\nWhen a line does not fit, the formatter breaks one construct that starts on it and lays the file out again, until\nthe line fits or nothing on it is left to break. It takes the first kind in this order, and of that kind the\noutermost:\n1. a function's expression body (`=> e`): the expression moves to the next line, indented one level;\n2. a list or a one-line block or body (§3.3);\n3. the value of a match arm or a closure (`=> e`), moved to the next line;\n4. an initialiser or an assigned value (`= e`), moved to the next line;\n5. a binary expression: after its operator, with the right operand indented one level;\n6. a chain of member calls: before a `.`, indented one level.\n\nThe author's breaks of the same kinds (§3.1) are laid out by the same rules.\n\n## 4. Spaces\n\n- One space around binary operators (`a + b`, `x == y`, `a && b`), around `=` and the compound assignments, around\n  `->` and `=>`, after `,`, `;` (inside a one-line form) and `:` (`x: i32`, `x: 1`).\n- No space around `..` (`0..n`, `a[i..j]`, `a[..]`), `.`, inside parentheses, brackets and generic arguments\n  (`f(a)`, `[1, 2]`, `Vector<i32>`), before `(` in a call or declaration, after a unary operator (`-x`, `!b`,\n  `*p`), before `?` and `!` (`f()?`, `r!`), or before `,` and `;`.\n- Keywords: one space after `if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `return` (with a value), `fn` (in a declaration), `let`,\n  `var`, `borrow`, `mut`, `move`, `as` and the visibility keywords; `if (c) {`, `} else {`, `} else if (d) {`.\n- One space before an opening `{` that follows code on its line.\n- Attributes are followed by one space or a line break, as written: `@context(atomic) fn f()`.\n\n## 5. Comments\n\n- Comments keep their text and order. A comment on a line of its own is indented like the code that follows it (or,\n  at the end of a block, like the code before the `}`).\n- A comment after code on the same line (a trailing comment) follows it after one space. Trailing comments on\n  consecutive lines are aligned: each starts one space after the longest code among those lines. A line without a\n  trailing comment, or a blank line, ends the group.\n- Doc comments (`///`) stay directly before their item; a blank line between a doc comment and its item is removed,\n  since it would detach the comment (grammar.md §1.2).\n- A block comment (`/* … */`) inside a line keeps one space on each side.\n\n## 6. Guarantees and their tests\n\n- **Same tree:** for every file of the repository that parses (the corpus included), the formatted text parses\n  without error to an AST equal to the original's, spans aside (Nova's `FormatterTests`; in CeeSa",
    153 =>
        "fe, `tools/compare_fmt.py` compares `ceesafec fmt` with novac's).\n- **Comments:** the comments of the formatted text are those of the original, in order.\n- **Fixed point:** formatting the formatted text changes nothing.\n- **The repository's own code** (`lib/`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`, `integrations/`, the README's examples) is kept\n  formatted: `ceesafec fmt --check` runs over it in the test suite. The test corpus is not reformatted, since its\n  annotations name lines and some files test unusual layout; it is the formatter's test input.\n",
    154 =>
        "# CeeSafe Grammar — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1a implements the lexer and parser for exactly\nthis grammar). Later milestones extend it. Any syntax not listed here is rejected\nby the parser, even if `language-spec.md` shows it for a later milestone.\n\nNotation: EBNF. `'x'` is a literal token, `X?` optional, `X*` zero or more, `X+` one or more.\n\n## 1. Lexical grammar\n\n### 1.1 Source text\n- UTF-8. A leading BOM is ignored. Line terminators: `\\n`, `\\r\\n`, `\\r`.\n- Columns in diagnostics are 1-based and count UTF-16 code units. Lines are 1-based.\n- Non-ASCII characters may appear only inside comments, string literals and char\n  literals.\n\n### 1.2 Whitespace and comments\n- Whitespace: space, tab, line terminators, form feed.\n- `// ...` to end of line. `/* ... */` block comments do **not** nest. An unterminated block\n  comment is error N0003.\n- Comments and whitespace are discarded by the parser, except doc comments (M8a). The lexer records every comment's span\n  for the formatter (M10 T1, formatting.md).\n- **Doc comments** (M8a): a line comment that starts with exactly `///` (not `////`). A run of doc\n  comment lines, with only whitespace between them, is attached to the declaration whose first token\n  follows the run directly. Its text is each line after `///` with leading spaces removed. Doc\n  comments elsewhere are ordinary comments. Used by the `Safety:` rule (type-system.md §27.4).\n\n### 1.3 Identifiers and keywords\n```\nIdentifier = IdentStart IdentContinue*      (not a keyword)\nIdentStart = 'A'..'Z' | 'a'..'z' | '_'\nIdentContinue = IdentStart | '0'..'9'\n```\nA lone `_` is the token `Underscore` (reserved; no meaning in M1).\n\nKeywords (all reserved, whether or not M1 uses them):\n```\nas borrow break const continue defer drop else enum extern false fn for if impl\nimport in interface internal let loop match move mut namespace null own private\npublic return Self static struct this true type unsafe using var where while\nclass async await yield macro\n```\nPrimitive type names (`i32`, `u64`, `bool`, `void`, …) are **identifiers**,\nresolved by name resolution, not keywords.\n\n### 1.4 Integer literals\n```\nIntLiteral = (Decimal | '0x' HexDigits | '0b' BinDigits | '0o' OctDigits) IntSuffix?\nIntSuffix  = 'i8'|'i16'|'i32'|'i64'|'isize'|'u8'|'u16'|'u32'|'u64'|'usize'\n```\n- `_` separators are allowed only *between* digits (`1_000`, `0xFF_FF`). A separator\n  at the start or end of the digit sequence, or doubled, is error N0010.\n- The value must fit in 64 bits (unsigned), otherwise N0004. Whether it fits the\n  *type* is checked later (type checking).\n- A digit invalid for the radix is N0008. A missing digit after a prefix is N0008. An unknown suffix is N0009.\n- Decimal literals have no leading-zero restriction, and there is no octal-by-leading-zero:\n  `010` is ten.\n\n### 1.5 Float literals (typed since M11 G7: type-system.md §35.2)\n```\nFloatLiteral = Decimal '.' Decimal Exponent? FloatSuffix? | Decimal Exponent FloatSuffix?\nExponent = ('e'|'E') ('+'|'-')? Decimal\nFloatSuffix = 'f32' | 'f64'\n```\n`1.` and `.5` are not float literals. `1..2` lexes as `1` `..` `2`.\n\n### 1.6 String and char literals\n- `\"...\"` on one line. Escapes: `\\n \\r \\t \\\\ \\\" \\' \\0 \\u{H..H}` (1–6 hex digits,\n  a Unicode scalar value). Other escapes are N0006. A newline or EOF before the closing quote is N0005.\n- `'c'`: exactly one Unicode scalar value or one escape. Otherwise N0007. Its value is the scalar value, as an\n  integer literal (`type-system.md` §4, M11); it may also be a pattern.\n\n### 1.7 Punctuation and operators\n```\n( ) { } [ ] , ; : . .. @ ? -> =>\n+ - * / % ! & | ^ ~ && || << \n= == != < <= > >=\n+= -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<=\n```\nThe lexer **never** produces `>>` or `>>=`. It emits single `>` (and `>=`) tokens.\nThe parser forms the shift operator `>>` from two *adjacent* `>` tokens, and `>>=` from `>`\nimmediately followed by `>=`. That keeps nested generic arguments\n(`A<B<C>>`) unambiguous in later milestones.\n\nAny other character is N000",
    155 =>
        "1 (unexpected character).\n\n## 2. Syntactic grammar (M1)\n\n```\nCompilationUnit = NamespaceDecl? ImportDecl* Item* EOF\nNamespaceDecl   = 'namespace' QualifiedName ';'     (* retired by ADR 0013: accepted, no effect *)\nImportDecl      = 'import' Identifier ('.' Identifier)? ';'   (* M10 T5: a dependency, or one of its items; type-system.md §2 *)\nQualifiedName   = Identifier ('.' Identifier)*\n\nItem       = Attribute* Visibility? (FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | StaticDecl | StructDecl | EnumDecl | ExternBlock)\nAttribute  = '@' Identifier ('(' AttributeArg (',' AttributeArg)* ')')?   (* M8b; type-system.md §28.1 *)\nAttributeArg = Identifier | StringLiteral | IntLiteral\nExternBlock = 'extern' StringLiteral '{' (ExternFn | ExternStruct)* '}'  (* M8b; §28.2 *)\nExternStruct = Attribute* Visibility? 'struct' Identifier ('{' ExternField* '}' | ';')   (* C6b; type-system.md §31 *)\nExternField = Attribute* FieldDecl                                           (* C9: @cname; type-system.md §31.1 *)\nExternFn   = Attribute* Visibility? 'fn' Identifier '(' (Param (',' Param)* ','?)? ')' ('->' Type)? ';'\nTypeParams = '<' TypeParam (',' TypeParam)* '>'               (* M6a; type-system.md §20 *)\nInterfaceDecl = 'interface' Identifier TypeParams? '{' MethodSig* '}'   (* M6b; §21 *)\nMethodSig  = Attribute* 'fn' Identifier '(' Receiver (',' Param)* ')' ('->' Type)? ';'   (* attributes: M13 G2, `@effects`; §37.3 *)\nImplDecl   = 'impl' Type 'for' Type '{' (Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl)* '}'   (* M6b; §21; attributes C7a *)\nTypeParam  = 'region' Identifier                               (* M6x: a region, before any type parameter; §25.1 *)\n           | Identifier (':' Type ('+' Type)*)?                (* bounds: M6b *)\n(* `region` is contextual: only `region` followed by a name declares a region. In a type, a region\n   argument is a region name or `static`, written first in the argument list (`Token<src>`,\n   `StringView<r>`, `Slice<r, T>`) or in `borrow(r) T`; type-system.md §25.2. *)\nEnumDecl   = 'enum' Identifier TypeParams? '{' (Variant (',' Variant)* ','?)? '}'        (* M2d *)\nVariant    = Identifier ('(' Type (',' Type)* ','? ')')?\nVisibility = 'public' | 'internal' | 'private'\n\nFunctionDecl = 'unsafe'? 'fn' Identifier TypeParams? '(' ParamList? ')' ('->' Type)? FunctionBody   (* 'unsafe': M8a *)\nParamList    = (Receiver | Param) (',' Param)* ','?\nReceiver     = ('borrow' 'mut'?)? 'this'   (* M2a: by value; M2b: reference receivers *)\nParam        = Identifier ':' Type\n\nStructDecl   = 'struct' Identifier TypeParams? '{' Member* '}'          (* M2a; TypeParams M6a *)\nMember       = Visibility? FieldDecl | Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl | DropBlock   (* method attributes: C7a, type-system.md §32 *)\nDropBlock    = 'drop' Block                (* M2c *)\nFieldDecl    = Identifier ':' Type ';'\nFunctionBody = Block | '=>' Expr ';'\n\nConstDecl = 'const' Identifier ':' Type '=' Expr ';'\nStaticDecl = 'static' 'mut'? Identifier ':' Type '=' Expr ';'   (* M14 K1; type-system.md §39 *)\n\nType = QualifiedName\n     | 'borrow' 'mut'? Sources? Type          (* M2b references; Sources only in return types *)\n     | '[' Type ';' IntLiteral ']'             (* M2d fixed array *)\n     | ('Slice' | 'SliceMut') '<' Type '>'     (* M2d slices *)\n     | 'Result' '<' Type ',' Type '>'          (* M3a; type-system.md §17 *)\n     | Type '?'                                (* M6c: optional; `borrow T?` is an optional reference *)\n     | '(' Type ')'                            (* M11 (G6): `borrow (T?)` is a reference to an optional *)\n     | Attribute* 'fn' '(' (Type (',' Type)*)? ')' ('->' Type)?   (* M11 (G8a): a function value, type-system.md §36.1;\n                                               G8b′: `@effects(…)` before `fn`, §36.3 *)\n     | Identifier '<' Type (',' Type)* '>'     (* M6a: an instantiation of a generic struct or enum; also Box, Pool, … *)\n     | '*' 'mut'? Type                         (* M8a: raw pointer; type-system.md §27 *)\n    ",
    156 =>
        " | 'dyn' Type                              (* M13 G2: an interface instantiation; only behind `borrow` or in\n                                                  `Box<…>`; type-system.md §37. `dyn` is a keyword only here *)\n     | 'dyn' 'mut'? Attribute* 'fn' '(' (Type (',' Type)*)? ')' ('->' Type)?   (* M13 G3: an owning closure's type, §36.5 *)\nSources = '(' Identifier (',' Identifier)* ')'   (* only if a type follows the ')'; else '(' Type ')' *)\n\nBlock = '{' Statement* '}'\nStatement = Block\n          | LocalDecl\n          | IfStmt\n          | 'while' '(' Expr ')' Block\n          | 'loop' Block\n          | 'break' ';'\n          | 'continue' ';'\n          | 'return' Expr? ';'\n          | 'using' Identifier (':' Type)? '=' Expr ';'      (* M2c *)\n          | 'defer' Statement                               (* M2c *)\n          | MatchStmt                                       (* M2d; type-system.md §15.2 *)\n          | 'for' '(' Identifier 'in' Expr ('..' Expr)? ')' Block   (* M2d; type-system.md §16.4 *)\n          | 'unsafe' Block                                  (* M8a; type-system.md §27.4 *)\n          | Expr AssignOp Expr ';'        (* assignment: a statement, not an expression *)\n          | Expr ';'\nLocalDecl = ('let' | 'var') Identifier (':' Type)? ('=' Expr)? ';'\nIfStmt    = 'if' '(' Expr ')' Block ('else' (IfStmt | Block))?\nAssignOp  = '=' | '+=' | '-=' | '*=' | '/=' | '%=' | '&=' | '|=' | '^=' | '<<=' | '>>='\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Braces are **mandatory** after `if`, `else`, `while` and `loop`, so there is no dangling-else ambiguity.\n- Assignment is a statement, so `if (x = 1)` is a syntax error. The assignment target\n  must be a name or member access (N0104 otherwise). Whether it is *assignable*\n  (a `var`) is checked later.\n- A `LocalDecl` without an initialiser must have a type annotation (enforced by the\n  checker, not the parser).\n\n### 2.1 Expressions and precedence\n\nFrom lowest to highest binding. All binary levels are left-associative except\ncomparisons, which are **non-associative**: `a < b < c` is error N0103.\n\n| Level | Operators | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `\\|\\|` | short-circuit |\n| 2 | `&&` | short-circuit |\n| 3 | `== != < <= > >=` | non-associative |\n| 4 | `\\|` | |\n| 5 | `^` | |\n| 6 | `&` | |\n| 7 | `<< >>` | |\n| 8 | `+ -` | |\n| 9 | `* / %` | |\n| 10 | `as` | `Expr 'as' Type`, left-assoc (`x as u32 as u64`) |\n| 11 | unary `- !`, `borrow`, `borrow mut`, `move` | prefix, right-assoc (`- -x`) |\n| 12 | postfix: call `(...)`, member `.name` | |\n\nThe ordering puts bitwise operators **above** comparisons (as in Rust and Swift), so\n`x & 1 == 0` parses as `(x & 1) == 0`. This avoids a well-known C/C# pitfall.\n\n```\nPrimary = IntLiteral | FloatLiteral | StringLiteral | CharLiteral | 'true' | 'false'\n        | Identifier GenericArgs? | '(' Expr ')' | 'this'\n        | 'unsafe' '{' Expr '}'                              (* M8a: an unsafe expression *)\n        | IfExpr | MatchExpr                                 (* M11 (G5): type-system.md §15.3 *)\n        | '(' (ClosureParam (',' ClosureParam)*)? ')' '=>' (Block | Expr)   (* M11 (G8b): a closure, §36.2 *)\n        | 'move' '(' (ClosureParam (',' ClosureParam)*)? ')' '=>' (Block | Expr)   (* M13 G3: a move literal, §36.5 *)\n        | Identifier GenericArgs? '{' FieldInits? '}'       (* struct literal, M2a; GenericArgs M6a *)\n        | '[' Expr (',' Expr)* ','? ']' | '[' Expr ';' IntLiteral ']'   (* array literals, M2d *)\nClosureParam = Identifier (':' Type)?             (* a '(' … ')' followed by '=>' is a closure *)\nIfExpr    = 'if' '(' Expr ')' Branch 'else' (IfExpr | Branch)\nMatchExpr = 'match' '(' Expr ')' '{' (ValueArm (',' ValueArm)* ','?)? '}'\nValueArm  = Pattern '=>' Branch\nBranch    = Expr | Block                             (* a Block branch must not complete *)\nFieldInits = FieldInit (',' FieldInit)* ','?\nFieldInit  = Identifier ':' Expr\nGenericArgs = '<' Type (',' Type)* '>'      (* only when followed by '(', '.' or '{' — see below *)\nPostfix = Primary ( '(",
    157 =>
        "' ArgList? ')' | '.' Identifier | '[' IndexOrRange ']'\n                  | '?' | '!' )*                     (* M3a: propagate / unwrap a Result *)\nIndexOrRange = Expr | Expr? '..' Expr?               (* M2d: element or slice *)\nArgList = Arg (',' Arg)* ','?\nArg     = 'mut' Expr | Expr                (* `mut x` for a `borrow mut` parameter, M2b *)\n```\n\n**Generic arguments in expressions** (`truncate<i64>(x)`, and since M6a `id<i32>(x)`,\n`Pair<u8>.make()` and `Pair<u8> { … }`): after an identifier, `<` starts generic arguments if the\ntokens up to the matching `>` can only form types (identifiers, `.`, `,`, `;`, `*` (M8a), `?` (M9a: optional\ntypes, as in `Vector<Box<Pair>?>.create()`), integer literals, `borrow`, `mut`, and balanced `<…>` and `[…]`) and the `>` is followed by `(`, `.` or `{`, or (since\nC7d, for a generic function's instance as a value, `release<T>`, type-system §34.1) by `)`, `,`, `;`\nor `]`, which cannot follow a comparison's `>` (it needs an operand). Otherwise `<` is the less-than\noperator. Write `(f < T) > (x)` to mean the comparison.\n\n**Struct literals** (M2a): in expression position, an identifier *immediately followed by `{`* is\na struct literal. This is unambiguous in CeeSafe because every `{` that opens a block follows a\nkeyword (`else`, `loop`), a `)` (conditions are parenthesised) or a type (`-> T {`), never an\nexpression.\n\nA `Receiver` (`this`) is only meaningful as the first parameter of a function declared inside a\n`struct`. The parser accepts it as any first parameter; the checker rejects it elsewhere (N0232).\n\n### 2.2 Nesting limit (M15 S3)\n\nNesting is limited to **1,000 levels** per file. Each of these is one level:\n- a nested block or statement;\n- a type (each `Box<…>` inside another is one level);\n- a pattern;\n- a unary operand;\n- each link of a left-associative chain, so `1 + 1 + 1` is two levels deep, as are `a.b.c` and `f()()`. The links\n  are binary operators, `as`, calls, members, indexes, `?` and `!`.\n\nEvery later pass recurses over the tree the parser builds, so its depth is what is bounded. Past the limit the parser\nreports **N0110** once, at the token where the limit was passed, and stops parsing that file. No other diagnostic in\nthe file follows it. Hand-written code stays far below the limit: none of the repository's 525 files comes near it.\nBefore M15, 100,000 levels overflowed ceesafec's stack, ending with SIGSEGV and no message (#3). ceesafec runs every command\non its own 256 MiB stack, so input within the limit compiles whatever the operating system's main-thread stack is\n(1 MiB on Windows; measured in milestone-15.md S3).\n\n## 3. Error recovery (normative for diagnostics tests)\n\n- Expected-token errors (N0100) are reported at the current token. When the missing\n  token is `;`, they are reported at the end of the previous token instead. The missing token is\n  *not* consumed.\n- After an error, the parser suppresses further diagnostics until it re-synchronises:\n  - in a block: at the next `;` (consumed), `}` (not consumed), or statement keyword\n    (`let var if while loop return break continue`). Balanced `{ }` groups are skipped\n    whole. An item keyword (`fn`, visibility) ends the block, which reports the missing `}`;\n  - inside a `struct` body: at the next member start (identifier followed by `:`, `fn`, or a\n    visibility keyword) or the closing `}`;\n  - at the top level: at the next declaration keyword (`fn const public internal private\n    namespace struct class enum interface impl let var static extern import using type`)\n    outside any skipped `{ }` group, or at EOF. Each misplaced declaration is therefore reported\n    once (N0101).\n- Every parse loop consumes at least one token per iteration or terminates, so\n  parsing always terminates.\n- Past the nesting limit (§2.2), N0110 ends the file's parse: no later diagnostic is reported in\n  that file.\n",
    158 =>
        "# Intermediate Representations (as implemented)\n\nStatus: describes the code as of M1 increment 1c. The design rationale is in `architecture.md` §2.\nThat document is the plan; this one is the implementation record.\n\n## 1. AST\n\n`src/syntax/ast.cee`. Tables of nodes with spans. Printed by `ceesafec parse --dump-ast`.\n\n## 2. HIR\n\n`src/hir/hir.cee`. It is typed and fully resolved:\n\n- Constant references are replaced by their values.\n- Parentheses and expression-bodied functions are removed.\n- `else if` becomes nested `if`.\n- Assignment targets are locals.\n\nPrinted by `ceesafec check --dump-hir`. Static semantics: `type-system.md`.\n\n## 3. NIR (CeeSafe IR)\n\n`src/nir/`. A control-flow graph of basic blocks per function, modelled on Rust MIR.\nIt is not SSA. M1 subset:\n\n```text\nfunction  = locals: [local]          -- _0.._(n-1) are the parameters, in order\n            blocks: [block]          -- bb0 is the entry block\nlocal     = id, type, name?          -- name is null for compiler temporaries\nblock     = id, statements: [stmt], terminator\nplace     = local projection*                  -- projection = '.' field | Deref (M2a, M2b) | Downcast(V) | Index(operand) (M2d)\nstmt      = Assign(place, rvalue)\n          | Eval(rvalue)             -- for calls whose result is unused (void)\n          | StorageDead(local)       -- scope end; temporaries at the end of their statement (M2b)\n          | Drop(place, flag?)       -- inserted by drop elaboration (M2c); guarded by a bool flag local if present\n          | Unpack(place, V, [local?])          -- M3c: move every payload of variant V out of a consumed enum local\n          | BoxFree(place, flag?)               -- M3c: free a partially moved box without destroying its contents\n          | BoundsCheck(index, length)          -- panics unless index < length (M2d)\n          | RangeCheck(low, high, length)       -- panics unless low <= high <= length (M2d);\n                                                 -- with a `Text` place (M6c), also unless both bounds\n                                                 -- are UTF-8 character boundaries of that StringView\n          | LengthCheck(left, right)            -- panics (\"slice lengths differ\") unless left == right (M9b)\nrvalue    = Use(operand)\n          | Binary(op, operand, operand, span)     -- op semantics: type-system.md §6 (may panic)\n          | Unary(op, operand, span)\n          | Cast(operand, type)                    -- lossless `as`; also round<T> to a float (type-system.md §35.4);\n                                                 -- also a coercion of `borrow T`/`Box<T, A>` to a dyn type (M13 G2, §37.2):\n                                                 -- the pair (T, dyn I) is in the program's DynTables; loans and moves\n                                                 -- flow through it as through any cast\n          | Truncate(operand, type)\n          | FloatToInt(operand, Panic | Checked | Saturating, type, span)   -- M11 G7: truncate<T> of a float (§35.4)\n          | Aggregate(struct, [operand])           -- field values in declaration order (M2a)\n          | Ref(Shared | Mut, place)               -- creates a loan (M2b; lifetimes.md)\n          | Discriminant(place) | EnumAggregate(V, [operand])   -- M2d enums\n          | Array([operand]) | ArrayRepeat(operand)             -- M2d array values\n          | Slice(Shared | Mut, place, low, high)  -- a slice of an array or deref'd slice; a loan of the whole place\n          | Length(place)                          -- a slice's length\n          | Call(function, [operand])              -- a call; after closure-call specialisation (optimisations.md\n                                                 -- §5) it may name a variant of the function (Variant > 0), and a\n                                                 -- CallClosure through a known closure becomes a Call of its body.\n                                                 -- A dyn method (IsDynMethod, no body; §37.3) dispatches through\n     ",
    159 =>
        "                                            -- the table of argument 0, a dyn reference; checked as a call of\n                                                 -- its signature, so loans and effects need no new rule\n          | Builtin(kind, [operand])               -- Console.write/writeLine; CallFunction(f, args…): a call through a\n                                                 -- function value (M11 G8a), result loans from every reference argument;\n                                                 -- CallClosure(f, args…) likewise through a closure (G8b), f passed on\n                                                 -- as the body's environment parameter\noperand   = Place(place, move?) | Const(value, type) | String(text)   -- move: non-Copy type or explicit `move` (M2c)\n                                         -- a float Const holds the float's bits (M11 G7, type-system.md §35.6)\nterminator= Goto(bb) | Branch(operand, then_bb, else_bb) | Return(operand?)\n          | Switch(operand, [(value, bb)], otherwise_bb)    -- match (M2d)\n          | Exit(operand)                          -- Process.exit\n          | Unreachable                            -- after a call of type never\n          | Panic(kind)                            -- `e!` on Err (M3a), `o!` on null (M6c); aborts\n```\n\nLowering rules (HIR → NIR, `NirBuilder`):\n\n- **Evaluation order is explicit.** Operands are evaluated left to right into operands or\n  temporaries. If the right operand of a binary operator (or a later argument) contains a call,\n  an earlier operand that reads a local is first copied into a temporary. This keeps\n  left-to-right semantics when calls can mutate state (M2+).\n- `&&` and `||` become branches into a boolean temporary. The right operand is evaluated\n  only when needed.\n- `x op= e` becomes `x = Binary(op, x, e)`; the same applies with a place such as `p.a.b`.\n- A struct literal evaluates its initialisers **in source order** into operands, then builds an\n  `Aggregate` with them in declaration order.\n- A field read of a place is a place operand (`_3.a.b`). A field read of any other value (a call\n  result) first spills the value into a temporary. A method call passes the receiver as the first\n  argument, evaluated before the explicit arguments.\n- `while` becomes `cond → body → cond`, and `loop` becomes `body → body`. `break` and `continue`\n  jump to the innermost loop's exit or head.\n- Statements after a `return`, `break`, `continue`, `Process.exit` or never-typed call are\n  unreachable and are not lowered. Blocks with no predecessors are not emitted.\n- Constants are already folded in HIR. NIR performs no optimisation in M1.\n\n- References (M2b):\n  - an explicit or implicit borrow becomes `t = Ref(k, place)`;\n  - auto-deref reads `(*r)`;\n  - a use of a `borrow mut` variable becomes `Ref(Mut, (*r))` (reborrow);\n  - a call-site borrow of a value spills it into a temporary, which gets `StorageDead` at the end of\n    the statement;\n  - for a `borrow mut this` receiver, the receiver's `Ref` is emitted after the other arguments (B5);\n  - every statement carries a source span for borrow diagnostics.\n- The **borrow checker** (`src/nir/borrow.cee`, `lifetimes.md`) runs on NIR inside\n  `Compilation.Create`, so `ceesafec check` reports borrow errors too.\n- **Moves and destruction (M2c).**\n  - Scopes hold locals *and* deferred statements. Leaving a scope (its end, `break`/`continue`,\n    or `return`) emits, in reverse order of position, `StorageDead` for locals and a fresh\n    lowering of each deferred statement.\n  - By-value parameters form the outermost scope.\n  - `return e` computes `e` into an unscoped temporary first, then ends the statement's\n    temporaries and every scope, then returns.\n  - **Move paths** (`src/nir/move_paths.cee`): each local is a tree, expanded field by field\n    wherever a field is assigned or moved; all siblings are expanded, so children partition their\n    parent. A forward dataflow over the leaves tracks maybe-init, ",
    160 =>
        "maybe-uninit and maybe-moved.\n    `MoveChecker` uses it for N0212/N0250–N0253.\n  - **Drop elaboration** (`src/nir/drops.cee`) runs only when every check passed:\n    - it inserts `Drop` before `StorageDead` of a local that needs drop, and before an assignment\n      that overwrites one; the assignment becomes `t = rvalue; Drop(p); p = t`;\n    - what is dropped depends on the state: whole, nothing, a flagged drop, or per field for a\n      partially moved struct;\n    - one bool flag per leaf is updated after every statement that changes that leaf's state.\n\n    The C backend emits drop glue `ceeD_<len><Name>(T *)`: the `drop` block, then each field that\n    needs drop, in declaration order.\n\n- **Arrays, slices and `for` (M2d).**\n  - A slice type is a reference to the unsized `[T]`; `s[i]` is the place `(*s)[i]`.\n  - An index is evaluated into a `usize` temporary (or kept as a constant), then checked with\n    `BoundsCheck` against the array's constant length or the slice's `Length`, then used as an\n    `Index` projection. Element places are never move paths: an element's init state is its\n    array's.\n  - An assignment evaluates its target's indexes (and checks them) before the value.\n  - `a[lo..hi]` evaluates the base place, then the bounds left to right, then `RangeCheck` (none\n    for `a[..]`), then `Slice`.\n  - **Bulk operations (M9b).** `d.copyFrom(s)` lowers the receiver slice, then the source slice, each into a\n    temporary, then `LengthCheck(Length(s), Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceCopy(d, s)`, which copies\n    with `memmove` semantics. `d.fill(v)` is the built-in `SliceFill(d, v)`. Neither is two-phase.\n    `d.swap(i, j)` (M11 G8c) lowers the slice and both indices, then `BoundsCheck(i, Length(d))` and\n    `BoundsCheck(j, Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceSwap(d, i, j)`. `v.retain(keep)` lowers the vector\n    reference and the closure, then a temporary `SliceMut` reborrowed from the vector's elements, a call of\n    lib/core's `SliceAlgorithms<T>.retain` on it and the closure, and `VectorTruncate(v, kept)` (type-system §36.4).\n  - `for` evaluates its bounds, or its sequence place and length, once into locals that live until\n    the loop exits. A `usize` counter is compared with the end at the head. The body binds the\n    variable in its own scope; `continue` jumps to the counter's increment, and `break` to the\n    exit, which ends the header's locals.\n\n- **Results (M3a).**\n  - `e?` and `e!` move `e` into a temporary, switch on its tag, and in the `Ok` block read\n    the payload `t as Ok .0`.\n  - On `Err`, `?` builds `Err(t as Err .0)` of the function's return type and returns through the\n    same path as `return`; `!` ends in `Panic`.\n  - **Optionals (M6c)** lower the same way with `Some` (tag 1) as the success variant: on `null`,\n    `?` returns the `null` of the function's return type and `!` ends in `Panic(UnwrapNull)`.\n    `o == null` (HIR `IsNull`) takes a shared borrow of `o` (or of a temporary), reads\n    `Discriminant(*r)` and compares it with 0, so `o` is not consumed.\n  - **Vectors and strings (M6d).** A vector dereferences, like a box, to its unsized elements: `v[i]`\n    is the place `v.*[i]`, with a `BoundsCheck` against `Length(v)`, and views are `Ref` of `v.*`.\n    The deref is not a move path, so moving an element out is a move out of a deref (N0251).\n    `push`/`append` lower their argument before the receiver's `Ref mut` (B5). `for (x in v)`\n    iterates over a view held in a header temporary, so the vector stays borrowed for the whole\n    loop. When the value written to a vector element contains a call, it is evaluated into a\n    temporary and the element's index is checked again just before the write (the call may have\n    shrunk the vector).\n  - **Slot maps and stable vectors (M6e)** are built-in calls on a borrow of the container\n    (`insert`, `getMut` and `remove` take `Ref mut`, with `insert` two-phase); `get`'s optional\n    reference carries the map's loan like any built-in result. A st",
    161 =>
        "able vector dereferences like\n    a vector, so `sv[i]` is the place `sv.*[i]`; the C backend renders its elements through the\n    chunk table. Liveness treats the targets of `Unpack` as definitions (fixed in M6e: a reference\n    payload bound by a consuming match used to be live from the function's start).\n  - **Region parameters (M6x).** A loan-carrying value stored into a field or element is lowered\n    into a temporary first, so the borrow checker sees a local whose loans flow into the target.\n  - **String views (M6c).** A literal is a constant operand of type `StringView` (no loans); a place\n    is needed only when it is spilled into a temporary. `s[lo..hi]` always emits a `RangeCheck`\n    with `Text`; `s.bytes()` is a `Cast` to `Slice<u8>` that keeps the view's loans.\n  - Moving a variant payload out of a local (only these lowerings do that) uninitialises the whole\n    local in the move dataflow, so the consumed result is not destroyed again.\n  - **Open temporaries.** The builder records the temporaries of every statement still being\n    lowered, with the scope they belong to. An early exit (`return`, `break`, `continue`, `?`)\n    ends them together with that scope, before its locals. Before M3a the temporaries of an\n    enclosing statement, such as a `match` scrutinee, were never ended on such paths.\n  - An expression statement whose value needs drop assigns it to a statement temporary, which is\n    destroyed at the end of the statement (type-system.md §17.5).\n\n- **Boxes and consuming matches (M3c).**\n  - Move paths go through box derefs (`NirPlace.IsMovePath`). Once a box's contents are tracked,\n    the box node has two children: its contents (fields below), then an *allocation* leaf that is\n    initialised while the box owns its memory.\n  - Drop elaboration destroys a partially moved box as: the initialised fields of its contents,\n    then `BoxFree` of the box. `BoxFree` is flagged if the allocation is only maybe initialised.\n  - A consuming `match` moves its scrutinee into a temporary. An arm that lists payloads starts\n    with one `Unpack`, which moves every payload into its binding or into a hidden local of the\n    arm and consumes the temporary as a whole. Other arms leave the value in the temporary, which\n    is destroyed at the end of the statement.\n\n- **Generic code (M6a).** NIR is built for generic versions too (type parameters are move types\n  that may need drop), so the move and borrow checkers run on them. Instantiations skip those\n  checks, which would only repeat the generic version's diagnostics. Drop elaboration and C\n  emission skip generic versions.\n\nPrinted by `ceesafec build --dump-nir`.\n\n## 4. LIR — deferred\n\n`architecture.md` §2.4 plans a C-shaped LIR between NIR and C. In M1 the C emitter consumes NIR\ndirectly. The C-level decisions (type names, helper selection, temporaries) are made in one place,\n`src/backend/c_emitter.cee`, because NIR operands are already flat. LIR will be introduced when a\nsecond consumer appears or when drop elaboration makes the emitter non-trivial (M2/M7).\nThis is a recorded deviation from the plan, not an omission.\n\n## 5. The NIR reference interpreter (M7c)\n\n`ceesafec run --interp <files> [-- <arguments>]` executes a program's NIR directly\n(`src/interpreter/`). It serves as an oracle for the C backend. The C backend and\nthe interpreter consume the same checked NIR, after drop elaboration. Agreement between them\ntherefore tests the C backend and its runtime, not NIR lowering.\n\n### 5.1 Observable behaviour\n\nThe interpreter must produce exactly what the native program produces:\n\n- the bytes written to standard output and standard error, in the same order: every write to\n  standard error first flushes standard output (`ceesafe_rt_hosted.c`);\n- the exit status:\n  - `main`'s result, or `Process.exit`'s code (the operating system keeps the low 8 bits);\n  - 134 after a panic, as `abort()` gives natively;\n- the panic message (`ceesafe panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:<column>`",
    162 =>
        ");\n- the allocation counters: `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and the `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` line. The interpreter\n  counts every attempt that `ceesafe_alloc` would. This includes vector growth, stable-vector chunks,\n  slot-map slots, arena and scratch chunks, pool slots, and `Process.run`'s argument copies. The\n  allocation-failure sweep therefore sees the same attempts.\n\nTo get these, the runtime's algorithms are reimplemented step for step:\n- `ceesafe_rt_core.c`: growth (doubling, at least 4), arena chunks with their 16-byte header,\n  scratch chunks of 64 KiB, the pool slot size, and the panic and report texts;\n- the emitter's helpers (`c-backend.md` §2): push, pop and clear, slot-map generations, and the\n  drop-glue order.\n\n### 5.2 Memory model\n\nMemory is **byte-addressed**. Values have the target layout of `TypeTable.LayoutOf`, which is the\nsame layout the generated C asserts. Pointers are 8-byte addresses, and null is 0. Memory is a set of\n*allocations*, each with a base address aligned to 16 (as `malloc` gives) and a size:\n\n| Kind | Created by | Ends at |\n|---|---|---|\n| local | a function's entry (every local, zero-filled, as the C locals are) | `StorageDead`, or the function's return |\n| heap | `ceesafe_alloc` | `ceesafe_free` |\n| static | a string literal (read-only), the empty-vector sentinel (size 0) | never |\n\n`StorageDead` ends a local's allocation. The next write to that local (a whole assignment, or a\nwrite or borrow through one of its fields) gives it a *new* allocation, so an old reference to it\nstays dead.\n\n**Interpreter faults.** Each of the following stops the program with\n`ceesafe interp: fault: <what> at <file>:<line>:<column>` on standard error and exit status 70:\n- an access to an ended allocation, or outside an allocation's bounds;\n- a dereference of null;\n- a misaligned access through a raw pointer (M8a): the address is not a multiple of the pointee's\n  alignment;\n- a write to static memory;\n- `ceesafe_free` of anything but the base of a live heap allocation;\n- destroying a stack-placed box whose slot has already ended (M9e), the placed counterpart of a double free; and\n  running a placed site again while its previous box is alive (`memory-model.md` §5.1);\n- a *use* of uninitialised bytes. Heap memory starts uninitialised. Each byte carries an\n  initialised bit, which copies carry along: copying a struct with padding is not a use.\n  Reading a scalar to compute with it, to branch on it, to compare it, as a tag or as an address\n  is a use.\n- a bounds check that bounds-check elimination removed, and that fails (M9c, `optimisations.md` §2.8): the\n  interpreter evaluates every check, and the compiled program would not have made this one\n  (`an eliminated bounds check fails at …`). `ceesafec run --no-bce` turns the analysis off. Likewise an operation whose\n  overflow check was eliminated (`optimisations.md` §3) and that would overflow faults (`an eliminated overflow check\n  fails at …`); `ceesafec run --no-oce` turns that off.\n\nNo correct program can fault. A fault means the compiler, its runtime, or its checks let an\nunsafe program through, or (since M8a) that unsafe code broke its contract (`unsafe.md` §2). This makes the interpreter a dynamic safety oracle: liveness, bounds and\ninitialisation. It does not track borrows (no Stacked Borrows), so it does not catch aliasing\nviolations that stay in bounds.\n\n### 5.3 Execution\n\n- Calls recurse on the host stack. The interpreter runs on its own thread with a 1 GiB stack.\n- Drop glue and the runtime helpers are interpreted in the same order as the emitted C. A user\n  `drop` function, for example, runs before its fields' drops.\n- Arithmetic follows `IntegerSemantics` (type-system.md §6–7), the same table the checked C helpers\n  implement.\n\n### 5.4 The host\n\n- **Output.** Standard output, standard error and standard input are streams supplied by the\n  caller. The CLI uses the process's own.\n- **Arguments.** `argv[0]` is the first source file's path. The argume",
    163 =>
        "nts after `--` follow it.\n- **Configuration.** The CLI reads `CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and `CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT` as\n  `ceesafe_platform_config` does (leading digits).\n- **Files and sockets** use the host C library through P/Invoke (`open`, `read`, `write`,\n  `close`, `unlink`, `socket`, …) on Linux and macOS, with the same flags and errno mapping as\n  `ceesafe_rt_io.c`. On Windows, I/O is an interpreter fault (\"unsupported\").\n- **Working directory.** An optional working directory resolves relative file paths and is\n  where child processes start. Tests use it, since they run in parallel in one process. The CLI\n  uses the process's own directory.\n- **`Process.run`** starts the program with lib/std's `Process.run`, after flushing standard output.\n  When the interpreter's output streams are the process's own, the child inherits them. When the\n  caller captures output (tests), the child's output is copied into the captured streams.\n\n**C code (M8b).** The interpreter runs no C. A call to an `extern \"C\"` function is interpreted only\nfor this model of the C library, used when the declaration's parameter and result types have the\nexpected shape (integers, `bool` or pointers of the right widths):\n`strlen` (reads bytes up to a zero byte, each access checked), `puts` (writes the bytes and a\nnewline to standard output, returns 0), `abs`, `malloc` and `free` (C5: a heap allocation of the\ninterpreter's memory, not counted as CeeSafe's), `memcmp`, `memcpy` and `memset` (checked accesses;\n`memcpy` with overlapping ranges is a fault, as it is undefined in C). Any other extern call is the\nfault `unsupported: call to C function '<name>'`. Exported functions are ordinary CeeSafe functions.\n**User allocators (C5).** A box with a user allocator gets its memory from the allocator's\n`allocate` method and returns it through `deallocate`, run as CeeSafe code; `this` is a one-byte\nread-only allocation per allocator type.\n**Function pointers (M8d).** The C address of an extern or exported function is a value in a range\nno allocation uses (from `0xFFFF000000000000`); a call through it runs the exported function, or\nthe C model for an extern. A call through null or any other value is a fault. Volatile accesses\nare performed like other accesses (the interpreter does every access once, in order).\n\n### 5.5a Placement and the drop trace (M5)\n\n- `InterpreterOptions.Placement` (on by default since M9e, like the C backend's) applies the same\n  `PlacementAnalysis` as the C backend. A stack-placed site stores the value in a new *local* allocation, which is\n  not counted. If the site's previous allocation is still live when it runs again, that is a fault: natively the site\n  has one slot (`memory-model.md` §5.1).\n- A `Drop` of the site's box local destroys the contents, then ends that allocation. A `BoxFree`\n  just ends it. Ending it there, where C's slot lives on until the function returns, makes a\n  late use a fault: a stronger check.\n- `InterpreterOptions.DropTrace`, when given a list, records every executed `Drop` statement as\n  `<function> <line>:<column> <type>`, in order. M5's drop-timing check compares the traces\n  with and without placement.\n\n### 5.5 Agreement tests\n\n- Nova's `InterpreterTests` run every run-pass and run-fail program (in CeeSafe, `tools/compare_interp.py` compares `ceesafec run --interp` with novac's on them). Standard output, standard error\n  and exit status must equal the corpus expectations. The native runs check the same\n  expectations under every C configuration.\n- The allocation-failure sweep runs the interpreter beside the native ASan build, for the\n  baseline and at every failure point. The outcomes must be equal.\n",
    164 =>
        "# CeeSafe Language Specification — Initial Draft (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0. Defines the **MVP surface** (milestones 1–4) and marks\n*future proposals* explicitly. Semantics of ownership and borrowing are in\n`ownership.md` (normative); storage and allocators in `memory-model.md`.\nThe formal grammar will be written in `grammar.md` during M1. Until then this\ndocument is the reference.\n\nLegend used in every example heading:\n- **[MVP-M1]** … **[MVP-M6]**: accepted for that milestone.\n- **[FUTURE]**: proposal only; not to be implemented without an approved ADR.\n\n## 1. Lexical structure (summary)\n\n- UTF-8 source. Identifiers `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` (ASCII in v0).\n- Comments `//`, `/* */` (non-nesting), doc comments `///`.\n- Integer literals: decimal, `0x`, `0b`, `0o`, `_` separators, optional suffix\n  (`10u8`, `3i64`). An unsuffixed literal takes its type from context; with no\n  context it defaults to `i32`. A literal that doesn't fit its type is a compile error.\n- Float literals `1.0`, `1e9`, suffix `f32`/`f64` (default `f64`).\n- String literals `\"...\"` (escapes `\\n \\t \\\\ \\\" \\0 \\u{...}`) have type\n  `StringView` with static region. Char literals `'a'` are integer literals of the character's Unicode\n  scalar value, `u32` by default, ASCII in a byte (M11; `type-system.md` §4). There is no `char` type.\n- Keywords: `namespace import public private internal fn let var struct enum\n  interface impl static const if else while for in loop break continue return\n  match using defer own borrow mut move unsafe extern true false null this Self\n  as type where drop`. Reserved: `class async await yield macro`.\n\n## 2. Types (MVP)\n\n| Type | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64 u8 u16 u32 u64` | Fixed-width two's complement; overflow panics (`ownership.md` §0) |\n| `isize usize` | Pointer-sized, per *target* |\n| `f32 f64` | IEEE 754; C `float`/`double` |\n| `bool` | `true`/`false`; C `_Bool` |\n| `char` | Unicode scalar value (u32 with validity invariant) |\n| `void` | Unit return type |\n| `Array<T, N>` / `[T; N]` | Fixed-size inline array (Copy if T is Copy) |\n| `Slice<T>` | Scoped: borrowed `(ptr, len)` view; `SliceMut<T>` exclusive |\n| `StringView` | Scoped: borrowed UTF-8 `(ptr, len)` |\n| `String<A>` | Owned UTF-8, allocator `A` |\n| `Box<T, A>` | Owned single heap object |\n| `Vector<T, A>` | Owned growable array |\n| `T?` | Optional. Niche-optimised for `Box` and references, otherwise `{bool, T}` |\n| `Result<T, E>` | Tagged union |\n| `borrow T`, `borrow mut T` | References (scoped). Never null |\n| `*T`, `*mut T` | Raw pointers (unsafe to dereference) |\n| `fn(A, B) -> R` | Plain function pointer (no closure environment) |\n\nDefault allocator type parameter: in the **userland** profile `A` defaults to\n`SystemHeap`. In the **kernel** profile there is no default; it must be explicit\n(`profiles.md`).\n\nConversions: no implicit numeric conversions except widening of an integer\n**literal**. `as` performs only lossless numeric conversions (`u8 as u32`,\n`i32 as i64`, `i32 as f64`). Lossy conversions use `truncate<T>()`,\n`checkedCast<T>()` or `f.toIntSaturating<T>()`.\n\n## 3. Declarations and statements (MVP)\n\n- `let x = e;` immutable binding. `var x = e;` mutable binding. A type annotation\n  is optional (`let x: u64 = 0;`). Bindings must be definitely initialised before\n  use (`var x: i32;` followed by assignment on all paths is allowed).\n- `fn name(p: T, ...) -> R { ... }`, `fn name(...) -> R => expr;`\n- Parameter modes: *by value* (default: copy if Copy, otherwise move),\n  `borrow T`, `borrow mut T`. `own T` is an explicit spelling of by-value for\n  move types, accepted for readability.\n- Call-site borrowing: an argument for a `borrow T` parameter is borrowed\n  implicitly. An argument for a `borrow mut T` parameter must be written\n  `mut x` (as with C# `ref`), so mutation is visible at the call site.\n  Explicit borrow expressions elsewhere: `borrow x`, `borrow mut x`.\n- `move x` explicitly moves (optional; moves of move types are implicit).\n- `if`/`else`,",
    165 =>
        " `while`, `loop`, `for i in a..b` (half-open integer range),\n  `for x in slice` (borrowing iteration), `break`, `continue`, `return`.\n- `match` over enums and integers, exhaustive.\n- `using x = e;`: pinned scoped binding (`ownership.md` R3).\n- `defer stmt;`: runs `stmt` at scope exit on every path (in reverse order of\n  `defer` statements). The deferred statement may not `return`/`break`/`?`.\n- Error propagation `e?`: on `Result<T,E>` yields `T` or returns `Err(e)`\n  converted by `From<E>` (M6; v0 requires identical `E`); on `T?` yields\n  `T` or returns `null`. Postfix `!` unwraps or panics.\n- Evaluation order: strictly left to right, receiver before arguments,\n  arguments before call, and right-hand side of assignment **after** the\n  left-hand side place is evaluated (as in C#). This is specified and the\n  C backend must not delegate it to C's unspecified order (`c-backend.md` §4).\n\n## 4. Example programs\n\n### Ex1 — Functions and arithmetic [MVP-M1]\n\n```ceesafe\nnamespace Demo;\n\nfn gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {\n    var x = a;\n    var y = b;\n    while (y != 0) {\n        let t = x % y;\n        x = y;\n        y = t;\n    }\n    return x;\n}\n\nfn square(x: i64) -> i64 => x * x;      // overflow panics\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    Console.writeLine(gcd(1071, 462));  // 21\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex2 — Structs and methods (Copy value type) [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Point {\n    public x: f64;\n    public y: f64;\n\n    public fn distance(this, other: Point) -> f64 {\n        let dx = this.x - other.x;\n        let dy = this.y - other.y;\n        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let p = Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };\n    let q = p;                            // Copy: p still usable\n    Console.writeLine(p.distance(Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 }));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n`this` alone = by value (copy for Copy types); `borrow this` and\n`borrow mut this` are the reference receivers.\n\n### Ex3 — Move-only resource with `drop` [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Fd {\n    raw: i32;\n\n    drop {                      // runs exactly once, when the owner dies\n        unsafe { Sys.close(this.raw); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn consume(fd: own Fd) -> void { }        // fd dropped at end of consume\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let fd = Fd { raw: 3 };\n    consume(fd);\n    // consume(fd);   // error[O3]: use of moved value `fd`\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nA struct with `drop` is never Copy. Fields are dropped after the `drop`\nblock, in declaration order.\n\n### Ex4 — Equivalent of classes: owned heap reference type [MVP-M3]\n\nCeeSafe v0 has **no `class` keyword** (reserved). The C#/TS \"object on the heap with\nidentity\" idiom is written as a `struct` behind `Box`:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) -> void {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });   // A = SystemHeap (userland)\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var acct = open(7)!;          // panics on allocation failure\n    acct.deposit(100);            // auto-deref + implicit mut reborrow of *acct\n    return 0;\n}                                  // Box dropped: Account dropped, memory freed\n```\n\n*[FUTURE]* `class Account { ... }` as sugar for a non-Copy type that is always\n`Box`-allocated (OD-4).\n\n### Ex5 — Heap allocation with an explicit allocator [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct User { id: u64; age: u32; }\n\nfn createUser<A: Allocator>(alloc: A) -> Result<Box<User, A>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.createIn(alloc, User { id: 100, age: 25 });\n}\n```\n\n`A` is an *allocator type* (`memory-model.md` §3). For free-standing allocators\n(`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap`) the value is a zero-sized token, so\n`Box<User, KernelHeap>` is one pointer wide.\n\n### Ex6 — Borrowing [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn inspect(buffer: borrow Buffer) -> usize {\n    return buffer.length;\n}\n\nfn modify(buffer: borrow mut Buffer) -> void {\n    buffer.clear();\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n  ",
    166 =>
        "  var b = Buffer.create()!;\n    let n = inspect(b);             // implicit shared borrow\n    modify(mut b);                  // explicit mutable borrow at call site\n    return n as i32;                // error: usize → i32 is not lossless; use checkedCast\n}\n```\n\n### Ex7 — Returning a borrow [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn first(values: Slice<i32>) -> borrow i32 {     // elided: derived from `values`\n    return borrow values[0];                     // bounds-checked\n}\n\nfn longer(a: StringView, b: StringView) -> borrow(a, b) StringView {\n    return if (a.length >= b.length) a else b;   // region ⊆ both loans\n}\n```\n\n### Ex8 — Fixed arrays and slices [MVP-M2]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn sum(values: Slice<i64>) -> i64 {\n    var total: i64 = 0;\n    for v in values { total += v; }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var data: [i64; 4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n    data[2] = 10;\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[..]));      // whole-array slice\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[1..3]));    // checked sub-slice\n    // data[4] = 0;                        // compile error: constant index out of range\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex9 — Vectors and invalidation [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();         // no allocation until first push\n    v.push(1)!;                            // push returns Result<void, AllocError>\n    v.push(v.length as i32)!;              // two-phase borrow (B5)\n\n    let r = borrow v[0];\n    // v.push(3)!;                         // error[B1]: `v` is borrowed by `r`\n    Console.writeLine(r);\n    v.push(3)!;                            // fine: `r` is dead (NLL)\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex10 — Strings [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn greet(name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n    var s = String.withCapacity(name.length + 7)?;\n    s.append(\"Hello, \")?;\n    s.append(name)?;\n    return Ok(s);\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let msg = greet(\"CeeSafe\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(msg.view());         // StringView borrowed from msg\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex11 — Error handling [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError { Empty, BadDigit(u8), Overflow }\n\nfn parseU32(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length == 0) { return Err(ParseError.Empty); }\n    var value: u32 = 0;\n    for c in text.bytes() {\n        if (c < '0' as u8 || c > '9' as u8) { return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)); }\n        value = value.checkedMul(10)?.checkedAdd((c - '0' as u8) as u32)\n                     .okOr(ParseError.Overflow)?;\n    }\n    return Ok(value);\n}\n```\n\n(`checkedMul` returns `u32?`; `?` on an optional inside a function returning\n`Result` is an error in v0. The example converts it with `okOr` and then uses `?`.)\n\n### Ex12 — Generics with interface constraints [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Comparable<T> {\n    fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow T) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn max<T: Comparable<T>>(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a, b) T {\n    return if (a.compare(b) >= 0) a else b;\n}\n```\n\nGenerics are monomorphised. Interfaces are static constraints in v0. Dynamic\ndispatch (`dyn Interface`) is *[FUTURE]* (OD-10).\n\n### Ex13 — Interfaces and implementations [MVP-M6]\n\n```ceesafe\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> f64;\n}\n\nstruct Circle { r: f64; }\nstruct Rect { w: f64; h: f64; }\n\nimpl Shape for Circle { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => 3.141592653589793 * this.r * this.r; }\nimpl Shape for Rect   { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => this.w * this.h; }\n\nfn total<S: Shape>(shapes: Slice<S>) -> f64 {\n    var t = 0.0;\n    for s in shapes { t += s.area(); }\n    return t;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex14 — Recursive structure (owned tree) [MVP-M3]\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Tree {\n    Leaf,\n    Node(Box<TreeNode>),\n}\n\nstruct TreeNode { value: i32; left: Tree; right: Tree; }\n\nfn insert(t: own Tree, v: i32) -> Result<Tree, AllocError> {\n    match (t) {\n        Tree.Leaf => return Ok(Tree.Node(Box.create(TreeNode {\n            value: v, left: Tree.Leaf, right: Tree.Leaf })?)),\n        Tree.Node(var n) => {\n            if (v < n.value) { n.left = i",
    167 =>
        "nsert(move n.left, v)?; }\n            else             { n.right = insert(move n.right, v)?; }\n            return Ok(Tree.Node(n));\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMoving out of `n.left` (a field of a uniquely owned `Box` contents whose type\nhas no `drop`) is a partial move (O2) immediately repaired by assignment.\nThe checker requires the field to be re-initialised before `n` is used whole.\nOn the `?` error path `n` is partially moved: drop elaboration drops only `n.right`\nand `n.value` (trivial), then frees the box. This is a key M3 test.\n\nDestruction of a deep tree is recursive in v0. A stack-depth concern is recorded\nin `risks.md` (R-9).\n\n### Ex15 — Scoped arena [MVP-M4]\n\n```ceesafe\nfn process(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap, chunkSize: 64 * 1024)?;\n\n    let counts = arena.allocArray<u32>(256)?;     // scoped to `arena`, zero-filled\n    for b in input { counts[b as usize] += 1; }\n\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for c in counts { if (c != 0) { distinct += 1; } }\n    return Ok(distinct);                          // returns a u64, not arena memory\n}                                                 // arena freed here; no live loans\n```\n\n`allocArray<T>` requires `T` without `drop` (R2).\n\n### Ex16 — Custom allocator [MVP-M4]\n\n```ceesafe\n// A fixed-buffer allocator over caller-provided storage.\nfn parseHeaders(raw: Slice<u8>) -> Result<usize, AllocError> {\n    var storage: [u8; 4096] = [0; 4096];\n    using fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);  // scoped to `storage`\n    let fields = fba.allocArray<Slice<u8>>(32)?;          // error[E3]: arena element type is scoped\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe error is intentional: storing borrowed slices inside region memory needs\nlifetime-parameterised containers (OD-3). The accepted v0 form stores\n`(offset, length)` pairs:\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Span { start: u32; len: u32; }\nlet fields = fba.allocArray<Span>(32)?;\n```\n\nA user-defined allocator implements the `Allocator` interface\n(`memory-model.md` §3.2) inside `unsafe` code with a documented contract.\n\n### Ex17 — C interoperability [M8b; `type-system.md` §28]\n\n```ceesafe\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn write(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> isize;\n}\n\n/// Safety: `bytes` is a valid borrowed slice; write() does not retain the pointer.\npublic fn writeAll(fd: i32, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> bool {\n    var off: usize = 0;\n    while (off < bytes.length) {\n        let rest = bytes[off..];\n        let n = unsafe { write(fd, rest.ptr, rest.length) };\n        if (n < 0) { return false; }\n        off += truncate<usize>(n); // n >= 0 here\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the caller passes `len` readable bytes at `data` (the C side's contract).\n@export(\"geo_checksum\")             // C-callable symbol; `ceesafe_` names belong to the runtime\npublic unsafe fn checksum(data: *u8, len: usize) -> u32 {\n    let s = Slice.fromRaw(data, len);\n    var h: u32 = 2166136261;\n    for (b in s) { h = (h ^ b as u32).wrappingMul(16777619); }\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\nM8b changed this example from its M0 form: `@export` alone makes a function C-callable (no\n`extern \"C\"` on definitions), and an export that reads through a pointer is `unsafe fn`, since a\nsafe CeeSafe caller could pass any pointer. `IoError.fromErrno()`, `checkedCast` and `wrappingMul` are\nnot built in yet. `run-pass/ffi-libc.cee` is the running form of this example, with `puts`, `strlen`\nand `memcpy` in place of `write`, which is not in every C library (MSVC calls it `_write`).\n\n### Ex18 — Unsafe systems operations: MMIO [M8d; `type-system.md` §29]\n\n```ceesafe\n@repr(C)\nstruct UartRegs {\n    data: Volatile<u32>;\n    status: Volatile<u32>;\n}\n\nstruct Uart {\n    regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>;\n\n    /// Safety: `base` must be the mapped address of a PL011-compatible UART that no other `Uart`\n    /// value aliases.\n    public unsafe fn attach(base: usize) -> Uart => Uart { regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>.at(base) };\n\n    public fn putByte(borrow mut this, b: u8) -> void {",
    168 =>
        "\n        while ((this.regs.get().status.read() & 0x20) != 0) { Cpu.spinHint(); }\n        this.regs.getMut().data.write(b as u32);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, `PhysAddr` and `VirtAddr` come from `lib/core`, CeeSafe sources bundled\nwith the compiler (§29.2). The address types are distinct, with no implicit conversions\n(`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`); `DmaAddr` comes with the kernel profile. M8d changed\nthis example from its M0 form: `attach` takes the address as a `usize`, and register access goes\nthrough `get()` / `getMut()`, whose results borrow the `MmioPtr`. It runs as\n`run-pass/mmio.cee` and, freestanding, as `freestanding/uart.cee`.\n\n### Ex19 — Kernel-context effects [FUTURE, C7]\n\n```ceesafe\n@context(irq)            // implies @noalloc @nosleep, transitively checked\nfn timerIrq(ctx: borrow mut IrqContext) -> void {\n    ctx.ackTimer();\n    // KernelHeap.create(...);   // error: allocation not permitted in irq context\n}\n```\n\n## 5. Packages and visibility (M10, ADR 0013)\n\n- A *package* is a directory with `ceesafe.toml`; its sources are the `.cee` files under `src/` (`tooling.md` §4).\n- All files of a package share one scope. Another package's `public` items are named with that package's name,\n  `geometry.Point`, or brought into a file's scope with `import geometry;` or `import geometry.Point;`\n  (`type-system.md` §2).\n- `public` (visible to other packages), `internal` (default: visible within the package), `private` (visible within\n  the declaring file).\n- `namespace X;` declarations are retired: accepted, with no effect.\n\n## 6. Not in the language (v0) — deliberate omissions\n\nExceptions, implicit reference counting, tracing GC, inheritance, implicit\nnumeric conversions, null references, `async/await` (reserved; post-M9),\nclosures that capture (M6+, OD-9), operator overloading (M6+), macros.\n",
    169 =>
        "# References and the Borrow Checker (M2b)\n\nStatus: normative for increment 2b. This document turns `ownership.md` invariants B1–B5 and E1–E3\ninto an algorithm on NIR. The surface rules for references are in `type-system.md` §13.\nWhere this document is stricter than `ownership.md`, it records a v0 simplification that later\nmilestones may relax.\n\n## 1. What makes the v0 problem small\n\nThe algorithm can be simple and **exact for v0** because of four language rules (type-system §13):\n\n1. **References are values of type `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` held only in locals and temporaries.**\n   They never appear in struct fields (E3) or constants.\n2. **A reference binding is immutable and initialised at its declaration** (`let r = …;`; no\n   `var r: borrow T`). Assigning to `r` writes *through* it (`*r = v`) and never re-points it.\n   So each reference local is assigned exactly once, which makes the points-to sets below\n   flow-insensitive without losing precision.\n3. **No nested references** (`borrow borrow T` is rejected).\n4. **A use of a `borrow mut T` value is an implicit reborrow** of its referent (§4). Two usable\n   mutable references to one place can therefore never coexist.\n\nTogether with lexical scoping, (1) and (2) mean a reference cannot outlive its referent *inside*\na function, except through a **temporary** borrowed at a call site, or through **`return`**. Both\nare checked explicitly (§6, §7). Conflicting accesses while a loan is live (B1/B2) are the\nremaining work, and they need liveness (§5).\n\n## 2. NIR additions\n\n```text\nprojection = Field(f) | Deref | Downcast(V) | Index(operand)\nplace      = local projection*                 -- (*r).f is  r Deref Field(f)\nrvalue    += Ref(Shared | Mut, place)           -- creates a loan\n           | Slice(Shared | Mut, place, lo, hi) -- M2d: also a loan, of the whole array or slice viewed\nstmt      += StorageDead(local)                 -- the local's storage ends (scope exit, statement end for temps)\n```\n\nLowering facts the checker relies on:\n- Every `Ref` is assigned directly to a local or temporary: `t = Ref(k, p)`.\n- A temporary lives until the end of the source statement that created it. At that point the\n  builder emits `StorageDead(t)`. The condition of an `if` or `while` counts as its own statement\n  (M15 S2): its temporaries end once it is evaluated, before the branch, and each evaluation of a\n  `while` condition ends its own. A borrow taken in a condition therefore does not reach the body.\n- User locals get `StorageDead` at the end of their block, and on `break`/`continue` for the\n  blocks being left. (`return` ends every local; references escaping through `return` are §7's job.)\n- A use of a `borrow mut T` operand at a call argument, an initialiser or a return is lowered as\n  `Ref(Mut, (*r))`, a reborrow. A coercion of `borrow mut T` to `borrow T` is `Ref(Shared, (*r))`.\n\n## 3. Loans and points-to sets\n\n- Each `t = Ref(k, p)` at program point P creates a **loan** `L = (k, p, P)`.\n- Each reference local `r` has a set `loans(r)` of the loans it may refer to (directly or through\n  reborrows). It is the least solution of:\n\n| NIR | Constraint |\n|---|---|\n| `t = Ref(k, p)` | `L ∈ loans(t)`; and if `p` contains `Deref` of a reference local `q`, then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)` (a reborrow keeps the original loan alive) |\n| `t = Slice(k, p, lo, hi)` | as `Ref(k, p)`: a slice is a reference to `[T]`, and its loan covers all of `p`, whatever the range |\n| `t = Use(Place(r))` with `r` of reference type | `loans(r) ⊆ loans(t)` |\n| `t = Call(f, args)` where `f` returns a reference | `loans(arg_i) ⊆ loans(t)` for every parameter `i` that `f`'s signature says the result is derived from (§7) |\n| reference **parameter** `p_i` | `Param_i ∈ loans(p_i)`, a placeholder loan for \"whatever the caller lent\" |\n\nBecause every reference local is assigned once (§1), solving these subset constraints by\nfixpoint iteration gives the same result as a flow-sensitive analysis.\n\n**Loan-holdin",
    170 =>
        "g locals (M4a).** Since M4a, every local of a *scoped* type holds loans\n(`type-system.md` §19.1), not only reference locals. The constraints generalise:\n- **Copies and moves.** When an assignment's rvalue reads a place rooted at a loan-holding local\n  `q` (`Use`, aggregates, enum values, built-in calls), then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. This covers a\n  payload read such as `t = (q as Ok).0`.\n- **`Unpack`.** `Unpack(q, …)` gives each target `loans(q)`.\n- **Borrowing a loan holder.** `Ref(k, p)` where `p` is rooted at a loan-holding local `q` and does\n  not go through a deref: `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. A reference to a scoped value keeps that\n  value's loans alive.\n- Liveness (§5) and the return check (§7) consider all loan-holding locals.\n\n## 4. Accesses\n\nEach NIR statement and terminator performs **accesses** to places, in evaluation order:\n\n| Construct | Access |\n|---|---|\n| operand `Place(p)` | **Read** p |\n| `Ref(Shared, p)` | **BorrowShared** p |\n| `Ref(Mut, p)` | **BorrowMut** p |\n| `Assign(p, …)` | the rvalue's accesses, then **Write** p |\n| `StorageDead(l)` | **Dead** l |\n\nTwo places **overlap** if they have the same root local and one projection list is a prefix of\nthe other. `a` overlaps `a.f`, `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`, and `(*r)` overlaps `(*r).f`.\nTwo `Index` projections always compare equal (M2d): `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]`, because v0 does no\nindex reasoning. Two mutable slices of one array therefore cannot be live at once, even if\ntheir ranges are disjoint.\nPlaces with different roots never overlap. That is correct because aliasing between different\nroots only arises through loans, and every loan is checked when it is created.\n\n**Conflicts** (B1, B2):\n\n| Live loan \\ access | Read | Write | BorrowShared | BorrowMut | Dead |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared | ok | **conflict** | ok | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n| Mut | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n\n## 5. Liveness (NLL)\n\n- A reference local is **live** at a point if some path from that point reads it (as an operand\n  or as the root of a place) before the function ends. Reference locals are never redefined\n  (§1), so there is no kill except at the definition. This is standard backward dataflow over the\n  NIR CFG.\n- The **live loans after point P** are the union of `loans(r)` over every reference local `r` live\n  after P.\n- **Check:** for every access `a` at point P to place `q`, and every loan `L` live after P with\n  `L` not created at P, it is an error if `L.place` overlaps `q` and the table in §4 says\n  conflict. The errors are N0240 (write), N0241 (borrow), N0242 (read) and N0243 (Dead: the\n  place stops existing while borrowed).\n\nThis accepts `let r = borrow x; print(r); x = 5;`, because `r` is dead after `print`. It rejects\n`let r = borrow x; x = 5; print(r);`.\n\n**Overwriting a reference is not a use of its referent (M11, G3).** A **Write** or **Dead** of a local `r` whose\ntype is a reference does not conflict with a loan whose place goes through `*r`, such as `(*r).next`. The write\nreplaces which object `r` points to; it does not change, move or end the object it pointed to. That object stays\nprotected by the loans the new reference inherited from `r` when it was made (§3: a reborrow `Ref(k, (*r)…)` gets\n`loans(r)`). A box is not a reference: overwriting a box destroys its contents, so a loan through `*b` still conflicts.\nThis accepts a cursor walking a list, `node = cursor!; … cursor = next_of(node);`, and a temporary that is reassigned\nin each iteration of a loop while a reference made through the previous value lives on.\n\n**Writes happen after the value (M2d).** In `Assign(p, rvalue)` the rvalue is evaluated first and\n`p` is written last. The **Write** access is therefore checked against the loans live *after*\nthe statement; the rvalue's own accesses use the loans live before it. This accepts\n`a[0] = sum(a[..])`: the slice is dead once `sum` has returned, before `a[0]` is written.\nRust's MIR gets th",
    171 =>
        "e same result by writing a call's result in a separate step.\n\n**B5, two-phase borrows (as implemented).** For a method call `e.m(args)` whose receiver is\n`borrow mut this` and `e` is a place, the receiver's loan is created **after** `args` are\nevaluated, right before the call. Arguments may therefore read `e` (`v.push(v.len())`), but\nthey cannot hold a borrow of `e` across the call. Evaluating a place has no side effects, so\ncreating its loan later does not change what the program computes. The loan exists only for the\ncall, which is exactly the interval in which it must be exclusive.\n\n## 6. Temporaries\n\n`f(makeValue())` with a `borrow T` parameter borrows a temporary that dies at the end of the\nstatement (`StorageDead`). If the result of `f` is derived from that argument and is stored in a\nreference local used after the statement, the `Dead` access conflicts with the live loan: N0243\n(\"temporary dropped while still borrowed\"). The fix is to bind the value to a local first.\n\n## 7. Returning references (E1, E2)\n\n- **Signature.** A function whose return type is a reference must name the parameters the result\n  may be derived from. With exactly one reference parameter (a `borrow`/`borrow mut` receiver\n  counts), that parameter is implied (elision). Otherwise the return type must be written\n  `borrow(a, b) T` or `borrow mut(a) T`, naming reference parameters (N0245 if missing or wrong).\n  `borrow mut` results may be derived from `borrow mut` parameters only.\n- **Body check at each `Return(op)`.** For every loan `L ∈ loans(op)`:\n  - `L = Param_i`: `i` must be among the named parameters (N0245);\n  - `L` is a real loan whose place starts with `Deref` of reference parameter `p_i`: allowed if\n    `i` is named (N0245 otherwise). Examples: `borrow this.items` with `this: borrow Self`, or\n    `borrow (*a).f`;\n  - any other real loan borrows a local or temporary of this function: N0244 (\"returns a reference\n    to local data\").\n- **Callers** use the signature as the `Call` constraint in §3.\n\n## 8. What the v0 checker deliberately does not do\n\n- No references in aggregates, and no reference re-pointing — **relaxed in M6x** (OD-3,\n  `type-system.md` §25): aggregates with region parameters, scoped collections and re-pointable\n  reference fields. The checker keeps one loan set per value; writes into fields, elements and\n  through references add the written loans to the holder and to what its mutable loans point to\n  (iterated to a fixpoint); calls apply their signature flows; and a loan conflicts only at points\n  reachable from where it was created (holders such as a vector receive loans after their own\n  definition). Destroying a value whose `drop` may read its loans is a use of them.\n- Index-sensitive disjointness (`a[i]` versus `a[j]`) came in 2d with arrays, conservatively: all elements\n  overlap. Since M13 G5, two different **constant** indices are different places, so `borrow mut a[0]` and\n  `borrow mut a[2]` may be live together. That is sound because a constant index names one element whatever the\n  array's length. Any index that is not a constant still overlaps every element.\n- Moves and drops are 2c. The `StorageDead` points emitted now are where drops will go.\n\n## 9. Diagnostics\n\nEvery borrow error names the conflicting access (primary span) and carries notes for the loan's\ncreation point and for a later use of the reference that keeps it live, when one exists.\n",
