        "ring<A>` / `StringView` | owns / scoped | as Vector | as Vector | UTF-8 invariant |\n| `Box<T, A>` | owns one object | — | loans of the box | `T?` niche |\n\nIterator invalidation is impossible in safe code: iterators are scoped values\nholding a loan of the collection.\n\n## 5. Placement analysis (M5; on by default since M9e) — rules\n\nThe analysis may change *where* a value lives only under these conditions:\n\n1. **Box → native stack / scratch**: allowed iff (a) the Box is created and\n   dropped in the same function on all paths, (b) it is never moved into a\n   callee or returned, (c) its address is not exposed to `unsafe`/FFI, (d)\n   the profile marks `A` as *placement-transparent* (`SystemHeap` in userland: yes;\n   **`KernelHeap`: no**, because kernel allocation is observable through\n   accounting, and failure injection must remain meaningful), and (e) the size is\n   below the profile's stack limit. Failure behaviour: the original program could fail\n   with `AllocError`, and the optimised one cannot. This is permitted only because the\n   placement-transparent contract declares allocation failure of that allocator\n   \"may spuriously succeed\".\n2. **Never** the reverse: a scoped/borrowed value is never promoted to the heap to\n   make a program type-check (master prompt §7 and rule 11). There is no such\n   code path, because placement runs after the borrow check on accepted programs only.\n3. Destruction order and timing are preserved (master prompt rule 12): drops of\n   demoted Boxes run at exactly the same NIR points.\n4. Every decision is reported by `--explain-allocations`:\n   `src/a.cee:12:9: Box<Node> placed on stack (does not escape; SystemHeap is placement-transparent)`\n   `src/a.cee:20:9: Box<Node> stays on heap: moved into call to 'insert' at 21:5`\n\n### 5.1 The M5 implementation (`PlacementAnalysis`)\n\nThe analysis works per function on NIR after drop elaboration. It is deliberately simple and\nconservative: a box it cannot follow stays on the heap. Rule 1's conditions become the\nfollowing checks.\n\n- **Sites.** A site is `R = Box.create(v)` or `Box.createIn(a, v)`, with `R` a whole local. The\n  site is **eligible** when:\n  - the allocator is `SystemHeap`, the only placement-transparent allocator so far (rule 1d).\n    `FailingAllocator` is not transparent: its failures are its purpose;\n  - `T` is at most 1 KiB, and the function's stack-placed boxes total at most 4 KiB (rule 1e);\n  - each site has at most one box alive at a time, so one storage slot per site suffices. A site in\n    no loop of the function's graph has this by construction. A site in a loop has it when every\n    local of its flow set `F` (below) ends, by its `StorageDead`, on every path from the site back to\n    itself (M9e): no local can then hold one execution's box when the site runs again, and since the box\n    does not escape, it has been dropped by then. A local that is not ended inside the loop keeps the\n    box on the heap (\"may outlive its loop iteration\"). The interpreter checks this rule: a site whose\n    previous box is still alive when it runs again is an interpreter fault.\n- **The flow set** `F` of a site is the set of locals that may hold its box: `R` itself, and every\n  whole local assigned (moved or copied) from a place rooted in `F` whose type is the box type or\n  `R`'s result type. That includes the unpacked `Ok` payload and copies of the result. The\n  analysis iterates until `F` stops growing.\n- **Escapes.** The box stays on the heap, with the first reason found, if:\n  1. a local in `F` has more than one definition. Its drops could then see a box from elsewhere;\n  2. a value of the box type or of `R`'s type, read from a place rooted in `F`, flows anywhere\n     but into a local of `F`: a call or built-in argument, an aggregate, a return, or a\n     non-local place (rule 1b);\n  3. a place rooted in `F` of the box type is borrowed **mutably** (`borrow mut Box<T>`). The\n     borrower could replace the box, and drop ours with the heap's drop glu",
