        "e. Borrowing the box\n     shared, or borrowing its contents either way, is fine: the borrow checker ends those\n     borrows before the box's drop;\n  4. a `Drop` destroys a place rooted in `F` that is neither a whole local of the box type nor\n     inside its contents. Destroying contents in place is fine: drop elaboration does that to\n     the fields left after a move out. A result holding the box, though, would be destroyed by\n     the heap's drop glue. A result *may* be destroyed where it cannot hold the box: in a block\n     dominated by a non-`Ok` target of a switch on that result's own discriminant, where the target\n     is entered only from the switch. That is the error path of `?` and `!`, whose drop glue sees\n     an `Err`.\n\n  Reads through the box (`b.f`, `b.f = x`, moving a field out) and `discriminant` of the result\n  are not escapes. Rule 1c (unsafe and FFI), from M8a: a function that makes a raw pointer from a reference, or a\n  reference or slice from a raw pointer, keeps all its boxes on the heap (reason \"address exposed\";\n  `type-system.md` §27.5). Rule 1a (created and\n  dropped in one function) follows from rules 2 and 4.\n- **Demotion.** A stack-placed site writes `v` into a per-site slot of the function's frame and\n  makes `Ok(&slot)` without allocating; that allocation cannot fail.\n  - A `Drop` of a box local in `F` destroys the contents only, and does not free.\n  - A `BoxFree` of one does nothing.\n  - Drops therefore run at the same NIR points with the same contents (rule 3).\n\n## 6. No-GC baseline and cycles\n\nNo tracing GC, no implicit RC. Ownership is a forest (H3). Graph-shaped data\nuses one of: `SlotMap` handles, arena + indices, or (future, OD-13) an explicit\n`Rc<T>`/`Weak<T>` library type with documented cycle-leak behaviour. In\nthe kernel, explicit refcounted *kernel objects* wrap CosmoOS's own refcount\nprotocol (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`). That is a different\ntype (`KRef<T>`) with type-specific release, not a generic `free`.\n\n## 7. Goose technique adoption summary\n\n| Goose technique (goose.md §9) | CeeSafe adoption | Milestone |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data stacks for scoped temporaries | `Scratch` regions (§3.4), with heap fallback and a no-MMU kernel mode | M4/M5 |\n| One-growable-on-top non-moving growth | Not adopted; `StableVector` instead | M6 |\n| Grow-only vs grow-shrink split | `StableVector`, monotone lengths feed BCE | M6/M9 |\n| Root inference by monomorphisation | **Rejected** (whole-program, fragile). NLL + E1–E4 instead | — |\n| Construction into destination / guaranteed NRVO | NIR destination places + guaranteed RVO for single-named-result functions | M2 (RVO), M7 |\n| Inline variable-size layouts | Future opt-in `@flat` types (OD-14) | post-M9 |\n| Relative references + verifier | Future library feature for zero-copy formats | post-M9 |\n| Type-safe pools | `Pool<T>`/`SlotMap<T>` with generations | M6 |\n| BCE with index types, 2^48 size cap, `checkfree` | Adopt the size cap in the spec now (all `len ≤ 2^48`), BCE + `@checkfree` in M9 | M0 (spec) / M9 |\n| `return … from` | Not adopted; `Result` + `?` | — |\n| Share-nothing workers | Possible library model later | post-M9 |\n\n**Spec rule adopted now (from Goose BCE):** every object, slice and collection\nlength is `≤ 2^48 − 1` elements. Allocators reject larger requests with `AllocError`. This makes\n`len * sizeof(T)` overflow-free for `sizeof(T) < 2^15` and lets index arithmetic\non `usize` be proven non-overflowing.\n",
    177 =>
        "# Optimisations — preconditions and checks\n\nStatus: **normative** from M9c (2026-10-25); §3 from M9d; §4 from M9e. Master prompt §16: an optimisation needs safety preconditions, tests,\ncorrectness checks, debugging support and measurements, against a correct baseline. This document states each\noptimisation's preconditions, which must hold for every program the checker accepts, and how the toolchain checks\nthat the optimisation changes nothing a program can observe. Results are in `milestone-9.md`.\n\n## 1. Rules for every optimisation\n\n- It must not change what a program does: its output, exit status, panics (which one, and where), and its\n  allocations as counted by the runtime. A removed check must be one that cannot fail. The one exception is\n  placement (§4), which removes allocations of a placement-transparent allocator by design.\n- It has a switch that turns it off (`--no-…`), so that the unoptimised build stays available as an oracle and for\n  measuring the gain.\n- It is decided from facts the compiler already proves (types, loans, checked arithmetic), never from heuristics\n  about what a program \"probably\" does, and never by weakening a check of `ownership.md`.\n- The interpreter checks it on every program it runs, where it can (below), and the run corpus and the fuzzer run\n  with it both on and off.\n- Its output is deterministic, like the rest of the compiler.\n\n## 2. Bounds-check elimination (M9c)\n\n### 2.1 What is removed\nA `BoundsCheck(i < L)` (`intermediate-representation.md`; the index check of `type-system.md` §16.3) whose condition\nholds every time the statement is reached. The C backend then emits no `cee_check_index` for it. Nothing else is\nremoved: range checks (`a[lo..hi]`), length checks (`copyFrom`), overflow checks and every other panic stay. Because a\nremoved check never fails, which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nThe analysis is `src/backend/check_elimination.cee`, which also decides overflow checks (§3). It runs on each function's final NIR (after drop\nelaboration), and its result is the set of checks it proved. The C backend and the interpreter consult that set.\nThe NIR itself is not changed.\n\n### 2.2 Terms and facts\nA **term** is an integer the analysis can name at a program point:\n- the current value of a tracked local (§2.3);\n- an integer constant;\n- the length of a tracked slice, string view, vector or string local.\n\nA **fact** relates two terms: `a = b`, `a ≤ b`, `a < b`. Terms are mathematical integers and facts are integer\nrelations. That is sound because CeeSafe's arithmetic is checked: `+`, `-`, `*` panic rather than wrap (§6), and `as`\nis lossless (§7). A **definition** `x = a op b` records how a local was computed, for `op` among `+`, `-`, `%`, `&`\nand `>>` on unsigned integers. A **condition** `c = a cmp b` records the comparison that produced a `bool` local.\n\n### 2.3 Tracked locals\nA fact may mention only a tracked local:\n- an integer, `bool`, slice or `StringView` local, for its value (and a slice's or view's length);\n- a `Vector` or `String` local, for its length.\n\nA tracked local changes only through an assignment to it, which the analysis sees, or through a mutable loan of its\nown storage: `borrow mut i`, or `push`, `pop`, `clear` and `append`, which take `borrow mut` of a vector. While such a\nloan is live, every other use of the local is rejected (B1–B2), so no new fact about it can be made. After the loan,\nnothing may write the local through a pointer made from it (U2, ADR 0010). A shared loan cannot change it. So facts\nabout a local end at each mutable `Ref` or `Slice` of a place rooted at it without a dereference, and a value or\nlength read after that is a new fact.\n\nA tracked local's facts end at:\n- an assignment to a place rooted at the local without a dereference;\n- a mutable borrow of such a place, as above;\n- being a target of `Unpack`;\n- its `StorageDead`.\n\nA local that is moved out is uninitialised until it is assigned again, and that ass",
    178 =>
        "ignment ends its facts.\n\nA fact that ends with a local but also holds of a term known to be equal to it is kept, rewritten to that term.\nSo `t = length(s); m = t - 1; StorageDead(t)` still knows `m < len(s)`.\n\n### 2.4 Where facts come from\n- **Copies**: `x = y` and `x = y as T` (lossless) give `x = y`. `x = length(s)` gives `x = len(s)`. `x = c` gives\n  `x = c`, and `x = c op d` for constants and `+`, `-`, `*` gives `x` its exact value when that is in `x`'s range (M9d).\n  `x = e as T` where `e` is not a term (a field, an element) gives `min(E) ≤ x ≤ max(E)` for `e`'s type `E` (M9d).\n- **Definitions**: `x = a op b` with unsigned operands, unless `x` is itself one of `a`, `b` (as in `i = i + 1`, where\n  the old value is gone). If a definition `y = a op b` of the same terms already holds, `x = y` holds after the\n  assignment, even when `a` or `b` is `x`'s old value: `i = i + 1` after a test of `i + 1 < n` gives `i < n`.\n- **Comparisons**: a `bool` local `c = a cmp b` comparing integers (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) gives two implications:\n  if `c` is true, the relation holds; if false, its negation (`a < b` false is `b ≤ a`). A copy of `c` or `!c`\n  carries them, and a `Branch` on `c` adds the relation of the edge it takes. A `bool` local assigned a constant is\n  known to hold it.\n- **Passed checks**: after `BoundsCheck(i < L)`, `i < L`; after `RangeCheck(lo, hi, L)`, `lo ≤ hi` and `hi ≤ L`;\n  after `LengthCheck(a, b)`, `a = b`.\n\nDerived facts:\n- a < b implies a ≤ b;\n- ≤ and < are transitive (and a ≤ b < c gives a < c);\n- constants compare as integers;\n- equal definitions (the same operation on equal terms) have equal results;\n- x = a − b gives x ≤ a and b ≤ a (the subtraction did not panic), and x < a when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- t = L − o with a constant c ≥ 1 and c ≤ t gives o < L (some length remains after o);\n- x = a % n gives x < n;\n- x = a & m gives x ≤ m and x ≤ a;\n- x = a >> k gives x ≤ a;\n- x = a + b gives a ≤ x and b ≤ x, and a < x when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- x = o + i, with i < t (or i ≤ t) and t = L − o for the same `o`, gives x < L (or x ≤ L): an offset plus an index\n  below the remaining length stays below the length.\n- x = a + m and y = b + m (the same `m`) with a ≤ b give x ≤ y, and with a < b, x < y.\n\nA check `BoundsCheck(i < L)` is removed when `i < L` is derived from the facts that hold before it.\n\n### 2.5 The analysis\nForward dataflow over the control-flow graph. The facts at a block's entry are those holding on every incoming edge;\na block not yet reached holds everything. At a join of two paths the analysis keeps:\n- the facts of both paths;\n- an implication \"if `c` is `w`, then F\" of one path, if the other path knows `c` is not `w` (it holds vacuously\n  there) or knows F itself;\n- a comparison or definition F of one path as \"if `c` is not `w`, then F\", if the other path knows `c` is `w`.\n\n`a && b` and `a || b` lower to joins of a path that computed `b` and a path that assigned a constant, so\n`while (i < s.length && s[i] != 0)` and `if (k == 0 || n - k < 4) { return; }` keep what their tests showed.\n\nEvery rule keeps only facts that hold on both paths, so each fact in a block's entry holds on every execution that\nreaches it. Loops are iterated until no entry changes. The join is not a plain intersection, so the iteration is\nbounded (64 visits per block on average). A function that reaches the bound has nothing removed. Implications use a\nlength rather than a compiler temporary known to equal it, since the temporary may be gone where the implication is\nused. Derived facts are computed by saturation, which does not depend on the order facts were found, so the result\nis deterministic.\n\nA proof considers at most **128 facts** (M15 S3); where more hold, the check stays. The cost of saturation grows with\nthe number of facts, cubically, and a long straight-line function adds one at each step, so a sum of 300 terms took\n17 s to compile before the budget. That covers both t",
    179 =>
        "his section's index checks and §3's overflow checks. The most\nfacts any proof needs in the repository's programs, benchmarks and libraries, or in CosmoOS's Nova sources, is 44\n(measured 2026-10-30). Their generated C was byte-identical with and without the budget.\n\n### 2.6 Unsafe code\nThe rule of ADR 0010 is the only assumption about `unsafe` code. The unsafe code in this repository (`lib/core`,\n`integrations/cosmoos/**`, `tests/corpus/**`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`) was audited for writes through a pointer\nmade from a reference after its target was used directly (2026-10-25): none, as recorded in `milestone-9.md` (9c).\n\n### 2.7 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-bce` and `ceesafec run --no-bce` keep every check (`CEmitOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`,\n  `InterpreterOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`).\n- `ceesafec build --explain-bounds-checks` prints each index check of the package, in source order, with\n  `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 2.8 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every bounds check, removed or not. If a removed one would fail, it stops with an\n  interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated bounds check fails\"): a compiler bug. Every interpreter run checks\n  this: the corpus agreement tests, the fault-injection sweep, the property tests and the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **The run corpus** builds every program with the analysis (every configuration) and without it (`clang -O2 no-elim`),\n  so output and panics are compared both ways. So does the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **Nova's `CheckEliminationTests`** and `tests/corpus/bounds-checks/*.cee`: snapshots of `--explain-bounds-checks`\n  (`.bce`) for loops that must be proved, and for near misses that must keep their check (a reassigned index, a\n  mutated vector, a borrowed index, `<=` bounds, a loop bounded by another slice's length, a stable vector, whose\n`push` takes only `borrow this`).\n\nStable vectors (`type-system.md` §24.2) are not tracked: their length grows through a shared borrow.\n\n### 2.9 Character boundaries in ASCII text (after M13)\nA slice of a `StringView` (`v[lo..hi]`, type-system §22.2) checks the range and that `lo` and `hi` are character\nboundaries. In text whose bytes are all ASCII (each below 0x80), every index is a boundary, so the boundary check\ncannot fail. When the text is a local every assignment of which is a string literal of ASCII bytes, or a copy of\nsuch a local, the C backend checks the range only (`cee_check_range` for `cee_check_str_range`). The range check stays.\n- The analysis is per function and flow-insensitive, like §5.1's; it is part of `CheckElimination` and runs under the\n  same switch (`--no-bce` keeps the boundary check too). `--explain-bounds-checks` lists each slice of a view with\n  `boundary removed` or `boundary kept`.\n- The interpreter checks boundaries on every slice; if one whose check was removed is not a boundary, it stops with\n  an interpreter fault (\"an eliminated boundary check fails\"), as for §2.8.\n- The run corpus's and the fuzzer's `clang -O2 no-elim` configuration builds without it.\n\n## 3. Overflow-check elimination (M9d)\n\n### 3.1 What is removed\nThe overflow check of an integer `+`, `-` or `*` (`type-system.md` §6) or of a negation, when the exact result is in\nthe range of its type every time the operation runs. The C backend then writes the operation without a check\n(`(T)(a + b)`, with no undefined behaviour, since the exact result fits). Nothing else is removed: division and\nremainder keep their zero-divisor and `MIN / -1` checks, shifts their amount checks, and conversions their range\nchecks. A removed check never fails, so which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nIt is part of the same analysis as §2 (`src/backend/check_elimination.cee`), with the same facts at the same program\npoints, and it adds no new kind of fact. §2's facts treat checked arithmetic as exact; that stays true, since an\noperation whose check is removed was proved not to overflow.\n\n### 3.2",
    180 =>
        " Bounds of a term\nEvery term has a range that holds whatever the facts say: a local's is its type's (`i32`: −2³¹ to 2³¹ − 1), a\nlength's is that of `usize`, and a constant's is itself. From the facts before the operation, a term `a` has:\n- an **upper bound**, the least of `max(u)` over the terms `u` with `a ≤ u` derived (§2.4), and of `max(u) − 1`\n  over those with `a < u` derived;\n- a **lower bound**, the greatest of `min(l)` over the terms `l` with `l ≤ a`, and of `min(l) + 1` over those with\n  `l < a`.\n\nAn operand that is not a term (an element read from memory, a field) has its type's range.\n\n### 3.3 When an operation cannot overflow\nWith the operands' bounds `[lo(a), hi(a)]` and `[lo(b), hi(b)]` and the result type's range `[MIN, MAX]`, the check is\nremoved when one of these holds:\n- **by interval**: every combination of the bounds stays in `[MIN, MAX]`: for `a + b`, `lo(a) + lo(b) ≥ MIN` and\n  `hi(a) + hi(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a - b`, `lo(a) − hi(b) ≥ MIN` and `hi(a) − lo(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a * b`, the four products of\n  the bounds; for `-a`, `−hi(a) ≥ MIN` and `−lo(a) ≤ MAX`;\n- **an unsigned subtraction** `a - b` with `b ≤ a` derived;\n- **an unsigned addition** `a + b` whose sum `s`, added to the facts as the definition `s = a + b` (exact: §2.4's\n  rules for `+` hold of the mathematical sum), has an upper bound of at most `MAX`. That covers an offset plus an\n  index below the remaining length (`to + i < L`, §2.4), which the interval rule does not.\n\nAn operation whose operands are all constants is decided by its exact result alone, whatever the facts (M11, found by\nthe G8c fuzz campaign). Under a condition that is never true, such as `if (1u16 < 0u16)`, the facts contradict and\nevery rule above would hold vacuously. The check would then be removed from code that never runs, which is sound,\nbut the C would hold an overflowing constant expression such as `1 + 2147483647`, which C compilers reject under\n`-Werror`.\n\nThe bound rule is what makes a counter safe: in `for (i in 0..n)` the increment `i + 1` runs after `i < n`, so\n`hi(i) ≤ max(n) − 1` and `i + 1` does not overflow. A loop over a bound held in a constant (`for (s in 0..100)`) is\nthe same.\n\n### 3.4 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-oce` and `ceesafec run --no-oce` keep every overflow check\n  (`CEmitOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`, `InterpreterOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`). `--no-bce` and `--no-oce`\n  are independent.\n- `ceesafec build --explain-overflow-checks` prints each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation of the package, in source\n  order, with `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 3.5 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every operation exactly, removed or not. If a removed one would overflow, it stops\n  with an interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated overflow check fails\"). As for §2, every interpreter run\n  checks this.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program without either elimination (`clang -O2 no-elim`) as well as\n  with both, and compare.\n- **Nova's `CheckEliminationTests`** and `tests/corpus/overflow-checks/*.cee`: snapshots of `--explain-overflow-checks`\n  (`.oce`) for operations that must be proved (counters, constant bounds, `b ≤ a` subtractions, offsets below a\n  length, widened operands) and near misses that must keep their check (a counter bounded by `<=` the type's\n  maximum, a sum of two unbounded values, a subtraction tested the wrong way, a signed negation that may be `MIN`).\n\n## 4. Placement (M5; on by default from M9e)\n\n### 4.1 What changes\nA `Box.create` site that `PlacementAnalysis` places (`memory-model.md` §5) keeps its value in a slot of the\nfunction's frame instead of the heap. The rules and preconditions are `memory-model.md` §5 and §5.1; they are not\nrepeated here. Since M9e a site in a loop may be placed when its box ends within the iteration (§5.1).\n\n### 4.2 What a program can observe\nOutput, exit status, panics and drop timing are u",
    181 =>
        "nchanged. Allocation counts are not. A placed box is no allocation\nattempt, so the allocation report (`CEESAFE_ALLOC_REPORT`) shows fewer attempts, and fault injection\n(`CEESAFE_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`) cannot make a placed box fail. That is allowed only for an allocator whose contract says\nallocation \"may spuriously succeed\": `SystemHeap` in userland (memory-model.md §5, rule 1d). `FailingAllocator`,\n`KernelHeap` and user-defined allocators are never placed, so counting and failure injection stay exact for them.\nA test that needs every `SystemHeap` attempt counted builds with `--no-placement`.\n\n### 4.3 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default (M9e). `ceesafec build --no-placement` and `ceesafec run --no-placement` keep every box on the heap\n  (`CEmitOptions.Placement`, `InterpreterOptions.Placement`). `--placement` is still accepted.\n- `ceesafec build --explain-allocations` prints every allocation site of the package with its decision and reason. The\n  libraries' sites are left out, as in §2.7's listings, since the libraries are part of every package.\n\n### 4.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** applies the same decisions. A placed box lives in its own allocation, which ends at the box's\n  drop, so a use after it is a fault. A site whose previous box is still alive when it runs again is a fault (\"a\n  stack-placed box's slot is reused while its previous box is alive\"), since natively the site has one slot.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with placement (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-placement`), and compare.\n- **Nova's `PlacementTests`**: the same output, panics and drop trace with and without placement, never more allocation\n  attempts, and native code that agrees with the interpreter on everything, the allocation report included.\n  `tests/corpus/placement/*.alloc` are reviewed snapshots of `--explain-allocations`.\n\n## 5. Closure-call specialisation (OD-9a; ADR 0019)\n\n### 5.1 What changes\nA call through a closure (`CallClosure`, type-system §36.2) whose closure is *known* becomes a direct call to the\nclosure's body function, which the C compiler may inline. A call that passes a known closure to a function's\nclosure-typed parameter goes to a **variant** of that function: a copy in which that parameter is bound to the\nclosure's body, so the calls through it inside are direct too.\n\n- **Known.** In a function, a local holds a known closure with body `K` when every assignment to it is one of:\n  a reference to field 0 of an environment local every assignment of which is an aggregate whose field 0 is\n  `K`'s address (a closure literal, or an `fn` value's adapter, made in this function); a copy, cast or reborrow of a\n  local holding a known closure with body `K`; or, in a variant, the parameter bound to `K`. Any other assignment\n  makes the local unknown. The analysis is per function, flow-insensitive, and iterated to a fixed point.\n- **Variants.** One per (function, bindings), where the bindings map each bound parameter index to a body. They are\n  made from a worklist in the order the program's functions and their calls are visited, so the result is\n  deterministic. A recursive call with the same known argument goes to the same variant. Generic functions are\n  specialised as their instances (§20.4); a function with no NIR (an `extern`) is never specialised.\n- **Not specialised:** a closure read from a field, an element or a `dyn fn` (§37), a `Box<dyn fn…>`, a closure whose\n  value depends on a branch, and a `Thread.both` argument (called through the runtime, c-backend §2).\n- A variant is the same NIR with call targets fixed: the same statements, checks and frame. Borrow and effect\n  checking run before the pass and are not repeated.\n\n### 5.2 What a program can observe\nNothing: output, exit status, panics and allocations are unchanged. Only the C function a call goes to changes, and the\ngenerated C has one more function per variant (`ceeXS<n>_<name>`, c-backend §2.1).\n\n### 5.3 Switch a",
    182 =>
        "nd diagnostics\n- On by default. `ceesafec build --no-closure-specialisation` and `ceesafec run --no-closure-specialisation` keep every\n  closure call indirect (`CEmitOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`, `InterpreterOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`).\n- `ceesafec build --explain-closure-calls` prints each call through a closure with its function and line, and whether it\n  became direct (and to which body) or why not, then each variant made.\n\n### 5.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** runs the specialised NIR when the pass is on, so the corpus's comparison of interpreter and\n  native output covers it.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with the pass (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation`), and compare.\n- **Nova's `ClosureSpecialisationTests`**: the C of `benchmarks/sort` has no indirect comparator call left, and the run corpus\n  gives the same output with and without the pass. `tests/corpus/closure-calls/*.calls` are reviewed snapshots of\n  `--explain-closure-calls`.\n",
    183 =>
        "# Ownership, Borrowing and Lifetimes — Formal Safety Design (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. This document is normative for Milestone 2, unless\nan item is marked *future*. **B1–B5 and E1–E3 are implemented as specified in `lifetimes.md` (M2b),**\nwhich records the v0 simplifications: immutable, once-initialised reference bindings; and B5\nimplemented as \"the receiver's loan starts after the arguments\". Related: `memory-model.md`, `language-spec.md`,\n`intermediate-representation.md`, `unsafe.md` (M8a).\n\n## 0. Scope of the guarantee\n\n**Memory safety** for CeeSafe means: no execution of a well-typed program that\ncontains no `unsafe` code (and links only correctly-specified `unsafe` libraries)\nperforms any of:\n\n1. use-after-free; 2. double free; 3. dereference of a dangling reference;\n4. out-of-bounds access; 5. read of uninitialised memory; 6. type-confused\naccess; 7. invalid ownership transfer; 8. unsynchronised concurrent access where\nat least one access is a write (data race; only once threads exist, M-later).\n\n**Not** included in memory safety, but each with defined behaviour:\n\n| Condition | Defined behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| Integer overflow (`+ - *`, negation, `abs`) | **Panic** in all profiles (OD-6). `wrapping*`, `saturating*`, `checked*` methods available |\n| Division / remainder by zero, `MIN / -1` | Panic |\n| Shift by ≥ bit width | Panic (`wrappingShl` masks) |\n| Out-of-bounds index | Panic (`get(i)` returns `T?`) |\n| Narrowing numeric cast | `as` is **only** allowed where lossless; `truncate<T>(x)`, `checkedCast<T>(x)` explicit |\n| `T?` unwrap of `null` | Panic (`!` postfix operator) |\n| Allocation failure | **Value** (`Result<_, AllocError>`); panicking convenience APIs exist only in the userland profile |\n| Stack overflow | Profile-defined: userland relies on guard page → process termination; kernel profile uses static stack-depth checking (see `profiles.md`) |\n| Resource exhaustion, deadlock, logic errors, leaks via `forget` | Not prevented |\n| Panic | **Abort**, no unwinding (OD-5). Destructors do not run on panic. Userland: print + `abort()`; kernel: CosmoOS `panic()` |\n\nLeaks are memory-safe (as in Rust), but the safe standard library must not leak\nin ordinary use, and no CeeSafe feature may use leaking as a substitute for\nlifetime soundness (master prompt §5.6).\n\n## 1. Vocabulary\n\n- **Place**: a storage location expression: a local, a field of a place\n  (`p.f`), an element of a place (`p[i]`), or the target of a reference\n  (`*r`, written implicitly in surface syntax).\n- **Copy type**: a type whose values may be duplicated bitwise: primitives,\n  `borrow T` (shared), raw pointers, and `struct`/`enum` types all of whose\n  fields are Copy and which declare no `drop` (and are not marked `@noncopy`).\n  Everything else is a **move type** (affine).\n- **Loan**: the creation of a reference to a place. Kinds: `shared`\n  (`borrow T`) and `mut` (`borrow mut T`).\n- **Region**: a set of points in a function's control-flow graph (NIR)\n  during which a loan must remain valid.\n- **Scoped type**: any type that contains a reference (`borrow T`,\n  `borrow mut T`, `Slice<T>`, `StringView`, arena handles, guard-protected\n  handles), transitively, including through generic arguments. A non-scoped type is\n  **free-standing**: it may outlive any particular stack frame.\n- **Owner**: the place that holds a value of a move type. Destroying the owner\n  destroys the value.\n\n## 2. Invariants\n\nThe checker establishes these properties on the NIR (control-flow IR) of every\nfunction. \"At point p\" means on entry to the NIR statement at p.\n\n### Ownership\n\n- **O1 — Unique owner.** Every live value of a move type is held by exactly one\n  initialised place.\n- **O2 — Move de-initialises.** Moving from a place makes it *uninitialised*.\n  A move out of a place is permitted only if the place is a local, or a field\n  path rooted at a local, whose type has no `drop` (partial move). It is\n  never permitted through a reference, an index, or out ",
    184 =>
        "of a `drop`-implementing\n  struct.\n- **O3 — Definite initialisation.** A place may be read, borrowed, or have a\n  field assigned only if it is *definitely initialised* on every path reaching\n  that point. (Forward must-dataflow over NIR. The same analysis implements\n  use-after-move detection.)\n- **O4 — Exactly-once destruction.** At every point where a place goes out of\n  scope (`StorageDead`), and before every assignment that overwrites an\n  initialised place, the compiler inserts `drop(place)` if the place is\n  definitely initialised, or a guarded drop if it is *maybe* initialised (a drop\n  flag). A definitely uninitialised place gets no drop. Drop order: locals\n  in reverse declaration order; struct fields in declaration order; vector\n  elements in index order.\n- **O5 — Partial initialisation.** Struct literals initialise all fields\n  atomically from the program's point of view. Their C lowering constructs\n  in place, and no partially built struct is observable. A partially *moved*\n  struct (O2) drops only its remaining initialised fields.\n\n### Borrowing\n\n- **B1 — XOR aliasing.** At any point, for any place P, the live loans that\n  overlap P are either all shared, or exactly one is `mut`. Overlap is\n  path-prefix based: `a` overlaps `a.f`; `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`;\n  `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]` (no index disjointness in v0).\n- **B2 — Loan validity.** While a loan L of P is live (inside its region),\n  P must not be moved, dropped, go out of scope, or be assigned. If L is\n  shared, P must also not be mutated or mutably borrowed. If L is `mut`,\n  P must not be accessed at all except through L.\n- **B3 — Liveness regions (NLL).** A loan's region is the smallest set of\n  CFG points containing every point at which some reference derived from the\n  loan (transitively, through reborrows and copies of shared refs) may still be\n  used. It is computed by backward liveness over NIR, then by solving outlives\n  constraints. A shared reference copied into another variable propagates the loan.\n- **B4 — Reborrowing.** `borrow mut T` passed to a `borrow mut T` parameter\n  is implicitly reborrowed. The original is unusable until the reborrow dies\n  and becomes usable again afterwards (no move).\n- **B5 — Two-phase borrows.** For method calls `recv.m(args)` with a\n  `borrow mut this` receiver, the mutable loan of `recv` is *reserved* while\n  `args` are evaluated (permitting shared reads) and *activated* at the call.\n  Required for `v.push(v.length)`. This is the only two-phase site in v0.\n\n### Escape (lifetime) rules\n\n- **E1 — No escape beyond the owner.** A value of scoped type may flow only to:\n  (a) locals of the same function; (b) callee arguments; (c) the function's\n  return value, if the elision rule E2 links it to a parameter; (d) fields of\n  scoped *local* aggregates (tuples/anonymous temporaries), which then become\n  scoped.\n- **E2 — Return elision.** A function returning a scoped type must have exactly one\n  `borrow`/`borrow mut` parameter (including `this`), or must annotate the\n  source: `fn pick(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a) T`. The returned\n  value's region is constrained to be within the named parameter's loan at the call\n  site. A function with no borrow parameters cannot return a scoped type, except\n  `static` data (string literals, `static let`), which have the `static` region.\n- **E3 — Scoped fields only with region parameters.** In v0, user `struct`/`enum`\n  declarations could not contain scoped types. Since M6x (OD-3, ADR 0006) a struct\n  or enum may hold scoped fields if it declares region parameters\n  (`struct Parser<region src> { input: StringView<src> }`), and generic instances\n  with scoped type arguments are scoped themselves (`type-system.md` §25.3–§25.4).\n  Region values and pool boxes still stay in locals.\n- **E4 — No scoped globals or heap.** Scoped values may not be stored in\n  `static`s, constants, `Box`, pools, or arrays. Since M6x, `Vector`,\n  `StableVector`, `SlotMap` and r",
    185 =>
        "egion memory may hold scoped values; the container\n  is then scoped and carries their loans (`type-system.md` §25.4, §25.7).\n\nE1–E4 deliberately trade expressiveness for an implementation of tractable size\n(see \"Why not full Rust lifetimes\" below).\n\n### Regions (arenas) and guards\n\n- **R1 — Allocation borrows the region.** `arena.alloc(...)` takes `borrow this`\n  and returns a scoped value carrying a *shared loan of the arena*. Therefore:\n  - the arena cannot be dropped, moved, reset, or mutably borrowed while any\n    allocation from it is live (B2);\n  - arena allocations cannot escape the arena's scope (E1–E4).\n- **R2 — Trivially-destructible contents (v0).** Arena allocation is\n  permitted only for types with no `drop` (transitively). Bulk release is\n  then semantically equivalent to individual destruction. Arena-with-drop-list is\n  *future* (OD-8).\n- **R3 — Pinned scoped bindings.** `using x = e;` declares a binding that cannot\n  be moved, reassigned, or returned; it is dropped exactly at the end of the\n  enclosing block. Arenas, lock guards and quiescence guards are normally bound\n  with `using`. (Types may require `using` by declaration: `@scopedOnly`.)\n- **R4 — Guard-protected borrows.** An API may return values scoped to a\n  guard (`table.lookup(borrow guard, key) -> Obj?` where `Obj` is scoped to\n  `guard`). By B2/E1 these cannot outlive the guard. This is the mechanism for\n  epoch/quiescence-protected kernel lookups (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`).\n\n### Heap\n\n- **H1 — Box ownership.** `Box<T, A>` is a move type that owns exactly one\n  allocation of `T` obtained from allocator `A`. Its drop runs `T`'s drop, then\n  `A.free`. `A` must be a free-standing *allocator type* (§3 of\n  `memory-model.md`). Allocations from non-free-standing allocators are scoped\n  values (R1).\n- **H2 — Collections.** `Vector<T, A>` owns its buffer. Element access returns\n  loans of the vector (`borrow this`/`borrow mut this`), so growth (which takes\n  `borrow mut this`) is rejected while element borrows are live (B1/B2).\n  Stable references into collections use `SlotMap<T>`/`Handle<T>` with\n  generation validation (library type, M6).\n- **H3 — No owning cycles.** Only `Box` and owning collections own, and their\n  ownership graph is a forest by construction (O1). Back-references and\n  graphs use indices, handles, or arenas.\n\n### Threads (M13 G4; ADR 0016, type-system.md §38)\n\n- **T1 — Only `Send` values cross.** A value reaches another thread only by being moved into a spawned closure, or\n  returned from one through `join`. Each is checked `Send` (N0334). A `Send` value holds no loan and no raw pointer,\n  so after the move the spawner reaches nothing the new thread owns.\n- **T2 — Sharing is through `Arc` of `Share` types.** The only safe way for two threads to reach the same memory is\n  an `Arc<T>` with `T: Share`, which lends only `borrow T`. A v1 type can be changed through a shared reference only\n  if it is one of the library's `@threadSafe` types: `Mutex<T>` (whose guard lends `borrow mut T` to one thread at a\n  time) and the atomics (whose operations are atomic). So two threads can write the same memory only through a\n  mutex or an atomic: there is no data race in safe code.\n- **T3 — The library's `@threadSafe` types** keep T2 in their `unsafe` code. `Mutex.lock` takes the platform lock\n  before lending. The guard's drop releases it, after the guard's loan has ended (B-rules). `Arc`'s count changes\n  only by atomic operations, and only the owner that drops it to zero destroys the value. A spawned thread is\n  joined before `main` returns, so no thread outlives the values `main` dropped (O2). `@threadSafe` is accepted only\n  in `lib/std`, so user code cannot claim it (N0290).\n- **T4 — Fork-join borrows end with the call** (`Thread.both`, §38.6; ADR 0018). A closure passed to `Thread.both`\n  may reach the caller's values through references, because it runs only during the call and the call returns only\n ",
    186 =>
        " after the thread running it has ended. Both closures' loans are live for the whole call, so the B-rules keep them\n  from reaching one value in conflicting ways. A value reached from both threads is reached shared by both and is\n  `Share`; one reached mutably is reached by one thread only and is `Send`. So T2's conclusion holds: there is no data\n  race in safe code.\n\n### Unsafe\n\n- **U1 — Capabilities.** Raw pointer dereference, pointer arithmetic, calls to\n  `extern` or `unsafe fn`, `transmute`, access to `static mut`, MMIO and inline\n  assembly are allowed only inside `unsafe { }` or an `unsafe fn`.\n- **U2 — Contract.** Every `unsafe` block must preserve O1–O5, B1–B5, E1–E4,\n  R1–R4, H1–H3 and T1–T3 *as observed by safe code*. Every `unsafe fn` and every safe\n  function containing `unsafe` must state its contract in a `/// Safety:` doc\n  comment. A lint enforces that the comment exists.\n- **U3 — Raw pointers are inert.** Raw pointers (`*T`, `*mut T`) are Copy,\n  carry no loan, and are ignored by the borrow checker. Converting a raw pointer\n  to a reference (`unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(p) }`; `type-system.md` §27.3) creates a reference with an\n  *unconstrained* region, which must be immediately bounded by a safe wrapper's\n  signature (as with Rust's unbounded lifetimes; this is the main hazard in\n  unsafe code).\n\n## 3. How the compiler rejects invalid programs\n\nPass order (see `architecture.md`):\n\n1. **Type check (HIR)**: kinds (Copy / move / scoped), E2 annotations,\n   E3/E4 structural checks, `unsafe` context (U1), `using` restrictions (R3),\n   arena element constraint (R2).\n2. **NIR construction**: explicit `StorageLive/StorageDead`, `Move`, `Copy`,\n   `Borrow(kind, place)`, two-phase reservation markers, and the `drop` sites\n   still unelaborated.\n3. **Definite-initialisation / move analysis** (O2–O3): forward dataflow, with a\n   per-place bitset of {init, uninit, maybe}.\n4. **Borrow check** (B1–B5, E1–E2, R1, R4):\n   a. compute liveness of every reference-typed local;\n   b. generate loans at `Borrow` statements and region variables for every\n      reference type occurrence;\n   c. generate outlives constraints from assignments, calls (signature\n      instantiation), and returns (E2);\n   d. solve regions as sets of points (NLL-style fixpoint);\n   e. for every access, report a conflict if any live loan in the solved region at\n      that point conflicts (B1/B2).\n5. **Drop elaboration** (O4): inserts static drops and drop flags. Runs after the\n   borrow check, so drops are checked as accesses in step 4 using the\n   *unelaborated* drop sites.\n\nDiagnostics name the loan's creation point, the conflicting access, and the\nlater use that keeps the loan alive, as three labelled spans. This is the\nsingle most important usability feature of the checker (see `diagnostics.md`).\n\nExample compile-fail cases (each becomes a test in `tests/corpus/compile-fail/`):\n\n```ceesafe\nfn dangling() -> borrow i32 {         // error E2: no borrow parameter to derive from\n    let x = 5;\n    return borrow x;\n}\n\nfn useAfterMove(b: Box<Node>) -> void {\n    consume(b);\n    print(b.value);                    // error O3: `b` used after move at line 2\n}\n\nfn invalidate(v: borrow mut Vector<i32>) -> void {\n    let first = v[0];                  // Copy of i32: fine\n    let r = borrow v[0];               // shared loan of *v\n    v.push(1);                         // error B1: mutable loan while `r` live\n    print(r);                          //   ... `r` used here\n}\n\nfn arenaEscape() -> Slice<u8> {        // error E2: no borrow parameter\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap)!;\n    return arena.allocArray<u8>(16)!;\n}\n```\n\n## 4. Why not full Rust lifetimes in v0\n\nNamed lifetime parameters on aggregates, higher-ranked bounds and variance are\nwhere most of Rust's borrow-checker complexity and annotation burden live.\nE1–E4 keep the analysis entirely **intraprocedural** (signatures summarise\ncallees completely), with no lifetime parameters on",
    187 =>
        " types. That is enough for:\nborrowing parameters, slices, string views, iterators over a borrowed collection\n(as a local), arena-scoped computations, and guard-scoped kernel lookups. It is\nnot enough for: long-lived parsers holding `StringView`s, structs of\nreferences, or zero-copy data structures. Those are OD-3, designed in M6f\n([`od3-design.md`](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/od3-design.md)) so that v0 programs remain valid.\n\n## 5. Behaviours requiring runtime checks\n\n| Check | Elidable when | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Array/slice bounds | Index proven `< len` by range analysis (M9) | Lowering emits `ceesafe_check_index` |\n| Integer overflow | Value ranges proven (M9) | `ceesafe_add_i32` etc. |\n| Division by zero / `MIN/-1` | Divisor is a non-zero, non-minus-one constant | — |\n| Shift width | Constant shift amount | — |\n| Optional unwrap `!` | Flow-typing after `!= null` | — |\n| Drop flags | Static init state known (most cases) | Drop elaboration |\n| Allocation failure | Never elided (it is a value) | — |\n| Handle generation check (`SlotMap`) | Never in v0 | Built-in (M6e; library in M8) |\n| Kernel: stack depth | Static call-graph bound (kernel profile) | Link-time report |\n\n## 6. Testing obligations (M2 acceptance)\n\n- Every invariant above has at least two compile-fail tests and one compile-pass\n  test that is \"close\" to failing (for example NLL cases where a loan ends\n  before the conflicting access).\n- Mutation suite: for each compile-pass test, automated mutants (swap statement\n  order, remove a scope, duplicate a move) whose expected verdict is computed\n  independently, by hand-labelling or a reference interpreter, not by the\n  checker itself.\n- Generated C for compile-pass tests is run under ASan + UBSan in CI\n  (supporting evidence only).\n",
    188 =>
        "# CeeSafe Compilation Profiles for CosmoOS (+ Freestanding Runtime Design)\n\nStatus: C0 proposal (deliverables 3 and 7); §4, the freestanding profile, is normative from M8c. Facts are cited from\n`docs/research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md` **[PA §n]** and\n`docs/research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md` **[KA §n]**.\n\nA *profile* fixes three things: (1) the language features permitted, (2) the\nruntime library linked, (3) the C flags and link recipe. Profiles are selected\nwith `ceesafec build --profile <p> --target <t>`. A profile is **not** \"userland\nwith the standard library switched off\" (prompt §2). Each feature is either\nallowed or not, and the list below is normative.\n\n## 1. Profile matrix\n\n| Feature | `hosted` (Linux/macOS dev) | **A: `cosmoos-user`** | **B: `cosmoos-kernel`** | `freestanding` (generic; M8c, §4) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Libraries | core + std | core + std(cosmoos) | core + kernel | core |\n| Default allocator type param | `SystemHeap` (malloc) | `SystemHeap` (CosmoOS libc malloc) | **none**: must be explicit | none |\n| Panicking allocation helpers (`!` on `AllocError`, `create().orPanic`) | allowed | allowed | **lint error** | lint error |\n| `f32`/`f64` | yes | yes (no `<math.h>` in libc: the math methods are portable C in `rt.h`, type-system §35.8) | **forbidden** (`-mgeneral-regs-only`) [KA §7–8]; enforced by `ceesafec --no-float` (N0297, §35.9) | target-defined |\n| Threads, `Send`/`Sync` | later | via `cosmo_thread_*` only [PA impl 4] | kernel threads via KThread wrapper (C7) | no |\n| Native stack value limit | 64 KiB | 64 KiB | **512 B** per value; **2 KiB per frame** (CosmoOS `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`, error) [KA impl 21] | configurable |\n| Whole-call-chain stack budget | — | — | report + error against **16 KiB** (shared with IRQs) [KA §8] | configurable |\n| Scratch regions | reserved VM | reserved VM via `mmap` | fixed per-thread buffer, explicit checks | fixed buffer |\n| Panic | stderr + `abort()` | stderr + `abort()` via libc | CosmoOS `panic()` | user-provided `ceesafe_panic` |\n| Context effects (`@context`, `@effects`, `sleep`/`alloc`) | checked | checked | checked | checked (C7a, type-system §32: obligations arise only where declared) |\n| `extern \"C\"` imports | any | libc public headers only (L1, OD-15) | bindings file (exports only for modules) | any |\n| `static var`, raw pointers, MMIO | unsafe | unsafe | unsafe (+ MMIO types) | unsafe |\n| TLS (`@threadLocal`) | yes | yes (`__thread` works) [PA §4] | **no** (no TLS in the kernel) | no |\n| Recursion | yes | yes | allowed, but makes the stack bound unprovable → the function must be `@unboundedStack` and is excluded from IRQ/atomic contexts | yes |\n\n## 2. Profile A — `cosmoos-user`\n\n### 2.1 Target and code generation [PA §3, impl 1]\n- Triples: `x86_64-unknown-none-elf`, `aarch64-unknown-none-elf`. LP64, max\n  alignment 16, `long double` = 16 bytes (unused by CeeSafe), `char` signedness\n  differs (irrelevant: CeeSafe never uses `char`).\n- C flags: CosmoOS `USER_CFLAGS` exactly (`build/toolchain.mk:27-61`,\n  `libc/libc.mk:12-18`): `-std=c11 -ffreestanding -nostdinc -fno-pic -fno-pie\n  -fno-stack-protector`, no unwind tables, CosmoOS warning set with `-Werror`.\n  CeeSafe does **not** maintain its own copy of these flags. It reads them from the CosmoOS\n  build (`make print-USER_CFLAGS`-style query or `compile_commands.json`; see\n  `build-integration.md`).\n- Link: static ET_EXEC at 0x400000 with `userland/user.ld`, `crt0.o`, `libc.a`.\n  No `.init_array` support exists, so **CeeSafe must never emit constructors**. The\n  `.note.cosmo` note from `crt0` must be present; without it the binary silently\n  runs as a Linux binary [PA impl 6]. The CeeSafe build checks the note with `llvm-readelf -n`.\n\n### 2.2 Runtime: `runtime/cosmoos/user/`\n- `main(int argc, char** argv, char** envp)` is generated. It wraps argv into\n  `Slice<StringView>` without copying, calls CeeSafe `main`, runs no `atexit`, and returns the\n  exit code. Deterministic cleanup has ",
    189 =>
        "completed when CeeSafe `main` returns.\n- `SystemHeap` = `malloc`/`free`/`realloc`. Over-aligned (`> 16`) layouts are\n  served by over-allocating and storing the original pointer in a header (no\n  `aligned_alloc` in libc) [PA impl 3].\n- Arenas and scratch regions take chunks from `mmap(MAP_ANONYMOUS)` (a public libc\n  function, consistent with L1).\n- Console/IO: `write`/`read` on fds. Files: `open/read/write/close/stat/dirent`.\n  Processes: `spawn*` + `waitpid` (no fork/exec). **Implemented in C3** (type-system.md §26,\n  `runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c`): files (`open/read/write/close/unlink`), TCP over IPv4, `spawnvp` +\n  `waitpid`, and argv; `stat`/`dirent` and time are not yet. Time: `clock_gettime`\n  (monotonic + realtime). Sockets: CosmoOS-shaped socket API. Absent in libc:\n  math, poll/select, DNS, `setjmp`, sigaltstack [PA impl 2]. CeeSafe's std\n  omits them in v0.\n- **L1 policy** (OD-15): the CeeSafe runtime calls **only public libc functions**, never\n  raw syscalls, until the CosmoOS owner rules on whether CeeSafe's runtime counts as \"library\".\n\n### 2.3 Alternative A-musl (not recommended for C1)\nCosmoOS already builds musl 1.2.5 + compiler-rt and ships musl programs under\nthe Linux personality [PA impl 7]. CeeSafe could target it for richer POSIX\ncoverage, but it is not \"native CosmoOS\" (Invariant 7) and is kept as a\nlater option (OD-16). The C backend needs no changes for it, only a different\nprofile runtime.\n\n## 3. Profile B — `cosmoos-kernel`\n\n### 3.1 Permitted language features (normative)\n- All of core: structs, enums, generics, slices, `Option`/`Result`, borrowing,\n  `Box<T, A>` with an **explicit** kernel allocator type, `using`, `defer`.\n- No floats, no TLS, no implicit allocation (no default `A`; no allocating\n  operators; string formatting writes into caller-provided buffers or explicit\n  `KernelHeap` strings).\n- No panicking allocation. Panics in general are **discouraged**: the kernel\n  profile lint `kernel-panic-sites` reports every panic site (bounds, overflow,\n  unwrap). `@noPanic` on a function makes any remaining panic site a compile\n  error. That is the kernel analogue of Goose's `checkfree` (goose.md §9.9).\n- Context effects enforced (`kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md` §6).\n- Two sub-modes: `--kernel-link=builtin` (internal APIs allowed, KQ-5) and\n  `--kernel-link=module` (exported symbols only; generates `COSMO_MODULE`\n  metadata, `init/shutdown`) [KA impl 23].\n\n### 3.2 Code generation [KA §7, impl 20–22]\n- clang only. `-std=c11 -ffreestanding -nostdinc`, `-Werror` with the kernel\n  warning set, `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`, `-fno-strict-aliasing`, non-PIC.\n  - x86-64: `-mcmodel=kernel -mno-red-zone -mgeneral-regs-only`.\n  - AArch64: `-mcmodel=small -mgeneral-regs-only -mno-outline-atomics -ffixed-x18`.\n- Flags are taken from CosmoOS's build per **configuration and arch**.\n  `CONFIG_*` macros change inline header behaviour, so CeeSafe objects are never shared between debug\n  and release builds [KA impl 22].\n- **Kernel struct layouts come from the kernel's own headers** (ADR 0008; [KA impl 26]). CeeSafe never\n  writes down the layout of `spinlock_t`, `struct kobject`, `struct cosmoboot_info` or any other C\n  type. It imports them: an `extern \"C\"` block with `@header(\"kernel/bootinfo.h\")` declares\n  `struct cosmoboot_info { fb_phys: u64; … }` with only the fields CeeSafe uses, by their C names\n  (type-system §31; implemented in C6b, whose `c6/fbvalid.cee` does exactly this).\n  Generated C `#include`s the headers of the exact module ABI (currently 11) and accesses the real\n  members, compiled with the build's own flags, so the layout is always the one of this\n  configuration and architecture. Each declared field is checked at C compile time (it exists, and\n  has exactly the declared type), so a configuration that changes a field fails to build, naming it,\n  instead of miscompiling. Imported types are used behind pointers and references only, since their\n  size is the C compiler's, not CeeSafe's",
    190 =>
        ". `@repr(C)` mirrors remain for types CeeSafe itself defines and\n  exports (type-system §28.5).\n- The mechanism is not CosmoOS-specific (ADR 0008): the same declarations import Linux kernel or C\n  library structs from those platforms' headers (§5).\n\n### 3.3 Freestanding runtime: `runtime/core/` + `runtime/cosmoos/kernel/`\n\n| Runtime need | Freestanding core (no libc) | CosmoOS kernel binding |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_panic(kind, src)` | plain `extern` (no weak symbols), which the profile must define | calls CosmoOS `panic(\"ceesafe: %s at %s:%u\")` |\n| `ceesafe_mem_copy/move/set/cmp` | `__builtin_mem*` (may lower to calls) | kernel exports `memcpy/memmove/memset/memcmp` (string functions exported [KA impl 20]) |\n| Checked arithmetic | `static inline` in `rt.h`, no libcalls | same |\n| Allocation | none (allocators are library types over externs) | `kmalloc/kfree`, `kmem_cache_*`, … via bindings |\n| Source locations | static `const` tables | same (in `.rodata`; no relocations beyond the allowed set) |\n| Stack metadata | `-fstack-usage` output parsed by `ceesafec` | call-graph depth check against 16 KiB |\n\nThe core runtime is ~300 lines of header plus a few C functions. It has **no global\nmutable state** (so no TLS or per-CPU questions) and **no constructors**.\n\nAcceptance (C4): `llvm-nm -u` on a kernel-profile object lists only symbols\nfrom the profile's allowlist (`memcpy`, `memset`, `memmove`, `memcmp`,\n`panic`, plus bound kernel APIs); `llvm-readelf -r` shows only relocation types\naccepted by the module loader [KA §6.4]; there are no `.init_array`, `.tdata`/`.tbss`,\n`.eh_frame` or COMMON symbols.\n\n## 4. The `freestanding` profile (M8c; normative)\n\n`ceesafec build --profile freestanding` compiles a package for an environment with no C library and\nno operating system: a kernel, firmware, or a module that another runtime links. `--profile hosted`\nis the default and is what every earlier milestone built. (`cosmoos-user` is hosted with CosmoOS's\nflags, C1–C3; `cosmoos-kernel` is C4–C8 and builds on this profile.)\n\n### 4.1 What freestanding code may use\nEverything in the language that needs no runtime service: integers, `bool`, structs, enums,\narrays, slices, `StringView`, optionals, `Result` and `AllocError`, references, generics and\ninterfaces, `unsafe` and raw pointers (type-system §27), the C FFI (§28), `FixedBufferAllocator`\nover caller-provided storage, `Ptr`, `Slice.fromRaw`, and `truncate`.\n\nThese need a hosted runtime and are rejected in freestanding code (N0293), wherever they are\nnamed (a type, a call, a struct literal):\n\n| Name | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `Console`, `Process` | standard streams, exit, arguments and processes are operating-system services |\n| `File`, `TcpListener`, `TcpStream`, `IoError` | files and sockets |\n| `SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator` | there is no system heap; `FailingAllocator` wraps it |\n| `Box`, `PoolBox`, `Pool`, `Arena`, `Scratch`, `Vector`, `String`, `SlotMap`, `StableVector` | each allocates from one of the two allocators above (`Scratch` from the runtime's heap-backed stack) |\n\nExempt, since they need no runtime: `Box.createIn` with a user allocator (C5, type-system §30) and `Box.intoRaw` / `Box.fromRaw`\n(C7d, type-system §34.2).\n\nA `main` function is N0293 too: there is no C `main` to call it. A freestanding package is entered\nthrough its `@export` functions (§28.4; custom entry points are M8d). Allocators that a freestanding\nenvironment provides (CosmoOS's `KernelHeap`, C5) will come with user-defined allocators.\n\n### 4.2 Runtime and the panic hook\n- The program is the generated `main.c`, `ceesafe/rt.h`, and the core runtime `ceesafe_rt_core.c` compiled\n  with `CEE_FREESTANDING` defined, which leaves out everything that needs a platform service (output,\n  exit, heap accounting, arenas, scratch frames, pools and collections). What remains has no\n  mutable global state and no constructors: the fixed-buffer allocator, UTF-8 validation, and the\n  checked arithmetic in `rt.h`.\n- **`ce",
    191 =>
        "esafe_panic` is the environment's.** Generated code calls\n  `void ceesafe_panic(cee_panic_kind kind, cee_loc loc)` (declared in `ceesafe/rt.h`) for every panic:\n  overflow, bounds, unwrap, and the rest. The freestanding runtime does not define it; the code that\n  links the object must, and it must not return (panic = abort, OD-5). `kind` is an\n  `cee_panic_kind`; `loc.file` is the CeeSafe source path (a zero-terminated string), `loc.line` and\n  `loc.column` 1-based. The core's `ceesafe_panic_reason(kind)` gives the same text the hosted runtime\n  prints (\"integer overflow\", …). CosmoOS's kernel binding is `integrations/cosmoos/c4/ceesafe_rt_kernel.c` (C4).\n- The C compiler may itself emit calls to `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset` and `memcmp` (struct copies,\n  zeroing), as it may in any freestanding C (C11 §4 and the compilers' documentation); the\n  environment provides them.\n\n### 4.3 `--emit obj`\n`ceesafec build --emit obj -o out.o` compiles each C file with `-c` (and the flags given with\n`--cflag`) and joins the objects into one relocatable object: with `<linker> -r` when `--ld <linker>`\nis given (`ld.lld` for the ELF cross builds, as CosmoOS links), otherwise with the C compiler's `-r`\n(passing it the `--target=…` flag). It works in\nboth profiles; with `--profile freestanding` it is the normal output.\n\nFreestanding builds always add `-ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables\n-fno-unwind-tables` (c-backend §1).\n\n### 4.4 The symbol allowlist (acceptance, M8 criterion 1)\nThe undefined symbols of a freestanding object (`llvm-nm -u`) are only:\n`ceesafe_panic`, `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, and the functions the package declares in\n`extern \"C\"` blocks. Checked by `tools/freestanding_check.py` (Nova's `FreestandingTests`) for the host and for both CosmoOS triples\n(`x86_64-unknown-none-elf`, `aarch64-unknown-none-elf`).\n\nOn the CosmoOS triples the objects also have (Nova's `FreestandingTests`, M8e): no constructor or destructor\nsections (`.init_array`, `.fini_array`, `.ctors`, `.dtors`), which CosmoOS never runs; no TLS\n(`.tdata`, `.tbss`), which the kernel lacks; no unwind tables (`.eh_frame`); no COMMON symbols; and no\nmutable global state (`.data` and `.bss` empty) except the program's static items (type-system §39, ADR 0024),\nwhose thread-safety the checker requires (`Share`, or `unsafe` for a `static mut`). Until M14 CeeSafe had no statics,\nso this said \"no mutable global state\" without the exception.\n\n## 5. Other C codebases (Linux and others)\n\nCeeSafe is multi-platform: nothing in the language, the C backend or the profiles above is specific to\nCosmoOS. The freestanding profile (§4), the C FFI (type-system §28) and imported C types (ADR 0008)\nwork against any C codebase, the Linux kernel included:\n- **Flags** come from the codebase's own build, as they come from CosmoOS's Make here (for Linux,\n  from Kbuild for the configured kernel), never copied by hand.\n- **Types** come from its headers, imported as in §3.2, so a field CeeSafe uses is checked against the\n  configured kernel's real layout.\n- **Symbols** are its exports (for Linux, `EXPORT_SYMBOL`/`EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL` for modules), checked like\n  CosmoOS's in C4.\n- **Panics** go to the codebase's own reporting (`ceesafe_panic`, §4.2; for Linux, a `BUG()`-style report\n  or a recoverable error path, decided with that integration).\n\nA Linux kernel-module profile is a future integration (not scheduled); it needs only a binding of\n`ceesafe_panic`, the build's flags, and the headers, as C4 and C5 did for CosmoOS.\n\n",
    192 =>
        "# Testing\n\nStatus: describes CeeSafe's tests at the fork (CeeSafe's Decision 0006). Tests that were run are recorded, with dates\nand results, in the repository's `ROADMAP.md` §10 and the plans under `docs/`; they are never mixed with proposals.\nNova's own test history up to the fork is in [Nova's testing.md](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/blob/v0.6.0/docs/testing.md).\n\nSanitizer results are supporting evidence, never proof of the language's safety.\n\n## 1. Test kinds\n\n| Kind | Where | Oracle |\n|---|---|---|\n| Corpus (§2) | `tests/reference/tests/corpus/*`, `tools/run_corpus.py` | the files' own annotations and snapshots |\n| Regression against the fork (§3) | `tools/compare_*.py`, `tools/run_corpus.py --reference` | novac 0.6.0's output, with ceesafec's names mapped back (`tools/names.py`) |\n| Fixed point | `tools/fixed_point.py`, `bootstrap/fixed-point/` | ceesafec built by itself emits byte-identical C, generation after generation |\n| Mutation suite (Nova M2) | `tools/mutation_suite.py` | mutants of seed programs; the expected (code, line) diagnostics come from an independent oracle, not from the checker |\n| Fault-injection sweep (Nova M3) | `tools/fault_injection.py` | every run-pass program that allocates, built with ASan+UBSan, run once per allocation attempt *k* with attempt *k* failing; each run returns from `main` with `live=0` or panics with \"unwrapped an Err value\", with no sanitizer report |\n| Collection properties (Nova M6) | `tools/collection_properties.py` | a model of each built-in collection predicts the exact output of random operation programs (values, lengths, the growth rule of `type-system.md` §23.1, stale handles, drop order), also under the fault sweep |\n| Freestanding | `tools/freestanding_check.py` | the freestanding programs build with `-ffreestanding -nostdinc` for `x86_64-` and `aarch64-unknown-none-elf` and run against their C harnesses |\n| Debugging | `tools/debugger_check.py` | a `-g` build under lldb or gdb with `editors/debug`'s commands (`tooling.md` §3) |\n| Linking with Nova | `tools/link_collision.py` | a novac-built and a ceesafec-built library link into one program; two novac-built ones do not |\n| CosmoOS (Nova's C1–C9, K3) | `integrations/cosmoos/c*.py`, `k3.py` | ceesafec's C under CosmoOS's own flags, host tests, fuzz targets and module loader rules, and in its QEMU boots; CosmoOS's tree is only read |\n| Same C on another host | `tools/host_c_check.py` | ceesafec built on Windows (MSVC, clang) emits the same C as on macOS and Linux |\n| Specification (§4) | `tools/spec_check.py`, `tools/guide_check.py` | every diagnostic code is explained; the guide's examples do what the pages say; links resolve |\n\n`tools/contract_tests.py` runs all of these but the fixed point and the host check, against one ceesafec.\n\n## 2. Corpus format\n\nRunner: `tools/run_corpus.py --compiler out/ceesafec --corpus tests/reference/tests/corpus`.\n\n- `parse-pass/*.cee`: must parse with **no** diagnostics. The AST dump (`ceesafec parse --dump-ast`) must equal the\n  sibling `.ast` snapshot. **Every new or changed snapshot is reviewed by hand before it is committed.** A snapshot is\n  an expected value, not a recording.\n- `parse-fail/*.cee`: annotate each expected diagnostic on the line where it is reported, as `// error[N0100]`, or\n  `// error[N0100]@14` to also pin the column. A missing **or extra** diagnostic fails the test, so it also checks\n  error recovery.\n- `check-pass/*.cee`: must type-check with no diagnostics. The HIR dump (`ceesafec check --dump-hir`) must equal the\n  sibling `.hir` snapshot.\n- `check-fail/*.cee`: must **parse** cleanly and produce exactly the annotated semantic diagnostics.\n- A file with a line `// profile: freestanding` is checked in the freestanding profile (`profiles.md` §4), and one with\n  `// option: no-float` as if built with `--no-float` (`type-system.md` §35.9).\n- `run-pass/*.cee` and `run-fail/*.cee` are compiled to C, built with strict warnings and `-Werror`, and run.\n  Dire",
    193 =>
        "ctives:\n  - `// expect-stdout: text`: one per output line, in order; `// expect-stdout:` alone is an empty line.\n  - `// expect-exit: N`: the exit status (default 0).\n  - `// expect-panic: reason`: placed on the line where the panic must be reported. The test requires exit status\n    134 (SIGABRT) and `ceesafe panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:` on standard error.\n- `ffi/` programs are linked with their C harness (`NAME.c`, and `NAME.types.h` for imported C types,\n  `type-system.md` §31); `freestanding/` programs with their environment.\n- `emit-c/*.cee` have reviewed snapshots of the generated `main.c`; `placement/`, `bounds-checks/`,\n  `overflow-checks/` and `closure-calls/` have snapshots of the `--explain-…` reports (`optimisations.md`).\n- The C configurations: the contract suite builds the run families under the host's `cc` with `-Werror`, and under\n  clang `-O1` with ASan+UBSan, `-fno-sanitize-recover=all` and the runtime's allocation report (`--check-leaks`: a\n  program that exits normally with live allocations fails). Every configuration must meet the expectations, so the\n  run corpus is also a differential test.\n\nAt the fork the corpus is Nova's at `v0.6.0` with `.cee` names (`tools/fork_corpus.py`), and its snapshots are still\nNova's: `run_corpus.py` compares ceesafec's output with them after mapping CeeSafe's names back (§3). A CeeSafe-only\nchange to the language adds corpus files with CeeSafe's names and snapshots written by ceesafec.\n\n## 3. Regression against the fork\n\nnovac 0.6.0's output, which equalled ceesafec's at the fork, is the frozen baseline (the repository's\n`docs/fork-point.md`). The comparison tools run novac in a Nova checkout at `v0.6.0` and ceesafec on CeeSafe's copy of\nthe same files, and compare novac's output with ceesafec's mapped back by `tools/names.py`: diagnostics and JSON,\nthe AST, HIR and NIR dumps, the C in eight forms and the reports, CeeSafe's own C, packages, the command line, `fmt`,\n`doc`, `run --interp`, and the `lsp` and `mcp` servers. A mapping could hide a name that was not renamed, so the C\ncomparisons also fail on any Nova C name left in ceesafec's output.\n\nA deliberate difference from Nova is a change to this specification first, and then a recorded exception in the\ncomparisons (as `names.FORK_DIVERGENCES` is).\n\n## 4. The specification's own checks\n\n- `tools/spec_check.py`: every diagnostic code `ceesafec` reports has a row that `ceesafec explain` finds; every\n  relative link in `spec/` and `guide/` resolves; `spec/api/core.md` is what `ceesafec doc` writes for `lib/core`; no\n  Nova name is left outside the inherited ADRs and the places that name Nova on purpose.\n- `tools/guide_check.py`: every `ceesafe` code block of `guide/` is compiled as a file named `main.cee`:\n  - a block must type-check; with a `main`, it is also run in the interpreter, and a `text` block right after it must be\n    exactly its output;\n  - an HTML comment directly before the block changes that:\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: fail N0241 -->`: the block must fail with exactly these codes, and a `text` block after it must be\n      exactly the compiler's messages;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: freestanding -->`: checked with the freestanding profile, not run;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: check -->`: type-checked, not run (it calls C);\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: panics -->`, `<!-- ceesafe: exits 1 -->`: the run's exit status;\n    - `<!-- ceesafe: skip -->`: not checked, for a block that cannot stand alone (a second package's file, a fragment).\n      Use it sparingly, and check the example some other way.\n- The algorithms that find and rank sections for `explain` and the MCP server are compared with novac's on Nova's\n  documents, through a ceesafec built with them (`tools/reference_docs.py`).\n\n## 5. Not yet ported\n\nNova's random program generator (`tests/Nova.Fuzz`, Nova M7d and K7) and its 24-hour differential campaign are\nROADMAP P9 items; until then the fuzz evidence is Nova's, for the language as it was at the fork.\n",
    194 =>
        "# Tooling (M10)\n\nThe commands of `ceesafec` beside `build`, `check` and `run`. The formatter has its own document (`formatting.md`).\n\n## 1. Documentation generator (T2)\n\n```sh\nceesafec doc file.cee...                 # Markdown for the files' public items, on standard output\nceesafec doc lib/core -o spec/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"\nceesafec doc --missing file.cee...       # list the public items without a doc comment; exit 1 if there are any\n```\n\nThe files are one package, as for `check` (a directory means every `.cee` file under it, in ordinal path order).\n`lib/core` is documented only when it is named. A file with a syntax error stops the command: its diagnostics are\nprinted and nothing is written. The package does not have to type-check: documentation is made from the syntax, so it\ncan be written for code that does not build yet.\n\n### 1.1 What is documented\n\n- Every item declared `public`: a function, a constant, a struct, an enum, an interface, and a function in an\n  `extern \"C\"` block.\n- Inside a public struct: its `public` fields, its `public` methods, and whether it has a `drop` block. Inside a public\n  enum: every variant. Inside a public interface: every method.\n- An `impl I for T` block, listed under `T` as \"Implements `I`\" when `T` is a public struct or enum of the package.\n\nNothing else is listed. Items in a `namespace` are listed under their simple names, as the language names them today\n(type-system.md §1).\n\n### 1.2 The page\n\nThe output is deterministic: the same files give the same bytes.\n- A title (`--title`, or the first file's name without its extension) as a level-1 heading, then a list of the\n  documented items, each a link to its section.\n- One level-2 section per item, in the order of the files as given and of the items in each file. Its heading is the\n  item's kind and name (`struct Volatile`, `fn sortBy`).\n- The item's **signature** in a `ceesafe` code block: the item's attributes and its declaration up to its body (the\n  `{ … }` or `=> …` of a function, the members of a struct), as `ceesafec fmt` prints them (formatting.md). A struct's\n  block lists its public fields; an enum's, its variants; an interface's, its methods.\n- The item's **doc comment** (grammar.md §1.2): its lines as written, which are Markdown. An item without one has\n  only its signature.\n- A struct's public methods follow as level-3 sections (`StructName.method`), each with its signature and doc\n  comment; then \"Implements `I`\" for each `impl`.\n\n### 1.3 Missing documentation\n\n`--missing` writes nothing but a line per public item without a doc comment, `path:line: kind name`, and exits 1 if\nthere is any. It is a lint for a package's public surface; nothing requires it to pass.\n\n### 1.4 In the repository\n\n`spec/api/core.md` is generated from `lib/core` with `ceesafec doc lib/core -o spec/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"`, run\non the embedded `lib/core` (its sources under CeeSafe's names). `tools/spec_check.py` fails if it is out of date\n(Nova's `DocTests`).\n\n## 2. Language server (T3)\n\n```sh\nceesafec lsp        # a Language Server Protocol server on standard input and output\n```\n\nThe server speaks LSP 3.17 over JSON-RPC 2.0, with `Content-Length` framing, on standard input and output. It is part\nof `ceesafec` and has no dependency beyond CeeSafe's own libraries. The editor starts it; `editors/vscode` is a VS Code\nextension that does (§2.4).\n\n### 2.1 What a document is checked with\n\nA document that is a source of a package (a `ceesafe.toml` above it whose sources include it) is checked with its\npackage, as `ceesafec check` in the package would check it (§4.4): the editor's text for the document, the package's\nother files from disk, and the dependencies its `ceesafe.lock` names, never fetching; the manifest's profile and `float`\napply. Any other document, or one whose package does not resolve, is checked as a package of its own, with\n`lib/core`, as `ceesafec check file.cee` would check it, using the text the editor holds. Its prof",
    195 =>
        "ile is hosted, unless\nits first line is `// profile: freestanding` (the corpus convention, `testing.md` §3).\n\n### 2.2 Capabilities\n\n| Request or notification | What the server does |\n|---|---|\n| `initialize` | Answers with the capabilities below: full-text synchronisation, hover, definition, document symbols, formatting, quick fixes |\n| `textDocument/didOpen`, `didChange`, `didClose` | Keeps the text; after an open or change, checks the document (§2.1) and publishes its diagnostics. A close clears them |\n| `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` (sent) | Every diagnostic of the check: its range, severity, code (`N0205`) and message, with each note as related information |\n| `textDocument/hover` | On a name or an expression: its type, as CeeSafe writes it. On a local or parameter, `let x: T` or `var x: T`; on a function or method, its signature; on a field, `name: T` |\n| `textDocument/definition` | On a local, parameter, function, method, field, struct, enum or interface name: the location of its declaration, if it is in the document |\n| `textDocument/documentSymbol` | The document's items and their members, as a tree: functions, structs (fields and methods), enums (variants), interfaces, constants |\n| `textDocument/formatting` | The document formatted by `ceesafec fmt` (`formatting.md`), as one edit; nothing if it has a syntax error |\n| `textDocument/codeAction` | The fixes (§5.2) of the diagnostics in the requested range, as `quickfix` actions (§5.5) |\n| `shutdown`, `exit` | Stops |\n\nAnything else is answered with the JSON-RPC error `MethodNotFound` (requests) or ignored (notifications).\n\nPositions are LSP positions: zero-based lines, and columns in UTF-16 code units, which the server converts to and from\nthe documents' UTF-8 bytes. Hover and definition use the typed tree of the last check; if the document does not parse,\nthey answer null and only the diagnostics are shown.\n\n### 2.3 Errors\n\nThe server never stops on a bad document. A request that fails inside the compiler is answered with the JSON-RPC\nerror `InternalError` and the exception's message, and the server goes on. A message that is not valid JSON-RPC is\nanswered with `ParseError` when it has an id.\n\n### 2.4 The VS Code extension\n\n`editors/vscode` holds:\n- a TextMate grammar for `.cee` files (keywords, literals, comments, attributes), whose keyword list is the\n  compiler's (`editors/vscode/test/keywords.test.mjs` compares them; Nova's `LspTests`);\n- a language configuration (comments, brackets, auto-closing pairs);\n- the client, which starts `ceesafec lsp` (the `ceesafe.server.path` setting, default `ceesafec` on the `PATH`) through the\n  `vscode-languageclient` library, bundled with it into `dist/extension.cjs`.\n\nIt is built with [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) (`vp`), not with `ceesafec`:\n\n```sh\ncd editors/vscode\nvp install            # the dependencies, pinned by pnpm-lock.yaml\nvp test               # the grammar and the server tests below\nvp run check          # lint and formatting\nvp run package        # bundle the client (vp pack) and make ceesafe-language-0.1.0.vsix (vsce)\n```\n\nIts tests: `test/grammar.test.mjs` tokenizes CeeSafe lines with `vscode-textmate`, as VS Code does, and checks the scope\neach token gets; `test/server.test.mjs` drives a built `ceesafec lsp` through `vscode-jsonrpc`, the library under VS\nCode's client (diagnostics, hover, definition, an unknown method, shutdown and exit).\n\n## 3. Debugging (T4)\n\nA CeeSafe program is debugged as the C it compiles to, with the C mapped back to CeeSafe lines. No debugger needs to\nknow about CeeSafe.\n\n### 3.1 A debug build\n\n`ceesafec build -g file.cee -o program` writes `#line` directives into the C (`c-backend.md`, source maps) and compiles\nit with `-g -O0`. A debugger then shows CeeSafe files and lines: breakpoints set by CeeSafe line stop there, and stepping\ngoes from one CeeSafe statement to the next. A line can take more than one step, since one CeeSafe statement can be several\nC statements (a checked add",
    196 =>
        "ition, a temporary).\n\n| | lldb | gdb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop at a CeeSafe line | `breakpoint set -f demo.cee -l 12` (`b demo.cee:12`) | `break demo.cee:12` |\n| Stop when a check fails, before the program aborts | `b ceesafe_panic` | `break ceesafe_panic` |\n| Where am I | `bt` | `bt` |\n\n### 3.2 Names\n\nThe C names follow `c-backend.md` §2.1, so a CeeSafe name can be found in the debugger:\n\n| CeeSafe | C |\n|---|---|\n| function `length` | `ceeF_length` |\n| method `Counter.bump` | `ceeM_7Counter_bump` (the type's name, prefixed by its length) |\n| local or parameter `sum` | `v_sum_1` (a number makes each local's name unique in its function) |\n| a temporary the compiler made | `t_3` |\n| field `x` | `f_x` |\n| struct `Point` | `ceeT_Point` |\n\n### 3.3 CeeSafe names in the debugger\n\n`editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py` and `editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py` add two commands, which print with CeeSafe names:\n- `ceesafe-locals`: the current frame's locals and parameters, `name = value`, without the compiler's temporaries; struct\n  values show their fields by their CeeSafe names;\n- `ceesafe-bt`: the backtrace, one frame a line, `CeeSafe name at file:line` (`Counter.bump at demo.cee:3`).\n\nLoad them with `command script import editors/debug/ceesafe_lldb.py` (lldb) or `source editors/debug/ceesafe_gdb.py` (gdb).\n\nSplit C (`--split`, c-backend.md §2.4) renames the program's functions to `ceeF_<program>_…`: set `CEESAFE_PROGRAM` to\nthe program's name before starting the debugger, and the helpers read them as before.\n\n### 3.4 What is checked\n\n`tools/debugger_check.py` (Nova's `DebuggerTests`) builds a program with `-g` and runs it under lldb (macOS) or gdb (Linux), in batch mode. A breakpoint\nat a CeeSafe line must stop at that line, `ceesafe-locals` must show the locals by their CeeSafe names, and a breakpoint on\n`ceesafe_panic` must give a backtrace whose frames are CeeSafe functions at CeeSafe lines. On a machine with neither debugger,\nthe test does nothing, and the run log says which debugger ran.\n\n## 4. Packages (T6, ADR 0013)\n\n### 4.1 The manifest\n\nA package is a directory with `ceesafe.toml`. Its sources are the `.cee` files under the directories `sources` names\n(default `src`), in ordinal path order. The manifest is a subset of TOML: `[table]` headers, `key = value` lines\nwhose value is a string, a boolean, an array of strings or an inline table of strings, and `#` comments.\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"app\"                 # required: an identifier, not a keyword and not \"core\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"            # optional; required to publish (§4.8), MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\nkind = \"executable\"          # \"executable\" (default), \"library\", or \"object\" (profiles.md §4.3)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float (type-system.md §35.9)\nkernel = \"cosmoos\"           # with profile = \"freestanding\": include lib/kernel, as --kernel cosmoos (ADR 0025)\ntrusted = true               # its types may be declared @threadSafe (ADR 0025 §3.2); listed for reviewers\nsources = [\"src\"]\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C files compiled and linked with an executable (type-system.md §28.6)\n\n[dependencies]\ngeometry = { path = \"../geometry\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }      # or rev = \"<commit>\", or branch = \"main\"\nshapes = \"2.1\"               # from the registry (§4.6): a version requirement\njson = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }   # from another index\n```\n\nA dependency's key is its package's `name`: a dependency is known by the name its own manifest gives it. A\ndependency of kind `executable` is N0321: only a library's items are used. Errors in the manifest are N0320 (its\nsyntax) and N0321 (a missing, unknown or invalid field), with the line.\n\n### 4.2 Resolving dependencies\n\nFrom the package being built, every dependency is resolved, and then its dependencies, recursively:\n- `path` is a directory relative to the manifest that names it, holding the ",
    197 =>
        "dependency's `ceesafe.toml`;\n- `git` is a repository; `rev` names a commit, `tag` and `branch` are resolved to one (§4.3). The commit is\n  checked out into the cache, `$CEESAFE_HOME/git/checkouts/<repository>/<commit>` (default `~/.ceesafe`), and the\n  dependency is the package at the checkout's root.\n\nA package name reached by two different sources (two paths, two commits, a path and a commit) is N0326, naming both\ndependents. A package that depends on itself, directly or not, is N0327. A missing directory or manifest is N0322.\nNothing runs during resolution except `git` (ADR 0013 D5).\n\n### 4.3 The lock file\n\n`ceesafe.lock`, beside `ceesafe.toml`, records the resolution so that every build uses the same code:\n\n```toml\n# Written by ceesafec fetch. Commit it with ceesafe.toml.\n[[package]]\nname = \"lz4\"\nsource = \"git+https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\"\ncommit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"         # the full commit id\nhash = \"sha256:…\"            # of the checkout's manifest and sources\n\n[[package]]\nname = \"geometry\"\nsource = \"path+../geometry\"\n```\n\n- `ceesafec fetch` resolves every dependency, fetching git repositories and resolving tags and branches (`git\n  ls-remote`, `git fetch`), and writes the lock. A `rev` is used as written; a `tag` or `branch` is the commit it names\n  at that moment, until the next `ceesafec fetch`.\n- Every other command reads the lock. It fails with N0324 if the manifest has a dependency the lock lacks or\n  records differently (run `ceesafec fetch`), and with N0325 if a git checkout's manifest and sources no longer have the\n  lock's hash. A missing checkout of a locked commit is fetched, unless `--offline` is given (N0323 if it cannot be).\n- A path dependency has no hash: it is code being worked on beside the package.\n- The hash is SHA-256 over, for the manifest and each source file in ordinal order, the path relative to the package\n  root, a zero byte, the file's bytes and a zero byte.\n\n### 4.4 Commands\n\n- `ceesafec new <name>` makes `<name>/ceesafe.toml` and `<name>/src/main.cee` (a `main` that prints a line); `--lib`\n  makes a library with `src/lib.cee` instead. It also writes `.gitignore` (`out/`) and the agent instructions of\n  §7: `AGENTS.md` by default, others with `--agent`, none with `--no-agent`.\n- `ceesafec agents [--agent …]` writes or refreshes the agent instructions of the package in the current directory or\n  above it (§7.3).\n- `ceesafec fetch [--offline] [--update [name]]` resolves and writes `ceesafe.lock` (§4.7).\n- `ceesafec publish` checks that the package can be published and prints its index entry (§4.8).\n- `ceesafec build`, `check`, `run`, `doc` and `fmt` without file arguments use the package of the nearest `ceesafe.toml` in\n  the current directory or above it. `build` writes `out/<name>` (an executable, or `out/<name>.o` for `object`), or\n  the C sources with `--emit c`; the manifest's profile, `float` and C files apply, and command-line options add to\n  them. `fmt` formats the package's own sources, not its dependencies'.\n- The language server (§2.1) checks a document that belongs to a package (a `ceesafe.toml` above it, whose sources\n  include it) with that package: the open document's text, the package's other files from disk, and its locked\n  dependencies. Without a lock, or outside a package, the document is checked alone as before.\n\n### 4.5 Errors\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| N0320 | `ceesafe.toml` or `ceesafe.lock` is not valid in the TOML subset of §4.1 |\n| N0321 | A manifest field is missing, unknown, or has an invalid value |\n| N0322 | A path dependency's directory or manifest is missing, or a dependency is named differently from its key |\n| N0323 | A git dependency cannot be fetched or resolved, or is needed with `--offline` and absent |\n| N0324 | The lock file is missing or does not match the manifest: run `ceesafec fetch` |\n| N0325 | A git checkout's manifest and sources do not match the lock's hash |\n| N0326 | One package name reached through two different sources |\n| ",
    198 =>
        "N0327 | A dependency cycle |\n| N0328 | An invalid version, version requirement, or index entry |\n| N0329 | No versions satisfy the requirements: the chain of requirements that conflict |\n| N0335 | `ceesafec publish`: the package cannot be published as it is |\n\n### 4.6 The registry (M13 G6, ADR 0017)\n\nA **registry dependency** names a package and a **version requirement**. The package is found in an **index**: a git\nrepository, by default `https://github.com/vkuttyp/CeeSafe-index`, or `$CEESAFE_INDEX`, or the dependency's `index`.\n\n- **Versions** are `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, three decimal numbers with no leading zeros, compared numerically. There\n  are no pre-release or build suffixes in v1.\n- **Requirements** are one or more comparators separated by commas, all of which must hold:\n  - `\"1.4\"`, `\"^1.4\"` and `\"^1.4.2\"` are caret requirements: at least that version, below the next major version, or\n    below the next minor for `0.x` (`\"0.3\"` is `>=0.3.0, <0.4.0`) and the next patch for `0.0.x`.\n  - `\"=1.4.2\"` means exactly that version.\n  - `\">=1.4\"`, `\">1.4\"`, `\"<2\"` and `\"<=1.9.3\"` compare. A missing minor or patch is 0.\n  - `\"*\"` means any version.\n- **The index** holds `packages/<name>.toml`, with one `[[version]]` table per published version, in publishing order:\n  ```toml\n  [[version]]\n  version = \"1.4.0\"\n  git = \"https://github.com/someone/geometry.git\"\n  commit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"                      # a full commit id\n  hash = \"sha256:…\"                         # of the commit's manifest and sources (§4.3)\n  dependencies = { shapes = \"2.1\" }         # its registry requirements, as its ceesafe.toml writes them\n  yanked = false                            # optional; a yanked version is never chosen for a new resolution\n  ```\n  Entries are only appended. A published package's dependencies are all registry dependencies, and those without\n  an `index` come from the index that published the package.\n\n### 4.7 Version solving\n\n`ceesafec fetch` resolves path and git dependencies as before (§4.2), gathering the registry requirements of the root\nand of every path and git package. It then chooses one version of each registry package, transitively, from the\nindex's entries:\n\n- The **newest** version that satisfies every requirement on it is chosen, and so on through its own dependencies.\n  On a conflict, the solver backtracks.\n- A version the lock already records is preferred while it satisfies the requirements, so `ceesafec fetch` changes\n  nothing that needs no change. `--update` prefers the newest for every package, and `--update <name>` for that one.\n- **One version per package name** (OD-26). A name reached through a path or git dependency and through the registry\n  is N0326.\n- If no choice satisfies every requirement, N0329 shows the requirements on the package that could not be chosen,\n  and where each came from:\n  `no version of 'shapes' satisfies ^2.1 (from geometry 1.4.0) and ^1.0 (from plot 0.3.0)`.\n- Each chosen version's commit is checked out as for a git dependency (§4.2). Its hash must be the index's (N0325),\n  and its manifest's name and version must be the entry's (N0328).\n- The lock records each as `source = \"registry+<index>\"` with `version`, `repository` (the git URL), `commit` and\n  `hash`. Other commands use the lock: an entry whose version no longer satisfies the manifest's requirement is\n  N0324.\n\n### 4.8 Publishing\n\n`ceesafec publish` (no network but `git`, and nothing written) checks the package in the current directory:\n\n- it is a library with a valid `version`;\n- every dependency is a registry dependency;\n- it type-checks with its dependencies;\n- its git working tree is clean, and its commit is on a remote branch.\n\nIt then prints the index entry for the version: the remote's URL, the commit, the hash and the requirements. To\npublish, add that entry to `packages/<name>.toml` in the index by pull request. The index's continuous integration\nruns `ceesafec index-check`, which checks every new entry:\n- the commit",
    199 =>
        " exists;\n- its manifest has the entry's name and version;\n- its hash and dependencies are the entry's;\n- the version is newer than every earlier entry.\n\nA new package name is reviewed by the index's maintainers. Later versions from the same repository merge after the\nchecks pass.\n\n## 5. Diagnostics for programs (M12 A1)\n\nPeople read diagnostics as text (`diagnostics.md`, \"Format\"). Programs, AI agents and editors among them, read the\nsame diagnostics as JSON, and can apply the fixes the compiler is sure of.\n\n### 5.1 `--diagnostics-format=json`\n\n`check`, `build`, `run` and `parse` accept `--diagnostics-format=json` (or `=text`, the default). With `json`,\ndiagnostics are not written to standard error; instead one JSON document is written to standard output, as one line,\nafter the command's own work. `build` still writes its outputs, and `run` still runs the program, if there are no\nerrors; the program's own output then precedes the document. The exit status is unchanged.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"errors\": 1,\n  \"warnings\": 0,\n  \"diagnostics\": [\n    {\n      \"code\": \"N0249\",\n      \"severity\": \"error\",\n      \"file\": \"main.cee\",\n      \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n      \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 11 },\n      \"message\": \"this parameter is 'borrow mut': write 'mut' before the argument to let the call modify it\",\n      \"summary\": \"`mut` argument marker missing or misplaced\",\n      \"notes\": [ { \"file\": \"main.cee\", \"start\": { … }, \"end\": { … }, \"message\": \"…\" } ],\n      \"fixes\": [\n        { \"title\": \"write 'mut' before the argument\",\n          \"edits\": [ { \"file\": \"main.cee\", \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 }, \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n                       \"newText\": \"mut \" } ] }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `version` is the format's version: fields may be added within a version, never removed or changed.\n- Positions are 1-based lines and columns, columns in UTF-16 code units, as in the text form. `end` is exclusive.\n- `file` is the path as the text form prints it.\n- `summary` is the code's \"Meaning\" in `diagnostics.md`, which `ceesafec` embeds.\n- Diagnostics are in the order the text form prints them. Keys are written in the order shown.\n\n### 5.2 Fixes\n\nA fix is a list of edits that the compiler is sure resolves the diagnostic. It is offered only where the\nintent is unambiguous; a diagnostic with several plausible repairs offers none. Applying a fix removes that\ndiagnostic and introduces none at the edited place (checked by Nova's `FixTests` for every kind below, and in CeeSafe by applying every corpus fix as novac 0.6.0 does, `tools/compare_cli.py`, on every corpus\ncase that offers it). The kinds:\n\n| Code | When | Edit |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0249 | an argument for a `borrow mut` parameter lacks `mut`, and is a place that can be borrowed mutably | insert `mut ` before the argument; if the place is a `let` local, also `let` → `var` at its declaration (one fix) |\n| N0249 | an argument for a shared parameter has `mut` | delete the `mut ` |\n| N0211 | assignment to a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0248 | `borrow mut` (or a `borrow mut this` call) of a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0208 | `e as T` between integer types that can lose information | `truncate<T>(e)` (wrap-around, as the message says) |\n| N0100 | `expected ';'`, with no earlier error in the file, after a declaration, assignment, `return`, or a call statement (not after an expression that cannot be a statement, N0225) | insert `;` after the previous token |\n\n### 5.3 `ceesafec fix`\n\n`ceesafec fix [FILES]` (or the package, without files) applies every fix, writes the files, checks again, and repeats\nuntil no fix applies or after 8 rounds. It then prints the remaining diagnostics, as text or with\n`--diagnostics-format=json`, and exits 1 if errors remain. With `--dry-run` it changes no file and prints the\nedits of the first round. Edits to one file never over",
    200 =>
        "lap: when two fixes would, the later one waits for the next\nround.\n\n### 5.4 `ceesafec explain`\n\n`ceesafec explain N0249` prints the code's row of `diagnostics.md` (its meaning and example) and the paragraphs of\n`diagnostics.md` that mention it. An unknown code is exit status 2.\n\n### 5.5 In the language server\n\n`textDocument/codeAction` returns the fixes of the diagnostics that touch the requested range as `quickfix` actions,\neach with its diagnostic and a `WorkspaceEdit` for the document (§2.2). A fix that would edit another file is not\noffered.\n\n## 6. The MCP server (M12 A2)\n\n`ceesafec mcp` serves the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) on standard input and output, so an\nAI agent's host (Claude Code, an IDE, any MCP client) can use CeeSafe's compiler, interpreter and documentation as\ntools. It is part of `ceesafec`, with no other dependency, and serves the documents embedded in that `ceesafec`, so the\nanswers always match the compiler that gives them.\n\n```json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"ceesafe\": { \"command\": \"ceesafec\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } }\n```\n\n### 6.1 Protocol\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0, one message per line of UTF-8 on standard input and output; nothing else is written to standard\n  output. The server stops at the end of its input.\n- `initialize` negotiates the protocol version: the client's, if it is one the server supports (`2025-06-18`,\n  `2025-03-26`, `2024-11-05`), otherwise the newest of those. The server declares `tools`, `resources` and `prompts`,\n  none of which change while it runs, and gives brief usage `instructions`.\n- `ping`, `tools/list`, `tools/call`, `resources/list`, `resources/read`, `prompts/list` and `prompts/get` are\n  answered; other requests are `MethodNotFound` (-32601); notifications are ignored.\n- With version `2025-06-18`, each tool declares an `outputSchema` and its result carries `structuredContent`, with the\n  same data as JSON text in `content`. With earlier versions, only `content`.\n- A tool call that the tool cannot carry out (a file it cannot read, a source that does not parse when it needs to)\n  is a result with `isError: true` and the reason. Unknown tools and invalid arguments are `InvalidParams` (-32602).\n  The server never stops on a bad request.\n\n### 6.2 Sources\n\nEvery tool that compiles takes its program as either\n- `files`: an array of `{ \"path\": \"main.cee\", \"text\": \"…\" }`, compiled as one package, with nothing read from disk; or\n- `paths`: an array of `.cee` file paths, read from disk relative to the server's working directory;\n\nplus `profile` (`hosted`, the default, or `freestanding`) and `noFloat` (default false). Paths in results are the\npaths given.\n\n### 6.3 Tools\n\n| Tool | Input (besides §6.2) | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ceesafe_check` | | the diagnostics document of §5.1, with fixes |\n| `ceesafe_fix` | | `files`: each file's text with every fix applied, over rounds as `ceesafec fix` does (§5.3); `applied`: the number of fixes; `diagnostics`: what remains. Nothing is written to disk |\n| `ceesafe_format` | `text`, `path` (optional) | `text`: formatted (`formatting.md`); or an error with the syntax diagnostics |\n| `ceesafe_run` | `stdin` (text), `args`, `maxSteps` (default 10,000,000), `maxOutputBytes` (default 65,536) | `outcome` (`exited`, `panicked`, `fault`, `not-compiled`), `exitCode`, `stdout`, `stderr`, `truncated`, and the diagnostics if it did not compile |\n| `ceesafe_emit_c` | `explain`: any of `allocations`, `bounds-checks`, `overflow-checks` | `files`: the generated C (`main.c`, and `ceesafe_exports.h` when there is one; not the runtime); `explanations`: the `--explain-*` text |\n| `ceesafe_explain` | `code` | `code`, `meaning`, `example`, `paragraphs` of `diagnostics.md` (§5.4) |\n| `ceesafe_spec_search` | `query`, `limit` (default 10) | `results`: the sections whose heading or text contain every word of the query, best first: `document`, `heading`, `snippet`, `uri` |\n| `ceesafe_spec_section` | `document` (`type-system`, `grammar`, …), `section` (`31.",
    201 =>
        "2`, or heading text) | `text` of that section, with its subsections |\n| `ceesafe_symbol` | `path`, `line`, `column` (1-based) | the type or signature under the position, and its declaration's location, as hover and definition give them (§2.2) |\n\n`ceesafe_run` runs the program in the interpreter (`run --interp`) **sandboxed**:\n- files, sockets and processes are refused (a fault that names the refusal);\n- `maxSteps` statements at most (a fault), and `maxOutputBytes` of each output kept (`truncated` says if more was\n  written; the program goes on);\n- 256 MiB of interpreter memory at most: the heap then reports out of memory, which the program sees as\n  `AllocError.OutOfMemory`; a local or static allocation beyond it is a fault;\n- standard input is `stdin`, the arguments are `args`, and the run has no access to the server's environment.\n\nA fault's exit code is 70 and a panic's 134, as for `run --interp`.\n\n### 6.4 Resources\n\nEach embedded document is a resource `ceesafe://docs/<path>` (for example `ceesafe://docs/docs/type-system.md`,\n`ceesafe://docs/site/guide/basics.md`), `text/markdown`, named by its first heading. `resources/read` returns its text.\nAn unknown URI is the error -32002 (resource not found).\n\n### 6.5 Prompts\n\n| Prompt | Arguments | What it asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `write-ceesafe` | `task` | To write CeeSafe for the task: the agent guide (`guide/for-agents.md`), then the task, with the edit-check loop of `ceesafe_check` and `ceesafe_fix` |\n| `port-c-to-ceesafe` | `file`, `api` | To port a C file to CeeSafe as C6 and C9 did: imported C structs from the header, safe code over slices, `unsafe` only at the exported boundary, and the C's own tests, a differential test and a benchmark (`cosmoos/c9-report.md`) |\n\n## 7. Agent instructions in packages (M12 A4)\n\nAI coding agents read a file of instructions at the root of a project. `ceesafec new` writes one, so that an agent\nworking in a new CeeSafe package knows from the start what CeeSafe is, which commands check its work, and the mistakes to\navoid. The convention follows Vite+'s (`vp create`).\n\n### 7.1 The CeeSafe section\n\n`AGENTS.md` holds the **CeeSafe section**, between the lines `<!--CEESAFE START-->` and `<!--CEESAFE END-->`: what CeeSafe is and\nwhere its guide for agents is; the package's layout; the commands (`check`, `fix`, `run --interp`, `build`, `fmt`,\n`explain`, `--diagnostics-format=json`); how to use the MCP server; a checklist before finishing; and the common\nmistakes of the agent guide. The text is embedded in `ceesafec` (`templates/AGENTS.md`) and is the same for every\npackage. Everything outside the markers belongs to the package's authors: ceesafec never changes it.\n\n### 7.2 Agents\n\n`--agent` takes one or more of these names, separated by commas or by repeating the option; the default is `agents`.\nEach name writes the files below; `AGENTS.md` is written whenever any agent is chosen, since the others refer to it.\n\n| Name | Agent | Instructions | MCP configuration (`ceesafec mcp`, §6) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `agents` | Codex, Amp, OpenCode and others that read `AGENTS.md` | `AGENTS.md` | — |\n| `claude` | Claude Code | `CLAUDE.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Claude Code's import) | `.mcp.json` |\n| `gemini` | Gemini CLI | `GEMINI.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Gemini CLI's import) | `.gemini/settings.json` |\n| `copilot` | GitHub Copilot | `.github/copilot-instructions.md`: a copy of the CeeSafe section | `.vscode/mcp.json` |\n| `cursor` | Cursor | `.cursor/rules/ceesafe.mdc`: a copy of the CeeSafe section, applied always | `.cursor/mcp.json` |\n\nAn unknown name is a usage error (exit status 2), and so is `--agent` with `--no-agent`. The MCP configurations name\nthe server `ceesafe`, with command `ceesafec` and argument `mcp`, in each tool's format: `{\"mcpServers\": {\"ceesafe\": …}}`, or\n`{\"servers\": {\"ceesafe\": {\"type\": \"stdio\", …}}}` for VS Code.\n\n### 7.3 `ceesafec agents`\n\nIn a package, `ceesafec agents [--agent …]` writes the chosen agents' files (default `agents`):\n- a missing ",
    202 =>
        "file is written as `ceesafec new` writes it;\n- in an instructions file that has the markers, the text between them is replaced with the current CeeSafe section,\n  and the rest is kept; a file without them gets the CeeSafe section appended, after a blank line; a file that only\n  imports `AGENTS.md` (`@AGENTS.md`) is left as it is;\n- an MCP configuration that exists is left as it is, and the command says so: it may hold the authors' other servers.\n\nIt prints what it wrote and what it left. Run it after updating `ceesafec`, so the instructions match the compiler.\n\n## 8. The C compiler (`ceesafec build`)\n\n`ceesafec build` writes C and gives it to a C compiler. It needs no configuration when a usual compiler is installed.\n\n### 8.1 Which compiler\n\n`--cc <compiler>` names it; otherwise `$CC`; otherwise:\n- on Linux and macOS, `cc`;\n- on Windows, the first of these that exists: `clang` on `PATH`; `%ProgramFiles%\\LLVM\\bin\\clang.exe` (where the LLVM\n  installer, `winget install LLVM.LLVM`, puts it); `gcc` on `PATH` (MinGW-w64); `cl` on `PATH`; Visual Studio's `cl`\n  (§8.2). clang and gcc are fully supported; MSVC is best effort (`c-backend.md` §1.1).\n\nWith none, the error names what was looked for and how to install one.\n\n### 8.2 Visual Studio's environment (Windows)\n\nclang on Windows and `cl` compile against Visual Studio's C library and the Windows SDK, which they find through the\nenvironment that Visual Studio's developer prompt sets (`INCLUDE`, `LIB`, `PATH`). When that environment is missing\n(`INCLUDE` unset) and the compiler is clang or `cl`, `ceesafec build` sets it up for the compiler, as the developer prompt\nwould:\n1. find Visual Studio, or its Build Tools, with the C++ tools for this machine: `vswhere -latest -products *\n   -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.ARM64` on an ARM64 Windows,\n   `…VC.Tools.x86.x64` otherwise (other products that `vswhere` knows, such as SQL Server Management Studio, have no\n   C++ tools and are not taken);\n2. run its `VC\\Auxiliary\\Build\\vcvarsall.bat` for the machine's architecture (`arm64` or `x64`) and take the\n   environment it leaves;\n3. run the compiler in that environment. The user's own environment is not changed.\n\nWithout Visual Studio, clang still works where the environment is already set; otherwise the error says to install\nVisual Studio's \"Desktop development with C++\" workload (or the Build Tools), or MinGW-w64.\n\n### 8.3 Flags\n\n| Compiler | Flags |\n|---|---|\n| clang, gcc, `cc` | `c-backend.md` §1's: `-std=c11`, the strict warnings, `-ffp-contract=off -fno-math-errno`, `-O2` (or `-g -O0`), `--cflag`s, `-o <output>`; on Windows also the 8 MiB stack, `-Wl,/STACK:8388608` for clang and `-Wl,--stack,8388608` for gcc |\n| `cl` (MSVC) | `/nologo /std:c11 /W3 /O2` (or `/Zi /Od` with `-g`), `--cflag`s, `/Fo:` the build directory (so no `.obj` lands in the project), `/Fe:<output>`, `/link /STACK:8388608` (`c-backend.md` §1.1) |\n\nA compiler is MSVC when its file name is `cl` or `cl.exe`. `--emit obj` (one relocatable object) needs clang or gcc.\n\n### 8.4 The output\n\nThe executable is `out/<name>` in a package, or `-o <path>`. On Windows, a path without an extension gets `.exe`, so\n`ceesafec build` makes `out\\hello.exe`. The output's directory is created if it does not exist.\n\n## 9. Porting C to CeeSafe: `c2cee`\n\nCosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 moves kernel C into Nova, the language CeeSafe forked from, one file at a time (SA2, SA3), and\nevery port repeats the same mechanical steps: map the C types, declare each function the file calls, choose `@header`s\nthat do not include the port's own header back, find what must stay in a C floor, rename members that are keywords,\nwire the build, and run the same checks. `integrations/cosmoos/c2cee.py` does the analysis and the checks, and drafts\nthe `.cee` file, the floor and the build lines (§9.4). It is Nova's `c2nova` (written after the fork, at Nova\n`3ba382b`) for CeeSafe: its drafts equal `c2nova`'s under CeeSafe's names. CosmoOS keeps its ports",
    203 =>
        " as Nova and builds\nthem with novac (CeeSafe's Decision 0003), so a finished port is checked with ceesafec and lands in CosmoOS's tree as a\n`.nova` file, the same language at the fork. It reads C through clang's own front end (`-Xclang -ast-dump=json`), so\nit needs nothing but clang and Python 3, and never edits a file.\n\n### 9.1 `c2cee.py analyse <file.c> [--header <h>] [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch x86_64|aarch64] [--json]`\n\nParses `<file.c>` twice, as a debug build and as a release one (`CONFIG_DEBUG=1`, `0`), with CosmoOS's include paths\nfor `--arch` (default x86_64), and reports:\n\n- **Functions** the file defines: their C and CeeSafe signatures, and whether a module export (`EXPORT_SYMBOL`) names them.\n- **Calls**, each callee with the header that declares it and what that means for the port:\n  - a function: an `extern \"C\"` declaration;\n  - `static inline` in a header: callable through that header (type-system §28.2);\n  - an ordinary function in one build and `static inline` in the other (lockdep's hooks): it **must** be declared\n    through its header, or the build where it is inline fails to link (CosmoOS #499);\n  - variadic (`panic`, `klog`): a C floor;\n  - through a function pointer: a C floor when the pointer is a member of an imported struct (§31.1).\n- **Builtins**: `__builtin_return_address` (CeeSafe's `callerAddress()` in an export), `__atomic_*` (an atomic view of the\n  field; a `bool` field needs a `u32`, since lib/core's `AtomicBool` is four bytes), `__atomic_thread_fence` (a floor:\n  CeeSafe has no fence).\n- **Macros** expanded in the file's functions, from their expansion locations: `wait_event*` (wait_floor.c's helpers),\n  `list_for_each_entry*` (a loop over lib/kernel's `List` or `offsetOf`), `might_sleep` (`wait_floor_check_sleep`),\n  `KASSERT`, `WARN`, `EXPORT_SYMBOL` (a floor).\n- **Locals of struct type** that hold C state (a wait entry, a timer): a CeeSafe local cannot (§33.1), so a floor helper.\n- With `--header <h>`, the **structs it defines**, field by field: the CeeSafe type, members whose names are CeeSafe keywords\n  (renamed, or `@cname` on an imported struct), `const` pointer members (a `@repr(C)` struct's carry no `const`),\n  fields used atomically, and every other file in the tree that names a member.\n- **`@header` candidates**: the headers that declare what the file uses, each marked unusable when it includes `<h>`,\n  directly or not, since the generated types' header is included from `<h>` (ADR 0030; `thread.h` → `completion.h` →\n  `wait.h`).\n\nThe report is text, or JSON with `--json`. It exits 0 unless clang fails.\n\n### 9.2 `c2cee.py verify [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch x86_64|aarch64|both] [--no-boot] [--max-load <n>] [--novac <path>]`\n\nRuns a port's checks in a CosmoOS tree, in order, with `NOVAC` set to `--novac` (default `~/.nova/bin/novac`, the\nrelease CosmoOS pins; its Make builds the tree's Nova with it), and prints one line per step and a summary:\n1. `make nova-check` (the generated files match their sources under the pinned novac);\n2. `make host-test`, with every recorded trace digest it prints;\n3. `make check-docs`;\n4. per architecture: the debug and the release build, `make analyze` (and, for the first architecture, `make\n   fuzz-build`, whose shims define some of a port's functions over its types), and unless `--no-boot` a debug and a release boot\n   (`make test`). Before each boot it waits until the one-minute load average is under `--max-load` (default 8),\n   since the boot tests' time bounds fail on a loaded host. A boot that fails after the load average reached the\n   bound at any point during it (another job started meanwhile; it is sampled every five seconds) is run once more\n   on a quiet host: both lines are printed, and the second\n   decides.\n\nIt exits 1 if any step failed, naming the first.\n\n### 9.3 Checks of the tool\n\n`c2cee.py selftest` analyses `integrations/cosmoos/c2cee/fixture.c` with `fixture.h` and compares the report with\n`fixture.expected.json`, and drafts its",
    204 =>
        " port (§9.4) and compares that with `fixture.draft.cee` and\n`fixture_floor.draft.c`. The fixture holds one of each case of §9.1.\n\n### 9.4 `c2cee.py port <file.c> --header <h> --out <dir> [--cosmoos <tree>] [--arch …]`\n\nWrites a **draft** of the port into `<dir>`, never into the tree: `<name>.cee`, `<name>_floor.c` and `BUILD.md` (the\n`ceesafec check` of the finished port, CosmoOS's `nova-gen.py`, `kernel.mk` and `host.mk` lines, and what to change in\n`<h>`). The draft is unsafe CeeSafe that mirrors the\nC, statement by statement, for a person to finish and review; every place it could not translate, or translated with a\ndecision someone must check, carries `TODO(c2cee)` with the reason, and it never drops a construct silently.\n\n- **Names** stay C's (functions, parameters, locals, statics), so the draft reads beside the original; a name that is a\n  CeeSafe keyword gets `_` appended. Each function's body is one `unsafe` block, and its doc comment has a `Safety:` line\n  marked `TODO(c2cee)`. A function `EXPORT_SYMBOL` names, or that the header declares, is `@export(\"name\")`; one the\n  file passes as a function pointer is `@callback`.\n- **Types**: `<h>`'s structs become `@repr(C)` structs (a keyword member renamed, `fn` to `callback`, `class` to\n  `lock_class`, otherwise with `_` appended, and listed in `BUILD.md`); every other struct the file uses is imported,\n  with the fields it reads; typedefs of integers are their CeeSafe integer, of structs `@ctype` imports.\n- **Declarations**: an `extern \"C\"` block per kind: callees whose headers may be `@header`s (§9.1), and the rest without\n  a header; the floor's functions; `@constant` macros used in static initialisers.\n- **Statics**: integer, `bool`, pointer and array statics, and those initialised by a C constant macro (`SPINLOCK_INIT`),\n  are CeeSafe `static mut`s; a static of a C struct type, or one whose initialiser names the static itself\n  (`WAITQUEUE_INIT(name)`), stays in the floor, and the draft reaches it through a floor function that returns its\n  address.\n- **Statements and expressions**: blocks, declarations, `if`, `while`, `for` (as a `while`, unless its body has a\n  `continue`), `do` (as a `loop`), `return`, `break`, `continue`, assignments and compound assignments, `++`/`--` as\n  statements, calls, `?:` (an `if` expression), casts (`as` when it cannot lose information, else `truncate`), pointer\n  comparisons with null (`isNull()`), `*p` (`read`/`write`), `p->f` (`Ptr.toBorrow`, and for a write a `let` of\n  `Ptr.toBorrowMut` first), `&x` (`borrow mut … as *mut T`), `sizeof` (`layoutOf`), string literals, the `__atomic`\n  builtins on integers and pointers (an atomic view), `__builtin_return_address` (`callerAddress()`). Anything else,\n  `switch`, `goto` and `continue` in a `for` among them, is a `TODO(c2cee)` with the C text.\n- **Macros**: `KASSERT(c)` becomes `if (!(c))` and a floor call that panics with the condition's text; `might_sleep()`\n  `wait_floor_check_sleep`; `wait_event(q, c)` and `wait_event_timeout(q, c, t)` `wait_floor_until(_timeout)` with the\n  condition as a `@callback` over a context struct of the locals it reads; other macros are translated from their\n  expansion.\n- **The floor**: one C function per call of a variadic function (`panic`, `klog`, `kwarn`), with the format fixed and\n  the other arguments as parameters; the statics that stay in C, with their accessors; file-scope `STATIC_ASSERT`s; the\n  `EXPORT_SYMBOL` lines.\n- **Builds**: a function that differs between the debug and the release build (an `#if CONFIG_DEBUG` inside it, or\n  present in one build only) is drafted from the debug build with a `TODO(c2cee)` naming the difference: a draft from\n  one build alone would change the other.\n",
    205 =>
        "# Type System and Static Semantics — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1b implements it). Later milestones extend these\nrules. Where `language-spec.md` describes a later feature, it does not apply until then. Error\ncodes are listed in `diagnostics.md`.\n\n## 1. Types\n\n| Type | Values | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64` | two's complement, −2^(n−1) … 2^(n−1)−1 | |\n| `u8 u16 u32 u64` | 0 … 2^n−1 | |\n| `isize` / `usize` | pointer width of the **target** (64 on every M1 target) | from `TargetInfo`, never the host |\n| `bool` | `true`, `false` | |\n| `void` | no value | function return type only |\n| *never* | no value; the expression does not complete | internal only (`Process.exit`); not nameable |\n\nNot in M1 (error N0216): floats, `char`, strings as values, user-defined types, references. Floats came in M11\n(`f32`, `f64`, §35); character literals are integers (§4).\nIn M1 a string literal was allowed **only** as a direct argument of `Console.write`/`writeLine`;\nsince M6c it is a `StringView` value (§22.2).\n\n`void` may appear only as a return type (writing `-> void` is the same as omitting the\nreturn type). A *never* expression is accepted wherever any type is expected.\n\n## 2. Names and scopes\n\nA **package** is the unit of compilation and of naming (ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4): its files, its dependencies, and\n`lib/core`. A build checks the package being built together with every package it depends on, directly or not.\n\n- **Package scope.** All files of a package share one item scope. Items are referenced by simple name. Two items of\n  one package with the same name are an error (N0201), even in different files. `namespace` declarations are accepted\n  and have no effect (retired by ADR 0013; they will become an error).\n- **Dependencies** (M10 T5). A package names its dependencies in its manifest (`tooling.md` §4). A dependency's\n  **public** items are reached by qualifying them with the dependency's name:\n  - in a type, `geometry.Point`, `geometry.Pair<u8>`;\n  - in an expression, `geometry.area(p)`, `geometry.LIMIT`, `geometry.Shape.Circle(2)`, a struct literal\n    `geometry.Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; in a pattern, `geometry.Shape.Circle(r)`.\n\n  A static function of a generic type of a dependency is called through an import (`Pair<u8>.make()`), since\n  `geometry.Pair<u8>.make()` does not parse (grammar.md §2.1: in an expression, generic arguments follow a simple\n  name only).\n- **Dependency names are reserved.** In a package, nothing else may have the name of one of its dependencies: no\n  item, parameter, local, loop variable or pattern binding (N0315). So `geometry.x`, with `geometry` a dependency,\n  always means the dependency's item, and the compiler resolves it before type checking (`PackageNames`).\n- **Imports.** `import geometry;` at the top of a file brings every public item of the dependency `geometry` into\n  that file's scope; `import geometry.Point;` brings one (N0311 if it has none of that name, N0312 if it is not\n  public). An import applies to its file only. The name before the `.` must be a dependency of the package (N0310).\n- **Visibility between packages.** Only `public` items are visible to other packages, and of a public struct only its\n  `public` fields and methods; `internal`, the default, keeps an item within its package, and `private` within its\n  file (§12). Naming another package's non-public item is N0312. A public function, constant, field or struct whose\n  signature names a type its dependents cannot see (a public function returning an internal struct) is N0314. An\n  `impl I for T` applies wherever `I` and `T` are both visible.\n- **Built-in scope** (outside the package scope, so a user item hides a built-in): `Console.write`,\n  `Console.writeLine`, `Process.exit`, `truncate`, and the primitive type names.\n- **`lib/core`** is in scope in every package, unqualified, and no package may redeclare one of its names (N0201,\n  §29.2).\n- **Function scope.** Parameters, t",
    206 =>
        "hen nested block scopes. A local is visible from the end of\n  its declaration to the end of its block.\n- **No shadowing of locals.** Declaring a local or parameter whose name is already a local or\n  parameter in scope is an error (N0203), as in C#. Shadowing an *item* with a local is allowed.\n- **Lookup order** for a simple name: locals (innermost first), the package's items, the file's imports, `lib/core`,\n  built-ins. Two imports that both provide a name are an error where the name is used (N0313), not where it is\n  imported. For `a.b`: if `a` is a local or an item of the package or of `lib/core`, `.b` is a member as before;\n  otherwise, `a` being a dependency (and so not a local or item, N0315), `a.b` is that dependency's item `b`.\n- **Messages and C names** write an item of a dependency with its package's name, `geometry.Point`; the C backend\n  gives it a prefixed name, `ceeT_geometry_Point` (`c-backend.md` §2.1).\n\n## 3. Bindings and mutability\n\n- `let x = e;` declares an immutable local and **requires** an initialiser (N0220).\n- `var x = e;` / `var x: T = e;` / `var x: T;` declare a mutable local. Without an initialiser\n  the type is required (N0221), and the variable must be definitely assigned before every read (§7).\n- Without an annotation, a local's type is the type of its initialiser. An initialiser of type\n  `void` is an error (N0224).\n- Parameters and `const` items are immutable. Assigning to them, or to a `let`, is N0211.\n- Assignment targets in M1 are locals only. Member targets (`a.b = …`) are N0216.\n\n## 4. Integer literals\n\n- A suffixed literal (`7u8`) has the suffix's type.\n- An unsuffixed literal takes the **expected type** from context (§5) if that type is an\n  integer type. With no integer expectation it defaults to `i32`.\n- The value must be representable in the literal's type (N0206). A literal directly under\n  unary minus is checked as a negative value, so `-128i8` and `-9223372036854775808` (as `i64`) are valid.\n- **Character literals** (M11, G4a). `'k'` is an integer literal whose value is the character's Unicode scalar value\n  (`grammar.md` §1.6, escapes included: `'\\n'` is 10). It takes its type from context like an unsuffixed literal,\n  so `c == 'k'` with `c: u8` compares bytes, and `'0'..'9'` is a range. With no integer expectation it is a `u32`.\n  In an 8-bit type it must be ASCII (at most `0x7F`), since a larger scalar value is more than one byte in UTF-8 and\n  would not equal any byte of the text (N0206). It is a pattern too (§15.2). CeeSafe has no `char` type: text is UTF-8\n  bytes (§22.2), and a character type is left for when text gains character iteration.\n\n## 5. Expected types (bidirectional checking)\n\nEach expression is checked against an optional expected type:\n\n- local initialiser → the annotated type; assignment value → the target's type;\n  argument → the parameter type; `return` value → the return type; `const` value → its\n  declared type; `if`/`while` condition → `bool`; each value branch of an `if` or `match` expression → the\n  expression's expected type (§15.3).\n- An expression's *actual* type must equal the expected type, unless it is *never* (N0205).\n- **Flexible operands.** An expression is *flexible* if it consists only of unsuffixed integer\n  literals combined with unary `-`, parentheses and the arithmetic/bitwise binary operators, or (M11) if it is an\n  `if` or `match` expression whose expression branches are all flexible (§15.3). Since M11 (G7), unsuffixed float\n  literals combined the same way are flexible too; they take an expected `f32` or `f64` instead (§35.2).\n  For a binary operator whose operands must have the same type:\n  1. if the operator's result type is the operand type and an integer type is expected, both\n     operands are checked against it;\n  2. otherwise, if exactly one operand is flexible, the other operand is checked first\n     (without expectation) and its type becomes the flexible operand's expected type;\n  3. otherwise both are c",
    207 =>
        "hecked without expectation (flexible ones default to `i32`).\n\n  So `x + 1` with `x: u8` types `1` as `u8`, and `let y: u64 = 2 * 3;` types both literals as `u64`.\n\n## 6. Operators\n\nAll binary operators require both operands to have the **same type**. There are no implicit\nconversions.\n\n| Operators | Operand types | Result | Runtime behaviour (implemented in 1c) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+ - *` | integer | same | overflow → panic |\n| `/ %` | integer | same | zero divisor → panic. `MIN / -1` → panic (overflow). `/` truncates toward zero, `%` has the sign of the dividend, and `MIN % -1` is `0` |\n| `& \\| ^` | **unsigned** integer | same | |\n| `<< >>` | left: **unsigned** integer; right: any integer type | left type | amount `< 0` or `≥ bit width` → panic. `>>` is logical |\n| `== !=` | integer or `bool` | `bool` | |\n| `< <= > >=` | integer | `bool` | |\n| `&& \\|\\|` | `bool` | `bool` | short-circuit |\n| unary `-` | **signed** integer | same | `-MIN` → panic |\n| unary `!` | `bool` | `bool` | |\n\n**Explicit overflow behaviour** (M11, G4b; OD-6). The operators panic on overflow. Where a program wants another\nbehaviour, every integer type has nine methods. Each takes one argument of the receiver's type, and a flexible\nliteral adapts:\n\n| Method | Result | On overflow |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x.wrappingAdd(y)`, `x.wrappingSub(y)`, `x.wrappingMul(y)` | `T` | the result modulo 2^bits, in two's complement for signed types |\n| `x.checkedAdd(y)`, `x.checkedSub(y)`, `x.checkedMul(y)` | `T?` | `null` (otherwise `Some` of the result) |\n| `x.saturatingAdd(y)`, `x.saturatingSub(y)`, `x.saturatingMul(y)` | `T` | the nearest of `T`'s minimum and maximum |\n\nNone of them panics. Their names are reserved on integer types: an `impl` for an integer type cannot define a method\nwith one of these names (N0228). In C they are `static inline` helpers of `rt.h` with no undefined behaviour: a\nwrapping operation computes in the unsigned type of the same width and converts back with `cee_as_iN`\n(`c-backend.md` §6).\n\nBitwise operators and shifts are restricted to unsigned types in M1. This avoids the\nimplementation-defined and undefined parts of C's signed bit operations. Reinterpret\nwith `truncate<u32>(x)`. Relaxing this later is backward compatible. A shift amount that is a\nflexible literal is typed as `u32`. Other mismatches are N0207.\n\n## 7. Conversions\n\n- `e as T` is allowed only when **every** value of the source type is representable in `T`\n  (N0208 otherwise):\n  - identity;\n  - `uN → uM` and `iN → iM` for M ≥ N;\n  - `uN → iM` for M > N;\n  - `bool →` any integer type (`false`→0, `true`→1).\n  `isize`/`usize` use the target width. Floats (M11, §35.4): `f32 → f64`, and the integers that a float type holds\n  exactly.\n- `truncate<T>(e)`: `e` of any integer type, `T` any integer type. The result is `e`\n  modulo 2^bits(T), reinterpreted in `T` (two's complement). It is always defined. Use it for\n  narrowing and for signed↔unsigned reinterpretation.\n\n## 8. Calls and statements\n\n- A callee must be a package function, or one of the built-ins below (N0210 otherwise).\n  Functions are not values in M1 (since M11 they are, §36.1). The argument count must match (N0209), and each argument is\n  checked against its parameter type.\n- Built-ins:\n  - `Console.write(x)` and `Console.writeLine(x)`: `x` is an integer, a `bool`, or a string\n    literal. `writeLine()` with no argument writes a newline. Both return `void`.\n  - `Process.exit(code: i32)`: has type *never*.\n  - `truncate<T>(e)`: see §7. Exactly one type argument (N0216 for other generic arguments).\n- An expression statement must be a call (N0225). `1 + 2;` is rejected.\n- `break`/`continue` must be inside `while` or `loop` (N0214).\n- `return;` only in a `void` function. `return e;` only in a non-`void` function (N0215).\n\n## 9. Control flow facts\n\n- **Definite assignment.** A local may be read only if it is assigned on every path from its\n  declaration to the read (N0212). Compound assignment (`x += ",
    208 =>
        "1`) reads `x`. Code that is\n  unreachable is not checked. M1 has no moves, so the analysis is a forward must-analysis\n  over the structured statements; it moves to NIR in M2.\n- **Termination.** A statement *may complete* unless it is a `return`, `break` or\n  `continue`, or a call of type *never*. It also may not complete if it is a block containing\n  such a statement, an `if` with an `else` whose two branches both cannot complete, or a `loop`\n  with no `break` that targets it. A `while` loop may always complete, whatever its condition:\n  write `loop` for infinite loops. An `if` or `match` expression (§15.3) cannot complete when none of its\n  branches can: its condition or scrutinee does not complete, or every branch is a block that cannot complete.\n- **Missing return.** If the end of a non-`void` function body may be reached, that is N0213.\n\n## 10. Constants\n\n- `const N: T = e;` where `e` is a constant expression: literals, other constants, and unary,\n  binary, `as` and `truncate` applied to constant expressions. Calls are not constant (N0217).\n- Constants are evaluated at compile time with the runtime semantics of §6. An operation that\n  would panic at runtime (overflow, division by zero, bad shift) is a compile error (N0217).\n- A constant that depends on itself is N0218.\n- Uses of a constant are replaced by its value, typed as the constant's declared type.\n- **Constant arrays** (C9, ADR 0014): `T` may be `[E; N]` with `E` an integer type or `bool`, and `e` an array\n  literal `[e1, …, eN]` or repeat `[v; N]` of constant expressions. A use is the whole array's value, like an\n  array literal: it can be indexed and iterated, and bound to a local to be sliced or borrowed. Arrays of other\n  types, and of arrays, are N0216.\n- `@export` on a constant makes it C data (§28.4).\n\n## 11. `main`\n\nIf a function named `main` exists, it must take no parameters and return `i32` or\n`void` (N0219). Whether `main` is *required* is decided by `ceesafec build` (increment 1c).\n\n## 12. Structs (M2a)\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64;\n    y: i64;\n\n    fn sum(this) -> i64 => this.x + this.y;                    // method: `this` by value\n    fn origin() -> Point => Point { x: 0, y: 0 };             // static function\n}\n```\n\n- **Declaration.** Top level only. Field names and member names (fields and functions) are unique\n  within the struct (N0228). Field types are any non-`void` M1 type or another struct. A struct\n  must not contain itself by value, directly or through other structs (N0227: infinite size).\n  Recursive data needs indirection (`Box`, M3). Empty structs are allowed.\n- **Names.** A struct name is a package item (unique with functions and constants, N0201) and a\n  type name. In expressions, `S.f(...)` calls the static function `f` of `S`.\n- **Copy.** In M2a every struct is a Copy type: its fields are Copy and `drop` does not exist yet.\n  Assignment, argument passing and return copy the value. OD-7's rule (\"Copy iff all fields Copy\n  and no `drop`\") takes effect in 2c.\n- **Literals.** `S { f1: e1, …, fn: en }` must initialise **every** field exactly once (missing:\n  N0229, unknown: N0230, duplicate: N0231), in any order. Each `ei` is checked against the field's\n  type. Initialisers are **evaluated in source order** (`intermediate-representation.md`), not in\n  declaration order.\n- **Field access.** `e.f` where `e` has struct type `S` and `f` is a field of `S` has `f`'s type\n  (unknown field: N0230; `e` is not a struct: N0234). An access chain `a.b.c` is a *place* if `a`\n  is a local or `this`.\n- **Field assignment.** `a.f = e` and `a.f op= e` require the root local `a` to be a `var` (N0211).\n  `this` is immutable in a by-value method. The root must be definitely assigned *as a whole*\n  first (N0212): M2a has no partial initialisation.\n- **Methods.** A function in a struct whose first parameter is `this` is a method. `this` has the\n  struct's type and is an immutable binding (a copy for Copy structs). It is called as `e.m(args)`,\n  ",
    209 =>
        "where `e` is evaluated first and passed as `this`. A static function (no `this`) is called as\n  `S.f(args)`. Calling a method as `S.m()`, or a static function as `e.f()`, is N0233. `this`\n  anywhere other than a method's receiver position or body is N0232.\n- **Private fields (C7c).** A field declared `private` (`private data: T;`) is accessible only in the\n  source file that declares its struct (`language-spec.md` §5: \"visible within the declaring type or\n  file\"), and in any instance of a generic one there: read, assigned, borrowed and given in a literal.\n  Anywhere else, naming it is N0296. A struct with private fields can therefore only be made by code in\n  its own file, so types that cooperate, like a lock and its guard, can share their fields. This is the one place where a visibility\n  modifier takes effect within a package (§2). It lets a type keep an invariant that safe code cannot break,\n  such as \"the data is reached only through a guard\" (`integrations/cosmoos/c7/sync.cee`).\n  `public` and `internal` fields, and the default, stay visible throughout the package.\n- **Operators.** No operator applies to struct values in M2a (`==` included: N0207).\n  `Console.write` cannot print structs (N0207).\n- **Layout.** Fields are laid out in declaration order with C's rules (`@repr(C)` by default,\n  `memory-model.md` §2). The compiler computes size, alignment and field offsets from `TargetInfo`.\n  The generated C checks them with `_Static_assert` (`c-backend.md` §2), so a disagreement with the\n  real C compiler fails the build instead of miscompiling.\n\n## 13. References (M2b)\n\nThe algorithm that enforces these rules is in `lifetimes.md`.\n\n### 13.1 Types\n- `borrow T` (shared) and `borrow mut T` (exclusive), where `T` is any non-reference, non-`void`\n  type. `borrow borrow T` is rejected (N0246). At runtime a reference is a non-null pointer.\n- **Where references may appear (v0):**\n  - parameter types;\n  - `let` locals with an initialiser;\n  - return types (with a source list, §13.5).\n\n  They may **not** appear in struct fields (E3), constants, or `var` locals (N0246). Struct\n  fields of reference type need lifetime parameters (OD-3).\n- `borrow T` is Copy. A `borrow mut T` value is never copied: every use reborrows it (§13.3).\n\n### 13.2 Creating references\n- `borrow p` / `borrow mut p`, where `p` is a **place**: a local, `this`, a field path, or a path\n  through a reference such as `r.f` (`(*r).f`). Borrowing anything else is N0247; bind the value\n  to a local first.\n- `borrow mut p` requires `p` to be mutable: rooted at a `var` local, or reached through a\n  `borrow mut` reference (N0248). A `let` binding, a parameter, a by-value `this`, and anything\n  reached through a shared reference are immutable. That holds through a `borrow mut` or\n  `SliceMut` stored where a shared reference leads: in `h.v` with `h: borrow Holder` and\n  `v: borrow mut Vector<T>`, the vector cannot be changed, as with Rust's `& &mut T`.\n- **Call sites.**\n  - A `borrow T` parameter accepts a place or value of type `T`, borrowed implicitly. A value\n    becomes a temporary that lives until the end of the statement.\n  - A `borrow T` parameter also accepts `borrow T` or `borrow mut T`; the latter is coerced by a\n    shared reborrow.\n  - A `borrow mut T` parameter accepts `mut p`, where `p` is a mutable place of type `T`, or a\n    `borrow mut T` value (reborrowed).\n\n  The `mut` marker is required for a plain place and is rejected where the parameter is not\n  `borrow mut` (N0249). It makes mutation visible at the call site, as C#'s `ref` does.\n- **Receivers.** `fn m(borrow this)` and `fn m(borrow mut this)` declare reference receivers;\n  `this` then has type `borrow S` / `borrow mut S`. In `e.m(…)` the receiver is borrowed\n  implicitly, with no marker. For `borrow mut this`, `e` must be a mutable place or a\n  `borrow mut S` value (N0248).\n\n### 13.3 Using references\n- **Auto-deref.** Where a value of type `T` is needed (an operand, a by-value argument, an\n  ini",
    210 =>
        "tialiser of type `T`, a return of type `T`, `Console.write`), a `borrow T` or\n  `borrow mut T` operand is read through: `*r`. Field access `r.f` and method calls\n  `r.m()` also look through references.\n- **Writes through references.** `r = v`, `r op= v` and `r.f = v` write to the referent. This\n  requires `borrow mut` (N0211: \"cannot assign through a shared reference\"). A reference local\n  itself can never be re-pointed.\n- **Reborrow of `borrow mut`.** Using a `borrow mut T` value as a call argument, an initialiser\n  or a return value creates a new mutable loan of `*r`. The original reference is unusable while\n  the new one is live, and usable again afterwards (B4).\n  This holds for a field of type `borrow mut T` or `SliceMut<T>` (`s.left`) as for a variable: it is\n  reborrowed, not moved out of its struct, and inside a closure it is a mutable use of the struct.\n  Its referent must be mutable from where it is used (§13.2): a field reached through a shared\n  reference cannot be reborrowed mutably (N0248).\n- References support no operators of their own: `==` compares the referents (via auto-deref).\n\n### 13.4 Borrow rules (summary of `lifetimes.md`)\nWhile a loan is live (the reference, or something derived from it, is used later), the borrowed\nplace may not be:\n- written, mutably borrowed, or go out of scope, if the loan is shared;\n- accessed at all, if the loan is `borrow mut`.\n\nDiagnostics: N0240 (write), N0241 (conflicting borrow), N0242 (read while mutably borrowed),\nN0243 (out of scope or temporary dropped while borrowed).\n\n### 13.5 Returning references\n- A function returning `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` with exactly one reference parameter (counting\n  a reference receiver) returns something derived from that parameter.\n- Otherwise the return type names the sources: `fn longer(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) ->\n  borrow(a, b) T`. Missing, unknown or non-reference sources are N0245, and so is a\n  `borrow mut` result naming a shared parameter. After `borrow`, a `(` begins a source list only when it holds\n  names separated by commas and a type follows the `)`; otherwise it begins a parenthesised type (M11, §22.1), so\n  `borrow (T?)` is a reference to an optional and `borrow(a) (T?)` names a source.\n- The body may only return references derived from the named parameters (N0245). A reference to\n  a local or temporary of the function is N0244.\n\n## 14. Move types, destruction, `using` and `defer` (M2c)\n\nNormative for 2c. It implements `ownership.md` O1–O5 and R3. The NIR analyses are described in\n`intermediate-representation.md`.\n\n### 14.1 Copy and move types (OD-7)\n- **Copy:** integers, `bool`, and `borrow T`. A struct is Copy iff it has no `drop` block and every\n  field is Copy.\n- **Move types:** structs with a `drop` block or a move-type field, and `borrow mut T` (whose uses\n  are reborrows, §13.3, so in practice it is never moved out of a variable).\n- **Needs drop:** a struct with a `drop` block, or one with a field that needs drop. Every type that\n  needs drop is a move type. The converse does not hold.\n\n### 14.2 `drop` blocks\n```ceesafe\nstruct File {\n    fd: i32;\n    drop { Sys.close(this.fd); }      // `this` is `borrow mut File`\n}\n```\n- At most one `drop` block per struct (N0228). Inside it, `this` has type `borrow mut S`. The block\n  cannot be called explicitly. It runs exactly once when a value of `S` is destroyed (O4), and the\n  fields that need drop are destroyed **after** it, in declaration order.\n- Moving a field out of a value whose type has a `drop` block is N0252: the `drop` block needs the\n  whole value. Moving out of `this` inside `drop` is impossible anyway (§14.3).\n\n### 14.3 Moves\n- **Using a move-type place by value moves it**:\n  - an initialiser or right-hand side;\n  - a by-value argument or a by-value `this` receiver;\n  - a struct-literal field;\n  - a `return` value.\n\n  The place is then uninitialised. Reading, borrowing or moving it again before it is reassigned is\n  **N0250** (\"use of moved value\"); this is",
    211 =>
        " O2 and O3.\n- **`move p`** explicitly moves out of the place `p`, even for a Copy type. Afterwards `p` is\n  uninitialised, which ends a value's use early.\n- **Partial moves** (O2): moving `p.f` out of a local is allowed if no struct on the path from\n  `p` to `f` has a `drop` block. `p` is then partially initialised. `p.f` may be reassigned, after\n  which `p` is whole again. Reading `p` as a whole, or borrowing it, needs all of it initialised\n  (N0250).\n- **Not allowed:**\n  - moving out of a place reached through a reference (N0251), because the referent must stay\n    valid; this includes `this` in `borrow`/`borrow mut` methods;\n  - moving out of a `using` binding (N0253).\n- **Moves and borrows:** moving a place while it is borrowed is a conflict, treated like a write\n  (N0240).\n\n### 14.4 Initialisation (replaces §9's first bullet)\nThe analysis runs on NIR move paths (`intermediate-representation.md`):\n- A place must be **definitely initialised** to be read, borrowed or moved. If it may never have\n  been assigned, that is N0212. If it may have been moved, that is N0250.\n- Assigning a field `p.f` requires the rest of `p` to be initialised (no piecewise construction\n  of an uninitialised struct). Re-initialising a moved-out field is allowed.\n  - If some enclosing struct of `p.f` has a `drop` block, that whole struct must be initialised,\n    `f` included: such a struct is never partially moved (N0252), so a missing field means the\n    whole value was moved. This also covers a struct whose only field is `f` (found by the\n    mutation suite, M2d).\n\n### 14.5 Destruction (O4)\n- A value that needs drop is destroyed exactly once, at the first of these:\n  - the end of its variable's scope: the block end, a `break`/`continue` leaving the block, or a\n    `return`;\n  - the end of its statement, for a temporary. An `if` or `while` condition is its own statement:\n    its temporaries are destroyed once it is evaluated, before the body runs, on every evaluation\n    (M15 S2; before M15 they were never destroyed, #1);\n  - being **overwritten** by an assignment, in which case the old value is destroyed first.\n- **Order:** locals in reverse declaration order; then a struct's `drop` block; then its fields in\n  declaration order. By-value parameters are destroyed when the function returns, after the locals.\n- **Moved values are not destroyed.** A value that has been moved on *some* paths gets a runtime\n  **drop flag**. A partially moved struct destroys only the fields it still has.\n- **`Process.exit` and panics do not run destructors or deferred statements** (panic = abort,\n  OD-5).\n\n### 14.6 `using`\n`using x = e;` declares a local that cannot be moved out of (N0253) and is destroyed at the end\nof its block, like any local (R3). It documents that the value's lifetime *is* the scope: a lock\nguard, an arena, a file.\n\n*Since M4b:* a `using` binding is **pinned, not immutable**. Its contents may be changed and it may\nbe borrowed mutably (`arena.reset()`, `frame.enter()`). It cannot be assigned as a whole (N0211)\nor moved (N0253). This also lets scoped values that must be mutably borrowed, such as scratch\nframes, live in a `using` binding, since they cannot be `var`.\n\n### 14.7 `defer`\n`defer s;` schedules the statement `s` to run when the enclosing block exits on any path:\n- the block end;\n- `break`/`continue` out of it;\n- `return` (after the return value is computed).\n\nDeferred statements and local destructions run in reverse order of their position, so a `defer`\nruns before the locals declared before it are destroyed, and after the locals declared after it.\n`s` may not contain `return`, `break`, `continue` or another `defer` (N0254).\n\n## 15. Enums and `match` (M2d)\n\n```ceesafe\nenum Shape {\n    Empty,\n    Circle(i64),\n    Rect(i64, i64),\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> i64 {\n    match (s) {\n        Shape.Empty => { return 0; }\n        Shape.Circle(r) => { return 3 * r * r; }\n        Shape.Rect(w, h) => { return w * h; }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 15.1 Declarations and va",
    212 =>
        "lues\n- `enum E { V1, V2(T1, …), … }` at the top level. Variants are unique (N0228) and have zero or\n  more positional payload types: any non-reference, non-`void` type (E3 applies to payloads too).\n  An enum must not contain itself by value (N0227).\n- **Values.** `E.V` for a variant without payload; `E.V(e1, …)` with exactly the payload arity\n  (N0209). Each `ei` is checked against its payload type.\n- **Copy and drop.** An enum is Copy iff every payload type is Copy. It needs drop iff some payload\n  needs drop; destroying it destroys the payloads of the active variant. Enums have no `drop`\n  block (v0).\n- **Layout.**\n  - A tag (`u8` for ≤ 256 variants, else `u16`/`u32`), then a union of the variants' payloads, each\n    laid out like a struct.\n  - Size and alignment follow C's rules for `struct { tag; union { … }; }`. The generated C\n    asserts them.\n  - CeeSafe does not reorder or niche-optimise (v0).\n\n### 15.2 `match`\n```text\nMatchStmt = 'match' '(' Expr ')' '{' Arm* '}'\nArm       = Pattern '=>' Block ','?\nPattern   = '_' | IntLiteral | '-' IntLiteral | 'true' | 'false' | 'null'\n          | QualifiedName ('(' Payload (',' Payload)* ')')?        -- E.V, E.V(x, _), Ok(E.A(x)) (M11)\nPayload   = Binding | Pattern                                      -- a nested pattern: M11 (G1)\nBinding   = Identifier | 'var' Identifier | '_'        -- 'var': M3c (§18.4)\n```\nA payload that is a single identifier is a binding; `Some(…)`, `Ok(…)`, `Err(…)` and `E.V…` there are patterns.\n- This section's `match` is a **statement**; §15.3 has the expression form. Arms are tried in order; the first\n  that matches runs its block.\n- **Scrutinee types:**\n  - an enum: variant patterns;\n  - an integer: literal patterns of that type;\n  - `bool`: `true`/`false`;\n  - or a reference to one of these.\n- **Exhaustiveness** (N0256): enum matches must cover every variant or end with `_`. Integer\n  matches need `_`. `bool` matches need both values or `_`. An arm that can never match (a\n  repeated variant or literal, or anything after `_`) is N0257.\n- **Bindings.** A variant pattern binds each named payload to a new immutable local in the arm:\n  - **Scrutinee of type `E` (a value):** the binding is a *copy* of the payload. Since M3c a\n    non-Copy payload is moved out, and the match consumes its scrutinee (§18.4).\n  - **Scrutinee of type `borrow E` / `borrow mut E`:** the binding is a `borrow T` /\n    `borrow mut T` to the payload, and the borrow checker treats it like any reborrow of the\n    scrutinee.\n  - `_` binds nothing.\n- The scrutinee is evaluated once. When it is a place it is not moved (only Copy payloads are\n  read). A temporary scrutinee lives until the end of the `match` statement.\n- **Nested patterns** (M11, G1). In a payload's position, a pattern instead of a binding matches the payload itself:\n  a variant pattern on an enum payload (`Ok(E.A(x))`, `Some(Some(_))`), a literal on an integer or `bool` payload\n  (`Some(3)`), or `null` on an optional payload (`Ok(null)`). Each is checked against the payload's type exactly as\n  a whole `match` is checked against the scrutinee's, at any depth.\n  - **Order.** Arms are tried in order. Within an arm, the outer variant is tested first, then each nested pattern,\n    left to right and depth first. Testing only reads tags and values: an arm that does not match moves nothing.\n  - **Bindings** inside nested patterns follow the rules above. On a value scrutinee they copy, or move (§18.4).\n    On a reference scrutinee they are references to the nested payload.\n  - **A payload that is itself a reference** (`borrow E`) takes only a binding or `_`, not a nested pattern. Matching\n    through it is a separate `match` on that reference (N0205 otherwise).\n  - **Exhaustiveness and unreachable arms** are decided over the whole patterns. A match is exhaustive when every\n    value of the scrutinee's type matches some arm: N0256, whose message names one value that matches none, such\n    as `Ok(E.B)`. An arm is",
    213 =>
        " unreachable when every value it matches is matched by an earlier arm: N0257. The decision\n    is the usefulness algorithm of pattern-matching compilers (Maranget, *Warnings for pattern matching*, 2007), with\n    each enum's variants, `true`/`false`, `null`/`Some` and `Ok`/`Err` as the constructors. Integer literals never\n    cover their type: an integer position needs a binding or `_` somewhere.\n\n### 15.3 `if` and `match` expressions (M11, G5)\n```ceesafe\nlet sign = if (x < 0) -1 else if (x == 0) 0 else 1;\nlet name = match (shape) {\n    Shape.Circle(_) => \"circle\",\n    Shape.Square(side) => if (side > 10) \"big square\" else \"square\",\n    Shape.None => { return; },\n};\n```\n```text\nIfExpr    = 'if' '(' Expr ')' Branch 'else' (IfExpr | Branch)\nMatchExpr = 'match' '(' Expr ')' '{' (ValueArm (',' ValueArm)* ','?)? '}'\nValueArm  = Pattern '=>' Branch\nBranch    = Expr | Block\n```\n- **Where.** In expression position (an initialiser, an operand, an argument, a `return` value, …). An `if` or\n  `match` that begins a statement is the statement form (§9, §15.2), as before. An `if` expression needs its\n  `else` (N0100 otherwise).\n- **Branches.** A branch is an expression, the value of the whole when that branch is taken, or a block that\n  cannot complete (§9): one that ends in `return`, `break`, `continue` or a call of type *never*, such as\n  `{ return; }` or `{ Process.exit(1); }`. A block that can complete has no value: N0235. (A block that ends in a\n  value, as in Rust, is not part of G5.)\n- **Extent.** An expression branch extends as far right as an expression can: `if (c) 1 else 2 + 3` is\n  `if (c) 1 else (2 + 3)`. Write parentheses to use an `if` expression inside a larger one: `(if (c) 1 else 2) + 3`.\n  Value arms are separated by commas; a block arm needs none.\n- **Type.**\n  - With an expected type (§5), every expression branch is checked against it, and the whole has that type.\n  - Without one, the first expression branch that is neither flexible (§5) nor `null` is checked first, and its\n    type is the type of the whole; the other branches are checked against it. If every branch is flexible, the\n    type is its literals' default: `f64` if any is a float literal (§35.2), else `i32`. A branch of type *never* (and every block branch) fits any type. If no branch is an\n    expression, the whole has type *never*.\n- **Evaluation.** The condition (or scrutinee) is evaluated once, then exactly one branch. `match` expressions\n  follow §15.2 in everything else: the patterns, exhaustiveness (N0256) and unreachable arms (N0257), bindings by\n  copy, move or reference, and consuming a value scrutinee (§18.4).\n- **Ownership.** A branch's value is moved (or copied) into the result. A move in one branch leaves the place\n  maybe-moved after the whole, as after an `if` statement (§14.4). A reference produced by a branch carries that\n  branch's loans, so the result carries the loans of every branch that can produce it (`lifetimes.md`).\n- **Temporaries** made while evaluating a branch end when the branch ends, as at the end of a statement\n  (`lifetimes.md` §6). A reference to one cannot be the branch's value (N0243).\n\n## 16. Arrays, slices and `for` (M2d)\n\n### 16.1 Types\n- `[T; N]` is a fixed array of `N` elements. `N` is an integer literal (no suffix or `usize`) with\n  1 ≤ N ≤ 2^48 − 1, and the array is at most 2^31 − 1 bytes. It is laid out as `N` consecutive `T`s:\n  size `N × size(T)`, the alignment of `T`.\n  - **v0 restriction:** `T` must be a Copy type, so arrays are Copy and never need drop. Arrays of\n    move types need element-wise move tracking (future). Slices may have move-type elements\n    since M6b (§21.3).\n  - Otherwise arrays may appear anywhere a value may: locals, parameters, results, struct fields,\n    enum payloads and other arrays. Constants of array type are not supported yet (N0216).\n- `Slice<T>` / `SliceMut<T>` are **borrowed views** `(pointer, length)` of a run of `T`s in an array\n  or another slice. They fol",
    214 =>
        "low every rule for references (§13), with the same scoping:\n  - only in parameters, `let` locals with an initialiser, and return types with a source list;\n  - never in fields, payloads, array elements, constants or `var` locals (N0246);\n  - `Slice<T>` is Copy, and each use of a `SliceMut<T>` reborrows it; `SliceMut<T>` coerces to\n    `Slice<T>`;\n  - a slice holds a loan of the whole array (or slice) it was taken from (`lifetimes.md`);\n  - a function returning a slice has no `borrow(...)` source list: it must have exactly one\n    reference (or slice) parameter, which the result is derived from (§13.5's default rule);\n  - the element type follows the array rules: Copy, never a reference.\n- Internally, `Slice<T>` is `borrow [T]` and `SliceMut<T>` is `borrow mut [T]`, with `[T]` an\n  unsized array type that only exists behind a slice. That is why every reference rule applies.\n\n### 16.2 Values\n- `[e1, …, en]`: all elements have the same type (the expected element type, or the first\n  element's), n ≥ 1.\n- `[v; N]`: N copies of `v`.\n- `a.length` is a `usize`: the constant `N` for arrays, the stored length for slices. For an\n  array, `a` must be a place (a variable, field or element), because the constant would skip\n  evaluating it (N0216).\n\n### 16.3 Indexing and slicing\n- `a[i]`:\n  - `a` is an array or slice place, or a reference to an array;\n  - `i` is a `usize` (a flexible literal becomes `usize`);\n  - `i ≥ a.length` **panics** (\"index out of bounds\", reported at the index expression);\n  - `a[i]` is a place: it can be read (a copy), assigned (if mutable: an array rooted at a `var`\n    or reached through `borrow mut`, or a `SliceMut`), and borrowed.\n- `a[lo..hi]`, `a[lo..]`, `a[..hi]`, `a[..]` give a `Slice<T>` of elements `lo` up to but not\n  including `hi` (default `0` and `a.length`). `lo ≤ hi ≤ a.length` is checked; otherwise the\n  program **panics**.\n- `borrow mut a[lo..hi]` gives a `SliceMut<T>` and needs `a` to be mutable. As a call argument\n  for a `SliceMut<T>` parameter, `mut a[lo..hi]` means the same.\n- Slicing needs a place (N0247): slice a local, not a call result.\n- Evaluation order: the array place (with its own indexes), then `lo`, then `hi`. In an assignment\n  `a[i] = v`, the target's indexes are evaluated and checked before `v`.\n- The borrow checker treats all elements of an array as **overlapping** (no index reasoning in v0).\n  Borrowing `a[0]` and then writing `a[1]` while the borrow is live is rejected.\n\n### 16.4 `for`\nThe header is parenthesised, like `while` and `match`, so a name before the body is never read as a\nstruct literal.\n- `for (i in lo..hi) { … }`: `i` takes each value from `lo` up to but not including `hi`. Both bounds\n  are evaluated once, have the same integer type (flexible literals adapt), and `i` is immutable\n  in the body.\n- `for (x in seq) { … }`: `seq` is an array place, a slice, or a reference to an array. `x` is a copy\n  of each element from index 0 upward. The element is read when the loop reaches it, and the\n  length is read once at the start.\n- **Non-Copy elements** (M11, G2): when the element type `T` is not Copy, `x` is a `borrow T` to the element, in\n  place, instead of a copy (which could not be made: N0251). This is the rule of a `match` on a reference (§15.2). The\n  borrow checker treats it as a shared borrow of that element for as long as `x` is used. While it lives, the\n  sequence cannot be changed (§13.4, §23.1). A Copy element is still copied, as before.\n- `break` and `continue` behave as in `while`. `continue` proceeds to the next element.\n\n- The loop variable is immutable. It has the bounds' type for a range, and the element type\n  for a sequence (or `borrow T`, above). Like every local, it cannot shadow another (N0203).\n\n### 16.5 Bulk operations (M9b)\nTwo methods write a whole slice in one operation. A program computes the same thing with them as with\nthe element loop they replace; they exist so that the copy costs one check instead of one per element.\n\n",
    215 =>
        "```ceesafe\nfn copy(out: SliceMut<u8>, to: usize, src: Slice<u8>, from: usize, n: usize) {\n    out[to..to + n].copyFrom(src[from..from + n]); // two range checks, one length check, one copy\n}\n```\n\n- `d.copyFrom(s)`, with `d: SliceMut<T>` and `s: Slice<T>` (a `SliceMut<T>` coerces): if\n  `s.length != d.length` the program **panics** (\"slice lengths differ\", reported at the call) and\n  writes nothing. Otherwise each `d[i]` becomes `s[i]`, as if `s` were first copied to a temporary.\n  In safe code the two cannot overlap, because `s`'s loan would conflict with `d`'s (N0241, N0242);\n  if `unsafe` code makes them overlap (§27.3, `Slice.fromRaw`), the result is still defined.\n- `d.fill(v)`, with `v: T`: every element of `d` becomes `v`. Nothing is checked.\n- `T` must be Copy (N0259), because both methods copy elements.\n- The receiver is a `SliceMut<T>` (a parameter, a local or a call result; reborrowed, like any use of a\n  `SliceMut`), or a slicing expression `a[lo..hi]` in any of its forms, which is then taken as\n  `borrow mut a[lo..hi]`. That needs `a` to be mutable (N0248), as `mut a[lo..hi]` does for a\n  `SliceMut` argument. A `Slice<T>` receiver is N0211. Arrays and vectors are written through a slice:\n  `a[..].fill(0)`, `v.viewMut().fill(0)`.\n- Evaluation is left to right: the receiver (its place, bounds and range check), then the argument, then\n  the length check, then the writes. The receiver's loan starts before the argument is evaluated: these\n  calls are not two-phase (B5 applies only to `borrow mut this` methods of a place). So the argument\n  cannot read the destination: `s.fill(s[0])` is N0242, and `let v = s[0]; s.fill(v);` is the fix.\n- The result is `void`.\n- Not provided in M9b: a copy within one slice whose ranges may overlap, comparisons, searches.\n- **`d.swap(i, j)`** (M11 G8c), with `i, j: usize`: exchanges elements `i` and `j` of a `SliceMut<T>` (or of a\n  vector, which lends its elements mutably). `T` may be any element type, Copy or not: the elements are moved, not\n  copied. Both indices are checked first (panic \"index out of bounds\", reported at the call), and nothing changes\n  if either is out of range. `i == j` changes nothing. Effects: none.\n\nDiagnostics: N0259 (an element type that is not Copy, an invalid length, or a too-large array).\nThe other errors reuse N0205, N0211, N0216, N0230, N0246, N0247 and N0248 (`diagnostics.md`).\n\n## 17. Results and error propagation (M3a)\n\n```ceesafe\nenum ParseError { Empty, BadDigit(u8), Overflow }\n\nfn digit(c: u8) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (c < 48u8 || c > 57u8) { return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)); }\n    return Ok((c - 48u8) as u32);\n}\n\nfn twoDigits(a: u8, b: u8) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    return Ok(digit(a)? * 10 + digit(b)?);\n}\n```\n\n`Result`, `Ok`, `Err` and `AllocError` are built in (ADR 0003). User generics come in M6; the\nnames below then become library items with the same meaning.\n\n- **Splitting** (M13 G5): `s.splitAt(mid)` gives a `SliceHalves<T>` whose `left` is `s[..mid]` and `right` is\n  `s[mid..]`. `s.splitAtMut(mid)`, on a `SliceMut<T>` or a vector, gives a `SliceHalvesMut<T>` of two `SliceMut<T>`\n  that do not overlap, so both may be written at once, at a split known only at run time. Both panic (\"slice range\n  out of bounds\") if `mid` is past the end; the panic names lib/core's line, not the caller's. The halves borrow `s`\n  for as long as either lives. They are lib/core code (`SliceSplitting<T>`, `lib/core/slices.cee`).\n\n### 17.1 Types\n- `Result<T, E>` is a built-in enum with two variants, in this order: `Ok` with payload `T`, and\n  `Err` with payload `E`. Everything in §15.1 applies: layout, Copy iff `T` and `E` are Copy,\n  needs drop iff either does, and only the active variant's payload is destroyed.\n- `T` may be `void`, in which case `Ok` has no payload. `E` must not be `void`. Neither may be a\n  reference or slice (N0246, E3); arrays, structs, enums and results are fine.\n- A type contains `T` and `E` by value: `struct S { r: Result<S",
    216 =>
        ", i32>; }` is recursive (N0227).\n- `AllocError` is a built-in enum `{ OutOfMemory }` (Copy). The names `Result`, `Ok`, `Err` and\n  `AllocError` cannot be redeclared as items (N0201).\n- Two `Result` types are equal iff their `T` and their `E` are equal.\n\n### 17.2 Values\n- `Ok(e)`, `Ok()` (for `T = void`) and `Err(e)` need an **expected `Result` type** that fixes\n  `T` and `E`. That type comes from a declared variable type, a parameter type, a return\n  type (`return Ok(x);`), or a field or payload type. Without one, it is N0260 (\"write the\n  type\").\n- The payload is checked against `T` or `E` like a call argument against its parameter (§5).\n\n### 17.3 Matching\n- Patterns `Ok(x)`, `Ok(_)`, `Ok` (for `T = void`), `Err(x)` and `Err(_)` are written without a\n  type prefix. The bindings and exhaustiveness rules of §15.2 apply unchanged: a non-Copy payload\n  is bound by matching on `borrow r` (N0258 otherwise).\n\n### 17.4 `e?` and `e!`\n```text\nPostfix = Primary ( … | '?' | '!' )*      -- '!' is postfix only when it follows an operand\n```\n- **`e?` (propagate).**\n  - `e` must have a `Result<T, E>` type (N0262). The enclosing function must return\n    `Result<U, E>` with the same `E` (N0261); `From` conversions come in M6.\n  - If `e` is `Ok(v)`, the value of `e?` is `v` (of type `T`; `void` if `T` is `void`).\n  - If `e` is `Err(x)`, the function returns `Err(x)` at once, exactly like a `return`\n    statement. The statement's temporaries end, then every scope is left (drops and deferred\n    statements), innermost first. Like `return`, `?` is not allowed in a deferred statement\n    (N0254).\n- **`e!` (unwrap).** `e` must have a `Result<T, E>` type (N0262). The value is `v` for `Ok(v)`;\n  `Err(_)` **panics** with \"unwrapped an Err value\". Panic is abort, so nothing is destroyed.\n- In both cases `e` is evaluated once and consumed. The payload moves out of it (or is copied);\n  nothing else of `e` remains to be destroyed.\n- As an expression statement: `f()?;` and `f()!;` are allowed when the operand is a call, like\n  calls themselves (N0225 otherwise).\n- Precedence: postfix, like a call or member access: `parse(a)?.value` and `-x?` work as\n  expected (`-` applies to `x?`). `!` directly followed by `=` lexes as `!=`. Write `r! == 1`\n  with a space.\n\n### 17.5 Discarded values (correction to §14.5)\n- An expression statement whose value needs drop (for example a call `make();` returning a\n  `Res`) destroys the value at the end of the statement, like any other temporary. Before M3a\n  the value was never destroyed: an O4 violation, found while specifying 3a.\n\nDiagnostics: N0260 (no expected `Result` type for `Ok`/`Err`), N0261 (`?` in a function whose\nreturn type is not a `Result` with the same error type), N0262 (`?` or `!` on a non-`Result`).\n\n## 18. Boxes and allocators (M3b)\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n    fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) { this.balance += amount; }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var acct = open(7)!;\n    acct.deposit(100);       // through the box\n    return 0;\n}                            // the box is dropped: Account destroyed, memory freed\n```\n\n`Box`, `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator` are built in (ADR 0003).\n\n### 18.1 Allocator types\n- `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator` are built-in, field-less, Copy structs. They are the only\n  **allocator types** in M3; user allocators come with interfaces in M6 (`memory-model.md` §3.2).\n  Their values are written as struct literals: `SystemHeap {}`.\n- `SystemHeap` is the platform heap (`malloc`/`free` in hosted and CosmoOS userland builds).\n- `FailingAllocator` allocates from the platform heap, but can be told to fail, for testing\n  error paths in the language itself:\n  - `FailingAllocator.failAfter(n: u64)`: the next `n` allocations through `FailingAllocator`\n    succeed, and every later one fails;\n  - `FailingAllocator.reset()`: alloca",
    217 =>
        "tions never fail (the initial state).\n  The countdown is process-wide; frees are never affected.\n- Their names, and `Box`, cannot be declared as items (N0201).\n\n### 18.2 `Box<T, A>`\n- `Box<T, A>` owns exactly one value of type `T` in memory obtained from allocator type `A`\n  (H1). `Box<T>` means `Box<T, SystemHeap>`. `A` must be an allocator type (N0263). `T` is any\n  value type except `void` and references or slices (N0246).\n- A box is a **move type** that needs drop, and is one pointer wide (8 bytes on LP64).\n  Destroying it destroys `T` (its drop glue), then returns the memory to `A`.\n- A type does **not** contain the target of a `Box` by value, so recursive types are built with\n  boxes: `enum List { Nil, Cons(i32, Box<List>) }` is allowed (no N0227).\n- **Creation** (built-in functions):\n  - `Box.create(v)`: `Result<Box<T>, AllocError>`;\n  - `Box.createIn(a, v)` with `a` a value of allocator type `A`: `Result<Box<T, A>, AllocError>`.\n  `T` is the type of `v`. An expected type `Result<Box<X, _>, _>` provides the type hint for `v`\n  (so `Box.create(5)` can make a `Box<u8>`). `v` is moved into the call. If the allocation\n  fails, `v` is destroyed and the result is `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`.\n- **Access through a box** works like access through a reference (§13.3), with the box's own\n  mutability:\n  - fields `b.f`, elements `b[i]` and method calls `b.m()` reach `T` (auto-deref);\n  - the contents are mutable iff the box is: the box variable is `var`, or the box itself is\n    reached through `borrow mut` or another mutable place. `b.f = 1` needs a `var b`;\n  - a `Box<T>` argument for a `borrow T` parameter borrows the contents, and `mut b` lends them\n    for a `borrow mut T` parameter;\n  - where a Copy `T` is expected, a `Box<T>` is read through (auto-deref);\n  - a `borrow Box<T>` converts to `borrow T`, and a `borrow mut Box<T>` to `borrow mut T` or\n    `borrow T`: a reborrow of the contents (for example, a payload bound by reference in a match);\n  - `borrow b` borrows the box itself (`borrow Box<T>`).\n- **Borrowing rules:** a place inside a box is part of the box. Moving, overwriting or dropping\n  the box while a loan of its contents is live is rejected (N0240, N0243), as for any place and\n  its fields. (Unlike a reference going out of scope, a box going out of scope destroys what it\n  points to.)\n- **Moving out of a box's contents**: see §18.3 (M3c).\n- No null boxes exist. A `Box` is never Copy and has no `==`.\n\nDiagnostics: N0263 (an allocator argument that is not an allocator type).\n\n### 18.3 Moving out of a box (M3c)\n- A box held in an **owned place** owns its contents as a part of that place: a local, or a\n  field path of one, not reached through a reference. Its fields may be moved out\n  (`let l = n.left;`, `move n.left`), exactly like fields of a struct local (§14.3–14.4):\n  - the box cannot be used as a whole again (moved, passed, matched, borrowed) until every\n    moved-out field is re-initialised by assignment (N0250);\n  - a field of a `T` with a `drop` block cannot be moved out (N0252);\n  - through a reference, a box's contents are as immovable as anything else (N0251).\n- Destroying a partially moved box destroys the fields still initialised (in declaration order),\n  then returns the memory to the allocator. `T`'s drop glue does not run, and `T` has no `drop`\n  block by the rule above.\n- There is no way to move the whole contents out in v0 (`T` would need an unbox operation, M6).\n\n### 18.4 Moving payloads out in `match` (M3c; replaces N0258)\n```text\nBinding = Identifier | 'var' Identifier | '_'\n```\n- `var x` makes a mutable binding; `x` an immutable one. Both may bind any payload type.\n- **Consuming match.** If the scrutinee is a value (not a reference) and some arm binds a\n  non-Copy payload, the match **consumes** its scrutinee:\n  - the scrutinee is moved when the match starts. A place scrutinee is left uninitialised, as by\n    `move`; a `using` binding cannot be consumed (N0253);\n  - in an arm whose patter",
    218 =>
        "n lists payloads (`E.V(…)`), every payload of the variant moves out:\n    named ones into their bindings, and `_` ones into hidden locals of the arm. Payloads not bound\n    by name are therefore destroyed when the arm ends, before the bindings' own scope ends.\n    A payload matched by a nested pattern (M11) moves into a hidden local too, and the same rule applies to it:\n    if its pattern lists payloads, they move out of it in turn;\n  - in any other arm (`_`, `E.V` without a list, a variant without payload), the value is\n    destroyed when the match statement ends.\n- Otherwise (Copy payloads only, or a reference scrutinee) the rules of §15.2 apply unchanged.\n- N0258 is no longer reported (since M3c).\n\n### 18.5 Destroying deep structures (R-9)\n- A box's drop glue is recursive in general: destroying a `T` destroys its fields, which may own\n  further boxes. For a recursive type that would use stack in proportion to the structure's depth.\n- **Tail iteration.** The compiler finds the *tail* of `T`: the last field (in destruction order) that\n  needs drop, followed through structs, and through enum variants' last payloads, until it\n  reaches a `Box<T>` again. If there is one, `Box<T>`'s glue becomes a loop:\n  - destroy everything of the current `T` except the tail, in the normal order;\n  - take the tail box's pointer (null for variants that end elsewhere);\n  - free the current memory, then continue with the tail's contents.\n- Only the moment the memory is freed changes: the current node is freed before the tail is\n  destroyed instead of after. No program can observe that difference; every `drop` block runs in\n  the order §14.5 defines.\n- Consequence: lists (`Cons(x, Box<List>)`) are destroyed in constant stack depth. Trees use\n  stack in proportion to the number of non-tail steps on a path (the left children in\n  `{ left, right }`). Balanced trees therefore use logarithmic depth, and a left-degenerate tree\n  still recurses (R-9 records this).\n\n## 19. Regions (M4)\n\n`Arena`, and in later increments `FixedBufferAllocator`, `ScratchFrame`, `Pool<T>` and\n`PoolBox<T>`, are built in (ADR 0004). The safety rules are R1–R4 (`ownership.md`): an\nallocation borrows its region.\n\n### 19.1 Scoped types (M4a)\nA type is **scoped** if a value of it may hold loans:\n- references and slices (§13, §16);\n- `Result<T, E>` whose `T` or `E` is scoped (a *scoped result*);\n- the scoped region values: `FixedBufferAllocator` (§19.4), `ScratchFrame` (§19.5) and\n  `PoolBox<T>` (§19.6).\n\nEvery rule for references applies to scoped types (N0246):\n- they may appear only in parameters, locals with an initialiser, and return types;\n- never in fields, payloads of user enums, array elements, box contents or constants.\n\nReferences and slices must be `let` locals, because assigning to a reference writes through it.\n*Since M4c,* other scoped values (scoped results and region values) may be `var`: assigning one\nreplaces it, like any value. For the borrow checker, the local then holds the loans of every value\nit is assigned (a sound over-approximation).\n\nA function returning a scoped type needs return sources: the default rule (one reference\nparameter) or a `borrow(...)` list (§13.5). In this rule, \"reference parameter\" now means\n\"parameter of a scoped type\", and the receiver `this` counts when it is borrowed.\n\nThe borrow checker treats every local of a scoped type as holding loans (`lifetimes.md` §3).\nMoving or copying a scoped value moves or copies its loans, as for references. A scoped\nresult's loans reach the `Ok` payload through `?`, `!` and `match`.\n\n### 19.2 `Arena` (M4a)\n```ceesafe\nfn process(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap {}, 65536u64);\n    let counts = arena.allocArray(256usize, 0u32)?;   // SliceMut<u32>, a loan of `arena`\n    for (b in input) { counts[b as usize] += 1u32; }\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for (c in counts) { if (c != 0u32) { distinct += 1; } }\n    return Ok(distinct);             ",
    219 =>
        "                 // a u64, not arena memory\n}                                                     // the arena frees all its memory here\n```\n- `Arena` is a built-in move type that needs drop. It is not scoped: it owns its memory, which\n  it obtains from a free-standing allocator.\n- `Arena.create(a, chunkSize)`, with `a` an allocator value (`SystemHeap {}`, `FailingAllocator {}`)\n  and `chunkSize: u64`, makes an empty arena. It cannot fail: memory is obtained in chunks of\n  `chunkSize` bytes when needed. An allocation larger than a chunk gets a chunk of its own.\n- **Allocation** (R1). Both methods take `borrow this` and return a scoped result that carries a\n  **shared loan of the arena**:\n  - `arena.alloc(v)`: `Result<borrow mut T, AllocError>`, with `v` moved into fresh arena memory;\n  - `arena.allocArray(n, fill)` with `n: usize`: `Result<SliceMut<T>, AllocError>`, `n` copies\n    of `fill`. `T` must be Copy. `n` may be 0 (an empty slice); `n × size(T)` beyond the address\n    space is `Err(OutOfMemory)`.\n\n  The returned references are mutable: each allocation is fresh memory that nothing else refers to.\n- **R2:** `T` must not need drop, and must not be scoped (no references or slices in arena memory,\n  E3). Both are N0264.\n- `arena.reset()` takes `borrow mut this` and releases all memory. Because it is a mutable borrow,\n  it is rejected while any allocation is live (B1/B2). Dropping or moving the arena is likewise\n  rejected while allocations are live.\n- Destroying an arena releases all its memory at once (bulk release). Since no allocation\n  needs drop (R2), this equals destroying each allocation.\n- Failure: if a chunk cannot be obtained from the allocator, the allocation returns\n  `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`; the arena stays usable.\n\n### 19.3 What cannot escape (M4a, the R1 consequences)\n- An allocation cannot be returned from the function that owns the arena: N0244, like any\n  reference to a local.\n- It cannot be stored in a box, a struct, an enum payload or an array: those cannot hold scoped\n  values (N0246).\n- It cannot outlive the arena: a `reset`, drop, move or scope end of the arena while the\n  allocation is live is N0240–N0243.\n\nDiagnostics: N0264 (a type that a region cannot hold).\n\n### 19.4 `FixedBufferAllocator` (M4b)\n```ceesafe\nfn countWords(text: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u32, AllocError> {\n    var storage = [0u8; 4096];\n    let fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);   // a mutable loan of `storage`\n    let spans = fba.allocArray(64usize, Span { start: 0u32, len: 0u32 })?;\n    ...\n}\n```\n- `FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut s)` makes an allocator over caller-provided bytes. `s` is a\n  mutable place of type `[u8; N]`, or a `SliceMut<u8>`. The allocator holds a **mutable loan**\n  of the storage, so the storage cannot be read or written while the allocator or any of its\n  allocations is live.\n- `FixedBufferAllocator` is **scoped** (§19.1): a `let` local, a parameter or a return type with\n  sources, never stored. It is a move type without drop; nothing is freed.\n- `alloc(v)` and `allocArray(n, fill)` work as for `Arena` (§19.2), including R2 (N0264). Each\n  allocation is aligned within the storage. When the remaining bytes are not enough, the result\n  is `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`; the storage is never grown. There is no `reset`: to reuse the\n  storage, let the allocator end and make a new one.\n- An allocation's loans include the allocator's loan of the storage, so the storage stays\n  borrowed while any allocation is live, even after the allocator's last use.\n\n### 19.5 Scratch frames (M4b; ADR 0004)\n```ceesafe\nfn render(n: usize) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using frame = Scratch.enter();               // a root frame\n    let buf = frame.allocArray(n, 0u8)?;         // scoped to `frame`\n    {\n        using inner = frame.enter();             // a nested frame borrows `frame` mutably\n        let tmp = inner.alloc(1u64)?;\n        ...\n    }                                            // `inner`'s memory is rele",
    220 =>
        "ased here\n    return Ok(buf.length as u64);\n}                                                // `frame`'s memory is released here\n```\n- Scratch memory is a runtime-owned stack of heap chunks (`SystemHeap`); it is not the native stack.\n- `Scratch.enter()` makes a **root frame**. Only one root frame may be live at a time (per\n  thread; v0 is single-threaded): entering a second one **panics** (\"a scratch frame is already\n  active\"). Code that needs scratch memory below a frame receives the frame\n  (`borrow ScratchFrame`) and nests.\n- `frame.enter()` makes a **nested frame**. For the borrow checker a frame has two parts: its\n  *memory*, which allocations borrow, and its *allocation right*, which every allocation call\n  uses briefly. The rules:\n  - an allocation result carries a shared loan of the frame's memory;\n  - each `alloc`/`allocArray` call needs a shared borrow of the allocation right for the call\n    only;\n  - `enter()` takes a **mutable** loan of the parent's allocation right, which the nested frame\n    carries.\n\n  While a nested frame is live, the parent therefore cannot allocate or nest again (B1), but\n  allocations already made from the parent stay usable: they lie below the nested frame's\n  memory. Destroying or moving a frame conflicts with both parts. This makes frames strictly LIFO.\n- Calling a frame method needs a frame that is a place (`using frame = …`): N0247 otherwise.\n- `ScratchFrame` is scoped (§19.1) and needs drop. Destroying a frame releases everything\n  allocated from it (and from frames nested in it), and returns chunks to the heap.\n- `alloc(v)` and `allocArray(n, fill)` work as for `Arena`, including R2. A failure to obtain a\n  chunk is `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)`.\n- Frames are bound with `using` (R3; §14.6): nesting borrows the frame mutably, and a scoped\n  value cannot be a `var`. Since frames are scoped, they cannot be returned from the function\n  that entered them.\n\n### 19.6 `Pool<T>` and `PoolBox<T>` (M4c)\n```ceesafe\nstruct Particle { x: i32; v: i32; }\n\nfn simulate() -> Result<i32, AllocError> {\n    using pool: Pool<Particle> = Pool.create(SystemHeap {}, 64u32);\n    var a = pool.take(Particle { x: 0, v: 2 })?;     // PoolBox<Particle>: a loan of `pool`\n    let b = pool.take(Particle { x: 10, v: -1 })?;\n    a.x += a.v;                                      // through the box\n    return Ok(a.x + b.x);\n}                                                    // b, a, then the pool are destroyed\n```\n- `Pool<T>` is a built-in allocator for up to `capacity` values of one type `T`.\n  - `Pool.create(a, capacity)`, with `a` an allocator value and `capacity: u32`, makes an empty\n    pool. `T` comes from the expected type (`let pool: Pool<T> = …`); without one it is N0260.\n  - It cannot fail: the slots are allocated from `a` at the first `take`.\n  - `Pool<T>` is a move type that needs drop; destroying it returns the slots to `a`. It is not\n    scoped.\n- `pool.take(v)` takes `borrow this` and returns `Result<PoolBox<T>, AllocError>`: `v` moves into\n  a free slot. The result is `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)` when all slots are taken, or when the\n  slots cannot be allocated; `v` is then destroyed.\n- `PoolBox<T>` owns one slot and its value, like a `Box` (§18.2):\n  - auto-deref, the box's own mutability, borrowing its contents, and moving fields out of it\n    (§18.3) all work as for `Box`;\n  - it is a move type that needs drop: destroying it destroys the `T` (its drop glue), then\n    returns the slot to the pool. Values are destroyed one by one, so **`T` may need drop**: R2\n    does not apply to pools;\n  - it is **scoped**: it carries the shared loan of the pool from `take`. It cannot be returned\n    past the pool, stored in a field or box, or outlive the pool (§19.3);\n  - `T` must not be scoped (E3; N0264).\n- Destroying the pool while a `PoolBox` from it is live is rejected (B2), so every slot is free\n  when the pool is destroyed.\n\n## 20. Generics (M6a)\n\n```ceesafe\nstruct Pair<T> {\n    first: T;\n    second",
